window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A new apprentice strikes an arc on a scrap plate for the first time and glances at it for a second without a helmet. Several hours later his eyes feel gritty and painful, and bright light hurts. Which condition does this ultraviolet exposure from the arc most directly cause in the front of the eye?", "options": {"A": "Retinal detachment caused by infrared heat focused through the lens onto the retina.", "B": "Photokeratitis, a corneal inflammation called arc eye or welder's flash, appearing hours after exposure.", "C": "Cataract formation developing within hours from heat stored in the lens of the eye.", "D": "Chemical conjunctivitis caused by shielding gas dissolving into the tear film and irritating the eyelids."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Photokeratitis, a corneal inflammation called arc eye or welder's flash, appearing hours after exposure.): Ultraviolet light burns the corneal surface much like a sunburn, and symptoms are delayed by several hours. Cataracts and retinal damage come from long term or much more intense exposures, not a single brief glance."}, {"id": 2, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop instructor hands students a safety document that OSHA and employers reference widely for protecting welders, cutters, and bystanders during arc welding, oxyfuel work, and allied processes. The document covers ventilation, fire prevention, protective clothing, and electrical hazards. Which publication is the instructor most likely holding up for the class?", "options": {"A": "AWS D1.1, the Structural Welding Code for Steel, which sets weld quality acceptance limits for buildings.", "B": "AWS A2.4, the standard symbols document used for reading weld, brazing, and inspection symbols on drawings.", "C": "ANSI Z49.1, Safety in Welding, Cutting, and Allied Processes, published by the American Welding Society.", "D": "ASME Section IX, which governs qualification of welding procedures and welder performance for pressure vessels."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (ANSI Z49.1, Safety in Welding, Cutting, and Allied Processes, published by the American Welding Society.): Z49.1 is the national safety standard for welding and cutting operations. The other three documents address structural quality, drawing symbols, and pressure part qualification rather than shop safety."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student asks why a stick welding machine can give a painful shock even before an arc is struck. The instructor explains that the machine has an open circuit voltage present between the electrode and the work whenever it is switched on. Manual arc welding power sources keep that open circuit voltage below roughly what level?", "options": {"A": "About 12 volts, which matches the electrical system of a typical passenger car battery.", "B": "About 80 volts, the upper limit commonly applied to manual arc welding power sources.", "C": "About 240 volts, which matches the household circuit used for a clothes dryer.", "D": "About 480 volts, which matches the three phase primary supply feeding the shop building."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 80 volts, the upper limit commonly applied to manual arc welding power sources.): Open circuit voltage on manual machines is typically 80 volts or less. That is far below the supply voltage, yet a sweating welder can still receive a serious shock from it."}, {"id": 4, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welding class has finished tack welding a bracket on a shop mezzanine, and wood pallets sit on the floor below. The hot work permit lists a fire watch. After the last arc is extinguished, how long should that fire watch generally stay in the area to catch smoldering fires that can start from sparks?", "options": {"A": "Five minutes, because steel cools below ignition temperature almost as soon as the arc stops.", "B": "Until the welder lifts the helmet, since only visible sparks signal a fire risk.", "C": "Two hours, a period required only when the work was done inside a closed tank.", "D": "At least 30 minutes after the hot work ends, which is the common fire code practice."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At least 30 minutes after the hot work ends, which is the common fire code practice.): Sparks can lodge in cracks and smolder before flames appear, so a post work watch of at least 30 minutes is standard. Five minutes is far too short for hidden ignition."}, {"id": 5, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A first year student arrives for a stick welding lab wearing thin cotton gloves bought at a discount store. The instructor says gloves for this process must handle spatter, radiant heat, and hot electrode stubs. Which glove choice is most appropriate for shielded metal arc welding in a shop?", "options": {"A": "Thin latex gloves that follow the hand shape and give the best feel for the holder.", "B": "Dry, heavy leather gauntlet gloves with a lining that cover the wrist and lower forearm.", "C": "Knit nylon gloves with a rubber palm coating to improve grip on the electrode holder.", "D": "Cotton canvas gloves, which resist heat well and do not conduct current to the hand."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dry, heavy leather gauntlet gloves with a lining that cover the wrist and lower forearm.): Leather gauntlets resist spatter and heat and insulate when kept dry. Latex and nylon melt or burn, and thin cotton offers little protection against hot slag or electrode stubs."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "During maintenance on a plasma cutting table, an instructor applies a personal lock and tag to the main disconnect switch. At the end of the shift she leaves the building with the key, and another instructor wants to restore power. Under standard lockout and tagout practice, who may remove that lock?", "options": {"A": "Any supervisor who holds a master key and verbally confirms that the repair work is finished.", "B": "The first person arriving at the machine next morning who needs power to start work.", "C": "Only the employee who applied it, unless a formal employer removal procedure applies.", "D": "Whichever instructor has the greatest seniority among those assigned to the shop that day."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Only the employee who applied it, unless a formal employer removal procedure applies.): Lockout protects the person who placed the lock, so removal belongs to that person. Exceptions exist only through a written employer procedure that verifies the person is not in danger."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student is assigned to weld stainless steel inside a small tank with gas tungsten arc welding and argon shielding gas. The supervisor explains that argon is heavier than air and collects in low spaces. What minimum oxygen concentration should a gas monitor show before entry is considered acceptable?", "options": {"A": "10 percent oxygen, because the human body needs only a small amount of oxygen when resting.", "B": "25 percent oxygen, which gives workers an added margin of breathing air inside the tank.", "C": "21 percent exactly, since any lower reading means the tank must be sealed off permanently.", "D": "19.5 percent oxygen by volume, checked with a calibrated monitor before entry."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (19.5 percent oxygen by volume, checked with a calibrated monitor before entry.): Below 19.5 percent oxygen the atmosphere is considered deficient, and argon can displace air quickly in low spots. Readings well above normal air create a fire hazard rather than a safety margin."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student must set up a portable stick welding station on a concrete shop floor, but wooden benches and stacked cardboard sit nearby. The hot work policy requires that combustibles be removed or protected. Within what distance of the work should combustible materials be moved or covered with fire resistant shields?", "options": {"A": "5 feet, roughly the length of an electrode lead extension on a small machine.", "B": "10 feet, the distance that sparks usually travel from a flat position fillet weld on plate.", "C": "35 feet (11 m) in every direction, including below, unless shielded with fire resistant covers.", "D": "100 feet, the distance usually required for outdoor hot work near large fuel storage tanks."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (35 feet (11 m) in every direction, including below, unless shielded with fire resistant covers.): Fire codes and Z49.1 guidance use a 35 foot radius because sparks and spatter can bounce or fall far from the arc. Anything that cannot be moved must be shielded."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Before starting an oxyfuel cutting job near wooden crates, a student looks for a fire extinguisher at the station. The wall holds several types. Which rated extinguisher is the most suitable for ordinary combustibles such as wood and paper plus small energized electrical equipment fires?", "options": {"A": "A class D extinguisher intended for combustible metals such as magnesium and titanium shavings.", "B": "A class K extinguisher designed for commercial kitchen cooking oils and animal fats in fryers.", "C": "A carbon dioxide extinguisher that is rated only for fires in wood and paper materials.", "D": "A multipurpose dry chemical extinguisher rated ABC, kept within easy reach of the work area."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A multipurpose dry chemical extinguisher rated ABC, kept within easy reach of the work area.): ABC dry chemical units handle ordinary combustibles, flammable liquids, and electrical fires. Class D and class K units are specialized, and carbon dioxide is not rated for class A fires."}, {"id": 10, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student flips the helmet up between passes to chip slag and strike the bead with a chipping hammer. The instructor reminds him that chipping sends hot fragments flying toward the face. What eye protection should be worn under the helmet for these tasks in the shop?", "options": {"A": "Tinted lenses of shade 5 that protect against both arc light and flying slag particles.", "B": "Clear safety glasses with side shields marked as meeting the ANSI Z87.1 impact standard.", "C": "Prescription eyeglasses without side shields, because the hardened lenses already resist impact from chips.", "D": "No added eyewear, since the helmet lens is lowered whenever hot slag is being removed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clear safety glasses with side shields marked as meeting the ANSI Z87.1 impact standard.): Safety glasses rated to Z87.1 with side shields protect against slag and also against stray arc flash when the helmet is lifted. Ordinary eyeglasses lack side protection and impact rating."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student grinds the zinc coating off a galvanized plate, then welds the joint with GMAW in a small room with poor ventilation. That evening he develops chills, fever, a metallic taste, and muscle aches that clear up within a day. Which hazard most likely explains these symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Welder's flash from ultraviolet light reflected and scattered by the bright zinc coating on the plate.", "B": "Carbon monoxide poisoning from incomplete combustion of the argon and carbon dioxide shielding gas mixture.", "C": "Metal fume fever from breathing zinc oxide fume released as the coating vaporizes.", "D": "Heat stroke caused by preheating the galvanized plate before the root pass in a warm room."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Metal fume fever from breathing zinc oxide fume released as the coating vaporizes.): Zinc oxide fume causes flu-like metal fume fever that typically resolves in a day or two. Ventilation or removing the coating at the joint prevents it, whereas flash affects only the eyes."}, {"id": 12, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student runs 1/8 inch E7018 electrodes at about 120 amps on a mild steel plate and reaches for the helmet lens chart posted in the shop. The chart lists several shade numbers by process and electrode size. Which filter lens shade is the customary choice for this stick welding setting?", "options": {"A": "Shade 4, the filter normally used for light torch brazing on thin sheet metal work.", "B": "Shade 14, intended for carbon arc welding and very high current heavy plate welding.", "C": "Shade 2, the light filter used for laboratory shields and light torch soldering jobs.", "D": "Shade 10, within the range recommended for stick electrodes up to 5/32 inch."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shade 10, within the range recommended for stick electrodes up to 5/32 inch.): Common charts place small and medium stick electrodes near shade 10. Lighter shades leave the eyes exposed to an arc brighter than they can tolerate, causing flash burns."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student who normally uses shade 10 on 1/8 inch electrodes moves to 1/4 inch E7018 electrodes at much higher amperage on thick plate. The arc looks noticeably brighter through the lens. Which change to the helmet filter is most appropriate for this heavier stick welding work?", "options": {"A": "Move to a lighter lens near shade 8, because large electrodes run cooler.", "B": "Keep shade 10 unchanged, because the correct lens depends only on the welding process used.", "C": "Move to a darker lens near shade 12, because higher current produces a brighter arc.", "D": "Move to a shade 6 lens, because the thicker flux covering absorbs part of the light."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move to a darker lens near shade 12, because higher current produces a brighter arc.): Arc brightness rises with current and electrode size, so recommended shade numbers rise too. Shade 12 is typical for 3/16 to 1/4 inch stick electrodes."}, {"id": 14, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student is gas welding 1/4 inch mild steel plate with an oxyacetylene torch and wants goggles that cut the glare of the flame and the yellow sodium flare. Which goggle lens shade is a typical selection for this medium thickness oxyfuel welding work?", "options": {"A": "Shade 1 clear goggles, since gas flames give off no light that can hurt the eyes.", "B": "Shade 12 lenses, the same dark filter used for heavy stick welding on thick plate.", "C": "Shade 5 or 6 goggle lenses, darker than the filters used for brazing.", "D": "Shade 14 lenses, needed because an oxyacetylene flame is as bright as a carbon arc."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shade 5 or 6 goggle lenses, darker than the filters used for brazing.): Gas welding calls for filters in the shade 4 to 6 range depending on thickness, with thicker work taking the darker end. Arc welding shades are far too dark for a torch flame."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student welds 304 stainless steel in a booth that has a movable fume extractor arm. The instructor explains that fume should be controlled at its source before relying on a respirator. Which action best applies this principle for chromium and manganese fume released from the weld pool?", "options": {"A": "Lean into the plume to watch the puddle and rely on the helmet to block fume.", "B": "Place the extractor hood near the arc so the plume is captured at the source.", "C": "Open a large door and stand in the doorway while welding so wind carries fume away.", "D": "Switch to a thinner filler wire so that less metal evaporates into the air."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Place the extractor hood near the arc so the plume is captured at the source.): Local exhaust placed near the arc removes fume before it spreads, and keeping the head out of the plume reduces exposure further. A helmet does not stop fume."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "On a humid day a student's leather gloves are soaked with sweat. He is kneeling on a steel table over damp concrete while stick welding, and he feels a tingling when he touches the electrode holder. What should he do first to correct the unsafe condition?", "options": {"A": "Increase the amperage slightly so the arc starts fast and he touches the holder less.", "B": "Shut off the machine, then switch to dry gloves and a dry insulating surface.", "C": "Wrap a wet rag around the holder handle so the heat and tingling are absorbed away.", "D": "Keep welding but touch the electrode only with the back of his hand during changes."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shut off the machine, then switch to dry gloves and a dry insulating surface.): Wet gloves and a conductive wet floor greatly lower body resistance and provide a path for current. Dry insulation between the welder and the work is the basic control."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student welds a heavy plate suspended from a crane by a chain and clamps the work lead to a table leg three feet away. Sparks appear at the chain links while the machine is running. What is the best correction for this setup?", "options": {"A": "Connect the work lead to the crane hook so the chain becomes the main return conductor.", "B": "Clamp the work lead directly to the workpiece near the weld.", "C": "Wrap the chain with a second cable to raise its capacity for carrying welding current.", "D": "Switch to a longer electrode holder lead to reduce the current that reaches the chain."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clamp the work lead directly to the workpiece near the weld.): Welding current takes any available path back to the machine, and chains, cranes, and cables can be damaged by arcing. Clamping close to the weld keeps the circuit controlled."}, {"id": 18, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "While inspecting a stick welder before class, a student finds the electrode lead insulation worn through near the holder, with bare copper strands visible. The lab is busy and the job planned is short. What is the correct action regarding that cable?", "options": {"A": "Wrap the exposed section with several layers of masking tape and continue the planned job.", "B": "Use the cable only on days when the floor stays dry and nobody is standing nearby.", "C": "Tag the cable out of service and have it repaired or replaced before any further use.", "D": "Wear a second pair of thick gloves, since leather insulates against the exposed copper strands."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tag the cable out of service and have it repaired or replaced before any further use.): Exposed conductors can shock the welder or arc to nearby metal. Damaged leads must be removed from service and properly repaired, because tape and extra gloves do not restore insulation rating."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student is asked to patch an empty steel drum that once held gasoline. A coworker says it is empty, so welding is safe. The drum has a bung that is currently closed. What must happen before any hot work is allowed on this container?", "options": {"A": "Keep the bung closed and weld quickly so vapors stay sealed inside during the repair.", "B": "Blow compressed air into the drum for a minute and begin welding once the sound changes.", "C": "Clean and purge the drum, test the atmosphere, and obtain a hot work permit before welding.", "D": "Rely on the empty label and weld with low amperage to reduce the chance of ignition."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean and purge the drum, test the atmosphere, and obtain a hot work permit before welding.): Residual gasoline and vapor can explode when heated, even in an empty container. Cleaning, purging, testing, and permit approval are required, and a closed drum can rupture violently."}, {"id": 20, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Before servicing a hydraulic press brake in the weld shop, an instructor reviews the lockout and tagout sequence with new students who will help with maintenance next week. Which order of steps correctly follows standard energy control practice for this machine?", "options": {"A": "Apply the lock first, notify affected workers afterward, then shut down the machine normally.", "B": "Shut down, release stored energy, remove guards, and apply only a tag without any lock.", "C": "Press start to test the machine, then notify workers and finally isolate the energy source.", "D": "Notify workers, shut down, isolate, lock and tag, release stored energy, verify zero energy."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Notify workers, shut down, isolate, lock and tag, release stored energy, verify zero energy.): The standard sequence prepares workers, stops the machine, isolates and locks the source, relieves residual energy, and then confirms the machine cannot start. Testing before isolation exposes workers to danger."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student is preparing to weld overhead on a steel frame with SMAW, where hot spatter and slag will fall around the shoulders and neck for most of the afternoon session. The instructor checks clothing before work begins. Which outfit best protects against burns for this task?", "options": {"A": "Polyester sports shirt with rolled sleeves, cuffed jeans tucked into boots, and open shirt pockets.", "B": "Short sleeve cotton shirt with a thin nylon apron, because light fabric keeps the welder cool.", "C": "Flame resistant jacket buttoned to the neck, cuffless pants over boots, covered pockets.", "D": "Fleece sweat pants and a sleeveless vest, since soft thick fabric blocks sparks and spatter."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Flame resistant jacket buttoned to the neck, cuffless pants over boots, covered pockets.): Synthetics melt onto skin, and cuffs and open pockets catch spatter. Flame resistant natural fiber clothing with covered openings gives the best protection in out of position work."}, {"id": 22, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welding lab stores one oxygen cylinder and one acetylene cylinder in a shared rack because floor space is tight. The instructor says this violates storage rules for oxidizers and fuel gases. What separation satisfies the commonly applied requirement for these two cylinders?", "options": {"A": "20 feet apart, or a noncombustible barrier 5 feet high with a half hour fire rating.", "B": "Touching each other in the same rack, so the pair stays matched for the cutting rig.", "C": "About 5 feet apart across open floor with no barrier between the two cylinders.", "D": "In the same rack provided that the protective valve caps are removed for quick access."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (20 feet apart, or a noncombustible barrier 5 feet high with a half hour fire rating.): Oxygen feeds fires, so it is kept away from fuel gases by distance or by a fire rated barrier. Open floor of only a few feet does not meet the requirement."}, {"id": 23, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student must move an argon cylinder across the shop on a hand truck after class ends. The instructor warns that a broken valve can turn a cylinder into a projectile. Which practice best protects against that hazard during the move?", "options": {"A": "Roll the cylinder on its bottom edge by tipping it slightly and steering with both hands.", "B": "Remove the cap for easier handling and lay the cylinder on its side on the cart.", "C": "Leave the protective valve cap on and chain the cylinder upright to the cart.", "D": "Carry the cylinder by the valve to keep the weight balanced against the body."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Leave the protective valve cap on and chain the cylinder upright to the cart.): The cap shields the valve from impact, and securing the cylinder upright prevents tipping. Lifting by the valve or rolling can damage it and release high pressure gas."}, {"id": 24, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student welding on a steel deck notices sparks falling through floor openings into a storeroom below, where cardboard boxes are stacked. The permit has not been updated for the lower level. What is the best response before work continues?", "options": {"A": "Sweep the sparks into a pile on the deck so they cannot reach the openings below.", "B": "Spray the cardboard lightly with water and continue because damp paper cannot ignite.", "C": "Cover the openings with fire resistant material and station a fire watch below.", "D": "Continue welding at lower amperage so the spatter is too small to fall through."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cover the openings with fire resistant material and station a fire watch below.): Sparks can travel and fall through openings and ignite materials out of the welder's sight. Sealing the gaps and watching the exposed area addresses the actual path of ignition."}, {"id": 25, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A classmate squints, tears up, and complains of sand in the eyes about five hours after a lab in which he looked at an arc through a gap in the booth curtain. Which first aid step is most appropriate for this welder's flash exposure?", "options": {"A": "Rest in a dark room, apply cool damp cloths, and seek care if pain persists.", "B": "Rub the eyes firmly and rinse with hot water to clear the grit sensation away.", "C": "Apply tinted eye drops from the first aid kit and return to welding practice.", "D": "Wait out the pain while staying in the bright shop so the eyes adjust to light."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rest in a dark room, apply cool damp cloths, and seek care if pain persists.): Arc eye is a surface burn that usually heals in a day or two with rest and cool compresses. Rubbing worsens corneal damage, and bright light increases discomfort."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "In an open lab, a student runs GMAW on a bench while other students work only a few steps away. The instructor wants to protect the bystanders from arc radiation. Which setup best accomplishes this goal for people near the station?", "options": {"A": "Welding screens rated to block ultraviolet and visible arc light around the station.", "B": "A canvas tarp from the paint area, since any opaque cloth blocks arc radiation well.", "C": "A shouted warning before each weld, with onlookers expected to look away on cue.", "D": "Sunglasses handed to onlookers, because dark lenses cut the light enough for brief exposure."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Welding screens rated to block ultraviolet and visible arc light around the station.): Purpose made screens block ultraviolet and visible light and resist burning. Ordinary tarps can ignite, and warnings or sunglasses do not prevent flash burns."}, {"id": 27, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student's jacket has been exposed to a leaking oxygen hose in a confined area, and the cloth smells and feels different. The student is about to walk past a grinding station that is throwing sparks. What should happen first?", "options": {"A": "Brush the jacket off with a stream of oxygen from the torch to clear the surface.", "B": "Move to fresh air and let the clothing ventilate well away from any sparks or flames.", "C": "Walk past the grinder quickly because oxygen only improves the flame and does not ignite cloth.", "D": "Soak the jacket in solvent from the parts washer to remove the oxygen residue."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move to fresh air and let the clothing ventilate well away from any sparks or flames.): Oxygen saturated clothing can ignite violently from a single spark. Ventilation in fresh air away from ignition sources lets the fabric return to normal."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop measures noise at about 90 decibels averaged over the day near a plasma cutting table and air carbon arc gouging station. A student asks whether hearing protection is needed since the sound is not painful. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "No, protection is needed only when the noise is painful enough to cause ringing immediately.", "B": "No, the welding helmet already covers the ears enough to reduce noise to a safe level.", "C": "Yes, because exposures at or above 85 decibels over an eight hour average require controls.", "D": "Yes, but only for visitors, because trained workers adapt their hearing to loud processes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, because exposures at or above 85 decibels over an eight hour average require controls.): Hearing damage builds up without pain. At 85 decibels as an eight hour average, employers must provide protection and a conservation program."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student finishes a weld and sets the live electrode holder on the steel bench so the next rod is ready to restart. The instructor stops him and moves the holder. Where should the holder be placed when it is not in use?", "options": {"A": "Laid on the steel bench so the electrode stays in position for the next restart.", "B": "Pushed into the pocket of the jacket so both hands stay free for slag chipping.", "C": "Hung on an insulated hook, off the table and away from grounded metal.", "D": "Leaned against the work lead clamp so it cannot roll off the table."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hung on an insulated hook, off the table and away from grounded metal.): A live holder resting on grounded metal can arc, burn, or start a fire when someone leans on the table. An insulated hook keeps it isolated until the next weld."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student welds a fillet joint on a vertical plate and leans over the bead, so the rising fume plume drifts directly across his face under the helmet. Which adjustment best reduces his fume exposure while still giving him a clear view of the puddle?", "options": {"A": "Hold his breath during each pass and then breathe deeply between welds in the plume.", "B": "Reposition the head out of the rising plume and use local exhaust at the joint.", "C": "Raise the amperage so the weld is finished faster and the fume is produced briefly.", "D": "Lower the helmet visor closer to the face because the lens blocks the fume particles."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reposition the head out of the rising plume and use local exhaust at the joint.): Fume rises in a hot plume, so keeping the breathing zone out of it matters most. Ventilation carries away what remains, but a visor or faster welding does not remove exposure."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student switches from welding to knocking down a weld bead with an angle grinder fitted with an abrasive wheel. Bright sparks and wheel fragments are thrown toward the face from close range. Which protection is most appropriate for this grinding operation?", "options": {"A": "A face shield worn over safety glasses to guard against sparks and wheel fragments.", "B": "The welding helmet with the dark lens lowered and no further eye protection.", "C": "Tinted shade 5 goggles alone, since grinding sparks are cooler than arc spatter.", "D": "Ordinary prescription glasses, because the grinder guard stops everything thrown from the wheel."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A face shield worn over safety glasses to guard against sparks and wheel fragments.): Grinding throws particles at high speed, so impact rated eye protection plus a full face shield is needed. A dark helmet lens is for arc light and gives poor impact coverage during grinding."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is assigned to weld inside a tank vessel through a single manhole after the atmosphere test passed. The permit lists the hazards. A classmate suggests that no one else needs to stay near the opening since the manhole is wide enough to climb through. What is correct?", "options": {"A": "No attendant is needed because the wide manhole allows the welder to climb out unaided.", "B": "The attendant may leave once the welder has been inside for fifteen minutes without trouble.", "C": "A trained attendant stays outside with a way to communicate and remove the welder quickly.", "D": "A sign posted beside the manhole replaces the attendant for work lasting under an hour."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A trained attendant stays outside with a way to communicate and remove the welder quickly.): Confined space entry requires an outside attendant because conditions can change and a welder can be overcome quickly. Opening size does not remove that need."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is working inside a steel tank on a hot day using an AC stick welding machine, and his clothing is soaked with sweat. A safety review looks for the single change that best lowers shock risk in this specific situation. Which change should the reviewer recommend?", "options": {"A": "Use a voltage reducing device and insulate the welder with a dry mat and gloves.", "B": "Increase the amperage on the machine so the arc stays steady despite the sweating.", "C": "Add a second ground clamp on the tank wall farther from the weld than the first.", "D": "Wear a damp cotton shirt over the clothing to cool the body and reduce sweating."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a voltage reducing device and insulate the welder with a dry mat and gloves.): Sweat and a conductive tank wall reduce body resistance, so cutting open circuit voltage and keeping the body dry and insulated attacks the hazard directly. The other changes leave the shock path in place."}, {"id": 34, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A safety class debates how a machine rated at about 80 volts open circuit can kill a welder. One student says low voltage cannot be dangerous. The instructor needs to correct the claim with the most accurate statement about electrical injury. Which statement should she make?", "options": {"A": "Voltage alone decides injury, so anything under 100 volts cannot harm a person.", "B": "Welding current is direct current only, which cannot cause the heart to fibrillate.", "C": "Shock injuries come from arc heat, and the path through the body does not matter.", "D": "Current causes harm, and wet skin lets dangerous current pass through the chest."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Current causes harm, and wet skin lets dangerous current pass through the chest.): Body resistance falls sharply when skin is wet, so even 80 volts can drive tens of milliamps through the heart. Current path and magnitude determine severity, not voltage alone."}, {"id": 35, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student wearing an auto darkening helmet sees a bright flash at every arc strike on an out of position weld, because his sleeve covers the helmet sensors. The lens darkens correctly when tested in open view. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Keep welding and close the eyes briefly during each arc strike to avoid the flash.", "B": "Raise the sensitivity to maximum and cover the sensors further with a leather patch.", "C": "Wear a second helmet lens of shade 3 behind the first one to soften the flash.", "D": "Stop, reposition so sensors see the arc, and check the battery or use a fixed lens."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop, reposition so sensors see the arc, and check the battery or use a fixed lens.): Repeated flashes damage the eyes even when brief, so the sensor view must be restored or the fixed lens used. Blocking sensors or relying on blinking offers no reliable protection."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A hot work permit was issued for welding in bay A. A contractor asks to weld a bracket in bay B using the same permit because the work is identical and the same fire watch is present. Which response best reflects sound hot work practice?", "options": {"A": "Bay B needs its own inspection and permit, because permits cover a specific location.", "B": "The permit may be reused because identical work carries identical fire risks in every bay.", "C": "Verbal approval from the fire watch is enough to extend a permit into a neighboring bay.", "D": "The permit transfers once the fire watch carries a signed copy to the new work location."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bay B needs its own inspection and permit, because permits cover a specific location.): Each area has its own combustibles, openings, and vapors, so conditions must be checked and approved separately. A permit is tied to the specific place, work, and time period."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student plans to weld a metal wall panel with SMAW. The side facing him is bare steel, but he knows foam insulation is installed on the far side of the panel. Which statement best identifies the hazard and the right precaution?", "options": {"A": "The bare front surface shows there is no fire risk, so no extra precaution is required.", "B": "Spatter is the only ignition source, so a front side blanket is enough protection.", "C": "Foam insulation cannot burn when it is covered by steel, so welding may continue.", "D": "Conducted heat can ignite the hidden foam, so check the back and station a fire watch."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Conducted heat can ignite the hidden foam, so check the back and station a fire watch.): Steel conducts heat through to the far side and can ignite combustibles that the welder cannot see. Hidden combustibles are checked and watched, not assumed absent."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Two instructors and a student all need access to the same robotic welding positioner for repairs, and one instructor will leave before the others finish. They disagree about how to apply locks so everyone stays protected. Which approach best satisfies lockout practice?", "options": {"A": "One instructor locks it and tells the others by text message when the work ends.", "B": "The student applies one lock for the whole group because he has the least experience.", "C": "A single tag with all three names replaces the individual locks on the disconnect.", "D": "Each worker adds a personal lock to a group hasp, and power stays off until removed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Each worker adds a personal lock to a group hasp, and power stays off until removed.): Each worker needs personal control over the energy source, so a group hasp holds separate locks. Tags alone or one person's lock leave others unprotected."}, {"id": 39, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "After locking out the disconnect on a hydraulic shear, an instructor presses the start button and nothing moves, so the student says the machine is safe to open. The instructor notes that the accumulator still holds pressure. What is the correct next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the work, since the start button test confirms that the machine is safe.", "B": "Bleed the accumulator and block raised parts, since stored energy counts too.", "C": "Remove the lock briefly to cycle the machine once and release the stored pressure.", "D": "Tag the accumulator and continue, because hydraulic pressure cannot injure a worker."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bleed the accumulator and block raised parts, since stored energy counts too.): A dead control circuit does not prove that stored hydraulic, gravity, or spring energy is gone. All forms of energy must be released or blocked before work begins."}, {"id": 40, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Two welders share a bay with one exhaust unit. One welds 304 stainless and the other welds mild steel plate. A safety officer must rank their fume risk for the monthly review. Which statement correctly identifies the higher concern and why?", "options": {"A": "The mild steel welder, because iron oxide fume is more toxic than chromium compounds.", "B": "The stainless welder, since chromium and nickel in the fume raise cancer risk.", "C": "Both welders have equal exposure, since all welding fume has an identical composition.", "D": "Neither welder, because stainless welding produces little fume compared with mild steel."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The stainless welder, since chromium and nickel in the fume raise cancer risk.): Stainless steel fume can contain hexavalent chromium and nickel, which are known health hazards. Mild steel fume still needs control, but the composition makes stainless the greater concern."}, {"id": 41, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student with a full beard wants to use a tight fitting half mask respirator while welding stainless in a shop with limited ventilation. The instructor must choose the best option. Which statement describes the correct approach for protecting him?", "options": {"A": "The mask will seal properly if he pulls the head straps tight across the beard.", "B": "A disposable dust mask worn under the welding helmet gives the same fume protection.", "C": "A tight fitting mask cannot seal over facial hair, so use a loose fitting powered helmet.", "D": "A respirator is unnecessary if he keeps the helmet visor lowered during every pass."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A tight fitting mask cannot seal over facial hair, so use a loose fitting powered helmet.): Facial hair breaks the seal of tight fitting respirators, allowing contaminated air to leak in. Loose fitting powered units or a clean shaven face with fit testing solve this."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A gas monitor in a pit where an oxygen hose leaked reads 28 percent oxygen. A student says this is safer than normal air for breathing and wants to continue cutting with the torch. What is the correct response to this reading?", "options": {"A": "Continue cutting since extra oxygen helps the workers breathe more easily in the pit.", "B": "Add argon to dilute the oxygen and then continue cutting without further testing.", "C": "Test with a lit match to see whether the air is dangerous before continuing.", "D": "Ventilate and find the leak, because enriched oxygen makes materials burn fiercely."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ventilate and find the leak, because enriched oxygen makes materials burn fiercely.): Atmospheres above about 23.5 percent oxygen are enriched and greatly increase the chance and speed of fire. The leak must be fixed and the space ventilated before any hot work."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "An acetylene cylinder in the weld bay feels warm after a backfire traveled into the hose and regulator, and the student suspects internal decomposition inside the cylinder. He asks what to do. Which response best protects the people in the area?", "options": {"A": "Open the valve fully to vent the gas quickly and carry the cylinder outside the building.", "B": "Wrap it in a wet cloth and keep working because the warmth will pass in minutes.", "C": "Lay the cylinder on its side and cover it with sand to smother the heat inside.", "D": "Shut the valve if reachable, evacuate, call responders, and keep the cylinder cooled from afar."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shut the valve if reachable, evacuate, call responders, and keep the cylinder cooled from afar.): A warm acetylene cylinder can decompose and explode. Closing the valve if safe, evacuating, calling for help, and cooling from a distance limits harm."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A hot work job is planned next to a paint spray booth that is idle but still holds solvent vapors in its ductwork. The supervisor asks if welding may begin with a fire watch present. Which answer is correct under sound permit practice?", "options": {"A": "Welding may begin because an idle booth cannot ignite from sparks produced nearby in the bay.", "B": "Hot work stays off until the vapor source is shut down and the area is tested.", "C": "A fire watch alone provides adequate protection against ignition of flammable vapors in ductwork.", "D": "Welding at lower amperage reduces spatter enough to avoid any ignition of vapors in the booth."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hot work stays off until the vapor source is shut down and the area is tested.): Flammable vapors can ignite from sparks far from the arc, so the source must be controlled and the air tested. A watch cannot stop an explosion once vapors ignite."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student wipes a part with a chlorinated degreaser, then begins welding in the same area while some vapor remains in the air. The instructor stops the job and cites a toxic gas hazard from the arc. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "The solvent evaporates harmlessly in the arc, so only the eyes need extra protection.", "B": "Arc ultraviolet light turns chlorinated vapor into phosgene, so vapors must clear first.", "C": "Higher amperage burns off solvent residue before the root pass and removes the hazard.", "D": "A darker lens shade stops the solvent from reacting, since the hazard is mostly light."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arc ultraviolet light turns chlorinated vapor into phosgene, so vapors must clear first.): Ultraviolet radiation breaks down chlorinated vapors into toxic phosgene and hydrogen chloride. The part must dry and the vapors clear before any arc is struck."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student plans an overhead SMAW weld where slag and spatter will fall onto the shoulders and neck during a long afternoon session. The instructor reviews personal protective equipment for this specific hazard. Which combination best matches the risks of the position?", "options": {"A": "Short sleeves, a bare head, and an open collar so body heat can escape upward easily.", "B": "Leather sleeves or cape, welding cap, ear plugs, and a buttoned collar against spatter.", "C": "A cotton bandana over the hair and a loose synthetic jacket with cuffs rolled up high.", "D": "Light cotton gloves and glasses, since overhead spatter is less hot than flat spatter."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leather sleeves or cape, welding cap, ear plugs, and a buttoned collar against spatter.): Overhead work drops spatter onto the head, neck, and shoulders and can fall into ears and collars. Leather coverage and closed openings prevent burns."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student discovers that a portable stick welding machine in the lab has no ground conductor in its power cord, and the plug has been altered to fit an old outlet. A classmate says it works fine. What is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Keep using it because it works and the output circuit is separate from the cord.", "B": "Wrap the case with rubber matting so a fault cannot reach the welder who touches it.", "C": "Remove it from service until grounded, since a fault could energize the case.", "D": "Use it only on dry days since a ground wire matters mainly in wet conditions."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove it from service until grounded, since a fault could energize the case.): Grounding gives fault current a safe path and trips protective devices. Without it, an internal fault can make the case live and shock the next person who touches it."}, {"id": 48, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "During a hot work job in the lab the assigned fire watch holds a phone, watches the welder rather than the surrounding area, and has no extinguisher training. A safety officer must decide whether the watch is acceptable. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, any available worker may serve as the fire watch if the welder stays within sight.", "B": "Yes, because watching the weld itself is the most important task for a fire watch.", "C": "No, the watch needs extinguisher training and must cover all exposed areas without other duties.", "D": "No, but only the welder's direct supervisor is allowed to serve in that role on site."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, the watch needs extinguisher training and must cover all exposed areas without other duties.): The watch looks for sparks and smoldering fires in every place the work could reach, and needs training to act fast. Dividing attention defeats the purpose."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student powers an angle grinder from an extension cord on a damp shop floor where welding cables also cross the walkway. The instructor wants an upgrade that best reduces shock risk from a tool fault. Which action should be taken?", "options": {"A": "Plug the grinder into a ground fault interrupter outlet and keep cords off the wet floor.", "B": "Add a longer extension cord so the plug stays farther from water on the floor.", "C": "Wear rubber boots only, and leave the cord wherever it lies on the shop floor.", "D": "Run the cord underneath the welding cables so the cables protect it from damage."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Plug the grinder into a ground fault interrupter outlet and keep cords off the wet floor.): A ground fault interrupter cuts power in a fraction of a second when current leaks, which is critical on damp floors. Longer cords and boots alone do not stop a fault."}, {"id": 50, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop manager says buying a respirator for every welder is cheaper than installing local exhaust ventilation. A safety instructor disagrees and cites the hierarchy of controls from the written safety plan of the school. Which statement best supports the instructor's position?", "options": {"A": "Respirators rank first because they protect against every possible hazard in the shop.", "B": "Ventilation ranks last since it depends on weather and cannot control welding fume.", "C": "Local exhaust removes fume at its source, so engineering controls rank ahead of respirators.", "D": "Respirators and exhaust rank equally, so cost should be the only deciding factor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Local exhaust removes fume at its source, so engineering controls rank ahead of respirators.): The hierarchy favors removing or capturing the hazard before relying on personal protective equipment. Respirators depend on fit and use, so they come after engineering controls."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welding shop supervisor is training new hires on hazard communication. Each employee must be able to quickly find the exposure limit and the recommended respirator type for a flux-cored wire. The supervisor tells them to open the safety data sheet and go to the section covering which topic?", "options": {"A": "Stability and reactivity, which describes conditions and materials the product should be kept away from", "B": "Composition and ingredient information, which names each chemical along with its concentration range", "C": "Disposal considerations, which cover proper handling of leftover product and contaminated packaging materials", "D": "Exposure controls and personal protection, which lists limits and respiratory equipment recommendations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exposure controls and personal protection, which lists limits and respiratory equipment recommendations): Section 8 of the 16-section safety data sheet format holds exposure limits, engineering controls, and protective equipment guidance, including respirator selection. The reactivity and composition sections describe chemistry, not how to protect the worker."}, {"id": 52, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A fabricator spends the morning welding hot-dip galvanized handrail with no local exhaust. That evening he develops chills, fever, a metallic taste, and muscle aches that resemble influenza. His symptoms fade within a day or two without treatment. Which condition does this pattern describe?", "options": {"A": "Hexavalent chromium poisoning that permanently scars the lungs after one heavy exposure period", "B": "Metal fume fever caused by inhaling zinc oxide fume released from the heated coating", "C": "Carbon monoxide poisoning produced by incomplete combustion of shielding gas near the work area", "D": "Heat stroke brought on by prolonged preheating of the thick galvanized steel sections"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Metal fume fever caused by inhaling zinc oxide fume released from the heated coating): Zinc oxide fume from galvanized coatings causes metal fume fever, a short flu-like illness that usually clears within one to two days. Chromium poisoning would not resolve this quickly, and the symptom pattern does not fit carbon monoxide or heat illness."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A maintenance welder is asked to repair a worn tool made from copper-beryllium alloy. The job hazard analysis flags the alloy as needing special controls because its fume and dust can cause a serious lung disease that may appear years after exposure. Which disease is associated with beryllium?", "options": {"A": "Siderosis, a benign buildup of iron oxide particles that shows on chest X-rays", "B": "Manganism, a nervous system disorder producing tremor, slowed movement, and shuffling gait", "C": "Chronic beryllium disease, an immune-driven lung scarring that may appear years later", "D": "Metal fume fever, a short flu-like illness that clears within about two days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic beryllium disease, an immune-driven lung scarring that may appear years later): Beryllium sensitizes the immune system and causes chronic beryllium disease, which can develop long after the exposure ended. Siderosis comes from iron oxide and is considered benign, and manganism and metal fume fever come from other metals."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Welders who spend years in poorly ventilated spaces using high-manganese steel electrodes may develop tremors, balance problems, and mood changes. Occupational health staff call this condition manganism, and it can persist after the exposure ends. Which body system does overexposure to manganese fume primarily damage?", "options": {"A": "The kidneys, leading to protein loss in urine and a gradual drop in filtering ability", "B": "The skin, causing blistering and slow-healing ulcers at the points of contact", "C": "The blood, reducing the oxygen carrying capacity of the red cells", "D": "The central nervous system, producing symptoms that resemble Parkinson's disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The central nervous system, producing symptoms that resemble Parkinson's disease): Manganese fume is a neurotoxin, and chronic overexposure produces tremor, gait disturbance, and mood changes similar to Parkinson's disease. Kidney damage is the hallmark of cadmium instead."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder grinds and brazes silver-colored, cadmium-plated bolts in a small booth. The foreman stops the job and requires air monitoring, because heated cadmium produces a fume regulated by its own OSHA standard. Which organs are the main long-term targets of cadmium overexposure?", "options": {"A": "Liver and pancreas, with metabolic damage building up gradually over several decades", "B": "Kidneys and lungs, with a possibility of cancer after prolonged exposure", "C": "Eyes and skin, producing cataracts and chronic rashes in the exposed areas", "D": "Bones and joints, causing arthritis that mainly affects the hands and wrists"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Kidneys and lungs, with a possibility of cancer after prolonged exposure): Cadmium accumulates in the kidneys and damages the lungs, and it is also classed as a carcinogen. That is why plated fasteners get their own standard and exposure monitoring."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student in a SENSE Level 1 program scores 92 percent on the safety module knowledge exam, well above the 75 percent passing score used for the other modules. The instructor explains that this score still does not earn credit for the module. What is the reason?", "options": {"A": "The safety exam must be retaken whenever the score falls below the class average", "B": "The Level 1 safety exam requires a perfect score before the module is credited", "C": "Safety modules are graded only on the practical test, not on the knowledge exam", "D": "A score of 92 percent counts only after forty hours of documented shop time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Level 1 safety exam requires a perfect score before the module is credited): SENSE Level 1 safety is the exception to the 75 percent rule and must be passed at 100 percent. Students may study and retest, because a single missed safety question can mean a missed hazard in the shop."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop measures noise of 88 dBA, averaged over an eight-hour shift, near the plasma cutting table and air carbon arc station. Employees there have not yet had hearing tests. Under OSHA rules, which exposure level triggers a required hearing conservation program including audiometric testing?", "options": {"A": "An eight-hour average of 100 dBA, known as the ceiling level", "B": "An eight-hour average of 85 dBA, known as the action level", "C": "A single peak reading of 70 dBA measured at the cutting table", "D": "An eight-hour average of 95 dBA, known as the exposure limit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An eight-hour average of 85 dBA, known as the action level): OSHA sets the hearing conservation action level at an eight-hour time-weighted average of 85 dBA, which is below the 90 dBA permissible exposure limit. This shop is already above the action level, so testing and protector availability are required."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A helper is moving a full argon cylinder across the shop floor on a hand truck. The cylinder is chained to the cart, but the instructor points out one missing item that must be in place whenever a cylinder is moved or stored without a regulator attached. What is it?", "options": {"A": "A flashback arrestor, threaded onto the outlet of the cylinder valve", "B": "A pressure relief tag, attached to the handwheel by the gas supplier", "C": "The valve protection cap, screwed fully onto the cylinder neck", "D": "A grounding strap, clamped between the cylinder base and the cart frame"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The valve protection cap, screwed fully onto the cylinder neck): The protection cap shields the valve from impact. A broken valve can turn a high-pressure cylinder into a projectile. A flashback arrestor belongs on fuel gas equipment during use, not on a cylinder in transit."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A pipe welder finishes a repair weld on a platform beside stored wooden pallets, then begins to pack up. The hot work permit requires a trained person to stay and watch for smoldering material for a set time after welding stops. How long does OSHA require the fire watch to continue?", "options": {"A": "Until the weld has cooled to a temperature that is safe to touch", "B": "At least thirty minutes after the welding or cutting work is finished", "C": "About five minutes, long enough for visible sparks to stop falling", "D": "Two full hours, matching the length of the longest permitted shift break"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (At least thirty minutes after the welding or cutting work is finished): OSHA requires a fire watch for at least a half hour after hot work ends, because sparks can smolder unseen in combustibles. Waiting for the weld to cool or for sparks to stop falling is not the standard."}, {"id": 60, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A new helper sets the acetylene regulator on an oxyfuel cutting rig to 20 psig because the cut seems weak. The lead hand stops the work immediately. Free acetylene becomes unstable and can decompose explosively above a certain working pressure. What is the maximum safe acetylene working pressure?", "options": {"A": "15 psig, the upper limit for free acetylene gas in use", "B": "30 psig, the limit that applies when using large cutting tips", "C": "25 psig, the limit used when cutting steel thicker than one inch", "D": "10 psig, because anything higher damages the flashback arrestor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (15 psig, the upper limit for free acetylene gas in use): Acetylene must not be used above 15 psig, since higher pressure risks explosive decomposition. A weak cut should be fixed by checking the tip size and oxygen setting, not by raising the fuel gas pressure."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder must weld galvanized steel tubing inside a fabrication bay with a roll-up door and a portable fume extractor. The coating cannot be removed beforehand because the part is already assembled. Which control best reduces his exposure to zinc fume during the job?", "options": {"A": "Place the extractor hood close to the arc and keep his head out of the plume", "B": "Increase the amperage so the arc time and the total fume produced both drop", "C": "Wear a disposable dust mask and move the extractor to the far wall", "D": "Shut off the extractor whenever welding with a gas shield to protect the arc"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place the extractor hood close to the arc and keep his head out of the plume): Capturing fume at the source and keeping the head out of the rising plume gives the largest reduction. A hood placed far away captures little, and a dust mask is not designed for welding fume."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is joining 304 stainless pipe with flux-cored wire in a poorly ventilated pump room. Air sampling shows hexavalent chromium fume above the action level. Industrial hygiene staff recommend a control to try before relying on respirators. Which choice follows the preferred approach?", "options": {"A": "Issue each welder a disposable dust mask and a fresh pair of work gloves", "B": "Raise the shielding gas flow rate so fume is blown away from the welder", "C": "Rotate welders every hour so that each person's total time near the fume drops somewhat", "D": "Install local exhaust at the arc to capture fume before it reaches the breathing zone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Install local exhaust at the arc to capture fume before it reaches the breathing zone): Engineering controls such as local exhaust remove the contaminant at its source and rank above administrative measures and respirators. Extra gas flow disturbs weld quality and does not remove fume."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Before a welder enters a tank to repair a baffle, the entry supervisor tests the atmosphere with a four-gas meter. The tank previously held a solvent. Standard practice tests one hazard first, because the combustible gas sensor needs it to read accurately. Which reading comes first?", "options": {"A": "Toxic gases, since they can cause harm at much lower concentrations than other hazards do", "B": "Combustible gases, since an explosion risk takes priority over breathing hazards", "C": "Carbon dioxide, since it is the most common contaminant found in welding tanks", "D": "Oxygen content, because the combustible sensor needs adequate oxygen to read accurately"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oxygen content, because the combustible sensor needs adequate oxygen to read accurately): The usual order is oxygen, then combustible gases, then toxic gases. Many combustible sensors give false low readings in oxygen-deficient air, so oxygen must be confirmed first."}, {"id": 64, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is working inside a permit-required confined space, a vessel with a single 18-inch manway. A coworker stands at the opening with a radio and the entry log, and does not go inside. Which duty belongs to this attendant during the entry?", "options": {"A": "Enter the vessel to assist at the first sign the welder is in distress", "B": "Hold the welding leads and adjust the amperage on the machine as the welder directs", "C": "Stay outside, track who is inside, and summon rescue without entering the space", "D": "Feed the wire and adjust the machine settings from outside the vessel"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stay outside, track who is inside, and summon rescue without entering the space): The attendant stays outside, keeps the entry count, monitors conditions, and calls rescue. Entering to help is how a second person becomes a second victim, so attendants never take on tasks that pull them away from the post."}, {"id": 65, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is TIG welding aluminum inside a ten-foot-deep pit using argon shielding gas. After lunch he feels dizzy, and his lighter will not stay lit at the bottom of the pit. Argon is heavier than air. What is the most likely hazard?", "options": {"A": "Carbon monoxide buildup, because argon reacts with the arc to produce a toxic gas", "B": "Hydrogen sulfide exposure, because argon releases gas trapped in the pit soil", "C": "Heat stress, because argon traps radiant heat from the arc near the floor", "D": "Oxygen deficiency, because argon settled in the low area and displaced breathable air"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oxygen deficiency, because argon settled in the low area and displaced breathable air): Argon is inert and non-toxic but heavier than air, so it pools in pits and tanks and pushes out oxygen. Dizziness and a flame that will not burn point to low oxygen, which calls for atmospheric testing and ventilation."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A delivery driver lays an acetylene cylinder on its side in the shop for the weekend. On Monday, a welder wants to use it right away. Acetylene cylinders contain a liquid solvent, acetone, inside a porous filler. What should the welder do before connecting a regulator?", "options": {"A": "Connect the regulator and open the valve fully to drain any acetone", "B": "Shake the cylinder gently to redistribute the acetone through the porous filler", "C": "Stand it upright and let it rest for a period so the acetone settles back", "D": "Use it as is, since orientation has no effect on the gas drawn off"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stand it upright and let it rest for a period so the acetone settles back): Acetylene is dissolved in acetone, and a cylinder on its side can let liquid reach the valve and pass into the hose. Standing it upright and waiting lets the solvent settle, and acetylene cylinders are best kept upright at all times."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A new welder leaves a full shielding gas cylinder standing alone near the booth entrance, held only by its own balance, while he fetches coupons. A bump from a forklift could topple it. What is the required way to keep a cylinder in use from falling?", "options": {"A": "Lay it flat on the floor with wedges under the body and valve end", "B": "Lean it against the booth wall with the valve protection cap installed", "C": "Place it inside a bucket of sand so the bottom edge is held in place", "D": "Secure it upright with a chain or strap to a wall, rack, or cart"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Secure it upright with a chain or strap to a wall, rack, or cart): Cylinders in use or storage must be restrained upright by a chain or strap. A cylinder that falls can break its valve and become a projectile, and leaning or wedging does not hold it."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A student connects a regulator to an oxygen cylinder, stands beside the gauge face, and opens the cylinder valve slowly. Once the high-pressure gauge settles, the instructor asks how far the valve should be opened for oxygen service. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "One quarter turn only, so it can be shut off instantly", "B": "One and a half turns, the same as acetylene service", "C": "Half open, to avoid damaging the regulator diaphragm with the full cylinder pressure", "D": "Fully open it so the back seat seals and prevents leakage around the stem"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fully open it so the back seat seals and prevents leakage around the stem): Oxygen cylinder valves are opened fully so the stem seats at the back and does not leak at the packing. Acetylene valves are opened only a turn or so, to allow quick shutoff, which is a different rule."}, {"id": 69, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "While preparing a cutting rig, a welder smells acetylene and hears a slight hiss at the cylinder valve stem. The leak does not stop when he tightens the packing nut. Nothing else in the shop is running. What is the proper action?", "options": {"A": "Close the valve if possible, move the cylinder outdoors away from ignition, and tag it", "B": "Keep the cylinder in place and soap-test every fitting along the hose and regulator connections", "C": "Open the valve fully to purge the gas and then retighten the nut", "D": "Cover the valve with a rag and continue cutting at a lower pressure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Close the valve if possible, move the cylinder outdoors away from ignition, and tag it): A leaking fuel gas cylinder should be taken to a well-ventilated outdoor spot away from sparks and flames, then tagged so it goes back to the supplier. Working next to a leak invites a fire or explosion."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder receives a new spool of flux-cored wire whose container label shows a health hazard pictogram, an exclamation mark pictogram, and a one-word signal at the top. This label follows the OSHA hazard communication standard. What does the word Danger indicate compared with Warning?", "options": {"A": "It marks products that must be stored outdoors, while Warning marks indoor storage", "B": "It marks products regulated by OSHA, while Warning marks products regulated by the EPA", "C": "It marks hazards to the environment, while Warning marks hazards to workers", "D": "It marks the more severe hazard categories, while Warning marks the less severe ones"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It marks the more severe hazard categories, while Warning marks the less severe ones): Under the hazard communication standard, Danger signals the more severe hazard categories and Warning the less severe ones. The signal word is about severity, not storage location or which agency regulates the product."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder pours a small amount of cleaning solvent from a labeled drum into an unlabeled squeeze bottle for use at the bench. Another employee will use the bottle later in the shift. Under the hazard communication standard, what must be done?", "options": {"A": "Nothing, because the label on the drum already covers all of the workers", "B": "Label the bottle with the product name and words or symbols describing its hazards", "C": "Write only the date on the bottle since the safety data sheet is on file", "D": "Attach a copy of the safety data sheet to the bottle with tape for users"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Label the bottle with the product name and words or symbols describing its hazards): Workplace secondary containers need the product identifier plus general hazard information, unless the same employee uses the contents during the shift they were transferred. Because a coworker will use it later, the bottle needs a label."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A plasma cutting table produces about 95 dBA near the operator throughout a full shift. The shop has not yet changed the cutting setup or enclosed the table. Which step does OSHA hearing conservation practice require for this operator at this exposure level?", "options": {"A": "Provide hearing protectors and keep working toward engineering controls that lower the noise", "B": "Rely on the welding helmet, which blocks noise as it blocks the light", "C": "Move the operator to a different table every ten minutes, without issuing any hearing protection", "D": "Wait for a hearing test to show a loss before issuing any protection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Provide hearing protectors and keep working toward engineering controls that lower the noise): At 95 dBA over a shift the exposure is above the 90 dBA limit, so protectors are required while feasible engineering or administrative controls are pursued. A helmet gives little noise reduction, and waiting for hearing loss defeats the purpose."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "While welding in a hot sheet metal shop in July, a welder becomes pale, sweaty, and dizzy, and says he feels nauseated. His skin is cool and clammy, and he is alert and answering questions. What is the most appropriate first response?", "options": {"A": "Have him keep working at a slower pace in the same hot work area", "B": "Give him a salt tablet and a hot drink so he recovers faster", "C": "Move him to a cool place, loosen clothing, and give him cool water to sip", "D": "Keep him standing and walking so his circulation does not slow down"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move him to a cool place, loosen clothing, and give him cool water to sip): These are signs of heat exhaustion. Cooling, resting, and sipping fluids is the right first response, and he should be watched in case he worsens. Staying in the heat or adding hot drinks makes recovery harder."}, {"id": 74, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder working in a sealed tank on a hot afternoon suddenly becomes confused and stops sweating. His skin is hot and flushed, and he slurs his words before collapsing. Coworkers have water and ice packs available nearby. Which action should come first?", "options": {"A": "Give him large amounts of water to drink until he can stand again", "B": "Call emergency services and begin cooling him right away with water or ice packs", "C": "Let him rest in the shade and recheck him after about thirty minutes", "D": "Wrap him in blankets to prevent shock while the crew waits for him to wake up"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Call emergency services and begin cooling him right away with water or ice packs): Confusion, hot dry skin, and collapse indicate heat stroke, a life-threatening emergency. Rapid cooling and a call for help come first. Giving fluids to someone who is unresponsive risks choking, and waiting or insulating him makes things worse."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder spends most of the day bent over a low table making fillet welds on small brackets, and now reports lower back pain and sore wrists. The supervisor wants a simple ergonomic fix using existing equipment. Which change best addresses the problem?", "options": {"A": "Raise the work surface and use positioners so the wrists stay in a neutral position", "B": "Add a stool and let the welder keep the same bent posture while seated", "C": "Reduce break times so the welder finishes before the muscles stiffen", "D": "Replace the torch with a lighter model to reduce the weight carried in the welding hand"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the work surface and use positioners so the wrists stay in a neutral position): Bringing the work up to a comfortable height and positioning it so the wrists stay straight removes the awkward posture that causes the pain. Sitting in the same bent posture or shortening breaks leaves the cause in place."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A coworker welding in a damp trench grabs the electrode holder and collapses, still in contact with the energized lead. The machine is still running, and you reach the scene seconds later with no one else nearby. Which action should you take before touching him?", "options": {"A": "Grab him by the clothing and pull him free of the lead right away", "B": "Begin chest compressions while he remains in contact with the lead", "C": "Shut off the machine or use a dry insulating object to separate him from the lead", "D": "Wait for him to release the lead on his own before approaching"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shut off the machine or use a dry insulating object to separate him from the lead): A rescuer who touches a victim still in the circuit can be shocked too. De-energizing the machine or using a dry insulator breaks the circuit safely, and only then should first aid begin."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder's sparks ignite a mix of cardboard and wood scraps near the cutting bench, and the small fire is spreading slowly. The shop has dry chemical, carbon dioxide, and water extinguishers within reach. Which extinguisher classification applies to this fire involving ordinary combustibles?", "options": {"A": "Class B, which covers flammable liquids such as oils, gasoline, and solvents", "B": "Class A, which covers wood, paper, cloth, and similar ordinary combustible materials", "C": "Class C, which covers fires that involve energized electrical equipment", "D": "Class D, which covers combustible metals such as magnesium and titanium shavings"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Class A, which covers wood, paper, cloth, and similar ordinary combustible materials): Wood, paper, and cardboard are ordinary combustibles, which makes this a Class A fire. Class B is for flammable liquids, Class C is for energized equipment, and Class D is for combustible metals."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder wipes a part with a chlorinated degreaser and, with it still wet, starts a GTAW arc nearby. The ultraviolet light from the arc breaks down the solvent vapor. Which toxic gas can be produced in this situation if the arc continues?", "options": {"A": "Methane, a flammable gas produced when solvent cools in the weld pool near the arc", "B": "Phosgene, a highly toxic gas formed when chlorinated solvent vapors meet arc radiation", "C": "Methane, a flammable gas produced when solvent cools in the weld pool", "D": "Nitrogen, an inert gas released from the solvent that dilutes the shielding gas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Phosgene, a highly toxic gas formed when chlorinated solvent vapors meet arc radiation): Ultraviolet light from an arc can break down chlorinated solvent vapors into phosgene and other toxic compounds. Parts should be dry, and degreasing should be done well away from the arc."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A tack welder stood near a coworker's SMAW arc without eye protection for several seconds. Four hours later he wakes in severe pain with gritty, watering eyes and sensitivity to light. What caused this condition, and what is the usual treatment?", "options": {"A": "Metal splinters in the eye, which require an immediate flush with hot water", "B": "Ultraviolet burn to the cornea, treated with cool compresses and medical evaluation", "C": "Infrared cataract formation, which is permanent and treated with a day of bed rest", "D": "Chemical burn from flux fumes, which is treated by rubbing the eyes with a clean cloth"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ultraviolet burn to the cornea, treated with cool compresses and medical evaluation): Arc flash, or welder's flash, is an ultraviolet burn of the cornea that shows up several hours after exposure. Cool compresses and a medical check are standard, and rubbing the eyes would make the injury worse."}, {"id": 80, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A maintenance crew must cut a steel beam coated with old lead-based paint using an oxyfuel torch. The paint will be heated well above its melting point near the cut. Which hazard is the main concern, and which control is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Zinc fume from the primer, controlled by switching to a lower cutting oxygen pressure setting", "B": "Lead as a flammable vapor, controlled by cutting only in damp weather", "C": "Lead fume from the heated coating, controlled by removing the paint first or using exhaust", "D": "Lead radiation exposure, controlled by wearing a lead apron over standard clothing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lead fume from the heated coating, controlled by removing the paint first or using exhaust): Heating a lead-bearing coating releases lead fume, which is a toxic exposure. Stripping the coating from the cut zone or capturing the fume with exhaust, with respiratory protection as needed, are the standard controls."}, {"id": 81, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A new apprentice puts a drop of machine oil on the threads of an oxygen regulator fitting to make it screw on more smoothly. The instructor removes it and explains the danger. Why must oil and grease stay away from oxygen equipment?", "options": {"A": "The oil dissolves the brass fitting and slowly weakens the regulator body", "B": "The oil thins the oxygen, which lowers the flame temperature at the tip", "C": "The oil traps moisture that freezes inside the regulator even at normal shop temperatures", "D": "Oxygen under pressure can make oil or grease ignite violently without any outside flame"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oxygen under pressure can make oil or grease ignite violently without any outside flame): Pressurized oxygen can make hydrocarbons burn explosively with very little ignition energy. Oxygen fittings must be kept clean and oil-free, and no lubricant should go on regulator threads."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder must work on a rain-dampened steel deck using a stick welder with an open-circuit voltage of about 70 volts. His leather gloves are soaked through. Which precaution best reduces the chance of a dangerous shock under these conditions?", "options": {"A": "Keep the gloves wet because water conducts current away from the hand", "B": "Raise the machine amperage to burn through the moisture faster at the joint", "C": "Hold the electrode holder with bare skin to feel any leakage current", "D": "Wear dry gloves and boots, and insulate himself from the wet steel with a dry mat"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wear dry gloves and boots, and insulate himself from the wet steel with a dry mat): Wet clothing and wet footing greatly lower body resistance. Dry insulating gloves, boots, and a dry mat keep the welder out of the current path, as ANSI Z49.1 recommends."}, {"id": 83, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder joining stainless plate with GMAW sets a large pedestal fan beside the booth to blow fume away from his face. Afterward, inspection shows scattered porosity in several welds, though wire, gas, and settings were unchanged. What best explains the porosity, and what should be done?", "options": {"A": "The fan overcooled the plate, so preheat the plate before each weld pass", "B": "The fan raised moisture in the wire, so dry the spool before using it", "C": "The fan increased the arc voltage, so lower the wire feed speed on the welding machine", "D": "The fan disturbed the shielding gas, so use a local extractor, not a direct breeze"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The fan disturbed the shielding gas, so use a local extractor, not a direct breeze): A strong cross draft sweeps shielding gas away from the molten pool and lets air in, which causes porosity. Local exhaust placed beside the arc captures fume without disturbing the gas envelope."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "An employer plans to issue every welder a half-mask respirator rather than install fume extraction, arguing that respirators cost less. Industrial hygiene guidance places controls in a preferred order. Why is relying only on respirators generally the least favored approach for controlling welding fume?", "options": {"A": "Protection depends on fit and worker use, while engineering controls remove fume for everyone", "B": "Respirators block ultraviolet light poorly, so they cannot protect the eyes from welding fume", "C": "Respirators are prohibited by OSHA in any shop where welding is performed", "D": "Respirators release fume back into the air as the filters become loaded"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Protection depends on fit and worker use, while engineering controls remove fume for everyone): Respirators protect only the wearer, only when sealed and used correctly. Engineering controls remove the hazard from the air and protect everyone, which is why the hierarchy puts personal protective equipment last."}, {"id": 85, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Before entry, an atmospheric test of a storage tank shows 18.2 percent oxygen, no combustible gas, and no toxic gas. The crew leader says the reading is close enough because the tank is entered only for a short tack weld. Which conclusion is correct?", "options": {"A": "Entry is acceptable because only combustible and toxic readings matter for short tack welding jobs", "B": "Entry is acceptable if the welder wears a half-mask air-purifying respirator", "C": "Entry is acceptable because anything above 16 percent oxygen is safe for brief work", "D": "Entry is not permitted until ventilation brings oxygen into the 19.5 to 23.5 percent range"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Entry is not permitted until ventilation brings oxygen into the 19.5 to 23.5 percent range): An acceptable entry atmosphere is 19.5 to 23.5 percent oxygen. An air-purifying respirator filters contaminants but supplies no oxygen, so it cannot correct a deficient atmosphere."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Inside a poorly ventilated tank, the air feels stale, and a helper suggests piping in pure oxygen from a cutting cylinder to freshen it while the welder works. The lead hand rejects the idea. Why is using oxygen for ventilation dangerous in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Pure oxygen causes the welder's lungs to fill with fluid within a few minutes", "B": "Oxygen enrichment makes clothing and materials burn fiercely and can cause a flash fire", "C": "Pure oxygen reacts with the shielding gas and produces toxic ozone inside the closed tank", "D": "Oxygen lowers the humidity and causes static charge that disrupts the welding arc"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oxygen enrichment makes clothing and materials burn fiercely and can cause a flash fire): Enriched oxygen sharply increases flammability, so a spark can ignite clothing or oily surfaces in seconds. Confined spaces should be ventilated with fresh air, never with oxygen from a cylinder."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop evaluates ways to cut welder exposure to manganese fume on structural steel. Options include a lower-fume filler, local exhaust, a rotation schedule, and respirators. Following the hierarchy of controls, which pairing lists the options from most preferred to least preferred?", "options": {"A": "Respirators, rotation schedule, local exhaust, then a lower-fume filler", "B": "Local exhaust, respirators, lower-fume filler, then a rotation schedule", "C": "Lower-fume filler, local exhaust, rotation schedule, then respirators", "D": "Rotation schedule, lower-fume filler, respirators, then local exhaust"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower-fume filler, local exhaust, rotation schedule, then respirators): The hierarchy runs from substitution to engineering controls, then administrative measures, then personal protective equipment. Reducing fume at its source ranks highest, and respirators sit at the bottom."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder receives a new brand of hardfacing electrode, and no safety data sheet came with the order or appears in the binder. The supervisor says the product is just steel wire and tells the welder to proceed. Which response best reflects the hazard communication standard?", "options": {"A": "Proceed, since consumable electrodes are exempt from hazard communication requirements", "B": "Download the sheet of a similar brand and use that document instead", "C": "Proceed using the same precautions as the previous electrode brand", "D": "Request the sheet from the supplier and wait until the employer can provide it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Request the sheet from the supplier and wait until the employer can provide it): Employers must have a safety data sheet for each hazardous chemical and make it accessible. Welding consumables often contain metals such as chromium or manganese, so their sheets matter. A different brand's sheet may list different hazards."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder is told to braze cadmium-plated brackets in a tight corner of a fabrication bay. He proposes wearing a dust mask. The safety officer notes the real hazard is the fume produced as the plating heats. Which course of action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the dust mask, because plated coatings contain too little cadmium to really matter", "B": "Stop, have the exposure assessed, and use controls and respirators as the standard requires", "C": "Switch to a different shielding gas, which stops cadmium from vaporizing", "D": "Brush off the visible silver coating and weld at normal settings afterward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop, have the exposure assessed, and use controls and respirators as the standard requires): Heated cadmium plating produces a toxic fume covered by its own OSHA standard, which requires exposure assessment and proper controls. A dust mask does not filter fume adequately, and gas choice does not prevent vaporization."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Two welders work side by side with identical ventilation. One welds mild steel with E7018 electrodes, and the other welds 316 stainless with flux-cored wire. Industrial hygiene sampling is planned for both. Which difference will the results most likely show?", "options": {"A": "Mild steel welding releases more chromium because iron oxide fume carries it along", "B": "The two jobs produce identical fume composition because arc temperature sets it", "C": "Stainless welding releases hexavalent chromium and nickel, which mild steel does not release", "D": "Stainless welding produces little fume because the chromium forms a protective oxide skin layer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stainless welding releases hexavalent chromium and nickel, which mild steel does not release): Fume composition follows the filler and base metal. Stainless steel contains chromium and nickel, which become fume with hexavalent chromium, while mild steel fume is mostly iron oxide with some manganese."}, {"id": 91, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "Under OSHA's limit of 90 dBA for eight hours, a cutting operator works four hours at 85 dBA and four hours at 95 dBA. A supervisor says the average is 90 dBA, exactly at the limit, so no action is needed. Why is this reasoning flawed?", "options": {"A": "Averaging decibels works only for steady noise, so impulse noise must be added", "B": "Each level is weighted by allowed time, giving a dose of 125 percent, over the limit", "C": "The limit applies only to the louder four hours, so the dose is exactly 100 percent", "D": "Hearing protectors are required below 90 dBA, so any exposure needs them"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Each level is weighted by allowed time, giving a dose of 125 percent, over the limit): OSHA uses a 5 dB exchange rate, so the allowed time halves for each 5 dB increase. Four hours at 95 dBA is a full 100 percent of its allowance and four hours at 85 dBA adds a quarter of another, for 125 percent."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A new welder starts on Monday in a hot foundry bay, and the shop wants him on full production by Tuesday. A safety coordinator proposes a gradual schedule over the first week instead. Which reason best supports the coordinator's plan?", "options": {"A": "Sweat glands only develop after the first full week of employment in hot environments", "B": "Heat tolerance builds over days of gradual exposure, so new workers face higher risk", "C": "Gradual exposure lets the welder skip fluid breaks as the body adapts", "D": "Heat illness affects only workers who are overweight or new to the trade"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heat tolerance builds over days of gradual exposure, so new workers face higher risk): Acclimatization takes several days, and most heat-related deaths occur in the first days on the job. Easing in a new worker lowers risk, but it does not remove the need for water, rest, and shade."}, {"id": 93, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A full oxygen cylinder falls from a cart, and its valve snaps off at the neck because the protection cap was missing. The cylinder is leaking with a loud hiss. Compressed gas can turn a cylinder into a projectile. What should the nearby welders do first?", "options": {"A": "Roll the cylinder outside to the loading dock, standing behind it", "B": "Evacuate the area at once and report the emergency so trained responders can handle it", "C": "Plug the open neck with a wooden dowel until the gas flow stops", "D": "Chain the cylinder where it is and keep working while waiting for the supplier to arrive"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evacuate the area at once and report the emergency so trained responders can handle it): A cylinder with a sheared valve can rocket across the shop, and plugging or moving it puts people in its path. Clearing the area and calling for trained responders is the safe response."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop has only 12 feet between its oxygen and acetylene racks and wants to avoid moving them. The manager proposes a partition. Under OSHA fuel gas rules, which partition would be acceptable in place of the standard 20-foot separation?", "options": {"A": "Any plywood panel at least eight feet tall, painted with two coats of fire retardant paint", "B": "A steel panel two feet high, welded to the floor between the racks", "C": "A hanging fire blanket that covers the racks during overnight storage only", "D": "A noncombustible barrier at least five feet high rated for half an hour of fire"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A noncombustible barrier at least five feet high rated for half an hour of fire): OSHA allows a noncombustible barrier at least five feet high with a half-hour fire rating as an alternative to a 20-foot separation. Combustible plywood or a short steel panel does not meet that standard."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A school's SENSE program lets students retake each module exam. A student scores 100 percent on the Level 1 safety exam but only 70 percent on a welding processes exam. Both exams were taken during the same week. Which outcome follows under SENSE passing rules?", "options": {"A": "Neither module is credited until both exams reach the same percentage score", "B": "Both are credited because the safety score offsets the lower processes score", "C": "Safety must be retaken because processes exams require the very same perfect score", "D": "Safety is credited, but processes needs 75 percent, so that exam must be retaken"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Safety is credited, but processes needs 75 percent, so that exam must be retaken): Each module is judged on its own score. Safety is passed at 100 percent, and other modules at 75 percent, so a 70 does not pass. High scores in one module never offset a low score in another."}, {"id": 96, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder splashes liquid cleaning solvent into his eyes at the bench. The nearest plumbed eyewash is across the shop, a ten-second walk away. Coworkers disagree about whether to call a supervisor first or flush the eyes. What should happen?", "options": {"A": "Apply eye drops from the first aid kit and wait for the burning to ease", "B": "Flush at the eyewash right away for about fifteen minutes while help is called", "C": "Wipe the eyes with a clean shop towel and seek care at shift end", "D": "Walk to the supervisor's office first and complete the incident form"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flush at the eyewash right away for about fifteen minutes while help is called): Chemical eye splashes call for immediate, prolonged flushing, commonly about fifteen minutes, because damage continues until the chemical is diluted. Reports and calls happen alongside flushing, not before it."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder wants to cut a pipe support beside a wood-framed wall. Combustible materials lie within 35 feet of the cut, and gaps in the floor could let sparks fall to the level below. Which plan best follows hot work safety rules?", "options": {"A": "Dampen the wall with a hose and proceed without a fire watch because it stays wet", "B": "Proceed with the cut, because sparks only travel horizontally within six feet", "C": "Move or shield combustibles, cover floor openings, post a fire watch, and stage an extinguisher", "D": "Cut quickly in short bursts so the sparks have less time to land"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Move or shield combustibles, cover floor openings, post a fire watch, and stage an extinguisher): Hot work rules call for removing or shielding combustibles within the work zone, covering openings, posting a fire watch, and having extinguishing equipment ready. Sparks can travel much farther than six feet, and a damp wall dries quickly."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A welder with a full beard is issued a tight-fitting half-mask respirator for stainless steel welding. He says he has used one for years on other jobs with no trouble. Which statement best explains why the respirator program must address this?", "options": {"A": "Beards trap fume against the skin and can cause chromium burns across the jaw", "B": "Half-mask respirators only function properly with a one-day growth of stubble", "C": "Facial hair breaks the seal against the skin, so the respirator cannot give rated protection", "D": "Beards absorb fume and release it as a toxic gas inside the half-mask over time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Facial hair breaks the seal against the skin, so the respirator cannot give rated protection): Tight-fitting respirators rely on a seal against bare skin, and hair under the seal lets contaminated air leak in. Fit testing will fail, so the welder needs to be clean-shaven or use a loose-fitting powered hood."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "A shop installs large ceiling fans for general ventilation and considers the welding area adequately protected. Air sampling at the welder's lapel, taken during a full shift of structural steel work, still shows high manganese. Why might general ventilation alone fail to protect the welder?", "options": {"A": "It cools the fume so it rises faster and lingers longer near the welder", "B": "It is designed for heat control only and has no effect on airborne particles", "C": "It dilutes fume throughout the room but does not remove the plume from the breathing zone", "D": "It removes fume so quickly that the sampling instruments cannot measure the true exposure level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It dilutes fume throughout the room but does not remove the plume from the breathing zone): General ventilation mixes and dilutes contaminated air, while the plume rises directly past the welder's face. Local exhaust at the arc captures fume at the source before it reaches the breathing zone."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "safety_and_health_of_welders", "question": "During cutting, a welder hears a hissing squeal from the torch and sees the flame retreat into the tip, a flashback. Quick action limits damage to the hoses and regulators. Which sequence is correct for the first moves at the torch?", "options": {"A": "Close the torch fuel valve first, then the oxygen valve, then set the torch aside", "B": "Move the torch away from the work and wait for the squeal to stop", "C": "Open the oxygen valve fully to blow the flame back out of the tip", "D": "Close the torch oxygen valve first, then the fuel gas valve, then check the equipment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Close the torch oxygen valve first, then the fuel gas valve, then check the equipment): Closing the oxygen valve first starves the internal flame of oxidizer, and closing the fuel valve follows. The equipment must then be inspected before relighting. Waiting or adding oxygen lets the flashback continue."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabrication shop receives a drawing for a structural T-joint built from 3/8 inch A36 plate. The welding symbol shown calls for a fillet weld and carries the number 1/4 to the left of the fillet symbol. A new welder asks the lead hand what that dimension describes. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "The effective throat of the fillet, measured from the root to the face, is 1/4 inch.", "B": "The leg size of the fillet, measured along each plate surface, is 1/4 inch.", "C": "The finished weld length along the joint is one quarter of the plate length.", "D": "The thickness of the stem plate that must be fused to the base is 1/4 inch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The leg size of the fillet, measured along each plate surface, is 1/4 inch.): A number to the left of a fillet symbol states the leg size. Throat is a derived figure, about 0.707 times the leg on an equal leg fillet, so it is not what that number gives.", "image": "question_images/q_101.png"}, {"id": 102, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is assembling a bracket on a skid frame using shielded metal arc welding with E7018 electrodes. The drawing shows the symbol displayed, with a 1/4 inch fillet on both sides of the reference line. The welder holds the stem square to the base plate. What work does this symbol require on the joint?", "options": {"A": "A 1/4 inch leg fillet on each side of the stem, giving two welds in total.", "B": "A single 1/4 inch fillet on one side, with the opposite side left unwelded.", "C": "A 1/4 inch fillet on one side and a 1/2 inch fillet on the other.", "D": "Two 1/4 inch fillets placed on the same side of the stem plate."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A 1/4 inch leg fillet on each side of the stem, giving two welds in total.): Symbols on both sides of the reference line mean the weld is made on both sides of the joint. Equal sizes of 1/4 inch apply to each side separately, so two fillets are deposited.", "image": "question_images/q_102.png"}, {"id": 103, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop is welding a steel sleeve onto a base plate for a pump mount, using flux cored arc welding. The welding symbol shown has a small circle at the bend where the arrow meets the reference line. The welder is unsure how much of the joint the circle covers. What does the circle instruct?", "options": {"A": "The fillet weld is continued all the way around the joint without a break.", "B": "The weld is deposited only at the bend point where the arrow touches the joint.", "C": "The weld is to be made in the field instead of in the shop.", "D": "The completed weld surface must be ground to a smooth round contour."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The fillet weld is continued all the way around the joint without a break.): The weld-all-around circle sits at the junction of arrow and reference line. It calls for the weld to continue around the full joint, rather than stopping at the first edge or corner.", "image": "question_images/q_103.png"}, {"id": 104, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A steel erector is reviewing a connection detail for a roof truss gusset. The welding symbol shown includes a small solid flag at the junction of the arrow and the reference line. The shop foreman needs to schedule the work correctly for the project. According to AWS A2.4, what does the flag tell the foreman about this weld?", "options": {"A": "The weld is ground flush before inspection takes place.", "B": "The weld is repeated on every similar joint in the assembly.", "C": "The weld is made at the erection site, not in the shop.", "D": "The weld is tested by magnetic particle method after completion."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The weld is made at the erection site, not in the shop.): A flag at the arrow and reference line junction is the field weld symbol. It tells the shop not to make this weld during fabrication, and the crew welds it on site.", "image": "question_images/q_104.png"}, {"id": 105, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is building a machinery base using gas metal arc welding and sees the symbol shown. A fillet symbol is drawn on the reference line, and the note 3-8 appears to the right of it. The welder wants to avoid welding a continuous bead. How should the 3-8 note be read?", "options": {"A": "Each segment is 3 inches long, with 8 inches center to center.", "B": "Each weld segment is 8 inches long, with 3 inches between the segments.", "C": "Three welds are required, each placed 8 inches from the plate edge.", "D": "The weld is 3 inches long and begins 8 inches from the joint end."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Each segment is 3 inches long, with 8 inches center to center.): The first number at the right of the symbol is the segment length and the second is the pitch, measured center to center. Reading them in reverse is a common error.", "image": "question_images/q_105.png"}, {"id": 106, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is joining a 52 inch long channel to a base plate with intermittent fillet welds. The drawing uses the symbol shown, with 4-12 to the right of the fillet. A weld starts at one end, the pitch is held exactly, and the last weld finishes at the far end. How much weld is deposited in total?", "options": {"A": "20 inches of weld, made as five separate 4 inch segments along the joint.", "B": "16 inches of weld, made as four separate 4 inch segments along the joint.", "C": "24 inches of weld, made as six separate 4 inch segments along the joint.", "D": "13 inches of weld, made as one segment for every 4 inches of joint."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (20 inches of weld, made as five separate 4 inch segments along the joint.): Segments start at 0, 12, 24, 36 and 48 inches, and the last one ends at 52. That is five segments of 4 inches, which totals 20 inches.", "image": "question_images/q_106.png"}, {"id": 107, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A plate fitter is preparing a butt joint in 5/8 inch plate for a submerged arc job. The welding symbol shown has a V-groove symbol with the number 1/2 to its left. The apprentice asks what that number tells the fitter about the groove. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "It gives the root opening between the two plates, measured at the joint bottom.", "B": "It gives the leg size of the reinforcing fillet to be added on top.", "C": "It gives the depth of groove preparation on that side of the joint, 1/2 inch.", "D": "It gives the total thickness of the plates before any edge preparation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It gives the depth of groove preparation on that side of the joint, 1/2 inch.): For groove welds, the dimension to the left of the symbol is the depth of preparation. Root opening is shown inside the symbol, and the effective throat appears in parentheses if it differs.", "image": "question_images/q_107.png"}, {"id": 108, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A pressure vessel fabricator reviews a drawing for a thick plate butt joint that will be welded with GTAW root passes and SMAW fill. The welding symbol shown has a V-groove symbol on both sides of the reference line. How is this joint prepared and welded?", "options": {"A": "The edges are beveled on one face only, and the second face is back gouged.", "B": "The edges are left square, and a fillet is added on each face.", "C": "The edges are beveled from both faces, and welding is done from each face.", "D": "One plate is beveled while the mating plate edge stays square."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The edges are beveled from both faces, and welding is done from each face.): V-groove symbols on both sides describe a double-V joint. Preparing and welding both faces balances heat input and reduces the weld metal needed on thick plate.", "image": "question_images/q_108.png"}, {"id": 109, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder reads a symbol containing a bevel groove symbol, which has one vertical leg and one slanted leg. The assignment is a corner joint in 1/2 inch structural plate being welded with FCAW. The welder wants to confirm what the shape tells about the edge preparation. Which description matches the symbol shown?", "options": {"A": "Both members have edges beveled to an equal angle on the same face.", "B": "Only one of the two members has its edge beveled, while the other stays square.", "C": "Neither member is prepared, and the plates are simply butted together.", "D": "A round hole is drilled in one member and filled with weld metal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Only one of the two members has its edge beveled, while the other stays square.): A bevel groove symbol has one straight leg and one angled leg, showing that only one member is beveled. A V-groove uses two angled legs for both members.", "image": "question_images/q_109.png"}, {"id": 110, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "An apprentice is learning to read symbols on a drawing for a thin gauge sheet metal enclosure being welded with GTAW. The welding symbol shown contains two short parallel vertical lines on the reference line. The apprentice asks which joint preparation this groove symbol stands for. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "A U-groove, where the plate edges are machined to a curved profile.", "B": "A flare bevel groove, where one member has a rounded outside corner.", "C": "A square groove, where the plate edges are not beveled before welding.", "D": "A J-groove, where one edge has a hooked machined profile."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A square groove, where the plate edges are not beveled before welding.): Two parallel vertical lines form the square groove symbol, which means the edges are prepared straight with no bevel. The root gap is shown inside the symbol if one is needed.", "image": "question_images/q_110.png"}, {"id": 111, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A maintenance crew is repairing a conveyor frame made from overlapped 1/4 inch plates. The drawing uses the symbol shown, which has a small rectangle on the reference line and the number 3/4 to its left. The foreman asks a new helper what that number represents. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "The depth of the hole, measured from the outer face to the lower plate.", "B": "The distance between neighboring holes, measured center to center.", "C": "The diameter of the hole in the top member that gets filled with weld.", "D": "The leg size of the fillet that wraps around the hole edge."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The diameter of the hole in the top member that gets filled with weld.): A plug weld symbol is a rectangle, and the number at its left is the hole diameter. Pitch is shown separately at the right of the symbol, if holes repeat along the joint.", "image": "question_images/q_111.png"}, {"id": 112, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding supervisor hands a welder a drawing marked with the symbol shown. A letter and number combination appears in the tail of the symbol, in the form WPS-14. The welder asks where to find the process, filler and preheat to use. What does the tail entry point the welder to?", "options": {"A": "The fourteenth weld on the drawing, which must be made first in sequence.", "B": "Welding procedure specification number 14, listing the process and parameters.", "C": "The fourteen inch spacing required between any two adjacent welds.", "D": "The inspection report where the weld is signed off after completion."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Welding procedure specification number 14, listing the process and parameters.): The tail holds supplementary information such as a process, specification or procedure reference. Here it refers the welder to the procedure document that controls the weld.", "image": "question_images/q_112.png"}, {"id": 113, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "An inspector measures the fillets on a T-joint and finds a 1/4 inch fillet on both sides. The drawing carries the welding symbol shown, with different sizes listed on each side of the reference line. Both welds are sound and free of defects. What should the inspector conclude?", "options": {"A": "Both welds are acceptable because 1/4 inch meets the smaller of the two sizes.", "B": "The far side weld is undersized and needs 5/16 inch legs.", "C": "The weld on the arrow side is oversized and must be reduced to 1/4 inch.", "D": "The sizes are interchangeable, so the welder may pick either on each side."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The far side weld is undersized and needs 5/16 inch legs.): Each side has its own size, and the number applies only to the side where its symbol sits. A sound weld that is smaller than specified is still a nonconformance.", "image": "question_images/q_113.png"}, {"id": 114, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A beam to column connection is being welded for a building frame using SMAW. The drawing shows the symbol displayed with both the weld-all-around circle and a field weld flag. The erection foreman plans the crew's sequence for the site. Which instruction matches the symbol?", "options": {"A": "Make the fillet at the site, continuing it all the way around the joint.", "B": "Make the fillet in the shop, then grind it flush around the joint.", "C": "Make the fillet in the shop on one side, and the remaining side on site.", "D": "Make the fillet at the erection site, but only at the joint corner."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Make the fillet at the site, continuing it all the way around the joint.): The circle calls for continuous welding around the joint, and the flag moves the work to the job site. Both marks sit at the junction of arrow and reference line.", "image": "question_images/q_114.png"}, {"id": 115, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is adding a stiffener to a trailer frame and reads the symbol shown, where a fillet sits on the reference line and the number 6 appears to the right of the symbol. The welder wants to know whether this is a continuous or partial weld. What does the 6 indicate?", "options": {"A": "The weld is made in six passes, with each pass cleaned by wire brush.", "B": "The fillet leg grows to 6 sixteenths of an inch at the toe.", "C": "The weld is repeated six times along the full length of the joint.", "D": "The weld is 6 inches long, and the rest of the joint stays unwelded."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weld is 6 inches long, and the rest of the joint stays unwelded.): A single number to the right of a symbol is a weld length. With no pitch given, one weld of that length is made, and the remainder of the joint is not welded.", "image": "question_images/q_115.png"}, {"id": 116, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabricator is welding a lifting lug to a plate and checks throat size for a calculation. The welding symbol shown specifies an equal leg fillet with 1/4 inch legs. The designer wants the effective throat to be used in the strength calculation. What is the throat of this weld, to three decimal places?", "options": {"A": "0.250 inch, which equals the leg size stated on the symbol.", "B": "0.177 inch, which is the leg multiplied by 0.707.", "C": "0.354 inch, which is the leg multiplied by 1.414.", "D": "0.125 inch, which is half of the stated leg size."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.177 inch, which is the leg multiplied by 0.707.): For an equal leg fillet, throat = 0.707 × leg. With a 1/4 inch leg, 0.707 × 0.250 = 0.177 inch. Multiplying by 1.414 gives the hypotenuse, not the throat.", "image": "question_images/q_116.png"}, {"id": 117, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A structural drawing calls for the symbol shown on a heavy bracket: equal 3/8 inch fillets on both sides of a stem. An estimator needs the weld metal cross-section for one inch of joint. Treating each fillet as a right triangle with equal legs, what is the approximate total area per inch of joint?", "options": {"A": "About 0.070 square inch, from a single triangle on one side only.", "B": "About 0.281 square inch, from two triangles each 0.375 × 0.375.", "C": "About 0.375 square inch, from the leg size taken as the area.", "D": "About 0.141 square inch, from two triangles of 0.5 × 0.375 × 0.375."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 0.141 square inch, from two triangles of 0.5 × 0.375 × 0.375.): Each triangle is 0.5 × 0.375 × 0.375 = 0.0703 square inch. Two sides double it to 0.1406. Leaving out the one half factor gives 0.281, a frequent slip.", "image": "question_images/q_117.png"}, {"id": 118, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A crew is welding a heavy bracket onto a girder. The drawing carries the symbol shown, with a V-groove and a field weld flag at the junction. The shop foreman is deciding how to treat the joint during fabrication. What should the foreman do about this groove weld?", "options": {"A": "Weld the groove in the shop and only inspect it in the field.", "B": "Prepare the groove edges in the shop but leave the welding for the field.", "C": "Skip the groove preparation because the field crew will cut the edges.", "D": "Weld half the groove in the shop and finish the rest in the field."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prepare the groove edges in the shop but leave the welding for the field.): The field flag controls where the welding happens, while the groove symbol controls the edge preparation. Fit-up and bevels are usually completed in the shop for a field weld.", "image": "question_images/q_118.png"}, {"id": 119, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A boiler shop welds a rectangular collar, 4 inches by 6 inches on the outside, to a flat plate. The welding symbol shown has the weld-all-around circle on a 5/16 inch fillet. The estimator wants the total length of fillet to deposit. What length applies?", "options": {"A": "10 inches, which is half the perimeter of the collar.", "B": "20 inches, which is the perimeter of the collar.", "C": "24 inches, which is the area of the rectangle.", "D": "30 inches, which is the sum of the sides plus the diagonal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20 inches, which is the perimeter of the collar.): Weld all around means the full perimeter, so 2 × (4 + 6) = 20 inches. Area is a different quantity, and a diagonal is not welded.", "image": "question_images/q_119.png"}, {"id": 120, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is joining a 32 inch long bracket flange with intermittent fillets using the symbol shown, which has 2-10 at the right of the fillet. The first weld begins at one end, the pitch is maintained, and the last weld finishes exactly at the far end. How much total weld is laid?", "options": {"A": "6 inches, made as three segments of 2 inches each.", "B": "10 inches, made as five segments of 2 inches each.", "C": "8 inches, made as four segments of 2 inches each.", "D": "32 inches, made as one continuous bead along the joint."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (8 inches, made as four segments of 2 inches each.): Segments begin at 0, 10, 20 and 30 inches, and the last one ends at 32. Four segments of 2 inches give 8 inches in total.", "image": "question_images/q_120.png"}, {"id": 121, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fitter is working on a lap joint where a plug weld symbol is shown on the reference line with a 1/2 inch size. The symbol is placed on the side opposite the arrow. The fitter must decide which member receives the drilled or punched hole before assembly. Where does the hole go?", "options": {"A": "In the member that lies on the side opposite the arrow, then filled by welding.", "B": "In the member on the arrow side, because the arrow points to the hole.", "C": "In both members, so that the holes line up through the joint.", "D": "In neither member, because the plug is formed by weld metal alone."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (In the member that lies on the side opposite the arrow, then filled by welding.): For plug welds, the symbol side identifies which member carries the hole. A symbol on the opposite side puts the hole in the far member, and the arrow side member forms the backing surface.", "image": "question_images/q_121.png"}, {"id": 122, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A butt joint in 1/4 inch plate will be welded with GTAW. The welding symbol shown has a square groove symbol on the side opposite the arrow, with 1/8 to the left of it. The inspector asks what the dimension specifies. What does it control?", "options": {"A": "The root opening, which is the gap that must be left between plates.", "B": "The leg size of a fillet that will be laid over the groove.", "C": "The groove weld size, meaning the depth of penetration, which is 1/8 inch.", "D": "The thickness of the backing strip that is tacked under the joint."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The groove weld size, meaning the depth of penetration, which is 1/8 inch.): On a groove symbol, the number at the left is the groove weld size, which is the depth of penetration. Root opening is shown inside the symbol rather than left of it.", "image": "question_images/q_122.png"}, {"id": 123, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A tank builder is welding a flat cover to a shell flange and sees the symbol shown, with a 1/4 inch fillet on both sides and the weld-all-around circle. The welder asks how the circle applies to the two sides. Which reading is correct?", "options": {"A": "Each side is welded around the entire joint with a 1/4 inch fillet.", "B": "Only the arrow side is welded around, while the other side is skip welded.", "C": "Only the first side is welded around, and the other is made at the bend.", "D": "Each side is welded around, but the sizes reduce to 3/16 inch at corners."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Each side is welded around the entire joint with a 1/4 inch fillet.): The circle applies to the whole symbol, so welds are continuous around the joint on both sides. Sizes do not change at corners unless the drawing says so.", "image": "question_images/q_123.png"}, {"id": 124, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A structural drawing for a heavy T-joint on a crane runway girder shows the symbol displayed, with a V-groove on the arrow side and a fillet on the other side. A welder asks how the finished joint is built. Which description matches the symbol?", "options": {"A": "A groove weld is made from both faces, with no fillet required anywhere.", "B": "Groove weld from one face, with a reinforcing fillet from the other face.", "C": "A fillet is laid on the arrow side, with a groove cut on the other face.", "D": "Two fillets are laid on the same face, with a groove cut underneath."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Groove weld from one face, with a reinforcing fillet from the other face.): The groove symbol and the fillet symbol sit on opposite sides, so each weld is made from its own face. The fillet adds strength at the root side of the groove weld.", "image": "question_images/q_124.png"}, {"id": 125, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop makes a bracket with intermittent fillets using the symbol shown, with 3-6 to the right of the fillet. The flange is 21 inches long, a weld begins at the near end, the pitch is held, and the last segment ends at the far end. What total length of weld is deposited?", "options": {"A": "9 inches, made as three segments of 3 inches each.", "B": "15 inches, made as five segments of 3 inches each.", "C": "21 inches, made as seven segments of 3 inches each.", "D": "12 inches, made as four segments of 3 inches each."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (12 inches, made as four segments of 3 inches each.): Segments start at 0, 6, 12 and 18 inches, and the last one ends at 21. Four segments of 3 inches add to 12 inches.", "image": "question_images/q_125.png"}, {"id": 126, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drafting student is learning AWS A2.4 and asks where the symbol for an arrow side weld is placed. A shop trainer draws a horizontal reference line with an arrow leaving one end. On which side of the reference line is the weld symbol drawn when the weld goes on the arrow side?", "options": {"A": "On the upper side of the reference line, whichever way the arrow points.", "B": "On the side of the reference line that is opposite the arrow tail.", "C": "On the left half of the reference line, with the right half left clear.", "D": "On the side of the reference line that is closest to the arrow head."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (On the side of the reference line that is closest to the arrow head.): The arrow side is the side of the joint the arrow touches. A symbol on the side of the reference line nearest the arrow head means arrow side, and the opposite placement means other side."}, {"id": 127, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding inspector is checking a groove weld symbol that includes a root opening of 3/16 inch for a pipe joint welded with GTAW. The apprentice asks where the root opening dimension is drawn on the symbol. What is the correct location?", "options": {"A": "Inside the groove weld symbol, drawn within the V shape.", "B": "To the left of the groove symbol, in the same spot as the size.", "C": "In the tail of the welding symbol, with any process notes.", "D": "Above the reference line, next to the weld length."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inside the groove weld symbol, drawn within the V shape.): AWS A2.4 places the root opening inside the groove symbol. The groove depth goes to the left of the symbol, and the groove angle goes outside the symbol, so the three values do not clash."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A grinding operator must follow a drawing that shows a flush finish symbol over a fillet. A letter appears next to the finish symbol. The shop is using the standard AWS finish designators. Which letter tells the operator to finish the weld by grinding?", "options": {"A": "The letter C, which is the AWS designator for grinding the weld.", "B": "The letter G, which is the AWS designator for grinding the weld.", "C": "The letter M, which is the AWS designator for grinding the weld.", "D": "The letter P, which is the AWS designator for grinding the weld."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The letter G, which is the AWS designator for grinding the weld.): AWS finish designators are C for chipping, G for grinding, M for machining, H for hammering, R for rolling and P for peening. Only G means grinding."}, {"id": 129, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A new fitter looks at the right side of a weld symbol and sees a dimension that follows the fillet symbol, written 5 inches. The fitter confuses it with the leg size. According to AWS A2.4, what does a dimension placed to the right of the weld symbol describe?", "options": {"A": "The leg size of the weld, with the length shown to the left.", "B": "The throat of the weld, with the leg shown to the left.", "C": "The length of the weld, with the leg size shown to the left.", "D": "The root opening of the weld, with the angle shown to the left."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The length of the weld, with the leg size shown to the left.): Size goes to the left of the weld symbol and length goes to the right. Mixing the two sides is a common reading error on shop drawings."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A pipe fitter is reviewing a welding symbol that contains a fillet symbol with its vertical leg drawn on one side. The fitter is drafting a new drawing and wonders which side the vertical leg goes on. Under AWS A2.4, where is the vertical leg of a fillet symbol drawn?", "options": {"A": "On the right side of the symbol, so it points toward the tail.", "B": "On the side nearest the arrow, so it points at the joint.", "C": "On the left side of the symbol, whichever direction the weld runs.", "D": "On the side nearest the tail, so it matches the weld direction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (On the left side of the symbol, whichever direction the weld runs.): The vertical leg of the fillet triangle is always drawn on the left. That convention lets everyone read the symbol the same way, no matter which way the joint faces."}, {"id": 131, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A rookie welder sees a weld symbol on a bracket drawing that has a symbol drawn on the opposite side of the reference line from the arrow head. The welder asks the lead hand where the weld goes. What does a weld symbol on that side of the reference line indicate?", "options": {"A": "The weld is made on the same side of the joint as the arrow.", "B": "The weld is made on both sides of the joint at once.", "C": "The weld is made after the joint has been fully inspected.", "D": "The weld is made on the side of the joint away from the arrow."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weld is made on the side of the joint away from the arrow.): A symbol on the side of the reference line away from the arrow head is an other side weld, which goes on the far side of the joint from where the arrow touches. A symbol drawn on the arrow side of the line would instead call for the weld on the same side of the joint that the arrow touches."}, {"id": 132, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing calls for a V-groove weld with a 60 degree included angle on a symmetrical butt joint in 3/4 inch plate for a pressure piping job. A fitter needs to cut the bevel on each plate edge before welding with SMAW. What bevel angle per plate gives the specified included angle?", "options": {"A": "60 degrees on each plate, which adds up to 120 degrees.", "B": "45 degrees on each plate, which adds up to 90 degrees.", "C": "30 degrees on each plate, which adds up to 60 degrees.", "D": "15 degrees on each plate, which adds up to 30 degrees."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (30 degrees on each plate, which adds up to 60 degrees.): The included angle is the sum of both bevels. For a symmetrical joint, each plate carries half of 60 degrees, so 30 degrees per edge."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A structural drawing shows a V-groove symbol that reads 1/2 followed by 3/8 in parentheses. A welder preparing a butt joint in 5/8 inch plate for FCAW asks what the two numbers mean. What does each number indicate on this symbol?", "options": {"A": "The groove depth is 1/2 inch, and the effective throat is 3/8 inch.", "B": "The groove depth is 3/8 inch, and the effective throat is 1/2 inch.", "C": "The root opening is 1/2 inch, and the groove angle is 3/8 degree.", "D": "The leg size is 1/2 inch, and the weld length is 3/8 inch."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The groove depth is 1/2 inch, and the effective throat is 3/8 inch.): The depth of preparation is written first. The effective throat, when it differs from the depth, is written in parentheses after it."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabricator reads a symbol where the weld symbol sits on one side of the reference line for a lap joint in 3/16 inch plate, and a second reference line sits under the first with a G finish symbol and a flat line. The welder asks about the extra requirement. What does it call for?", "options": {"A": "A convex contour, finished by peening after the weld has been made.", "B": "A flush contour, finished by grinding after the weld has been made.", "C": "A concave contour, finished by chipping after the weld has been made.", "D": "A flush contour, finished by machining before the weld is made."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A flush contour, finished by grinding after the weld has been made.): A straight contour line with a letter calls for a flush surface, and G names grinding as the method. The finish operation happens after welding, not before."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop is welding a bracket where the drawing shows fillet symbols on both sides of the reference line for an intermittent weld, 2-6, and the symbols on the two sides are offset from each other along the line. A welder asks what the offset means. How should it be read?", "options": {"A": "The segments on the two sides sit directly opposite each other.", "B": "The segments on one side are longer than those on the other.", "C": "The segments on the two sides are staggered, not directly opposite.", "D": "The segments on both sides start from the same end of the joint."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The segments on the two sides are staggered, not directly opposite.): Offset symbols describe a staggered intermittent weld, where segments on opposite faces alternate. Chain welds, by contrast, line up directly opposite each other."}, {"id": 136, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding symbol has two reference lines stacked one over the other, and each line carries its own weld or operation. A new welder is not sure which operation to perform first. According to AWS A2.4, in what order are operations on multiple reference lines performed?", "options": {"A": "In order from the reference line farthest from the arrow inward to the nearest.", "B": "In order from left to right, whatever the stacking order of the lines.", "C": "In order from the reference line nearest the arrow outward to the farthest.", "D": "In any order the welder prefers, provided each operation is completed."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (In order from the reference line nearest the arrow outward to the farthest.): Multiple reference lines show sequence. The line closest to the arrow is first, and each line farther away follows, such as a groove weld first and a fillet reinforcement next."}, {"id": 137, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is working on a single V-groove butt joint welded from one side, where the drawing also includes a back weld. A younger welder asks about timing. According to AWS A2.4, when is a back weld made in relation to the groove weld?", "options": {"A": "After the groove weld, on the root side of the joint.", "B": "Before the groove weld, as a backing layer on the root side.", "C": "At the same time as the groove weld, from the same face.", "D": "After the groove weld, on the same face as the groove."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (After the groove weld, on the root side of the joint.): A back weld is placed on the root side of a groove weld after the groove weld is complete. A backing weld, by contrast, is made first so the groove weld has support."}, {"id": 138, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A design drawing for a socket attachment shows a welding symbol with a fillet and no size beside it. The welder cannot find a size on the symbol or in the tail. The drawing has a general notes block. How is the fillet size normally determined in that case?", "options": {"A": "The general note on the drawing that governs unsized fillet welds.", "B": "From the thickness of the thinner plate, taken as the leg size.", "C": "From the width of the reference line, measured on the print.", "D": "From the welder's judgment, based on the material being joined."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The general note on the drawing that governs unsized fillet welds.): When no size is on the symbol, the drawing's general note or specification controls it. A welder never guesses, since the designer calculated the required size."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A pipe fitter calculates the throat of a 3/8 inch equal leg fillet for a load check, using the standard relationship for a flat faced fillet. The result will be compared to the design requirement of 0.25 inch minimum throat. What throat does the 3/8 inch fillet provide?", "options": {"A": "About 0.375 inch, which exceeds the 0.25 inch minimum.", "B": "About 0.188 inch, which falls below the 0.25 inch minimum.", "C": "About 0.265 inch, which meets the 0.25 inch minimum.", "D": "About 0.530 inch, which exceeds the 0.25 inch minimum."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 0.265 inch, which meets the 0.25 inch minimum.): Throat = 0.707 × leg = 0.707 × 0.375 = 0.265 inch. That exceeds 0.25 inch, so the fillet is adequate, though the leg and throat must never be confused."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder reads a drawing for a heavy plate butt joint with a V-groove symbol on one side and a small melt-through symbol on the other side of the reference line. A young welder asks what the second symbol requires. What does the melt-through symbol call for?", "options": {"A": "Full penetration, with visible root reinforcement on the far side.", "B": "A fillet that is laid on the far side as a reinforcement pass.", "C": "A backing bar that is tacked to the far side before welding.", "D": "A weld that is ground flush on the face of the groove side."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Full penetration, with visible root reinforcement on the far side.): The melt-through symbol sits opposite the groove symbol and calls for complete joint penetration with root reinforcement showing on that side. The height may be stated beside it."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop drawing uses a fillet symbol with a 1/4 inch leg and the note 2-5 for intermittent welds on a 17 inch joint. The first segment starts at one end, the pitch is held exactly, and the last segment finishes at the other end. How many welds does the welder make?", "options": {"A": "Three welds, which total 6 inches of fillet along the joint.", "B": "Five welds, which total 10 inches of fillet along the joint.", "C": "Four welds, which total 8 inches of fillet along the joint.", "D": "Seventeen welds, which total 34 inches of fillet along the joint."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Four welds, which total 8 inches of fillet along the joint.): Segments start at 0, 5, 10 and 15 inches and end at 2, 7, 12 and 17. That is four segments of 2 inches, so 8 inches of weld."}, {"id": 142, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing for a rectangular frame shows a groove symbol with the number 60 drawn near it, plus a root opening of 1/8 inch. A fitter preparing the joint for a GMAW job asks where on the welding symbol the groove angle is written. Which placement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Outside the groove symbol, on the same side as the symbol.", "B": "Inside the groove symbol, directly under the root opening figure.", "C": "In the tail of the symbol, in front of the process abbreviation.", "D": "At the left end of the reference line, beside the arrow."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Outside the groove symbol, on the same side as the symbol.): The groove angle is drawn outside the groove symbol, and the root opening goes inside it. Keeping the two apart stops a fitter from swapping an angle for a gap."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop supervisor sees a welding symbol with a field weld flag on a gusset plate connection. To save crane time at the site, the supervisor has the fabricator weld it in the shop. What is the main fabrication risk of ignoring the flag?", "options": {"A": "Early welding can lock in misalignment the erection crew needed to adjust.", "B": "The weld would need a higher preheat in the shop than in the field.", "C": "The shop weld would have to use a larger fillet size than the drawing.", "D": "The weld could not be inspected because shop inspectors skip field welds."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early welding can lock in misalignment the erection crew needed to adjust.): The flag exists because the member positions are set during erection. A shop weld removes that adjustment and may leave holes or edges that no longer line up at the site."}, {"id": 144, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing shows a V-groove symbol with 45 degrees written outside it and 1/8 inch inside it for a butt joint in 1/2 inch plate. A welder bevels each plate edge to 45 degrees. The inspector rejects the preparation. Why does the inspector reject it?", "options": {"A": "The 45 degrees is the included angle, so each edge should be about 22.5 degrees.", "B": "The 45 degrees is the root face, so the plates needed a land cut instead.", "C": "The 45 degrees applies to one plate only, so the other must be square.", "D": "The 45 degrees is the bevel per plate, so the bevels were cut correctly."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 45 degrees is the included angle, so each edge should be about 22.5 degrees.): The groove angle on a symmetrical V is the included angle, the total between both bevels. Two 45 degree bevels give 90 degrees, which is about twice the angle specified."}, {"id": 145, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "An inspection checklist for a tank flange weld shows a fillet symbol with a curved contour mark and the letter M. After welding, the grinder blends the weld flat using a disc and says the surface is better than required. Which statement best describes the result?", "options": {"A": "The weld meets the symbol, because a flatter surface is better than a convex one.", "B": "It fails, because a convex contour finished by machining was specified.", "C": "The weld meets the symbol, because grinding and machining are the same operation.", "D": "The weld does not meet the symbol, because a concave contour was specified."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It fails, because a convex contour finished by machining was specified.): A curved contour mark calls for a convex shape and M names machining. Grinding to flat changes both the contour and the method, so it is a nonconformance even if the surface looks neat."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A butt joint in 3/8 inch plate carries a V-groove symbol with no size figure beside it. A welder deposits a partial penetration weld that fills about half the thickness and the result looks sound. Under AWS A2.4 practice, what should the inspector decide?", "options": {"A": "The weld is accepted, because half the thickness is the default groove size.", "B": "The weld is accepted, because a sound surface appearance meets the symbol.", "C": "Rejected, because an unsized groove symbol implies complete joint penetration.", "D": "The weld is rejected, because a 1/4 inch groove depth is the default size."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rejected, because an unsized groove symbol implies complete joint penetration.): When no size is shown for a groove weld, the symbol is read as complete joint penetration through the thickness. A half depth weld therefore leaves the joint under strength."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding symbol shows an intermittent fillet marked 4-10. A trainee welds 4 inch segments and leaves a 10 inch gap between each pair, measuring from the end of one segment to the start of the next. What did the trainee get wrong?", "options": {"A": "The 10 is the weld length, so each segment should run 10 inches.", "B": "The 10 is a center to center pitch, so the clear gap should be 6 inches.", "C": "The 10 is an end to end pitch, so the gap should be 14 inches.", "D": "The 4 is the number of welds, so four segments were needed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The 10 is a center to center pitch, so the clear gap should be 6 inches.): Pitch is measured from the center of one segment to the center of the next. With 4 inch segments, the clear gap is 10 − 4 = 6 inches."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A corner joint drawing shows a bevel groove symbol whose arrow line has a bend, with the arrow tip touching the horizontal plate. A fitter must decide which plate to bevel before welding with SMAW. Which plate gets the bevel?", "options": {"A": "The vertical plate, because the arrow bend points away from the beveled member.", "B": "The horizontal plate, because the arrow tip touches the member to be beveled.", "C": "Neither plate, because the bevel is cut into the weld metal afterward.", "D": "Both plates, because a bevel groove needs both edges prepared."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The horizontal plate, because the arrow tip touches the member to be beveled.): On a bevel or J groove symbol, the broken arrow points at the member to be prepared. The other member stays square, so only the plate touched by the tip is beveled."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A structural engineer specifies that a fillet weld needs an effective throat of at least 0.35 inch. The fabricator may choose from 1/4, 3/8, 1/2 and 5/8 inch equal leg fillets. Which is the smallest standard leg size that satisfies the throat requirement?", "options": {"A": "1/2 inch, since the required leg is about 0.35 ÷ 0.707 = 0.495 inch.", "B": "3/8 inch, since its throat is 0.375 inch measured along the leg.", "C": "1/4 inch, since its throat is 0.354 inch from doubling the leg.", "D": "5/8 inch, since the throat must be at least 0.35 times the leg."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1/2 inch, since the required leg is about 0.35 ÷ 0.707 = 0.495 inch.): Throat = 0.707 × leg, so the leg is 0.35 ÷ 0.707 ≈ 0.495 inch, and 1/2 inch is the next standard size up. A 3/8 inch fillet gives only 0.265 inch of throat."}, {"id": 150, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A symmetrical double-V groove in 3/4 inch plate carries a depth of 3/8 inch on the arrow side and 5/16 inch on the other side. A fitter calculates the root face, the unprepared land left between the two grooves. What land thickness is left?", "options": {"A": "1/8 inch, from 3/4 minus 3/8 minus 1/4 for the two grooves.", "B": "3/16 inch, from 3/4 minus 3/8 minus 3/16 for the two grooves.", "C": "7/16 inch, from 3/4 minus only 5/16 for one groove.", "D": "1/16 inch, from 3/4 minus 3/8 minus 5/16 for the two grooves."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (1/16 inch, from 3/4 minus 3/8 minus 5/16 for the two grooves.): Land = plate thickness − depth on each side = 12/16 − 6/16 − 5/16 = 1/16 inch. Forgetting one side's depth leaves a land that is far too thick."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fitter is preparing 3/4 inch carbon steel plate for a V-groove butt joint. The drawing for the joint shows a 30° bevel on each plate. The engineer asks for a wider groove for easier access, so the fitter machines each plate to 45°. What included angle does the joint now have?", "options": {"A": "The included angle becomes 90°, because the two 45° bevels add together across the groove.", "B": "The included angle becomes 45°, because the groove takes the same angle as one beveled plate.", "C": "The included angle becomes 75°, because only the added 15° counts toward the groove opening.", "D": "The included angle becomes 135°, because each bevel is then measured from the plate surface instead."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The included angle becomes 90°, because the two 45° bevels add together across the groove.): Included angle is the total angle between the two groove faces, so it equals the sum of both bevels. Measuring from the plate surface instead would give 45° per side, but the bevel is measured from the perpendicular.", "image": "question_images/q_151.png"}, {"id": 152, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shop drawing for a SMAW V-groove butt joint on 1/2 inch plate shows a 30° bevel on each plate. A revised detail narrows the groove to a 45° included angle to save filler metal, and an experienced welder warns about the change. Which problem is most likely to appear as the groove gets narrower?", "options": {"A": "Lack of sidewall fusion, because the electrode has less room to reach the groove faces.", "B": "Burn-through at the root, because a narrower groove concentrates the arc heat onto thinner base metal.", "C": "Slag trapping at the crown, because a narrower groove forms a convex cap on top.", "D": "Higher filler volume, because the smaller angle forces the welder to add more passes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lack of sidewall fusion, because the electrode has less room to reach the groove faces.): A tighter included angle restricts electrode angle and access, so the arc may bridge the sidewall without fusing it. The narrower groove does reduce filler volume, which makes the volume option backwards.", "image": "question_images/q_152.png"}, {"id": 153, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "Two pipe ends are machined for a groove weld, and the drawing note states that each end carries a 37.5° bevel. An apprentice asks which reference the bevel angle is measured from. The inspector, who is reviewing the cross section for the drawing package, explains that the angle is measured from what?", "options": {"A": "A line perpendicular to the surface of the pipe wall at the joint end.", "B": "The face of the opposite pipe once the two beveled ends have been brought together.", "C": "The weld axis running along the center of the finished root opening gap.", "D": "The outside surface of the pipe, measured directly at the toe of the weld."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A line perpendicular to the surface of the pipe wall at the joint end.): Bevel angle is the angle between the prepared edge and a plane perpendicular to the member surface. Two 37.5° bevels together give a 75° included angle, which is the common pipe groove.", "image": "question_images/q_153.png"}, {"id": 154, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fit-up report on a 1/2 inch plate butt joint, drawn with a 2 mm gap between the plates, shows that a tacker opened the gap to 5 mm without changing the 30° bevels. The root pass will be run with a 1/8 inch E6010 electrode. What is the most probable consequence of the wider gap?", "options": {"A": "The root will fuse more easily, so the second pass can be skipped to save time.", "B": "The root pass is more likely to burn through and needs extra filler to bridge it.", "C": "The included angle shrinks to 25°, which restricts electrode manipulation along both groove faces.", "D": "The joint becomes a fillet weld because the plates no longer touch each other."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The root pass is more likely to burn through and needs extra filler to bridge it.): A larger root opening allows deeper penetration but also takes more filler and invites burn-through, especially without a backing strip. Root opening does not change the bevel angle or the type of joint.", "image": "question_images/q_154.png"}, {"id": 155, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A new welder asks why the plates in the diagram are set apart by a 3 mm space instead of being pushed tightly together. Both plates are 3/8 inch mild steel, welded from one side with an open root and no backing strip. What is the purpose of that space at the bottom of the groove?", "options": {"A": "It lets the arc reach the joint bottom so the root pass fuses both plates.", "B": "It allows the plates to expand freely so the finished weld develops no residual stress.", "C": "It gives the inspector a place to measure the size of the finished fillet weld.", "D": "It keeps slag from the cover pass away from the beveled faces during the first pass."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It lets the arc reach the joint bottom so the root pass fuses both plates.): The gap lets the arc penetrate through the thickness at the root so the first pass fuses both members. It controls fusion, not stress, and it is a groove feature rather than a fillet measurement.", "image": "question_images/q_155.png"}, {"id": 156, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A structural shop must weld 1 inch plate in the flat position and wants to limit distortion and filler cost. An estimator compares a single V-groove with the double-V groove drawn for this job, which is prepared on both faces. What advantage does the double-V preparation offer on plate this thick?", "options": {"A": "It needs no root opening, because the second groove supplies the penetration for the first side.", "B": "It uses more filler metal but removes the need to inspect the joint afterward.", "C": "It uses less filler metal and allows alternating sides, which balances shrinkage and reduces angular distortion.", "D": "It creates a joint with one bevel angle, so the fitter can skip groove preparation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It uses less filler metal and allows alternating sides, which balances shrinkage and reduces angular distortion.): Preparing both faces cuts the groove volume roughly in half compared with a single V on thick plate, and alternating passes balances shrinkage. It still needs inspection and normal fit-up.", "image": "question_images/q_156.png"}, {"id": 157, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder has finished welding one side of the double-V groove butt joint drawn for a 1 inch plate and now needs to start the second side. The root of the first weld is still buried and may hold slag or porosity. What should the welder do before welding the second side?", "options": {"A": "Grind the crown of the first weld flat and begin welding the second side immediately.", "B": "Preheat the plate to the interpass temperature and weld over the root without further preparation.", "C": "Back gouge the root from the second side to sound metal, then weld the second groove.", "D": "Add a steel backing strip to the second side so the arc misses the first weld."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Back gouge the root from the second side to sound metal, then weld the second groove.): Back gouging removes the unfused root area and contaminants so the second side fuses into clean weld metal. Flattening the crown or welding over the root would leave a defect locked inside the joint.", "image": "question_images/q_157.png"}, {"id": 158, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabricator receives a drawing for 1 inch plate that shows a square-groove butt joint with a 2 mm root opening, welded from one side only by SMAW. The inspector doubts the detail will fuse through the section. Which change would let the joint achieve full penetration?", "options": {"A": "Increase the amperage until the arc produces enough heat to melt through the whole plate thickness.", "B": "Weld the joint vertically so that gravity pulls the molten pool toward the root.", "C": "Prepare a bevel or V-groove on the plate edges so the arc can reach the root.", "D": "Replace the root opening with a lap so the plates overlap before the weld is started."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prepare a bevel or V-groove on the plate edges so the arc can reach the root.): A square groove on thick plate leaves the arc unable to reach the root with adequate fusion, so the edges must be beveled. More amperage tends to cause undercut and burn-through rather than full fusion.", "image": "question_images/q_158.png"}, {"id": 159, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding student is told to place one fillet weld on the joint shown, on the side where the upright plate meets the horizontal plate. The instructor wants the student to count the weld toes after finishing. How many toes does one finished fillet weld have on this joint?", "options": {"A": "Two toes, one on the base plate and one on the upright plate.", "B": "One toe, located at the root where the two plates first touch each other.", "C": "Three toes, one on each plate and one more at the crown of the weld.", "D": "Four toes, because a fillet weld shows two toes along each of the plates."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two toes, one on the base plate and one on the upright plate.): The toe is the junction between the weld face and each base metal surface, so a single fillet weld has two. The root is a separate feature, and the crown does not form a toe.", "image": "question_images/q_159.png"}, {"id": 160, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder must run a single fillet weld on the joint drawn, with the base plate lying flat and the upright plate standing vertical. The weld is made on one side of the joint with the weld axis roughly horizontal. Under the AWS position designations, what is this fillet weld position?", "options": {"A": "The 2F horizontal position, because the weld axis runs horizontally against a vertical surface.", "B": "The 1F flat position, because the base plate lies in a flat plane under the weld.", "C": "The 3F vertical position, because the upright plate stands vertical above the base plate.", "D": "The 4F overhead position, because the weld is made against the underside of the upright."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 2F horizontal position, because the weld axis runs horizontally against a vertical surface.): In 2F the fillet is made on a roughly horizontal axis against a vertical surface and a horizontal one. The plate orientation alone does not set the position; the weld axis and the face being welded do.", "image": "question_images/q_160.png"}, {"id": 161, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing shows a T-joint in which only the upright plate is beveled at 45°, and the base plate is left square. The detailer must show on the weld symbol which member receives the preparation. Which AWS A2.4 feature shows that only the upright plate is prepared?", "options": {"A": "The arrow is bent or broken, with its head pointing toward the member to be beveled.", "B": "A flag placed at the junction of the reference line and the arrow shaft.", "C": "A circle drawn around the weld symbol where it sits on the reference line.", "D": "A second reference line added below the first, carrying a duplicate symbol."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The arrow is bent or broken, with its head pointing toward the member to be beveled.): On bevel and J-groove symbols, a broken arrow points at the one member to be prepared. A flag is for field welds and a circle for welds all around, so neither identifies the beveled member.", "image": "question_images/q_161.png"}, {"id": 162, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A detailer is writing the weld symbol for the T-joint drawn, where the upright plate carries a 45° bevel and the weld is on the arrow side. The detailer needs to place the groove angle on the symbol. Where does AWS A2.4 locate the groove angle?", "options": {"A": "Inside the weld symbol, on the same line as the root opening dimension.", "B": "Outside the weld symbol, on the same side of the reference line as the symbol.", "C": "In the tail of the weld symbol, beside the welding process designation and notes.", "D": "On the arrow shaft, written beside its pointed end where it meets the joint."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Outside the weld symbol, on the same side of the reference line as the symbol.): Groove angle is written outside the weld symbol and root opening goes inside it, both on the same side as the symbol. The tail carries process and notes, not geometry.", "image": "question_images/q_162.png"}, {"id": 163, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "During fit-up of a 1/2 inch plate butt joint, the inspector finds that one plate sits about 5 mm higher than the other at the joint, as the diagram shows. Tack welds are not yet placed. What should the fitter do before tacking the joint?", "options": {"A": "Realign the plates with clamps so the surfaces are flush within code limits.", "B": "Tack the joint as it sits and rely on the cover pass to blend the surfaces.", "C": "Grind the higher plate along the whole joint until its thickness matches the lower plate.", "D": "Increase the root opening to compensate, since a wider gap hides plate surface misalignment."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Realign the plates with clamps so the surfaces are flush within code limits.): Misalignment concentrates stress at the root and causes poor fusion, so it must be corrected by realigning before tacking. Grinding a plate thinner or widening the gap does not fix the offset.", "image": "question_images/q_163.png"}, {"id": 164, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A maintenance crew welds two overlapping steel plates, as the cross section illustrates, using SMAW. Neither plate is prepared with a groove. Which type of weld is normally made on the edge of the upper plate where it lies on the lower plate?", "options": {"A": "A square-groove weld placed in the narrow gap between the faces of the two plates.", "B": "A fillet weld along the edge where the top plate meets the surface below.", "C": "A flare-bevel groove weld made along the centerline of the lower plate.", "D": "A surfacing weld laid across the full width of the top plate."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A fillet weld along the edge where the top plate meets the surface below.): Lap joints are welded with fillet welds at the plate edge, and sometimes plug or slot welds through the top plate. No groove exists to fill with a groove weld.", "image": "question_images/q_164.png"}, {"id": 165, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing for a 3/8 inch plate V-groove butt joint specifies a feather edge with a 3 mm opening, but the shop procedure calls for a short land at the bottom of each bevel. A welder must explain what the land does. What is the land called and what is its purpose?", "options": {"A": "A root opening, which determines how far apart the two plates sit before tacking.", "B": "A bevel face, which sets the included angle of the groove and the filler volume needed.", "C": "A root face, which supports the arc on the root pass and resists burn-through.", "D": "A weld toe, which marks where the finished face blends into the plate surface."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A root face, which supports the arc on the root pass and resists burn-through.): The root face is the unbeveled portion of the groove face at the root and gives the arc something to melt into without falling through. The root opening is the gap between members.", "image": "question_images/q_165.png"}, {"id": 166, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder reads a detail that shows 25° of bevel on each plate of a V-groove butt joint instead of the usual 30°. Using the same SMAW electrode, which defect is most likely to increase because of the smaller groove angle?", "options": {"A": "Burn-through at the root, since the narrower groove leaves the plates much thinner there.", "B": "Excessive crown height on the cap, since a small angle produces a high weld reinforcement.", "C": "Lack of fusion along the groove sidewalls, since the electrode angle is restricted.", "D": "Cold cracking in the base metal, since a smaller groove angle raises the hydrogen level."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lack of fusion along the groove sidewalls, since the electrode angle is restricted.): With a 50° included angle the electrode cannot lean far enough to wet each face, so sidewall fusion suffers. The groove angle controls access and volume, not hydrogen content.", "image": "question_images/q_166.png"}, {"id": 167, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "An engineer specifies a groove weld on 1/2 inch plate that must be welded from one side and fused through the full thickness, with weld metal extending completely from the face to the root of the joint. Which term describes this weld?", "options": {"A": "A partial joint penetration groove weld, with fusion limited to part of the thickness.", "B": "A fillet weld whose effective throat equals the thickness of the thinner member.", "C": "A seal weld whose only purpose is to close the joint against leakage.", "D": "A complete joint penetration groove weld, with fusion through the plate thickness."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A complete joint penetration groove weld, with fusion through the plate thickness.): Complete joint penetration means weld metal fills the groove through the entire thickness. A partial penetration weld stops short of the root, leaving an unfused portion.", "image": "question_images/q_167.png"}, {"id": 168, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is told to weld the symmetric double-V butt joint in the drawing, prepared with 30° on each plate and a 2 mm root opening, by alternating passes from one side to the other. A trainee questions this order. What is the reason for alternating sides?", "options": {"A": "Alternating passes let each side cool fully, which prevents slag from forming in the groove.", "B": "Alternating passes raise the interpass temperature, which removes the need for back gouging at the root.", "C": "Alternating passes balance shrinkage on both faces, which keeps the plates from pulling out of flat.", "D": "Alternating passes increase the groove angle, which reduces the amount of filler needed overall."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alternating passes balance shrinkage on both faces, which keeps the plates from pulling out of flat.): Welding one side only pulls the plate toward that side, causing angular distortion. Alternating passes offsets the contraction forces. It does not change groove angle or eliminate back gouging.", "image": "question_images/q_168.png"}, {"id": 169, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabrication drawing calls for a fillet weld with 6 mm legs on an equal-leg T-joint, drawn as in the diagram. The inspector needs the theoretical throat to check effective weld size. What is the theoretical throat of that fillet weld?", "options": {"A": "About 6.0 mm, which is the same as the leg size for an equal-leg weld.", "B": "About 8.5 mm, which is the leg size multiplied by 1.414 for the weld.", "C": "About 3.0 mm, which is half of the leg size on the weld.", "D": "About 4.2 mm, which is the leg size multiplied by 0.707."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 4.2 mm, which is the leg size multiplied by 0.707.): For an equal-leg flat-faced fillet, throat equals leg × 0.707, so 6 × 0.707 ≈ 4.2 mm. Multiplying by 1.414 gives the hypotenuse, not the throat.", "image": "question_images/q_169.png"}, {"id": 170, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A detailer sketches the weld symbol for a joint like the one drawn, two 3 mm sheet plates butted edge to edge and welded without any bevel preparation. Which weld symbol shape does AWS A2.4 use to show this square-groove weld on the reference line?", "options": {"A": "A single vertical line with a slanted line attached to its upper end.", "B": "Two short parallel vertical lines drawn side by side on the reference line.", "C": "A right triangle sitting on the reference line with its vertical leg on the left.", "D": "Two slanted lines joined at the bottom to form a V on the reference line."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two short parallel vertical lines drawn side by side on the reference line.): The square-groove symbol is two parallel vertical lines. The slanted-and-vertical form is the bevel groove, the triangle is the fillet, and the V shape is the V-groove.", "image": "question_images/q_170.png"}, {"id": 171, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder reading a fabrication drawing sees a row of short, evenly spaced dashes running inside the outline of a plate. The detail marks a feature lying behind a visible surface, where the viewer cannot see it from that direction. What type of line is this, and what does it show?", "options": {"A": "A center line, which marks the axis of a hole or other symmetrical feature.", "B": "A cutting plane line, which marks where an imaginary cut passes through the object.", "C": "A hidden line, which shows an edge or feature that cannot be seen from that view.", "D": "An extension line, which carries the projection from a feature out to its dimension."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A hidden line, which shows an edge or feature that cannot be seen from that view.): Hidden lines are short evenly spaced dashes showing features concealed behind a surface. Center lines are long and short alternating dashes, so they are drawn differently."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabricator marks the axis of a bolt hole on a plate drawing so a layout person can locate it accurately. The line is thin and made of alternating long and short dashes that extend slightly past the edge of the circle. What is this type of line called?", "options": {"A": "A phantom line, which shows the alternate position of a part that moves in service.", "B": "A break line, which shows that part of a long object has been omitted.", "C": "A visible line, which outlines the edges that can be seen directly in the view.", "D": "A center line, which marks the axis of a circle, hole or symmetrical part."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A center line, which marks the axis of a circle, hole or symmetrical part.): Center lines use long and short alternating dashes and mark axes and circle centers. Phantom lines show alternate positions with a long and two short dashes, so the pattern differs."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "When laying out the views of a welded bracket, the draftsperson must choose which view becomes the front view. The bracket has a complex outline on one side and a plain flat surface on the opposite side. Which view is normally selected as the front view?", "options": {"A": "The view that shows the smallest area, so it uses the least room on the sheet.", "B": "The view that shows the most characteristic shape and the most detail of the part.", "C": "The view that shows the weld locations, even when the outline looks quite simple.", "D": "The view that faces the viewer when the part sits in its shipping position."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The view that shows the most characteristic shape and the most detail of the part.): The front view is chosen to describe the shape best and show the most detail, with other views arranged around it. Weld locations are shown by symbols, not by view choice."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing for a welded base shows a section view labeled A-A, with arrows at each end of a thick line drawn across the top view. A new welder is unsure what the arrows tell the reader. What do the arrows on the cutting plane line indicate?", "options": {"A": "The side of the part that must be welded first according to the fabrication sequence.", "B": "The direction in which the part must be rotated before the weld is started.", "C": "The side of the cutting plane line that is removed and discarded from the part.", "D": "The direction in which the viewer looks at the cut surface to see the section."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The direction in which the viewer looks at the cut surface to see the section.): The arrows point in the direction of sight, showing which half of the part remains in the section view. They do not give a welding sequence or part orientation."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A US shop receives a drawing laid out in third-angle projection, the system used on American drawings. A new fitter is learning how the views are arranged on the sheet. Where does the right side view normally appear relative to the front view?", "options": {"A": "Below the front view, lined up with the vertical centerline of the part.", "B": "To the left of the front view, on the same horizontal level as it.", "C": "To the right of the front view, on the same horizontal level.", "D": "Above the front view, lined up with the vertical centerline of the part."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To the right of the front view, on the same horizontal level.): In third-angle projection the top view sits above the front view and the right side view sits to the right. First-angle projection reverses these positions."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A supervisor must decide whether to send a customer an orthographic drawing or an isometric sketch of a welded bracket for approval of its size. The customer needs to check the true shape and dimensions of each face before approving fabrication. Which drawing type fits, and why?", "options": {"A": "Isometric, because the edges appear at true length along all three of its axes.", "B": "Isometric, because the single view shows hidden features without dashed lines being needed.", "C": "Orthographic, because it shows the whole part in one view that looks three-dimensional.", "D": "Orthographic, because each view shows a face in true shape and can be dimensioned."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Orthographic, because each view shows a face in true shape and can be dimensioned.): Orthographic views project each face without distortion, so shapes and dimensions are accurate. Isometric sketches look three-dimensional but distort angles, so they are poor for checking dimensions."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing shows the length of a steel plate as 2.500 ± 0.010 inch. An inspector reading the print wants to know the acceptable size range for the finished plate before checking it with a caliper. Which range of lengths is acceptable?", "options": {"A": "From 2.490 inch to 2.510 inch, including both limits.", "B": "From 2.500 inch to 2.510 inch, since the plus sign gives the only allowance.", "C": "From 2.480 inch to 2.520 inch, since the tolerance is applied twice at both ends.", "D": "From 2.490 inch to 2.500 inch, since a finished part may only be undersize."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (From 2.490 inch to 2.510 inch, including both limits.): A bilateral tolerance allows the size to vary equally above and below nominal, so 2.500 − 0.010 = 2.490 and 2.500 + 0.010 = 2.510. A unilateral tolerance would put the whole allowance on one side, which is not what plus or minus 0.010 describes."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A shaft seat on a weldment is dimensioned 1.250 inch with a tolerance of +0.005 and −0.000. A machinist measures the finished seat at 1.2548 inch with a calibrated micrometer. Should the inspector accept the part, and what is the reason?", "options": {"A": "Reject it, because the reading is above the nominal size of 1.250 inch.", "B": "Accept it, because 1.2548 falls between the limits of 1.250 and 1.255.", "C": "Reject it, because a tolerance with a zero on one side permits no variation.", "D": "Accept it, because the size is larger than nominal and larger sizes are allowed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept it, because 1.2548 falls between the limits of 1.250 and 1.255.): This unilateral tolerance sets limits of 1.250 and 1.255 inch, so 1.2548 is inside. Readings above nominal are acceptable only up to the stated upper limit."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "Three plates, each drawn 2.000 ± 0.005 inch thick, are stacked in a fixture and welded into one block. The customer asks for the largest overall thickness that the drawing tolerances permit, before welding distortion is considered. What is that largest thickness?", "options": {"A": "6.005 inch, because a single tolerance is applied to the total stack height.", "B": "6.000 inch, because the plus and minus allowances cancel across the three plates.", "C": "6.030 inch, because each tolerance is applied twice to each plate in the stack.", "D": "6.015 inch, because the three upper limits of 2.005 inch are added together."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (6.015 inch, because the three upper limits of 2.005 inch are added together.): Tolerance stack-up adds the individual limits: 3 × 2.005 = 6.015 inch maximum, and 3 × 1.995 = 5.985 inch minimum. Using only one tolerance, or applying each tolerance twice, gives a total that three stacked plates cannot actually reach."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "On a fabrication drawing, a dimension appears in parentheses, for example (12.50), next to a plate length. A new fitter asks the lead hand what the parentheses mean before cutting the plate for the weldment. What does a dimension in parentheses indicate?", "options": {"A": "A reference dimension given for information only, not used for fabrication or inspection.", "B": "A basic dimension that must be held to the tightest tolerance on the whole sheet.", "C": "A dimension that applies only after the welding is finished and the part has cooled.", "D": "A maximum dimension that the finished part must stay under when it is inspected."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A reference dimension given for information only, not used for fabrication or inspection.): Parentheses mark a reference dimension that repeats information derived from other dimensions. It carries no tolerance of its own and is not inspected."}, {"id": 181, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A weldment drawing gives each of three hole spacings in a chain and also an overall length that equals their sum, all with tolerances. The checker says the part is over-dimensioned, because the chain and the total cannot all be held. What is the usual correction for this problem?", "options": {"A": "Omit one dimension in the chain or mark the overall length as a reference dimension.", "B": "Add another chain dimension so the total can be checked a second time on the part.", "C": "Remove all of the tolerances so the fitter may choose the final spacing freely.", "D": "Move the overall length into the bill of materials so the drawing stays clear."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Omit one dimension in the chain or mark the overall length as a reference dimension.): Giving a full chain plus the overall length creates conflicting tolerances. Dropping one chain dimension or marking the total as a reference removes the conflict."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing's title block states: unless noted, two-place decimals ± 0.03 and three-place decimals ± 0.010. The shop works from this print only. A welded frame is dimensioned 4.250 inch, and the inspector measures 4.265 inch after welding. What should the inspector conclude about the frame?", "options": {"A": "Accept it, because a three-place dimension allows up to 0.030 inch of variation.", "B": "Reject it, because a three-place dimension allows 4.240 to 4.260 inch and the part is over.", "C": "Accept it, because welding distortion is exempt from the title block tolerances.", "D": "Reject it, because all dimensions on the drawing are held to 0.005 inch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reject it, because a three-place dimension allows 4.240 to 4.260 inch and the part is over.): 4.250 has three decimal places, so the ± 0.010 note applies and the limits are 4.240 to 4.260. The 0.03 allowance covers two-place dimensions only."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fitter looks at the bill of materials on an assembly drawing for a welded stand before cutting any steel. The list sits in a table near the title block. What information does a bill of materials normally list for each item?", "options": {"A": "The weld size, welding process and filler metal classification for each of the joints.", "B": "The inspection method, acceptance level and examiner qualification for each of the welds.", "C": "The preheat temperature, interpass limit and heat input range for each of the welds.", "D": "The item number, quantity, description and material of each part in the assembly."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The item number, quantity, description and material of each part in the assembly.): A bill of materials is a parts list with item numbers, quantities, descriptions and materials. Weld procedure and inspection data live elsewhere, such as the WPS and inspection plan."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder sees a number inside a circle with a leader line pointing at a channel on an assembly drawing of a welded stand. The same number appears in a row of the bill of materials near the title block. What is the purpose of the circled number?", "options": {"A": "It gives the order in which that part must be welded into the assembly.", "B": "It ties that part on the drawing to its row in the bill of materials.", "C": "It gives the weld size required at the joint where the leader ends.", "D": "It gives the number of times the part may be reworked before scrapping."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It ties that part on the drawing to its row in the bill of materials.): Balloon numbers cross-reference parts on the assembly view to the item numbers in the bill of materials, so the reader can find the size, quantity and material. The balloon is only a cross-reference, so it says nothing about welding sequence, weld size or how many times a part may be reworked."}, {"id": 185, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A bill of materials for a frame lists item 4 as six pieces of 2 × 2 × 1/4 inch angle, each cut 18 inch long. The shop stocks 10 foot lengths of that angle, and each saw cut wastes 1/8 inch. What is the minimum number of 10 foot lengths needed for item 4?", "options": {"A": "Two lengths, because a single 10 foot stick holds only five pieces of that size.", "B": "One length, because six pieces of 18 inches total exactly 120 inches with no waste.", "C": "One length, because six 18 inch pieces and the saw cuts use about 108.6 inches.", "D": "Two lengths, because each cut piece must come from its own separate stock stick."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (One length, because six 18 inch pieces and the saw cuts use about 108.6 inches.): Six pieces total 108 inches, and five saw cuts add 0.625 inch, so about 108.6 inches fit inside 120 inches. One stick is enough with roughly 11 inches left over."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabrication class reviews the five basic joint types defined by AWS before starting a unit on weld symbols. The instructor asks the class to name the full set and write it in their notes. Which list names all five of the basic joint types?", "options": {"A": "Butt, groove, fillet, plug and slot joints as the AWS lists them.", "B": "Square, bevel, J, U and flare joints as the AWS lists them.", "C": "Flat, horizontal, vertical, overhead and pipe joints, named for welding position.", "D": "Butt, corner, T, lap and edge joints as the AWS defines them."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Butt, corner, T, lap and edge joints as the AWS defines them.): The five basic joints are butt, corner, T, lap and edge. Groove, fillet, plug and slot are weld types, and flat through overhead are welding positions."}, {"id": 187, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "Two thin sheet metal flanges stand parallel, with their edges even, and a bead is run along the aligned edges to join them. A trainee is asked to classify the joint by its arrangement. Which basic joint type describes this arrangement?", "options": {"A": "A butt joint, because the two members lie in the same plane end to end.", "B": "An edge joint, because the edges of parallel members are joined together.", "C": "A lap joint, because the two members overlap with their surfaces touching.", "D": "A corner joint, because the two members meet at a right angle."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An edge joint, because the edges of parallel members are joined together.): An edge joint joins the edges of two or more members that are parallel or nearly parallel. A butt joint lies in one plane, and a lap joint overlaps the faces."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fitter reading a groove weld detail asks what the root face is. The drawing shows a V-groove with a short flat portion left at the bottom of each bevel, next to the gap between the plates. How is the root face defined?", "options": {"A": "The gap between the two members measured at the bottom of the groove.", "B": "The finished surface of the weld metal visible on the side of the joint.", "C": "The part of the groove face adjacent to the root that is not beveled.", "D": "The angle between the two prepared edges of the finished groove joint."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The part of the groove face adjacent to the root that is not beveled.): The root face is the unbeveled land next to the root. The gap between members is the root opening, and the angle between the faces is the included angle."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fitter beveling both pipe ends for a groove weld cuts each end at 22.5° from the square edge. A helper asks what total groove angle the weld will have, since the procedure lists an included angle. What is the included angle once the two ends are brought together?", "options": {"A": "45°, found by adding the two 22.5° bevels.", "B": "22.5°, since it equals the angle of one bevel.", "C": "67.5°, since each bevel adds 45° to the other.", "D": "90°, since the bevels are measured from the pipe surface."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (45°, found by adding the two 22.5° bevels.): The included angle is the sum of both bevel angles: 22.5° + 22.5° = 45°. The bevel angle alone describes only one prepared edge."}, {"id": 190, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A groove weld is cut 3/8 inch deep into 3/4 inch plate from one side only, with no back weld and an unfused portion left at the root. Which term describes this weld, and what does it mean for the joint?", "options": {"A": "Complete joint penetration, because the weld metal reaches the plate surface on one side.", "B": "Reinforced penetration, because the weld face rises above the plate by 3/8 inch.", "C": "Partial joint penetration, because the weld does not extend through the full plate thickness.", "D": "Effective throat penetration, because the groove depth equals half of the plate thickness."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Partial joint penetration, because the weld does not extend through the full plate thickness.): A groove weld that stops short of full thickness is a partial joint penetration weld, and its strength is based on the effective throat, not the plate thickness. A complete joint penetration weld would fill the full thickness, so that term cannot describe a groove that leaves an unfused root."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A drawing shows a joint where only one member has a bevel and the other member has a square edge. A welder calls it a bevel-groove joint rather than a V-groove joint. What distinguishes a bevel groove from a V-groove?", "options": {"A": "A bevel groove has a root face, while a V-groove has none at the root.", "B": "A bevel groove is welded from both sides, while a V-groove is welded from one.", "C": "A bevel groove has one member beveled and one square, while a V-groove has both beveled.", "D": "A bevel groove has an included angle of 90°, while a V-groove has 60°."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A bevel groove has one member beveled and one square, while a V-groove has both beveled.): The bevel groove prepares one member only, as in many T-joints, and the V-groove prepares both. Root face and angle sizes can be used with either one."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder keeps burning through the root pass on a V-groove butt joint with a feather edge and a 4 mm root opening. The WPS permits changes to fit-up. Which adjustment is most likely to stop the burn-through on the root pass?", "options": {"A": "Increase the included angle so the arc has more room at the root.", "B": "Raise the welding current to bring the root metal to a faster melt.", "C": "Reduce the root opening or add a root face so the arc has more metal.", "D": "Remove the root face so that the arc reaches the bottom more easily."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce the root opening or add a root face so the arc has more metal.): Burn-through comes from too little metal at the root, so closing the gap or adding a land gives the arc support. More current or a larger angle would not add root metal."}, {"id": 193, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welding student studies the definition of joint penetration for a groove weld during a classroom review of groove terms. The instructor asks which statement correctly defines joint penetration, a term that appears on welding drawings and inspection reports for groove welds. What is the correct definition?", "options": {"A": "The minimum depth a groove weld extends from its face into the joint, not counting reinforcement.", "B": "The height of the weld metal rising above the surface of the base metal.", "C": "The width of the weld face measured at the toes on the plate surface.", "D": "The distance between the two members at the bottom of the prepared groove."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The minimum depth a groove weld extends from its face into the joint, not counting reinforcement.): Joint penetration is measured from the weld face into the joint and excludes reinforcement. Weld height is reinforcement and the gap between members is root opening."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A technician needs to produce a freehand sketch of a repair bracket in the field, and an experienced mentor suggests a method for getting good proportions. What approach should the technician follow when starting the sketch of the bracket, before any final lines are drawn on the paper?", "options": {"A": "Draw the finished heavy outline of one corner first and expand outward from that corner.", "B": "Lightly block in the overall shape with construction lines, then darken final lines and add details.", "C": "Start by writing all of the dimensions, then fit the drawing around those numbers.", "D": "Trace the part with a marker onto the paper and skip the rough layout."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lightly block in the overall shape with construction lines, then darken final lines and add details.): Light construction lines establish proportion and let errors be corrected before final lines are darkened. Starting with dimensions or one heavy corner makes proportion errors hard to fix."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A trainee draws an isometric sketch of a welded block on grid paper, and the instructor checks the setup. The two receding axes must be drawn at a standard angle from the horizontal. At what angle are the receding axes drawn?", "options": {"A": "30° from the horizontal, giving three axes about 120° apart.", "B": "45° from the horizontal, giving two axes that meet at right angles.", "C": "60° from the horizontal, giving three axes about 60° apart.", "D": "15° from the horizontal, giving two axes that nearly line up."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (30° from the horizontal, giving three axes about 120° apart.): Isometric sketches use a vertical axis plus two axes at 30° from horizontal, so the three axes sit 120° apart. A 45° layout belongs to oblique drawing."}, {"id": 196, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A field technician sketches a repair bracket for a shop to fabricate overnight. The sketch has accurate proportions but no notes, and nobody will be reachable for questions until morning. What must be added so the shop can build it correctly?", "options": {"A": "A shaded version of the surfaces so the finished part looks realistic on paper.", "B": "Several extra views from the same direction to fill the sheet neatly.", "C": "A colored border and a title in large letters at the top of the paper.", "D": "Dimensions, material, weld symbols and notes covering any special requirements."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dimensions, material, weld symbols and notes covering any special requirements.): A working sketch must carry the information needed to build and inspect the part: sizes, material, weld details and notes. Shading and decoration add nothing to fabrication."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A site plan is drawn at a scale of 1/4 inch = 1 foot, and a welder is checking a steel beam length before ordering material. The beam measures 3 1/2 inches on the drawing with a rule. What is the actual length of the beam?", "options": {"A": "14 feet, found by dividing 3.5 inches by 1/4 inch per foot.", "B": "7 feet, found by multiplying 3.5 inches by 2 feet per inch.", "C": "28 feet, found by dividing 3.5 inches by 1/8 inch per foot.", "D": "3.5 feet, found by reading each inch on the drawing as a foot."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (14 feet, found by dividing 3.5 inches by 1/4 inch per foot.): At 1/4 inch per foot, 3.5 ÷ 0.25 = 14 feet. Mistaking the scale ratio gives 7 or 28 feet, so check the scale first."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A detail drawing is labeled scale 1:2, meaning the drawing is half the actual size, and a fitter is checking a gusset before cutting plate. The fitter measures the gusset on the print at 3 inches. What is the real gusset length?", "options": {"A": "6 inches, because the part is twice as large as it appears on the drawing.", "B": "1.5 inches, because the actual part is half the size shown on the drawing.", "C": "3 inches, because the printed measurement equals the real size of the part.", "D": "9 inches, because the scale adds half again to the length measured on paper."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (6 inches, because the part is twice as large as it appears on the drawing.): A 1:2 scale means one unit on paper equals two units on the part, so 3 × 2 = 6 inches. Reading the scale backward gives 1.5 inches."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A welder is handed a photocopy of a drawing that was reduced to 80 percent of its original size, and the print shows no dimension for a bracket's hole spacing. The welder wants to scale it with a ruler. What is the correct practice?", "options": {"A": "Do not scale the print; get the dimension from the engineer or the printed dimensions.", "B": "Scale the print with a ruler and correct the reading by adding 20 percent.", "C": "Scale the print with a ruler, since the scale note on the drawing still holds.", "D": "Set the spacing by eye and confirm it only when the weld is finished."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not scale the print; get the dimension from the engineer or the printed dimensions.): Copies can shrink or stretch, so the stated scale may no longer hold, and measuring a print is not a reliable source of size. Dimensions must come from the drawing itself or the designer."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "drawings_and_welding_symbols", "question": "A fabrication drawing includes the letters NTS in the title block near the scale entry, and a new fitter asks the lead hand what they mean before reading any dimensions. The title block sits at the lower right of the print. What does NTS stand for on the drawing?", "options": {"A": "Nominal tolerance size, meaning the dimensions are only approximate values for the part.", "B": "Normal thickness steel, meaning the plate is of standard mill thickness for the job.", "C": "Not to scale, meaning the drawing does not represent true proportions.", "D": "No temper specified, meaning the heat treatment is left to the fabricator to decide."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not to scale, meaning the drawing does not represent true proportions.): NTS means not to scale, so the drawing is not drawn proportionally and dimensions must be read, not measured off the print. The other expansions are not drawing abbreviations, and the point of NTS is to warn the reader not to scale dimensions from the print."}, {"id": 201, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student in a welding lab is reading the classification stamped on the flux coating of an electrode used to join mild steel plate. The electrode reads E7018. In the AWS A5.1 system, what does the first two digits, 70, tell the welder about this electrode?", "options": {"A": "Maximum recommended welding current for the electrode is 70 amperes", "B": "Electrode core wire diameter is seventy thousandths of an inch", "C": "Percentage of iron powder in the flux coating is seventy", "D": "Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi): The first digits of an A5.1 classification give minimum tensile strength in thousands of psi, so 70 means 70,000 psi. Current ranges come from the manufacturer's data sheet, not from the number."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabrication shop is setting up a shielded metal arc welding station to run E6010 electrodes on pipe root passes. The lead hand asks the new employee which polarity the machine must be set to. Which polarity is correct for E6010 electrodes?", "options": {"A": "Direct current electrode positive, also called reverse polarity", "B": "Direct current electrode negative, also called straight polarity", "C": "Alternating current only, because the sodium coating needs a changing arc", "D": "Either polarity with equal results since the coating is cellulose based"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Direct current electrode positive, also called reverse polarity): E6010 has a high-cellulose sodium coating designed for DCEP, which gives its deep, digging arc. It does not operate well on AC, and DCEN gives a weak, unstable arc with this coating."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An instructor explains the electrical output a power source needs for manual stick welding, where the welder's hand cannot hold the arc length perfectly steady. The instructor wants students to name the output characteristic that keeps amperage nearly constant while arc length varies slightly. What is it called?", "options": {"A": "Constant current, also called a drooping volt-ampere curve", "B": "Constant voltage, also described as a flat volt-ampere curve", "C": "Constant wire feed, also described as a rising volt-ampere curve", "D": "Constant frequency, also described as a pulsed volt-ampere curve"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Constant current, also called a drooping volt-ampere curve): SMAW machines use a drooping curve so current changes only a little when the arc lengthens or shortens. Constant voltage machines are built for wire-fed processes such as GMAW and FCAW."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is assigned a long horizontal fillet weld on structural plate in the flat position and wants the highest deposition rate from a stick electrode. The welding supervisor suggests an iron powder electrode with a heavy coating. In which positions can an E7024 electrode be used?", "options": {"A": "Flat position and horizontal position fillet welds only", "B": "Flat and vertical-up positions, though not overhead work", "C": "All positions the same as an E6010 electrode", "D": "Flat and overhead positions but not horizontal fillets"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flat position and horizontal position fillet welds only): The third digit 2 in E7024 means flat and horizontal fillet positions only. The heavy iron powder coating makes a large, fluid puddle that cannot be controlled vertically or overhead."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder opens a sealed can of E7018 electrodes at the start of a shift on a structural job and wants to keep the rods dry for the whole day. Where should the unused electrodes be placed once the can has been opened?", "options": {"A": "Inside the toolbox next to the welding leads and chipping hammer", "B": "In a rod oven held at the recommended holding temperature", "C": "On the workbench under a cloth to keep dust off", "D": "In a sealed plastic bag stored at ambient shop temperature"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In a rod oven held at the recommended holding temperature): Low-hydrogen coatings absorb moisture from the air, and moisture adds hydrogen to the weld. A heated rod oven keeps the coating dry. A bag or cloth at room temperature does not remove absorbed moisture."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "During a safety and technique lesson on stick welding, an instructor asks students to describe a good working arc length for a general purpose electrode on mild steel plate. Which description is closest to the accepted rule of thumb for a normal arc length?", "options": {"A": "About equal to the diameter of the electrode's bare core wire", "B": "About three times the diameter of the electrode's bare core wire", "C": "About half the thickness of the plate being welded", "D": "About the same as the full length of the flux coating"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About equal to the diameter of the electrode's bare core wire): A normal arc is roughly one core wire diameter, for example about 1/8 in with a 1/8 in rod. A much longer arc loses shielding and increases spatter and porosity."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A beginning welder is practicing on 1/4 in mild steel using an E6013 electrode and is impressed by how smooth the bead looks and how easily the slag lifts off. A classmate asks what kind of penetration this electrode usually gives. How is E6013 penetration normally described?", "options": {"A": "Deep penetration with a digging arc used for root passes", "B": "Very deep penetration suited only to thick sections above one inch", "C": "No penetration since the coating is designed only for surfacing", "D": "Light to medium penetration with a smooth, easy to control bead"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Light to medium penetration with a smooth, easy to control bead): E6013 has a rutile coating that gives a soft arc, a stable puddle and light to medium penetration, which makes it popular for sheet metal and fit-up that is not perfect. Deep digging belongs to E6010."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A lab handout explains polarity terms for stick welding. Students are asked to match the term direct current electrode positive, abbreviated DCEP, to the correct description of how the welding leads are connected at the machine terminals. Which statement describes DCEP?", "options": {"A": "Electrode holder connects to the negative terminal and work lead to the positive", "B": "Electrode holder on the positive terminal and work lead on the negative", "C": "Both leads connect to the same terminal on an alternating current machine", "D": "Electrode holder connects to ground while the work lead carries the arc current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Electrode holder on the positive terminal and work lead on the negative): In DCEP the electrode is the positive side of the circuit. This is also called reverse polarity and gives deeper penetration. DCEN, or straight polarity, is the opposite connection."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A materials instructor is comparing AWS filler metal specifications used in the school's welding shop. Students already know A5.1 covers carbon steel stick electrodes. They are asked which specification covers low-alloy steel covered electrodes such as E8018-B2 used on chromium-molybdenum steel. Which one is it?", "options": {"A": "AWS A5.18, the specification for carbon steel solid wires and rods", "B": "AWS A5.20, the specification for carbon steel flux cored electrodes", "C": "AWS A5.5, covering low-alloy steel covered arc welding electrodes", "D": "AWS A5.4, the specification for stainless steel covered electrodes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (AWS A5.5, covering low-alloy steel covered arc welding electrodes): A5.5 covers low-alloy covered electrodes, where letter and number suffixes such as B2 identify the alloy content. A5.18 and A5.20 cover wire processes, and A5.4 covers stainless."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "After finishing a root pass on a V-groove plate, a student is about to start the second pass. The instructor reminds the class that a particular cleaning step prevents trapped solid material in the joint. Which tools are normally used to remove slag between stick weld passes?", "options": {"A": "A chipping hammer followed by a wire brush", "B": "A pipe wrench followed by a flat file", "C": "A hand shear followed by a soft cloth", "D": "A center punch followed by a grinding wheel dresser"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A chipping hammer followed by a wire brush): A chipping hammer breaks the slag crust away and a wire brush cleans what remains. Skipping this cleaning between passes is a leading cause of slag inclusions in multipass welds."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding instructor demonstrates two ways to deposit weld metal along a butt joint root. In one, the electrode moves straight along the joint with little or no side to side motion. Students are asked to name this technique. What is it called?", "options": {"A": "A stringer bead, which stays narrow and follows a straight path", "B": "A weave bead, which is wide and sweeps across the joint", "C": "A tack weld, which only holds the parts in alignment", "D": "A stitch weld, which is run in short spaced segments"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A stringer bead, which stays narrow and follows a straight path): A stringer bead is made with little lateral motion, so the bead width is only a little wider than the electrode. A weave bead moves the electrode side to side to make a wider deposit."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop technician is reading the nameplate on a stick welding machine that lists 200 amperes at 60 percent. The apprentice asks what the percentage figure represents on a welding power source. What does the 60 percent duty cycle mean?", "options": {"A": "The machine delivers sixty percent of rated amperage once it warms up", "B": "The machine can operate for 60 minutes before a mandatory rest period", "C": "The machine converts sixty percent of input power into arc heat", "D": "The machine can weld at rated output for 6 minutes out of every 10 minute period"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The machine can weld at rated output for 6 minutes out of every 10 minute period): Duty cycle is the share of a ten minute period a machine can run at its rated current without overheating. At 60 percent that is six minutes of welding and four minutes of cooling."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using an alternating current transformer machine needs an electrode that runs smoothly on AC and also gives reasonable penetration on thicker mild steel. The shop stocks several cellulosic and rutile types. Which of these electrodes is designed to work on both AC and DCEP?", "options": {"A": "E6011, which has a potassium bearing cellulose coating", "B": "E6010, which has a sodium bearing cellulose coating", "C": "E7018, which has a calcium fluoride bearing low-hydrogen coating on DCEN only", "D": "E7024, which has a heavy iron powder coating usable on DCEN only"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (E6011, which has a potassium bearing cellulose coating): The potassium in E6011 stabilizes the arc on alternating current, so it keeps E6010's digging arc but runs on AC. E6010 needs DCEP only."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is joining 3/8 in mild steel plate in the flat position using 1/8 in E7018 electrodes. Her bead has good fusion but the weld sits high and ropey with narrow ripples. The instructor thinks the travel speed is too slow for the current setting. What change is most likely to correct the bead shape?", "options": {"A": "Increase the travel speed slightly so the bead flattens out", "B": "Reduce the current to the lowest end of the electrode range", "C": "Shorten the arc length to a quarter of the core wire diameter", "D": "Switch to a smaller electrode and keep the same travel speed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase the travel speed slightly so the bead flattens out): A slow pass piles metal into a tall, narrow bead. Moving a little faster spreads the deposit. Dropping current would worsen fusion, and a very short arc makes the electrode stick."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is running a 1/8 in E6013 electrode on 3/16 in plate in the flat position and records that the machine is set at 120 amperes. The manufacturer's range for this rod is 80 to 130 amperes. The arc is stable and the slag peels easily. How should the student judge this setting?", "options": {"A": "It is too high because 1/8 in rods must stay below 100 amperes", "B": "It is too low because thicker plate needs the top of the range plus 50", "C": "It is within the manufacturer's range and suits this plate thickness", "D": "It is invalid because the setting must match the plate thickness in thousandths"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is within the manufacturer's range and suits this plate thickness): The published range for the electrode is the controlling guide, and 120 A sits within 80 to 130 A. Good arc stability and easy slag release support the setting."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is about to run a vertical-up fillet weld on 1/4 in plate with E7018 electrodes and notices that the weld pool tends to sag. The welder is using the same amperage that worked in the flat position. Which adjustment best helps control the puddle in vertical-up?", "options": {"A": "Raise the current to the top of the range and move the electrode quickly", "B": "Switch to polarity DCEN and increase the arc length to cool the puddle", "C": "Lower the current slightly and use a short arc with a shelf motion", "D": "Hold the electrode perpendicular and avoid any weaving or pauses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower the current slightly and use a short arc with a shelf motion): A smaller, cooler puddle freezes sooner and resists gravity. A short arc and a small shelf motion build support under the weld. A high current and long arc make the puddle larger and fluid."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder has been stringing E7018 electrodes along a plate and sees the slag sticking in the corners of the previous pass, making restarts messy. The tip of the electrode also sticks when starting. Which technique improves starting low-hydrogen electrodes like this one?", "options": {"A": "Strike the arc directly in the crater and begin traveling immediately", "B": "Tap the electrode on the plate several times to chip away the coating", "C": "Strike the arc on the clean steel work lead before each restart", "D": "Strike ahead of the bead, move back over the crater, then travel"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Strike ahead of the bead, move back over the crater, then travel): Striking ahead and moving back to the crater lets the puddle fill and any porosity at the start burn off. Striking into the crater or on the lead risks stuck rods and arc strikes outside the joint."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fitter is preparing a single V-groove butt joint in 1/2 in mild steel for stick welding with an E6010 root pass. The root opening is 1/16 in with a 1/8 in root face. The welder reports incomplete root fusion on the first test coupon. Which fit-up change is most likely to help?", "options": {"A": "Close the root opening to zero and enlarge the root face", "B": "Add more bevel angle on the top side only and keep the root", "C": "Widen the root opening to about 1/8 in and trim the root face", "D": "Place a backing bar of carbon steel across the face of the joint"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Widen the root opening to about 1/8 in and trim the root face): A tight root with a large land makes the arc unable to reach the back of the joint. Opening the gap and trimming the root face allows E6010's digging arc to penetrate fully."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is using a 3/32 in E7018 electrode on 1/8 in mild steel sheet and burns through repeatedly at 110 amperes. The manufacturer's range for 3/32 in E7018 is roughly 70 to 110 amperes. What is the best first correction to reduce burn-through?", "options": {"A": "Raise the current to 130 amperes so the puddle freezes faster", "B": "Change to DCEN and increase arc length to spread heat", "C": "Drop the current toward 80 amperes and travel slightly faster", "D": "Slow the travel speed and hold the arc longer on each spot"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Drop the current toward 80 amperes and travel slightly faster): 110 A is at the top of the range for thin sheet. Lowering current and keeping the electrode moving limits the heat input. Slowing travel adds heat and makes burn-through worse."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An instructor sets up a lab exercise with a damp E7018 electrode that had been left out overnight. A student runs a short bead and notices heavy spatter, a harsh arc and a crumbling flux coating. What is the most likely cause of the poor arc behavior?", "options": {"A": "The electrode was classified for the wrong tensile strength", "B": "The machine duty cycle was exceeded during the exercise", "C": "Coating absorbed moisture while the rods sat outside a rod oven", "D": "The work lead was clamped to painted steel instead of bare metal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Coating absorbed moisture while the rods sat outside a rod oven): Low-hydrogen coatings pick up moisture quickly. Moisture causes spatter, a rough arc, a crumbling coating and later porosity or hydrogen cracking. The other options would not crumble the coating."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using an alternating current machine on a long steel bridge girder notices the arc pushing the molten pool off to one side as the electrode nears the end of the plate. The girder is heavily magnetized from nearby cranes. Which action is most likely to reduce this arc blow?", "options": {"A": "Move the work clamp to the far end of the plate and shorten the arc", "B": "Increase the arc length to let the magnetic field settle", "C": "Switch from alternating current to a larger diameter DC electrode", "D": "Raise the amperage so the arc overcomes the magnetic field"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Move the work clamp to the far end of the plate and shorten the arc): Arc blow comes from magnetic fields around the current path. Relocating the ground clamp changes the field, and a shorter arc gives the force less distance to act. A longer arc is pushed further."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is welding a horizontal fillet on 1/4 in plate with a 5/32 in E7024 electrode. The weld shows good appearance, but the student is dragging the electrode at a steep angle and the arc seems to wander. The instructor recommends contact technique. What does this technique involve with E7024?", "options": {"A": "Resting the heavy coating lightly on the joint and dragging it along", "B": "Holding the electrode two inches above the plate and sweeping side to side", "C": "Pointing the electrode straight into the joint and pushing it forward", "D": "Whipping the electrode back and forth to chip off coating before each pass"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Resting the heavy coating lightly on the joint and dragging it along): E7024's thick iron powder coating forms a cup that keeps the arc length consistent when the electrode is dragged lightly on the plate. This makes it easy to run high deposition fillets."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A structural job requires welding on 1 in thick A36 plate at 40°F. The inspector requires that the plate be warm to the touch before welding begins. A welder asks how this relates to stick welding E7018 on cold steel. What is the main benefit of preheating the plate?", "options": {"A": "Slows cooling so hydrogen escapes and the HAZ stays tougher", "B": "Increases the tensile strength of the E7018 electrode itself", "C": "Eliminates the need to remove slag between weld passes", "D": "Permits the welder to use a longer arc without porosity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slows cooling so hydrogen escapes and the HAZ stays tougher): Preheat reduces the temperature difference between the weld and the plate, so the weld cools more slowly. That lets hydrogen diffuse out and reduces hard microstructures that can crack."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is learning the proper travel angle for a stick weld on flat plate. Using E7018, the instructor tells her to drag the electrode with the top tilted in the direction of travel. A trainee asks for a typical travel angle for this technique. Which range is typical?", "options": {"A": "About 45 to 60 degrees from vertical, pushed toward the direction of travel", "B": "About 0 degrees, perfectly vertical with no tilt in either direction", "C": "About 5 to 15 degrees from vertical, dragging the electrode away from travel", "D": "About 30 to 40 degrees from vertical, tilted toward the work lead"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 5 to 15 degrees from vertical, dragging the electrode away from travel): A slight drag angle of 5 to 15 degrees lets the welder see the puddle and keeps slag behind the arc. Pushing at a steep angle can trap slag in front of the puddle."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a repair job, a welder must lay down a multipass fillet in the horizontal position on 1/2 in plate with E7018. The inspector asks whether stringers or a wide weave should be used on the first pass. Which choice is the preferred approach for the root pass?", "options": {"A": "A wide weave covering both plate surfaces in one pass", "B": "A back and forth whip motion that cools the puddle between strokes", "C": "A circular motion that holds the arc on the top plate longer", "D": "A stringer bead aimed into the root at the proper angle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A stringer bead aimed into the root at the proper angle): A stringer root pass places heat in the root and keeps the bead small enough to fuse both members. Wide weaves tend to cause lack of fusion and slag inclusion at the toes."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A teacher shows how a rod oven is used on a structural erection site. E7018 electrodes were exposed to the shop air for six hours, which is longer than the exposure limit. What action is correct before the welder uses them on code work?", "options": {"A": "Wipe the rods with a dry rag and use them within one hour", "B": "Recondition the rods in an oven at the stated drying temperature", "C": "Place the rods under the machine so exhaust heat dries the coating", "D": "Cut one inch from each rod to remove the moisture near the tip"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recondition the rods in an oven at the stated drying temperature): Rods that exceed the allowed exposure time must be dried in an oven at a controlled temperature, then held warm. Wiping or trimming does not remove moisture from the whole coating."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a pipe fitting job is using E6010 electrodes on a root pass and finds the arc is digging and the keyhole is large. Machine current is at the high end of the range. Which change is most likely to give better control of the keyhole?", "options": {"A": "Raise the amperage to the top of the electrode range", "B": "Lower the amperage a little and tighten the arc", "C": "Change polarity to DCEN to deepen the keyhole further", "D": "Lengthen the arc so more flux covers the pool"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower the amperage a little and tighten the arc): A big keyhole means too much heat or penetration. Lower current and a tighter arc reduce penetration and let the welder control the root. DCEN would weaken E6010's arc entirely."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabrication crew compares the electrode types for welding a single-pass lap joint on clean 14 gauge mild steel sheet. They want smooth appearance, easy slag removal and minimal burn-through on a standard AC machine. Which electrode is the most suitable choice for this job?", "options": {"A": "E6013 in a small diameter, run on low amperage with a short arc", "B": "E6010 in a large diameter, run on high amperage", "C": "E7024 in a large diameter, run in the flat position", "D": "E8018 in a large diameter, run on high amperage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (E6013 in a small diameter, run on low amperage with a short arc): E6013's soft arc and light penetration suit thin sheet and AC machines. E6010 digs too deeply, E7024 deposits too much metal, and large rods overheat thin steel."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student runs several beads with E7018 and observes that the electrode flux cracks away in chunks when bent slightly, even though the can was only just opened. The student wonders whether the electrode is damaged. What is the most reasonable explanation?", "options": {"A": "The tensile strength rating is too high for the plate thickness being welded", "B": "The machine is set to DCEN instead of DCEP for this type of electrode", "C": "The arc length is too short for the amperage setting that was chosen", "D": "Electrode was dropped or bent so the coating cracked away from the core wire"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Electrode was dropped or bent so the coating cracked away from the core wire): A cracked coating comes from physical damage such as bending or dropping, and damaged rods should be discarded because shielding becomes uneven. Polarity and arc length do not crack flux."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder wants to restart a weld on a long groove joint after stopping to change an electrode. The previous bead ended with a visible crater on the steel plate. Which restart procedure helps prevent a defect at the stop-start junction of the weld?", "options": {"A": "Strike the arc inside the crater and continue immediately at full speed", "B": "Start the new electrode on the work lead first, then move to the crater", "C": "Start behind the crater on a cleaned strip and travel away from it", "D": "Strike ahead of the crater, move back into it, then go forward"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Strike ahead of the crater, move back into it, then go forward): Striking ahead and reversing into the crater lets the new metal fuse with the old crater and fill it. Starting directly in the crater risks a lump or a missed section."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a fillet weld sees slag trapped along the toe of a bead on 1/2 in plate. The instructor shows that the welder is traveling at an inconsistent speed and leaving the slag ahead of the puddle at some points. What is the best correction?", "options": {"A": "Hold the electrode perpendicular and shorten the travel distance", "B": "Use a longer arc so the slag melts back into the puddle", "C": "Keep a steady drag angle and speed so slag trails behind", "D": "Lower the polarity setting and lengthen the electrode stick-out"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep a steady drag angle and speed so slag trails behind): Slag that runs ahead of the arc gets covered by weld metal and trapped. A steady speed and drag angle keep it trailing behind. A longer arc only worsens shielding."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is joining two pieces of 1/2 in plate with a single V-groove butt weld and plans a three-pass sequence: a root pass, a fill pass and a cover pass. The inspector asks what is the benefit of stringer passes over one very wide weave for the fill. What is the benefit?", "options": {"A": "Smaller beads limit heat input and reduce the chance of slag trapped between passes", "B": "Fewer passes are needed so the total labor time is cut nearly in half", "C": "Wide weaves are prohibited on all carbon steel by AWS D1.1", "D": "Stringers allow lower amperage because the arc is shorter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Smaller beads limit heat input and reduce the chance of slag trapped between passes): Narrow beads are easier to clean and fuse into the groove sidewalls with less heat input. Wide weaves can trap slag and create poor fusion at the toes, though D1.1 does not ban them outright."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is welding a pad of 1/8 in E7018 beads on plate to practice. After the first bead, the student sees the weld has a very slight concave profile and the weld ripples are evenly spaced. The student asks how a good bead should look overall. Which description fits?", "options": {"A": "Tall crown with sharp toes that stand out from the plate", "B": "Wide flat bead with coarse ripples and small slag pockets in the toes", "C": "Narrow crown with deep grooves along each edge to show penetration", "D": "Uniform width, even ripples and toes that blend into the plate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uniform width, even ripples and toes that blend into the plate): A sound bead has consistent width, regular ripples and smooth wetting at the toes. Sharp toes and grooves point to settings or technique problems."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using a 1/8 in E6010 electrode in the vertical-up position on a pipe stand is told to use a whipping motion by the instructor. An apprentice asks why this technique works so well with fast-freeze electrodes. Which statement best explains it?", "options": {"A": "The weld pool freezes quickly, so a short pause lets it set up", "B": "The coating produces a heavy slag that holds the puddle in place", "C": "The electrode burns hot enough to melt all of the slag away", "D": "The motion protects the root from air by moving the arc faster"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The weld pool freezes quickly, so a short pause lets it set up): E6010's fast-freeze behavior allows the welder to briefly lift off and let the puddle solidify, then return to the crater. Heavy slag is typical of E7024, not E6010."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a 3/16 in E7018 electrode on 1/2 in plate in the flat position at 140 amperes with a travel speed of about 6 inches per minute and the bead looks good. The welder's partner later runs the same settings at twice that speed. What is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "A narrow, thin bead with shallow penetration and poor fusion", "B": "A wider, taller bead with deeper penetration and more slag", "C": "A bead identical to the first since amperage controls bead size", "D": "A bead with a heavier crown but a smoother rippled surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A narrow, thin bead with shallow penetration and poor fusion): Faster travel deposits less metal per inch and puts less heat into the plate. The bead gets narrow with shallow fusion. Amperage alone does not set bead size."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "After a welder finishes a bead on a plate and views the cross section of a test coupon, the weld metal shows a round pit with a smooth wall near the surface, shown in the diagram. The instructor wants the cause the student would expect when the electrodes were stored in a toolbox for weeks. What cause fits this finding?", "options": {"A": "Travel speed was too slow so slag was rolled into the bead", "B": "Amperage was too low so the weld metal could not fuse the plate", "C": "The electrode was held at too steep an angle and pushed slag ahead", "D": "Moisture in the coating released gas trapped in the cooling weld"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moisture in the coating released gas trapped in the cooling weld): Gas pockets are porosity, and damp low-hydrogen coatings are a common source because absorbed water breaks down in the arc and releases hydrogen. The other causes lead to inclusions or fusion defects.", "image": "question_images/q_236.png"}, {"id": 237, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A coupon cut from a stick-welded butt joint is polished and etched. The diagram shows a groove melted into the base plate along one toe of the weld. The welder says the machine setting was well above the electrode's recommended range. Which correction best prevents this condition on the next coupon?", "options": {"A": "Increase the current further so the puddle fills the groove", "B": "Lengthen the arc so the heat is spread over a wider area", "C": "Slow the travel speed to double the amount of weld metal deposited", "D": "Lower the current into the published range and pause at the toes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower the current into the published range and pause at the toes): Melted-away base metal at the toe is caused by excess current, long arc or fast travel. Lowering current and pausing at the edges lets filler metal fill the melted area.", "image": "question_images/q_237.png"}, {"id": 238, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is reviewing a macro etch of a root pass on a thick butt joint prepared with a 1/8 in root face and a very tight root opening. The diagram shows the weld did not reach the back of the joint. Which fit-up and technique change is most likely to fix this finding?", "options": {"A": "Close the gap further so less filler metal is needed in the root", "B": "Use a longer arc to heat the sidewalls on the top surface", "C": "Widen the root gap, trim the root face and raise current", "D": "Switch to a larger electrode diameter and use a lower current"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Widen the root gap, trim the root face and raise current): The weld metal needs a path to the back of the joint, so a larger gap, a smaller land and enough current improve root fusion. A larger electrode at low current only deepens the problem.", "image": "question_images/q_238.png"}, {"id": 239, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A tester breaks open a fillet weld and finds a dark, irregular pocket of nonmetallic material inside the bead, as the diagram shows. The welder ran two passes with E7018 and chipped only the crown of the first pass before covering it. Which action would most likely have prevented this finding?", "options": {"A": "Use a longer arc on the second pass to reheat the slag below", "B": "Remove all slag from the first pass, toes and corners included", "C": "Increase the electrode stick-out so the flux burns away more fully", "D": "Run the second pass at lower current to avoid melting the first bead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove all slag from the first pass, toes and corners included): Slag left in the toes or corners is covered by the next bead and trapped. Complete cleaning between passes, with a chipping hammer and wire brush, prevents the inclusion.", "image": "question_images/q_239.png"}, {"id": 240, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is told to weld a 3/4 in A36 steel plate at 35°F with E7018 in a shop where rods have been out of the oven for about eight hours. During bend testing, hydrogen cracking appears near the toe. Which combination of factors most strongly explains this failure?", "options": {"A": "Excess slag removal between passes thinned the toe region", "B": "Constant current output made the arc too stable for fusion", "C": "Damp rods added hydrogen and the cold plate cooled the weld fast enough to trap it", "D": "Weaving too narrow a pattern left unfused metal along the sidewall"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Damp rods added hydrogen and the cold plate cooled the weld fast enough to trap it): Hydrogen cracking needs hydrogen, a susceptible microstructure and stress. Damp rods supply hydrogen and unpreheated cold plate cools fast. Cleaning slag and arc stability do not cause it."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is selecting an electrode for a fillet weld on rusty, lightly painted structural steel that cannot be cleaned before welding. The weld needs to burn through contaminants without much porosity. Which electrode choice best fits this condition and why?", "options": {"A": "E7018, because its low-hydrogen coating tolerates rust and paint best", "B": "E7024, because its thick flux covers contamination before it can burn", "C": "E6010, because its digging arc pushes through light surface scale", "D": "E6013, because its soft arc dissolves rust into the weld pool"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (E6010, because its digging arc pushes through light surface scale): E6010's forceful arc and gas shield penetrate surface scale and light paint. Low-hydrogen electrodes are the most sensitive to contamination and moisture, so E7018 is a poor choice here."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder has two matching joints of 1/4 in plate. She welds the first with E7018 on DCEP at 130 amperes and the second with the same rod on DCEN at 130 amperes. The second shows shallower fusion. Which statement best explains this difference?", "options": {"A": "DCEN adds more current to the arc than DCEP at the same dial setting", "B": "DCEP shortens the arc length and cools the puddle faster than DCEN", "C": "DCEN burns away the coating faster so more slag covers the plate", "D": "DCEP puts more heat into the base plate and gives deeper penetration than DCEN"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (DCEP puts more heat into the base plate and gives deeper penetration than DCEN): In DCEP, more heat is delivered to the work, which gives deeper penetration. DCEN puts more heat into the electrode, giving faster melt-off but shallower fusion."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a 1/8 in E7018 in a drag technique on a vertical groove weld at a travel angle near 30 degrees from vertical. She reports persistent slag trapped in the weld and wavy edges. The instructor asks which error is the most probable source. What is it?", "options": {"A": "Insufficient root opening that prevents heat from reaching the root", "B": "Excessive travel angle that lets slag run ahead into the puddle", "C": "Over-baked electrodes that leave too little flux for shielding", "D": "Machine open circuit voltage set too high for the electrode"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excessive travel angle that lets slag run ahead into the puddle): A steep angle moves the arc force and lets the molten slag run into the leading edge of the puddle, where it gets trapped. Staying near 5 to 15 degrees keeps slag behind."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld inspector finds lack of fusion on the sidewalls of a V-groove welded with E7018, although the face looked acceptable. The welder used 100 amperes with a 3/16 in rod on thick plate and a wide weave. Which pair of changes would best reduce this defect?", "options": {"A": "Lower the current further and widen the weave to cover both sidewalls", "B": "Lengthen the arc and slow the travel so the pool stays hot", "C": "Raise current into the rod's range and use narrow stringers", "D": "Switch to DCEN and drag the electrode flat against the bevel faces"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise current into the rod's range and use narrow stringers): A 3/16 in E7018 usually needs more than 100 A. Low heat and wide weaves leave the sidewalls cold. Higher current and narrower stringers aimed at the sidewalls improve fusion."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A cross section of an E7018 fillet weld shows the bead metal rolling over the plate surface at the toe without melting into it, as the diagram shows. A welder says the current was in range but the travel was very slow. Which change would best correct this condition?", "options": {"A": "Decrease the travel speed so more filler piles up at the toe", "B": "Travel faster and aim the arc so heat reaches the plate surface at the toe", "C": "Shorten the arc until the electrode touches the base plate", "D": "Use a smaller electrode and decrease the current to the minimum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Travel faster and aim the arc so heat reaches the plate surface at the toe): Weld metal that spills over cold plate is overlap, caused by slow travel, low heat or a poor angle. Moving faster and directing the arc at the plate lets the toe fuse properly.", "image": "question_images/q_245.png"}, {"id": 246, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welded coupon is broken open and a thin dark gap is found between the bead and the groove wall, as the diagram shows. The welder used a wide weave with a long arc and low current. Which single change would the instructor most likely stress first?", "options": {"A": "Lower the current and add a wider weave pattern across the face", "B": "Raise current to the recommended level and hold a short arc at the wall", "C": "Reverse polarity to DCEN and use a smaller electrode in the joint", "D": "Slow the travel speed and cool the plate with a damp rag between passes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise current to the recommended level and hold a short arc at the wall): An unfused sidewall means the plate did not melt, so more heat at the wall and a tighter arc are required. A damp rag cools the plate further and worsens the problem.", "image": "question_images/q_246.png"}, {"id": 247, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A tester slices a fillet weld made over a galvanized and primed surface and finds long, tunnel-like cavities inside the bead, as the diagram shows. The welder's rods were dry and fresh. Which cause best explains the pattern in the weld?", "options": {"A": "Slag from the previous pass was buried by the new bead", "B": "Gases from the surface coating were trapped as the weld cooled", "C": "Low current prevented the weld from reaching the joint root", "D": "The electrode was held at too steep a travel angle during the run"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gases from the surface coating were trapped as the weld cooled): Elongated cavities, called wormholes, form when gas from contaminants such as zinc or primer is trapped as the weld solidifies. Dry rods rule out moisture, so the surface is the likely source.", "image": "question_images/q_247.png"}, {"id": 248, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder ends a pass on thick plate by pulling the electrode quickly away at the finish. The stop point shows a small star-shaped cracking pattern in the last bit of weld, as the diagram shows. Which technique at the end of the bead would best prevent this?", "options": {"A": "Pull away quickly so the crater does not receive extra heat", "B": "Increase the travel speed through the final inch of the weld", "C": "Switch to a longer arc length in the last half inch of the bead", "D": "Pause at the end to fill the crater before breaking the arc"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pause at the end to fill the crater before breaking the arc): The crater at the end of a bead cools fast and has less metal, so it can crack. A short pause or small backstep fills the crater before the arc stops.", "image": "question_images/q_248.png"}, {"id": 249, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop supervisor reviews an E7018 weld on pressure vessel plate and is told rods came from a sealed can opened that morning, kept in a 275°F oven, with a 250°F minimum. The weld shows porosity near the start. The welder says the plate was oily. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Rod storage failed because the oven must be above 400°F during use", "B": "Porosity at the start means the machine duty cycle was exceeded", "C": "Oil on plate has no effect on low-hydrogen electrodes during welding", "D": "Rod storage was fine, so oil on the plate likely caused the gas"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rod storage was fine, so oil on the plate likely caused the gas): A holding temperature above the 250°F minimum is acceptable, so moisture from rod storage is unlikely. Oil is a hydrocarbon source of gas and is a common cause of porosity."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder must choose between a 5/32 in E7024 and a 5/32 in E7018 to weld a long horizontal fillet on thick structural plate in the shop with no special toughness requirements. Production speed is a priority. Which choice is better and what is its main tradeoff?", "options": {"A": "E7018, because deposition is higher, though it limits the work to flat positions", "B": "E7024, because deposition is higher, but it is limited to flat and horizontal work", "C": "E7024, because it gives better low-temperature toughness, though it needs a rod oven", "D": "E7018, because it works on AC only, though it gives deeper penetration"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (E7024, because deposition is higher, but it is limited to flat and horizontal work): E7024's iron powder coating gives the highest deposition rate, which suits long flat or horizontal fillets. The tradeoff is that it cannot be used out of position. It does not offer E7018's toughness."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder has a 200 ampere constant current machine and is welding a vertical joint with a 1/8 in rod. While adjusting, he notices the arc length varies by a quarter of an inch but the amperage meter hardly moves. A student asks why. What best explains this behavior?", "options": {"A": "The rectifier stores energy and releases it as arc length grows", "B": "The open circuit voltage rises as the arc is lengthened", "C": "The wire feeder speeds up to keep voltage steady as the arc changes", "D": "The drooping curve keeps current nearly steady as voltage changes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The drooping curve keeps current nearly steady as voltage changes): A drooping curve lets voltage change a lot while current changes only a little, which gives the welder control by hand. Stick welding has no wire feeder, so that option cannot apply."}, {"id": 252, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder has an E7018 electrode that has been out of the oven for 6 hours on a code job where the exposure limit is 4 hours. His supervisor says to use up the remaining rods because they look fine. Which response best reflects correct practice?", "options": {"A": "Reject or recondition them, as looks hide absorbed moisture", "B": "Use the rods at higher current to burn off moisture during welding", "C": "Accept the rods since only the last inch of each is affected", "D": "Use the rods for root passes only where moisture is less harmful"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reject or recondition them, as looks hide absorbed moisture): Absorbed moisture cannot be seen and exposure limits protect weld quality. Rods past the limit are reconditioned following the manufacturer's procedure or discarded. Higher current does not remove moisture."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welds a 1/4 in plate T-joint with a 1/8 in E6013 electrode at 90 amperes, then repeats it at 130 amperes with the same travel speed. The second bead undercuts both toes while the first looks sound. Which explanation best fits the evidence?", "options": {"A": "The higher current melted plate edge faster than the pool refilled it", "B": "The lower current dissolved the slag on the first bead more thoroughly", "C": "The higher current shortened the arc and trapped gas under the bead", "D": "The first bead had less penetration so the toes could not melt"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The higher current melted plate edge faster than the pool refilled it): Excess current at the same travel speed melts base metal at the toes faster than filler metal can fill the groove, which is undercut. The first setting was within range and gave balanced heat."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "During a plate fit-up, a welder sets a 1/16 in gap that varies up to 3/16 in along a 12 in joint, then welds with E7018. The narrow end shows poor fusion and the wide end shows burn-through. Which action would have best controlled the result?", "options": {"A": "Increase the current to blow through the narrow section", "B": "Fit the joint to a uniform gap and tack it before welding", "C": "Use a longer arc so the heat spreads across the full joint", "D": "Weld from the wide end only and skip the narrow section"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fit the joint to a uniform gap and tack it before welding): Variation in root gap changes how much metal and heat the joint needs. A consistent, tacked gap lets the same settings work along the entire length. More current only worsens burn-through."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder restarts a bead on E7018 by striking the arc inside the old crater and traveling at once. The restart shows a pore at the junction. Another welder says striking ahead then reversing would have helped. Which reasoning best supports that advice?", "options": {"A": "The reversal removes oxide from the electrode tip before it enters the crater", "B": "Striking ahead raises the current so the crater is filled with slag", "C": "Striking ahead preheats and reversing remelts the crater, freeing gas", "D": "The reversal shortens the arc to cut the deposit rate at the junction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Striking ahead preheats and reversing remelts the crater, freeing gas): Striking ahead warms the plate and the first deposit, and moving back remelts the crater so trapped gas can rise out. Striking inside the crater gives a cold, gassy start."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is told to weld thin 1/16 in sheet with a 3/32 in E6013 electrode. Even at 70 amperes, the plate keeps burning through. Which combination of changes is the best step before abandoning stick welding for another process?", "options": {"A": "Use a larger rod, raise current, and slow the travel speed", "B": "Switch to E7024 and weld at the same current with a longer arc", "C": "Use E6010 on DCEP with a keyhole technique at higher current", "D": "Drop to a smaller rod, lower the current and travel faster along the sheet"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Drop to a smaller rod, lower the current and travel faster along the sheet): Thin sheet needs low heat input, which a smaller rod, lower current and faster travel provide. E7024 and E6010 are high deposition and deep penetration choices that burn through thin sheet."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is told to qualify with E7018 on 3/8 in plate in the vertical position. During the test, the weld shows lack of fusion at the root and poor wetting at the toes. The welder used a very low current to control the puddle. Which statement best identifies the tradeoff?", "options": {"A": "Current above the range reduces slag and increases fusion at the root", "B": "Low current that controlled the puddle also starved the root of heat", "C": "Vertical welding requires current below the range on every thickness", "D": "Puddle control depends on arc length alone and not on current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low current that controlled the puddle also starved the root of heat): Out-of-position welding uses lower current than flat, but too low a setting leaves the root and toes cold. The welder should stay in range and use technique, like a shelf motion, to control the puddle."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop is welding 5/16 in plate on a transformer machine with E6011 on AC and sees an arc that goes out frequently at low amperage. A coworker says changing to a rutile electrode would help. Which reason best supports the coworker's advice?", "options": {"A": "E6013's coating burns hotter so it needs less current on AC", "B": "E6013 only runs on DC so it gives a steadier arc on AC", "C": "E6013's coating is cellulose based and drives gas deeper into the joint", "D": "E6013's stabilizers keep the arc lit as AC current crosses zero"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (E6013's stabilizers keep the arc lit as AC current crosses zero): On AC the current passes through zero 120 times a second, and rutile coatings contain ionizing stabilizers that restrike the arc. E6013 runs on AC and DC, and cellulose belongs to E6010 and E6011."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is asked why E7018 root passes on open-root pipe are less common than E6010 root passes, even though E7018 gives better toughness. The welder has run both on practice coupons in the shop. Which explanation best reflects the electrodes' characteristics in this application?", "options": {"A": "E7018 cannot be used on DCEP so it is unsuited to pipe work", "B": "E7018 has too much penetration so it burns through the root", "C": "E7018's soft arc and heavy slag trap slag and limit keyhole control", "D": "E7018 coating melts at a lower temperature so it blows out of the gap"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (E7018's soft arc and heavy slag trap slag and limit keyhole control): E6010's forceful arc cuts a keyhole in the root. E7018 has a softer arc and thicker slag and can trap slag in an open root, so it is used for fill and cap passes. It runs on DCEP."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "During stick welding of a long plate seam, the arc pushes the pool forward each time the welder nears the end of the plate on a DC machine. A supervisor suggests changing the work lead position and also changing to AC. Why might AC help this problem?", "options": {"A": "Alternating current raises amperage, so the arc overcomes the magnetic pull", "B": "Alternating current cools the plate, so magnetism leaves the steel faster", "C": "Alternating current is stronger than DC so it removes residual magnetism", "D": "AC reverses direction, so the magnetic field is weaker and arc blow drops"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (AC reverses direction, so the magnetic field is weaker and arc blow drops): Arc blow is caused by magnetic fields that DC builds in the plate. AC alternates polarity, which weakens steady fields. This is why changing to AC or moving the ground are standard fixes."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is setting up a machine to run a 1/8 in E7018 electrode on 1/2 in plate in the flat position. The electrode box lists a recommended range of 90 to 150 amperes for this diameter. The welder wants a sensible starting point before running a test bead. Which setting is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "About 60 amperes, below the range, to keep the puddle small and cool", "B": "About 180 amperes, above the range, to force deep fusion into the plate", "C": "About 75 amperes, below the range, to match the plate thickness in inches", "D": "About 120 amperes, near the middle of the published range for that diameter"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 120 amperes, near the middle of the published range for that diameter): Starting near the middle of the published range leaves room to adjust up or down after a test bead. Settings outside the range give poor arc behavior and defects, and plate thickness does not convert directly to amperes."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a code job, a welder strikes an arc on the surface of the base plate a few inches outside the joint to warm up a fresh electrode. The inspector marks the spot for rejection because the arc left a small hard burn on the steel. Where should the welder strike the arc instead?", "options": {"A": "On any clean area of plate beside the joint, then grind it smooth", "B": "Within the weld joint itself or on a run-off tab at the joint end", "C": "On the edge of the plate where the burn will be trimmed later", "D": "On the work lead clamp so the electrode warms before reaching the joint"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Within the weld joint itself or on a run-off tab at the joint end): An arc strike outside the joint can leave a hard, crack-prone spot in the base metal. Striking inside the joint or on a tab keeps the heat where weld metal will cover it. Striking on the clamp damages the clamp."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A beginner running a 1/8 in E6013 electrode on 1/4 in mild steel plate produces a spattery bead with a flickering arc and a hissing sound. The current is in range and the plate is clean. The electrode is held roughly three core wire diameters from the plate. What should the beginner change first?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the arc until it is about one core wire diameter long", "B": "Lengthen the arc further to let the spatter settle on the plate", "C": "Raise the current to the top of the range to quiet the arc", "D": "Tilt the electrode to a steeper push angle to keep the slag ahead"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shorten the arc until it is about one core wire diameter long): A long arc loses shielding and wanders, which causes spatter and an unstable sound. Shortening it to about one core wire diameter gives a steady crackle. Pushing at a steep angle can trap slag."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is told to join 5/8 in chromium-molybdenum steel plate with a plain E7018 electrode because the shop has no other stock. The inspector refuses the procedure before any welding is done. Which reason best explains the inspector's decision?", "options": {"A": "E7018 cannot be used on any plate thicker than 1/2 inch", "B": "The alloy base metal needs a matching A5.5 electrode such as E8018-B2", "C": "E7018 is only allowed on DCEN when welding alloy steel plate", "D": "E7018 melts at too low a temperature to join a high-alloy steel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The alloy base metal needs a matching A5.5 electrode such as E8018-B2): Low-alloy steels such as Cr-Mo need filler with matching strength and alloy content, which A5.5 electrodes like E8018-B2 supply. E7018 is a carbon steel electrode and runs on DCEP, and thickness alone does not rule it out."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder keeps opened E7018 rods in a heated cabinet set at 150°F because the shop owner says warm rods are fine. After a week, welds made on a code job show porosity and hydrogen cracking in the heat-affected zone. Which conclusion is best supported by this history?", "options": {"A": "The cabinet was below the 250°F minimum holding temperature, so rods reabsorbed moisture", "B": "The cabinet was too hot, so the coating lost its alloy content", "C": "Holding rods in any oven strips out the iron powder in the coating", "D": "Oven temperature has little effect once the sealed can has been opened"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The cabinet was below the 250°F minimum holding temperature, so rods reabsorbed moisture): Holding ovens for low-hydrogen rods should stay at or above 250°F so the coating does not pick up moisture. A cooler cabinet allows reabsorption, which leads to porosity and hydrogen cracking."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new apprentice in a fabrication shop is setting up a wire feeder at the GMAW station to weld quarter inch mild steel plate with solid wire. The instructor asks the apprentice to choose the power source output type and electrode polarity that suit standard solid wire GMAW on carbon steel. Which pairing should be selected?", "options": {"A": "Constant current output with direct current electrode negative polarity", "B": "Constant current output with alternating current and high frequency arc starting", "C": "Constant voltage output with direct current electrode positive polarity", "D": "Constant voltage output with direct current electrode negative polarity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Constant voltage output with direct current electrode positive polarity): GMAW machines hold voltage steady and let amperage follow the wire feed speed, and DCEP gives the penetration and arc stability solid steel wire needs. Constant current drooping outputs belong to stick and TIG."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "During a materials review for a structural fabrication course, students look at the classification printed on a spool of carbon steel GMAW wire marked ER70S-6. The instructor asks what the number 70 in this AWS A5.18 classification tells the welder about the weld metal produced by this solid wire electrode.", "options": {"A": "Recommended welding current of seventy amperes at one sixteenth inch diameter", "B": "Minimum tensile strength of seventy thousand pounds per square inch", "C": "Maximum diameter of the wire stated in seventieths of an inch", "D": "Percentage of manganese and silicon deoxidizers carried in the wire alloy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Minimum tensile strength of seventy thousand pounds per square inch): In A5.18 the first two digits give minimum tensile strength in ksi, so 70 means 70,000 psi. The final digit and letters identify the chemistry, not current or diameter."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop foreman is explaining shielding gas mixtures to a first year welding class using the cylinder labeled C25 on the GMAW cart. Students need to know what the common blend called 75/25 actually contains before they weld sheet metal and light plate with short circuit transfer on mild steel.", "options": {"A": "Seventy five percent carbon dioxide mixed with twenty five percent argon", "B": "Seventy five percent argon mixed with twenty five percent helium", "C": "Seventy five percent argon mixed with twenty five percent carbon dioxide", "D": "Seventy five percent argon mixed with twenty five percent oxygen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Seventy five percent argon mixed with twenty five percent carbon dioxide): The 75/25 mix is argon first and carbon dioxide second, which softens the spatter of straight CO2 while still driving good penetration on mild steel. The first number in a blend name is the argon share, so a mix read the other way around would be mostly carbon dioxide and behave very differently."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "In a GMAW lesson on metal transfer, the instructor describes a mode where the wire electrode touches the weld pool, the arc extinguishes, and the cycle repeats many times each second at low voltage. Students must name the transfer mode that matches this description for welding thin mild steel in all positions.", "options": {"A": "Spray transfer with a continuous stream of fine drops", "B": "Short circuit transfer used on thin steel in all positions", "C": "Globular transfer with large drops falling in an erratic pattern", "D": "Pulsed spray transfer with alternating peak and background current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Short circuit transfer used on thin steel in all positions): Short circuit transfer shows repeated wire to pool contact with the arc going out and reigniting, which keeps heat input low enough for thin sections and out of position welds. Spray and globular transfer both keep the arc on without wire contact, and pulsed spray needs a special power source, so none of them matches repeated contact."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding supervisor wants a crew to run axial spray transfer on half inch carbon steel plate in the flat position. Before the crew sets voltage and wire feed speed, she reminds them that spray transfer needs a shielding gas rich in one particular gas. Which gas must make up most of the mixture?", "options": {"A": "Carbon dioxide at roughly ninety percent or higher in the blend", "B": "Helium at roughly fifty percent with the balance as oxygen", "C": "Argon at roughly ninety percent or higher in the blend", "D": "Nitrogen at roughly ten percent with the balance as hydrogen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Argon at roughly ninety percent or higher in the blend): Spray transfer needs about 80 percent argon or more, and 90/10 argon CO2 is a popular choice. High CO2 content pushes the arc into globular transfer instead."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student compares welds made with 100 percent CO2 shielding gas to welds made with 75/25 argon CO2 on identical mild steel coupons using the same wire. The instructor asks which characteristic is typically associated with pure CO2 shielding in GMAW when compared with the argon blend.", "options": {"A": "Shallower penetration with a smoother spray style of transfer", "B": "Deeper penetration with a more spattery, globular style of transfer", "C": "Wider bead with the lowest possible spatter levels", "D": "Narrower bead with a stable pulsed arc and no oxide formation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Deeper penetration with a more spattery, globular style of transfer): Pure CO2 produces a hotter, more penetrating arc but it forces globular transfer and noticeable spatter, which is why many shops trade it for argon blends when appearance counts. A smoother spray style and the lowest spatter belong to argon rich blends, not to pure CO2."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a CV wire feeder, a welder is adjusting settings for a groove weld on structural steel and wants to understand each control. The instructor asks which GMAW setting directly determines the welding amperage delivered to the arc when a constant voltage power source is being used.", "options": {"A": "Wire feed speed, since current follows the amount of wire fed", "B": "Voltage dial, since current follows the arc length setting alone", "C": "Gas flow rate, since flow changes the electrical resistance of the shield", "D": "Travel angle, since angle sets how much current the machine releases"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wire feed speed, since current follows the amount of wire fed): With a constant voltage machine the output current self adjusts to burn off the wire as fast as it is fed, so wire feed speed is the amperage control. Voltage mainly sets arc length, and gas flow and travel angle do not set current in a constant voltage circuit."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A trainee on a GMAW practice plate keeps wire feed speed constant but raises the voltage by two volts between passes. The instructor asks what main change the welder should expect in the bead as a result of this adjustment on a constant voltage power source.", "options": {"A": "A longer arc and a wider, flatter bead profile", "B": "A much higher amperage and noticeably deeper penetration", "C": "A shorter arc and a narrower, taller bead profile", "D": "A faster wire melt rate and a heavier bead crown"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A longer arc and a wider, flatter bead profile): Voltage sets arc length in GMAW, so more volts stretches the arc and spreads the bead wider and flatter while amperage stays near the wire feed speed value. Raising voltage does not change the wire melt rate much, so it does not give a much higher amperage or a taller bead."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld inspector reviewing a GMAW procedure asks a welder to point out the electrode extension, often called stickout. The welder needs to identify the correct definition so the measurement can be checked against the written procedure before a qualification test coupon is welded.", "options": {"A": "Distance from the end of the gas nozzle to the surface of the base metal", "B": "Distance from the wire spool to the drive rolls inside the feeder", "C": "Distance from the end of the contact tip to the arc end of the wire", "D": "Distance from the center of the bead to the toe of the weld"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Distance from the end of the contact tip to the arc end of the wire): Stickout is measured from the contact tip to the wire end where the arc starts, and it controls how much resistance heating the wire gets before it enters the arc. Measuring from the gas nozzle instead gives a different distance that includes the tip setback, so it does not match stickout."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "In a class demonstration on travel technique, one welder pushes the gun ahead of the puddle while another drags it back toward the completed bead on the same mild steel plate. The instructor asks which effect is associated with the push technique when compared with the drag technique.", "options": {"A": "Deeper penetration with a taller bead and a heavier crown", "B": "Deeper penetration with a narrower bead and less spatter", "C": "Shallower penetration with a taller bead and a blocked view of the joint", "D": "Shallower penetration with a flatter bead and a clearer view of the joint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shallower penetration with a flatter bead and a clearer view of the joint): Pushing directs arc force ahead of the puddle, which spreads the bead out and reduces penetration. Dragging concentrates the arc on the pool, giving deeper, narrower, more convex beads."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop has just changed from a work area with a ventilation fan to an enclosed booth, and the lead welder reviews standard shielding gas settings for GMAW on mild steel indoors. Which flow rate range, measured in cubic feet per hour at the regulator, is the typical starting range used for solid wire?", "options": {"A": "About two to five cubic feet per hour at the regulator", "B": "About sixty to eighty cubic feet per hour at the regulator", "C": "About one hundred to one hundred twenty cubic feet per hour at the regulator", "D": "About twenty to thirty five cubic feet per hour at the regulator"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About twenty to thirty five cubic feet per hour at the regulator): Most GMAW setups on steel run roughly 20 to 35 cfh. Too little leaves the pool unprotected, while too much creates turbulence that pulls air into the shield."}, {"id": 277, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A wire feeder is being loaded with 0.035 inch ER70S-6 solid steel wire for a production run, and a new operator is unsure which drive roll groove style to install. The lead hand explains that solid hard wire needs a particular groove shape to grip and feed without deforming the wire.", "options": {"A": "A V shaped groove sized to the wire diameter", "B": "A U shaped groove sized to the wire diameter", "C": "A knurled groove sized for flux cored tubular wire", "D": "A smooth flat roll with no groove cut in the surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A V shaped groove sized to the wire diameter): V grooves bite into hard solid steel wire for positive feeding. U grooves cradle soft wire such as aluminum, and knurled rolls suit tubular wires that would crush on a V groove."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding instructor is covering consumables and asks the class about the contact tip in a GMAW gun. Students are working on a mild steel plate with a CV machine and a spool of solid wire. What is the main job of the contact tip during welding?", "options": {"A": "Directs shielding gas around the molten weld pool in a smooth cone", "B": "Grips the wire and pushes it forward from the spool", "C": "Transfers welding current to the moving wire as it passes through", "D": "Filters moisture and oxides out of the shielding gas stream"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transfers welding current to the moving wire as it passes through): The contact tip is a copper alloy part that touches the wire and passes current into it. The nozzle directs gas and the drive rolls push the wire."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is running 18 gauge mild steel sheet with 0.030 inch ER70S-6 in the vertical up position and wants low heat input and minimal distortion. The weld pool keeps sagging when the arc stays on too long. Which metal transfer mode and shielding gas combination suits this job?", "options": {"A": "Axial spray transfer using a 98/2 argon and oxygen blend", "B": "Short circuit transfer using a 75/25 argon and carbon dioxide blend", "C": "Globular transfer using one hundred percent carbon dioxide at high current", "D": "Axial spray transfer using one hundred percent argon at high current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Short circuit transfer using a 75/25 argon and carbon dioxide blend): Short circuit transfer keeps the pool small and cool, which holds in out of position welds on thin steel. Spray and globular modes carry too much heat and fluid pool for vertical sheet work."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator needs to weld 3/8 inch carbon steel plate in the flat position with high deposition and a smooth bead, and the machine has plenty of output. The wire is ER70S-6 at 0.045 inch. Which shielding gas gives spray transfer once voltage and wire feed speed exceed the transition current?", "options": {"A": "Seventy five percent argon with twenty five percent carbon dioxide", "B": "Ninety percent argon with ten percent carbon dioxide", "C": "Seventy five percent carbon dioxide with twenty five percent argon", "D": "One hundred percent carbon dioxide at thirty five cubic feet per hour"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ninety percent argon with ten percent carbon dioxide): A 90/10 argon CO2 mix holds enough argon to allow spray transfer above the transition current. A 75/25 mix on this wire usually stays closer to a short circuit and globular blend."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is making a horizontal fillet on quarter inch steel with a constant voltage machine. The weld shows heavy spatter and large molten droplets falling erratically across the joint. Wire feed speed sits just under the spray transition and the gas is straight CO2. What is the transfer mode that explains this?", "options": {"A": "Short circuit transfer, producing repeated wire contact with the pool", "B": "Pulsed spray transfer, producing one fine drop per current pulse", "C": "Axial spray transfer, producing a steady stream of tiny droplets", "D": "Globular transfer, producing large drops that detach irregularly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Globular transfer, producing large drops that detach irregularly): Large irregular drops with spatter at midrange current and CO2 gas are the signature of globular transfer. Spray would show a quiet fine stream, and pulsed transfer needs a pulse capable power source."}, {"id": 282, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding shop has a pulse capable inverter and must weld 1/8 inch carbon steel out of position without the heat buildup of spray transfer. The welder asks what the unit changes during pulsed GMAW to give a spray type arc at a lower average current. What does it do?", "options": {"A": "Alternates the shielding gas between argon and carbon dioxide each cycle", "B": "Alternates the electrode polarity between positive and negative each cycle", "C": "Alternates the wire feed direction forward and backward every few millimeters", "D": "Alternates a high peak current with a low background current"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alternates a high peak current with a low background current): Pulsed GMAW cycles a peak current above the spray transition with a lower background current, detaching one droplet per pulse while holding the average heat down for thinner or out of position work. Pulsed GMAW changes only the current waveform, not the gas, the polarity or the direction of wire travel."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is laying a 3/8 inch fillet in the horizontal position with 0.045 inch ER70S-6 and a CV machine. The arc sounds rough and wire is stubbing into the pool, and the stickout measures 1.25 inches. What is the best adjustment to restore a stable arc?", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the electrode extension to about two inches", "B": "Shorten the extension to about three eighths to half an inch", "C": "Reduce the gas flow to roughly ten cubic feet per hour", "D": "Switch the polarity to electrode negative with the same settings"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shorten the extension to about three eighths to half an inch): An excessive stickout adds resistance heating and lowers current while the arc becomes erratic. Bringing it back near 3/8 to 1/2 inch stabilizes the arc and increases amperage at the same wire feed speed."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder has set a wire feed speed of 300 inches per minute and now lengthens the stickout from half an inch to one inch without touching the dials. On a constant voltage machine, what change in welding current and penetration should the welder expect?", "options": {"A": "Lower current and shallower penetration since resistance heating rises", "B": "Higher current and deeper penetration because the arc is closer to the pool", "C": "Unchanged current and penetration because the machine locks the amperage", "D": "Higher current and shallower penetration because the wire melts earlier"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower current and shallower penetration since resistance heating rises): Longer stickout raises electrical resistance, so the wire melts with less machine current at the same feed speed. The result is lower amperage and less penetration into the base metal."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder pushing the gun on a lap joint of 3/16 inch steel notices a flat bead with shallow penetration at the root and poor visibility of the puddle edge. The weld must fuse deeply into the lower plate. What technique change helps most?", "options": {"A": "Change to a drag angle near five to fifteen degrees", "B": "Increase the push angle to about forty five degrees", "C": "Raise the stickout to about one and a half inches", "D": "Reduce the wire feed speed by half and push the gun harder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Change to a drag angle near five to fifteen degrees): A drag angle points the arc force into the pool and deepens penetration. A strongly increased push angle or a longer stickout would flatten the bead and reduce fusion at the root."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is making a groove weld on 1/2 inch plate and finds scattered pinholes on the bead surface. The nozzle shows a thick coat of spatter, and the flow meter reads a normal 30 cubic feet per hour. What is the most direct corrective action?", "options": {"A": "Increase the gas flow to eighty cubic feet per hour", "B": "Increase voltage to lengthen the arc and widen the bead", "C": "Clean spatter out of the nozzle so gas flows evenly", "D": "Change the wire to a larger diameter without changing settings"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean spatter out of the nozzle so gas flows evenly): Spatter blocking the nozzle disturbs laminar gas flow, letting air reach the pool and form porosity. Cranking the flow higher adds turbulence and would likely make the pinholes worse."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is joining 14 gauge mild steel sheet for an equipment enclosure with a CV machine and solid wire. The arc blows right through the plate in several spots. Wire feed speed is high and travel is slow. Without changing the wire or gas, which adjustment should reduce the burn-through?", "options": {"A": "Increase travel speed and lower the wire feed speed and voltage", "B": "Slow the travel speed and raise the wire feed speed further", "C": "Lengthen the stickout to two inches and keep the same travel speed", "D": "Switch to one hundred percent carbon dioxide and raise voltage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase travel speed and lower the wire feed speed and voltage): Burn-through comes from too much heat for the thickness. Faster travel and lower feed and voltage cut heat input, while slowing down or adding amperage would make the holes worse."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a groove weld complains that the bead edge looks rolled over and has not fused into the plate at the toes. The inspector sees a cold lap along both sides. Voltage reads low for the wire feed speed in use. What change is most likely to correct it?", "options": {"A": "Lower the voltage until the bead becomes narrow and tall", "B": "Reduce the gas flow to twelve cubic feet per hour", "C": "Switch to a smaller wire at the same feed speed", "D": "Raise the voltage until the bead wets into both toes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the voltage until the bead wets into both toes): Cold lap shows when too little heat and a tall narrow bead fail to wet the plate. Raising voltage widens and flattens the bead so it fuses at the toes."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is welding mild steel plate outside near an open bay door and gets worm tracks of porosity along the bead. The gas flow reads 30 cubic feet per hour and the equipment checks out. What should the welder do first to stop the defect?", "options": {"A": "Increase the gas flow to sixty cubic feet per hour", "B": "Block the draft with a screen or move the work out of the breeze", "C": "Lengthen the stickout to a full inch and a half", "D": "Change from ER70S-6 to a lower strength wire classification for outdoor work"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Block the draft with a screen or move the work out of the breeze): Wind carries shielding gas away from the pool and mixes air in. A screen restores protection, whereas more flow causes turbulence that pulls in even more air."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder prepares to weld mill scale covered structural steel with an ER70S-6 wire after an inspector questions whether a different wire should be used. The shop has no time to grind scale in one location. Which feature of ER70S-6 helps tolerate light surface contamination?", "options": {"A": "More silicon and manganese deoxidizers than S-3 wire", "B": "Higher chromium content than any other mild steel wire", "C": "A flux core that traps oxides in a slag blanket", "D": "A nickel coating that dissolves rust into the weld metal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (More silicon and manganese deoxidizers than S-3 wire): The 6 in ER70S-6 means extra deoxidizers that scavenge oxygen from light mill scale or rust. Cleaning is still required on heavy contamination, because deoxidizers only help up to a point."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is setting up a CV machine to weld 1/4 inch plate with 0.035 inch wire. After striking the arc, the wire stubs and pushes the gun back, and the arc is too short. Voltage is set low for the wire feed speed. Which change fixes it?", "options": {"A": "Decrease the voltage in small steps until the arc shortens further", "B": "Increase the gas flow in steps until the stubbing ends", "C": "Decrease the wire feed speed to zero and retry the start", "D": "Increase the voltage in small steps until the arc runs smoothly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase the voltage in small steps until the arc runs smoothly): A stubbing arc means voltage is too low for the wire feed rate, so the wire reaches the pool before melting. Raising voltage lengthens the arc and smooths transfer."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is checking a GMAW machine before a test coupon and finds the shielding gas hose has a small leak at the regulator fitting, though the flow meter looks normal. What defect is most likely to appear in the weld and why?", "options": {"A": "Undercut, because the leak lowers the arc voltage at the toes", "B": "Porosity, because air enters the gas stream and fouls the pool", "C": "Burn-through, because the lost gas raises current in the wire", "D": "Excessive crown height, because gas pressure drops at the nozzle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Porosity, because air enters the gas stream and fouls the pool): A leak before or at the regulator can pull air into the gas stream or reduce coverage at the nozzle, producing porosity. The other defects stem from heat and travel settings rather than gas losses."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a production floor, a welder runs 0.045 inch ER70S-6 with 75/25 gas and a gas flow of 60 cubic feet per hour on a fillet weld. The bead shows surface pinholes and a pitted look. What is the likely cause of the defect?", "options": {"A": "Excess gas flow creating turbulence that pulls air into the shield", "B": "Insufficient gas flow leaving the molten pool open to the surrounding room air", "C": "Excessive voltage lengthening the arc and thinning the bead", "D": "Insufficient stickout causing the wire to preheat too little"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excess gas flow creating turbulence that pulls air into the shield): Flow far above the 20 to 35 cfh range makes turbulent gas that entrains air. Lowering the flow to the normal range restores a smooth protective shield."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is welding a 1/4 inch fillet with a CV machine, wire feed set at 350 inches per minute, and voltage dialed down until the bead looks tall and ropey with poor wetting at the toes. The inspector asks what to change. What is the right adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Lower voltage a couple of volts so the bead sits taller", "B": "Raise the gas flow a few cubic feet per hour for more wetting", "C": "Raise voltage a couple of volts so the bead flattens out", "D": "Lower the wire feed speed to a quarter of its present value"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise voltage a couple of volts so the bead flattens out): Voltage that is low for the wire feed speed produces a convex, ropey bead that does not wet. A modest voltage increase flattens the profile and improves tie in at the toes."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld shop is deciding on stickout for short circuit transfer on 1/8 inch steel with 0.035 inch wire. The welder usually runs around five eighths inch of extension but the procedure sheet lists a different range. Which range does a typical short circuit procedure call for?", "options": {"A": "Roughly three eighths to one half inch of extension", "B": "Roughly one and a half to two inches of electrode extension", "C": "Roughly one eighth to one quarter inch of electrode extension", "D": "Roughly three to four inches of electrode extension"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Roughly three eighths to one half inch of extension): Short circuit GMAW typically uses about 3/8 to 1/2 inch of stickout. Spray transfer often runs a little longer, while very long extensions waste heat and weaken the arc."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder wants to compare drag and push techniques on a 3/8 inch groove weld in the flat position, using the same machine settings. The welder drags the gun at ten degrees and notices a taller, narrower bead. What other effect should be expected compared with pushing?", "options": {"A": "Less penetration into the base metal at the same settings", "B": "Wider spreading of the bead with lower crown height", "C": "Cooler arc with fewer variations in current and voltage", "D": "Greater penetration into the base metal at same settings"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Greater penetration into the base metal at same settings): Drag angle forces the arc and puddle to share the same ground, which increases penetration and narrows the bead. Pushing does the reverse by letting the pool run ahead of the arc."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder in a school lab notes that the wire spool tension on a feeder is set so tight that the wire feeds in jerks and the arc stutters. Which action will correct the problem without affecting other welding settings?", "options": {"A": "Tighten the drive roll pressure until the wire can no longer slip in the groove", "B": "Loosen the spool brake until the spool turns freely without overrunning", "C": "Lower the shielding gas flow until the wire stops stuttering", "D": "Raise the voltage until the arc smooths despite the stutter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loosen the spool brake until the spool turns freely without overrunning): Too much brake drag makes the drive rolls fight the spool and causes erratic feed. The brake should hold just enough tension to prevent the spool from overrunning when feeding stops."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is restarting a weld after replacing a worn contact tip and finds the wire feed erratic with frequent burnback to the tip. The tip bore is larger than the wire size listed on the procedure. What correction is called for?", "options": {"A": "Install a larger contact tip so the wire has more room", "B": "Install a contact tip sized for the wire diameter in use", "C": "Increase stickout so the wire heats before the tip", "D": "Reduce shielding gas flow so the arc stays close to the tip"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Install a contact tip sized for the wire diameter in use): A tip bore that is too large lets the wire wander and make poor electrical contact, leading to arcing inside the tip and burnback. Matching the tip to the wire restores steady current transfer."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is welding a groove weld on 1/2 inch plate with ER70S-6 and 90/10 gas in the flat position. The bead is smooth with minimal spatter but the shop wants to weld a vertical up pass. What should the welder change about the process for out of position welding?", "options": {"A": "Stay in axial spray transfer and increase the travel speed", "B": "Raise voltage and gas flow to stiffen the arc", "C": "Switch to short circuit or pulsed transfer to control the pool", "D": "Switch to straight carbon dioxide and reduce the wire feed speed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Switch to short circuit or pulsed transfer to control the pool): Axial spray produces a hot, fluid pool that sags out of position. Short circuit or pulsed transfer keeps the pool controllable in vertical and overhead positions."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld sample is cut and etched, and the cross section diagram shows a labeled GMAW defect on a fillet weld made with 75/25 gas. The welder had run a flow of 55 cubic feet per hour with a worn nozzle. Which cause best matches this defect?", "options": {"A": "Excessive travel speed pulling the arc ahead of the bead", "B": "Turbulent shielding gas drawing air into the molten pool", "C": "Wire diameter too large for the thickness of the plate", "D": "Voltage set too high so the arc lengthens past the toe"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Turbulent shielding gas drawing air into the molten pool): Gas flow well beyond the normal range causes turbulence and air entrainment, creating porosity. The other options relate to bead shape or heat input, not the protective gas.", "image": "question_images/q_300.png"}, {"id": 301, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a fillet weld on half inch steel and an inspector marks the defect labeled in the diagram along the toe of the bead. The welder was using high voltage, high travel speed, and a gun angle that favored one plate. What cause fits the labeled defect?", "options": {"A": "Insufficient heat so the bead rides on top of the cold plate surface", "B": "Low gas flow letting air contaminate the molten pool", "C": "A cold wire electrode causing poor deposit at the root", "D": "Excess heat and fast travel that melt a groove in the plate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Excess heat and fast travel that melt a groove in the plate): Undercut results when excessive voltage, fast travel, or a poor gun angle melt the plate edge faster than filler can refill it. Cold lap and porosity arise from different conditions.", "image": "question_images/q_301.png"}, {"id": 302, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator reviews a fillet weld in a failed test report and the diagram shows a labeled defect where molten metal rolled over the plate without fusing at the toe. Voltage and travel were both too low. What best explains the labeled defect?", "options": {"A": "Excessive heat and fast travel melting a groove into the plate", "B": "Contaminated shielding gas introducing porosity into the bead", "C": "Insufficient heat and slow travel letting the pool run ahead of the arc", "D": "Excess wire stickout burning through the root of the joint"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insufficient heat and slow travel letting the pool run ahead of the arc): Overlap forms when the molten pool rolls onto cold plate that did not reach fusion temperature, usually from low heat or slow travel. Undercut is the opposite case with too much heat.", "image": "question_images/q_302.png"}, {"id": 303, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is qualifying on 3/8 inch plate and fails a bend test with the diagram of the weld cross section showing a labeled defect at the sidewall. The welder ran short circuit transfer with low voltage, little weave, and a wide groove. What is the likely cause?", "options": {"A": "High heat input burning a groove into the sidewall", "B": "Wet shielding gas creating pinholes in the sidewall", "C": "Low heat input so the arc did not fuse the sidewall", "D": "Excess crown height pulling stress into the groove sidewall"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low heat input so the arc did not fuse the sidewall): Short circuit transfer in a wide groove with low voltage and no weave can leave the sidewall unfused, which is lack of fusion. It rarely shows from outside, so bend tests catch it.", "image": "question_images/q_303.png"}, {"id": 304, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is making a 1/4 inch fillet on a carbon steel bracket with 75/25 gas and 0.035 inch wire in the flat position. The bead looks ropey and the arc crackles softly with frequent stubbing. Wire feed speed is correct. Which single setting should be raised first?", "options": {"A": "Gas flow, to push the shield farther out onto the plate surface", "B": "Voltage, to lengthen the arc and let the bead flatten", "C": "Stickout, to preheat the wire before the pool", "D": "Travel angle, to point the arc more toward the plate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Voltage, to lengthen the arc and let the bead flatten): Stubbing with a ropey bead at a proper feed speed is a classic sign of voltage set too low. A small voltage increase lengthens the arc and relaxes the bead shape."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is joining two pieces of 10 gauge mild steel in the overhead position and wants to avoid dripping metal. The machine offers constant voltage with a wire feeder, and 75/25 gas is hooked up. Which wire feed speed and voltage choice is the sensible starting point?", "options": {"A": "High wire feed speed and high voltage in the spray range", "B": "High wire feed speed and low voltage so wire plunges deeply", "C": "Low wire feed speed and high voltage in the globular range", "D": "Low feed speed and low voltage in the short circuit range"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low feed speed and low voltage in the short circuit range): Overhead work needs a small, fast freezing pool, and short circuit settings supply that. Spray range settings make a fluid pool that falls away from the joint."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is laying a groove weld in the flat position with 90/10 gas and 0.045 inch wire. The arc is smooth but the weld has a very narrow, tall bead with deep penetration and the shop wants a flatter bead. The travel angle is a 15 degree drag. What adjustment helps?", "options": {"A": "Increase the drag angle to thirty degrees from the plate", "B": "Lower voltage and shorten the stickout to a quarter inch", "C": "Raise the gas flow to fifty cubic feet per hour", "D": "Change to a slight push angle and raise voltage a little"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Change to a slight push angle and raise voltage a little): A drag angle and low voltage build a narrow tall bead. Pushing slightly and raising voltage spread the bead and soften penetration."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld inspector is training new welders to read GMAW arc behavior on carbon steel. One welder reports a steady, crisp frying sound with almost no spatter at high current using 90/10 gas. Which transfer mode does this arc sound and appearance most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "Short circuit transfer, producing repeated contact with the pool", "B": "Globular transfer, producing large irregular drops with spatter", "C": "Axial spray transfer, producing a fine stream of drops", "D": "Pulsed transfer, producing a slow rhythmic crackle at low amperage"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Axial spray transfer, producing a fine stream of drops): A smooth hissing arc with little spatter is characteristic of spray transfer. Short circuit has a buzzing crackle, and globular transfer sounds rough with big droplets."}, {"id": 308, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs short circuit GMAW on a heavy 1 inch thick section with 75/25 gas, voltage and wire feed speed set to the low range, and passes the visual inspection. Ultrasonic testing then finds planar flaws at the sidewalls. Which explanation fits both observations best?", "options": {"A": "Short circuit transfer deposits little heat, so cold sidewalls can look fine yet remain unfused", "B": "Short circuit transfer runs so hot that the root burns away inside the weld", "C": "The gas blend is too rich in argon to fuse the sidewalls", "D": "Surface bead appearance reliably exposes internal lack of fusion right away"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short circuit transfer deposits little heat, so cold sidewalls can look fine yet remain unfused): Short circuit transfer has low heat input, so on thick plate the bead surface can look good while the sidewall did not reach fusion temperature. Visual inspection cannot find internal flaws like this."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop wants to switch from 75/25 argon carbon dioxide to 100 percent carbon dioxide to cut gas cost on 1/4 inch structural fillets. The weld quality manager worries about side effects. Which outcome is the most reasonable to expect from the change?", "options": {"A": "Smoother bead with less spatter and a flatter weld profile overall", "B": "Easy axial spray transfer at lower current levels", "C": "Deeper penetration offset by more spatter and a rougher bead", "D": "Lower oxidation of the weld metal and less silicon loss"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deeper penetration offset by more spatter and a rougher bead): Straight CO2 delivers penetration but forces globular transfer with extra spatter and more cleanup. The shop saves on gas yet pays in labor and appearance."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "Two welders use the same machine settings on 3/8 inch plate. One welds with a ten degree push angle and gets a flat bead. The other drags at ten degrees and gets a tall bead. An inspector must decide which weld better serves a thin, heat sensitive joint. Which is correct?", "options": {"A": "Dragging, because deeper penetration lowers the risk of burn-through in thin sections", "B": "Pushing, because shallower penetration lowers burn-through risk on thin material", "C": "Dragging, because a taller bead reduces heat in the base metal", "D": "Pushing, because a flatter bead raises heat in the base metal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pushing, because shallower penetration lowers burn-through risk on thin material): Pushing spreads the arc force and gives shallower penetration, which protects thin or heat sensitive parts. Dragging concentrates energy and raises the risk of burning through."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using a constant voltage supply raises wire feed speed from 250 to 400 inches per minute without touching the voltage dial. The arc becomes violent and stubs into the pool. What explains this behavior and what correction fits?", "options": {"A": "Higher feed speed lowers amperage so voltage should be reduced", "B": "Higher feed speed changes polarity so the cable must be swapped", "C": "Higher feed speed raises gas demand so flow should be doubled at the regulator", "D": "Higher feed speed raises amperage and needs more voltage for arc length"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Higher feed speed raises amperage and needs more voltage for arc length): On a CV machine amperage follows feed speed, and a higher current needs matching voltage to keep the arc length stable. Without it the wire plunges into the pool and stubs."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop is setting a procedure for axial spray on 1/2 inch steel with 0.045 inch wire. Test welds at 22 volts and moderate wire feed speed keep showing spatter and large drops, but at higher wire feed speed and voltage the arc turns smooth. What explains the change?", "options": {"A": "Gas flow has crossed a limit where the argon changes over into carbon dioxide", "B": "Polarity has switched on its own at higher wire feed speed", "C": "Current has passed the transition level where drops become a fine stream", "D": "Stickout has shrunk to zero at higher voltage settings"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Current has passed the transition level where drops become a fine stream): Spray transfer begins above a transition current for a given wire and argon rich gas. Below it the arc remains globular, and above it the drops detach as a fine axial stream."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding engineer compares pulsed GMAW with conventional spray on 1/4 inch carbon steel out of position. The engineer notes that pulsed welding gives fewer spatter problems and better pool control. Which statement best explains why it works for out of position work?", "options": {"A": "Higher average current keeps the pool hot and runny so it can flow upward", "B": "Lower average current gives a smaller pool while pulses detach drops cleanly", "C": "Constant current prevents the pool from changing temperature during the weld cycle", "D": "Pure carbon dioxide gas stiffens the pool against gravity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower average current gives a smaller pool while pulses detach drops cleanly): Pulsing delivers spray type droplet transfer at a lower average heat, keeping the pool small enough to hold against gravity in vertical or overhead work. A higher average current or pure carbon dioxide would make the pool larger and hotter, the opposite of what out of position work needs."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder qualifies in the flat position with 90/10 gas in spray transfer and then is asked to weld an overhead joint under the same procedure. The inspector refuses to accept the test. What is the best justification for refusing?", "options": {"A": "Argon carbon dioxide blends cannot be used overhead under any welding code or standard", "B": "Spray transfer makes too fluid a pool for overhead, so another procedure is needed", "C": "Overhead welding requires constant current power instead of constant voltage", "D": "Spray transfer deposits too little filler to pass an overhead test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spray transfer makes too fluid a pool for overhead, so another procedure is needed): Out of position work with axial spray gives a hot pool that falls out, so a short circuit or pulsed procedure is normally qualified. The procedure change is an essential variable in most codes."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder finds worm shaped porosity in repeated fillets made on heavily rusted structural steel at a repair shop. The shop uses ER70S-6 with 75/25 gas, and the regulator flow is in the normal range with a clean nozzle. Which cause should be addressed first to stop the porosity?", "options": {"A": "A contact tip bore that is too small for the wire diameter being used", "B": "An overly short stickout that does not preheat the wire", "C": "Surface rust and contamination on the base metal that need cleaning", "D": "A drag angle that is too steep for fillet welds"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Surface rust and contamination on the base metal that need cleaning): Rust and oil release gas that becomes porosity, and ER70S-6 deoxidizers only handle light contamination. Grinding to bright metal fixes the source."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An inspector discovers that porosity appears every time the welder works near one particular bench, though the settings, wire, and material match those used at other benches without trouble. The gas flow meter reads normally at thirty cubic feet per hour. Which investigation is the most useful next step?", "options": {"A": "Check for a draft from a fan or door and a leak in the gas line", "B": "Check the transformer taps for an incorrect secondary output current setting", "C": "Check the drive roll groove for a wrong diameter size", "D": "Check the wire spool for an incorrect alloy type"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check for a draft from a fan or door and a leak in the gas line): A location specific defect points to air movement or a gas delivery fault near that bench. Identical wire and settings elsewhere rule out the feeder and alloy."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder claims that raising stickout from half an inch to one and a quarter inches will give deeper penetration because the wire arrives hotter. The instructor disagrees, and the machine is a constant voltage unit with a fixed wire feed speed. What reasoning supports the instructor in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Longer extension raises machine current, so penetration increases just as the welder claims", "B": "Longer extension changes polarity, so penetration depends on the cable", "C": "Longer extension raises gas flow, so penetration depends on the shield", "D": "Longer extension lowers machine current, so penetration drops though the wire is hotter"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Longer extension lowers machine current, so penetration drops though the wire is hotter): Preheated wire melts with less current, and amperage drops at constant feed speed, so the pool receives less arc energy and less penetration results. A longer extension adds resistance heating in the wire, so it does not raise machine current or change polarity or gas flow."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A procedure for GMAW on thin carbon steel requires constant voltage DCEP with 75/25 gas and short circuit transfer. A welder proposes switching to DCEN to reduce burn-through, but the lead welder rejects this change. Which reasoning best supports the rejection of the proposed polarity change?", "options": {"A": "DCEN makes solid wire transfer erratic and spattery, so quality drops", "B": "DCEN raises penetration far above DCEP so the burn-through problem gets worse", "C": "DCEN prevents the machine from holding a constant voltage", "D": "DCEN requires a different diameter wire for any material"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (DCEN makes solid wire transfer erratic and spattery, so quality drops): Solid wire GMAW is stable on electrode positive. Electrode negative produces erratic transfer and heavy spatter, so heat should be lowered with feed and voltage settings."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder reduces wire feed speed and voltage to eliminate burn-through on thin steel sheet, but now sees cold lap at the toes with tall narrow beads on the test coupon. Gas, wire, and polarity are correct. What is the best way to resolve both defects together?", "options": {"A": "Return to the original high settings and drag the gun slowly across the whole joint", "B": "Raise the stickout to two inches and slow the travel", "C": "Keep the lower heat settings and speed up travel slightly with a flatter angle", "D": "Switch to 100 percent carbon dioxide and raise gas flow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep the lower heat settings and speed up travel slightly with a flatter angle): The two defects pull opposite directions on heat, so the fix is balancing bead shape with travel and angle rather than swinging heat. Faster travel avoids burn-through while a proper angle improves wetting."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A quality inspector reviews two fillets welded with identical settings. One used a draft free booth and shows clean beads. The other was welded near a large open door and has porosity. A coworker suggests increasing the gas flow in the second shop. Why is that a poor choice?", "options": {"A": "Higher flow raises voltage and makes the arc longer, thinning the bead", "B": "Higher flow cools the wire so it will not melt consistently", "C": "Higher flow becomes turbulent and pulls air into the shield, worsening the defect", "D": "Higher flow changes polarity at the contact tip and lowers current"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Higher flow becomes turbulent and pulls air into the shield, worsening the defect): Beyond the proper range, flow causes turbulence at the nozzle and aspirates air. Windscreens or a relocated job are the right fix, not raising flow."}, {"id": 321, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a 1/4 inch fillet in the horizontal position sees a labeled defect in the diagram of a cross section cut from the test coupon. The wire was set high, voltage was low, and the welder dragged the gun at a steep angle. Which cause best fits the labeled defect?", "options": {"A": "Excess voltage so the arc eats a groove along the toe of the weld", "B": "Gas contamination so gas pockets form in the bead", "C": "Insufficient stickout so wire melts inside the contact tip", "D": "Low heat for the feed rate so the bead sits tall on cold plate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low heat for the feed rate so the bead sits tall on cold plate): Low voltage with high feed speed makes a tall ropey bead that overlaps the plate without fusing. That cold, tall profile is the cause of the labeled overlap.", "image": "question_images/q_321.png"}, {"id": 322, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A cross section diagram of a root pass on 3/8 inch plate shows a labeled defect where the root of the joint was left unfilled. The welder used a narrow groove opening, low current, and a long stickout. Which change would best prevent this defect?", "options": {"A": "Reduce heat input and close the root gap to nearly nothing before welding", "B": "Lower the voltage and lengthen the stickout further", "C": "Increase heat input and open the root gap so the arc reaches the root", "D": "Reduce gas flow and increase the travel speed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase heat input and open the root gap so the arc reaches the root): Incomplete penetration comes from low heat, a tight root, or a long stickout that lowers current. More heat and a proper root opening let the arc reach the bottom of the joint.", "image": "question_images/q_322.png"}, {"id": 323, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A cross section diagram of a groove weld made with short circuit transfer shows a labeled defect along the sidewall. The welder used a wide groove, low voltage, and held the gun still at center. Which repair procedure is the best fix for next time?", "options": {"A": "Hold the gun still and lower voltage further to cut heat", "B": "Increase the gas flow so the shield reaches the sidewalls of the groove", "C": "Weave to wet the sidewalls and raise voltage enough to fuse them", "D": "Switch polarity so the arc shifts toward the sidewalls"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weave to wet the sidewalls and raise voltage enough to fuse them): Lack of fusion on a sidewall occurs when a cool short circuit arc stays in the center of a wide groove. Weaving and adequate voltage direct heat to the sidewalls.", "image": "question_images/q_323.png"}, {"id": 324, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A cross section diagram of a fillet shows a labeled defect along the toe. The inspector learns that the welder increased voltage to widen the bead, traveled quickly, and held the gun at a steep angle toward the vertical plate. What best explains the defect?", "options": {"A": "The weld pool cooled before it ever reached the edge of the plate surface", "B": "The arc melted the plate edge faster than filler could refill the groove", "C": "Moisture in the wire created gas pockets near the toe", "D": "The contact tip was too small and starved the arc of current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The arc melted the plate edge faster than filler could refill the groove): Excess voltage, fast travel, and an angle favoring one plate melt the base metal at the toe into a groove that filler does not fill, which is undercut. A pool that cooled early, wet wire or a small contact tip would produce other discontinuities rather than a groove melted along the toe.", "image": "question_images/q_324.png"}, {"id": 325, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding inspector rejects a GMAW fillet because a section cut from it shows gas pockets clustered near the surface. The welder insists the flow was set at 30 cubic feet per hour. Which finding would best confirm the real cause?", "options": {"A": "A contact tip with a bore sized to match the wire diameter in use", "B": "A stickout measured at three eighths of an inch from the contact tip", "C": "A cracked gas hose or loose fitting that lets air in past the meter", "D": "A travel angle held at ten degrees in the push direction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A cracked gas hose or loose fitting that lets air in past the meter): A flow meter reads gas leaving the regulator, not what reaches the nozzle. A leak downstream can admit air and form porosity even with a correct meter reading."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A procedure writer must choose between 75/25 and 90/10 argon carbon dioxide for a shop doing both thin sheet in short circuit and thick plate in spray. The shop wants one gas for both. Which compromise reflects how the mixes behave?", "options": {"A": "90/10 cannot run short circuit transfer on any thickness of steel plate or sheet", "B": "75/25 runs short circuit well but struggles to reach spray, while 90/10 reaches it", "C": "75/25 gives easy spray at low current on thin plate and sheet steel", "D": "Both blends reach spray at exactly the same current on all wires"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (75/25 runs short circuit well but struggles to reach spray, while 90/10 reaches it): The higher CO2 in 75/25 favors short circuit and globular behavior, while 90/10 allows spray above the transition current. One shop gas is a tradeoff, often 90/10 or a tri-mix."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is unhappy with spatter on 1/4 inch steel using 100 percent CO2 and wants to cut cleanup time without changing the machine. The inspector suggests a gas change. Which result is the most realistic after switching to 75/25 argon carbon dioxide?", "options": {"A": "Much more spatter and a hotter arc than straight carbon dioxide shielding gives", "B": "Less spatter and a smoother bead, with a bit less penetration than CO2", "C": "No change in spatter because gas does not affect transfer", "D": "Higher penetration and a narrow rope shaped bead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Less spatter and a smoother bead, with a bit less penetration than CO2): Adding argon smooths the arc and cuts spatter, though straight CO2 still gives slightly more penetration. This tradeoff is typical when moving to a blend."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new student asks why a constant voltage wire feed machine for GMAW does not provide a drooping output curve like the stick welding sets seen elsewhere in the lab. The instructor wants the main design reason given in the answer. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Constant voltage keeps amperage fixed no matter how fast wire is fed", "B": "Constant voltage allows the welder to change polarity during welding when needed", "C": "Constant voltage lets arc length self regulate as the wire feeds steadily", "D": "Constant voltage allows the welder to change polarity during welding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Constant voltage lets arc length self regulate as the wire feeds steadily): With a CV source, a change in stickout or arc length produces a large swing in current that quickly restores the set arc length, making the process self correcting for a steady feed. Constant voltage does not hold amperage fixed, and polarity is a setup choice rather than a design feature."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new procedure for 3/16 inch steel uses 0.035 inch ER70S-6, 75/25 gas, and short circuit transfer. During a qualification test the welder gets scattered burn-through and a few cold lap spots. Which diagnosis best fits both defects at once?", "options": {"A": "Gas contamination and moisture in the wire changing heat input", "B": "A consistently too long stickout that lowers amperage steadily across the whole test coupon", "C": "A consistent drag angle that keeps penetration shallow throughout", "D": "Uneven travel speed and gun angle swinging heat between high and low"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uneven travel speed and gun angle swinging heat between high and low): Burn-through and cold lap together point to uneven travel and angle: slowing in places overheats thin areas while speeding elsewhere leaves unfused toes. A steady technique corrects both."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using ER70S-6 and 75/25 gas gets good results on clean plate in the shop but gets porosity on a field job with the same settings. The foreman suspects the field cylinder is the problem since flow and wind seem acceptable. Which check best isolates the cause?", "options": {"A": "Raise the voltage two volts on the field job to see changes", "B": "Switch to a longer stickout on the shop job to compare the results", "C": "Lower the wire feed speed on the field job to compare", "D": "Weld a test bead in the field using the shop cylinder to compare"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weld a test bead in the field using the shop cylinder to compare): Swapping only the gas source on identical settings separates a bad cylinder or regulator from site conditions. Changing electrical settings would not tell the foreman anything about the gas."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new apprentice in the fabrication shop picks up a spool of flux cored wire for structural steel and reads the AWS A5.20 classification E71T-1C on the label. The first digit pair after the E tells the welder about the deposited weld metal. What does the 7 stand for?", "options": {"A": "Minimum yield strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi", "B": "Maximum recommended electrode diameter is 0.070 inch for the classification", "C": "Required Charpy impact test temperature is minus 70 degrees F for the deposit", "D": "Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi): The first digit in an A5.20 flux cored classification gives minimum tensile strength in units of 10,000 psi, so 7 means 70,000 psi. Yield is a separate, lower property for this wire, which is why the yield reading fails."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "During a shop orientation, an instructor holds up a spool marked E71T-1 and asks the group what the letter T means in the classification, since the same letter does not appear on stick electrodes or solid GMAW wire used in the same building. What does the T identify?", "options": {"A": "A tungsten alloyed wire intended for use on thin gauge sheet", "B": "A tubular electrode, meaning a hollow wire with a flux or metal powder core", "C": "A temperature rated electrode approved for low temperature service", "D": "A thin diameter solid wire drawn for short circuit transfer welding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A tubular electrode, meaning a hollow wire with a flux or metal powder core): In AWS A5.20 the T designates a tubular, flux cored electrode. Solid wires carry an S, which is the quickest way to tell the two families apart when reading a spool label."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a pipe rack crew is told to run self-shielded E71T-8 wire from a spool in the portable feeder. Before touching the trigger, the lead hand asks which polarity the machine leads should give for this type of wire. Which setting does the manufacturer normally call for?", "options": {"A": "Electrode positive, also called direct current electrode positive or DCEP", "B": "Alternating current with the high frequency control switched to continuous", "C": "Electrode negative, also called direct current electrode negative or DCEN", "D": "Electrode positive with the work lead moved to the negative terminal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Electrode negative, also called direct current electrode negative or DCEN): Most self-shielded flux cored wires, including E71T-8, are designed for DCEN because the deoxidizers and arc stabilizers in the core work best with the electrode negative. DCEP is the setting for gas-shielded E71T-1."}, {"id": 334, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A purchasing clerk is ordering wire for a plant that welds carbon steel plate and angle with flux cored electrodes and needs to match each wire to the correct AWS filler metal specification. Which specification covers carbon steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding such as E71T-1?", "options": {"A": "AWS A5.18, the specification for carbon steel solid wires and metal cored rods", "B": "AWS A5.1, the specification for carbon steel covered electrodes for stick welding", "C": "AWS A5.5, the specification for low alloy steel covered electrodes for stick welding", "D": "AWS A5.20, the specification for carbon steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (AWS A5.20, the specification for carbon steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding): A5.20 covers carbon steel flux cored wire. A5.18 covers solid and metal cored gas metal arc wire, and A5.1 and A5.5 are stick electrode specifications, so none of them applies to E71T-1."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A bridge repair job calls for weathering steel, and the engineer specifies an E81T1-Ni1 flux cored wire for the gas-shielded process. The shop buyer needs to look up the filler metal requirements. Which AWS specification covers low alloy steel flux cored electrodes of this type?", "options": {"A": "AWS A5.20, which is limited to carbon steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding", "B": "AWS A5.28, which covers solid and metal cored low alloy wire for gas shielded welding", "C": "AWS A5.29, which covers low alloy steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding", "D": "AWS A5.9, which covers bare stainless steel wire and strip for arc welding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (AWS A5.29, which covers low alloy steel electrodes for flux cored arc welding): A5.29 is the flux cored specification for low alloy steels, and Ni in the designator marks the nickel alloy. A5.20 is limited to carbon steel and A5.28 covers solid or metal cored wire, not flux cored."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator qualifies a gas-shielded FCAW procedure for E71T-1C wire on structural plate, and the welding supervisor writes the shielding gas on the procedure sheet. Which shielding gas is the standard choice for a wire carrying the C usage designator in its classification?", "options": {"A": "100 percent argon supplied from the cylinder through a flow meter", "B": "98 percent argon blended with 2 percent oxygen for spray transfer", "C": "100 percent carbon dioxide supplied from the cylinder through a flow meter", "D": "A helium and hydrogen mixture commonly used for stainless root passes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (100 percent carbon dioxide supplied from the cylinder through a flow meter): The C designator on an A5.20 wire means it was classified with 100 percent CO2. Pure argon gives an unstable arc with flux cored wire, and the other blends are meant for different processes or metals."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welding instructor sets up a flux cored machine for the class and explains that the power source must supply a certain output characteristic so the arc length stays steady while the wire feeder pushes the electrode forward. Which type of power source is normally used for FCAW?", "options": {"A": "A constant current drooping curve machine with a foot pedal that sets the amperage by hand", "B": "A transformer only machine with a fixed amperage tap and a stick lead", "C": "An engine driven machine with an inverter set to pulse for thin metal", "D": "A constant voltage power source paired with a wire feeder that sets the feed speed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A constant voltage power source paired with a wire feeder that sets the feed speed): FCAW uses a constant voltage supply so the arc length self-corrects as stickout varies. Constant current drooping machines are for stick and TIG, where the operator controls arc length by hand."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A trainee running E71T-1C wire on a constant voltage machine turns up the wire feed speed dial from 300 to 400 inches per minute while leaving the voltage knob alone. The instructor asks what the trainee should expect the machine to do. What is the main result of the higher feed speed?", "options": {"A": "Arc voltage rises because the machine lengthens the arc to match the wire", "B": "Welding amperage rises because the machine supplies more current to melt faster wire", "C": "Gas demand falls because the faster wire carries more flux into the pool", "D": "Arc voltage falls because the wire tip sits closer to the contact tip"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Welding amperage rises because the machine supplies more current to melt faster wire): On a constant voltage machine, wire feed speed sets amperage and the voltage dial sets arc length and bead profile. A faster feed demands more current to melt the wire, so amperage climbs."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A technician is replacing worn drive rolls on a wire feeder before a shift of flux cored welding with 0.045 inch tubular wire. The parts counter offers several roll styles, and the technician wants the one that grips the wire without flattening its thin sheath. Which drive roll style suits tubular wire?", "options": {"A": "Knurled V-groove rolls that grip the hollow sheath with moderate tension", "B": "Smooth U-groove rolls that cradle the wire on its full circumference to avoid marking", "C": "Smooth V-groove rolls that pinch the wire from two opposite sides", "D": "Flat faced rolls with a rubber coating to prevent surface scratches"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Knurled V-groove rolls that grip the hollow sheath with moderate tension): Knurled V-groove rolls give the grip a hollow flux cored wire needs without the heavy pressure that would crush it. Smooth U-groove rolls are used for soft solid wires such as aluminum."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop foreman writes a work instruction for gas-shielded FCAW on 1/2 inch plate with 0.045 inch E71T-1C wire. The sheet must state the typical electrode extension, measured from the contact tip to the work, that gives stable arc characteristics. What range does the instruction normally list?", "options": {"A": "About 1/4 inch to 3/8 inch of electrode extension from the contact tip", "B": "About 1-1/2 inch to 2 inches of electrode extension from the contact tip", "C": "About 3/4 inch to 1 inch of electrode extension from the contact tip", "D": "About 1/8 inch or less so the nozzle can rest on the plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 3/4 inch to 1 inch of electrode extension from the contact tip): Gas-shielded flux cored wire runs best with roughly 3/4 to 1 inch of stickout. Shorter extension overheats the tip, and a much longer one lowers current and weakens gas coverage."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "At the end of a shift a welder has half a spool of E71T-1C left on the feeder and asks the crib attendant how to protect it overnight in a shop that runs a humid summer climate with no dehumidifier. Which storage practice protects the wire from moisture pickup?", "options": {"A": "Leave the spool on the spindle with the cover open so air can dry the flux", "B": "Reseal the spool in its original plastic bag and store it in a dry area", "C": "Keep the spool in an unheated outdoor shed where air circulates freely", "D": "Wipe a light coat of oil on the wire so rust cannot form overnight"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reseal the spool in its original plastic bag and store it in a dry area): Flux in the core absorbs moisture from humid air, and that moisture later shows up as porosity and hydrogen in the weld. Resealing the spool in its bag in a dry room keeps the flux dry."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A crew finishes the first pass of a three pass fillet weld made with E71T-1C wire. The inspector walks up and asks what tools the welder will use to take the slag off the bead before the next pass, as FCAW leaves a glassy slag layer on the surface. What tools remove it?", "options": {"A": "A chipping hammer to break the slag off, followed by a wire brush", "B": "A flat file used along the bead, followed by a damp cloth", "C": "A propane torch to burn the slag away before the next pass", "D": "A gouging rod used to melt the slag loose from the toes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A chipping hammer to break the slag off, followed by a wire brush): FCAW slag is brittle and is knocked off with a chipping hammer, then the bead is brushed clean. Burning or filing it leaves residue that can be trapped by the next pass."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a windy erection site, a supervisor tells the crew to leave the gas cylinders on the truck because the wire they are using generates its own protective atmosphere from ingredients inside the core. Which flux cored process variant works this way, without an external shielding gas?", "options": {"A": "Self-shielded flux cored arc welding, called FCAW-S, which relies on core ingredients", "B": "Gas-shielded flux cored arc welding, called FCAW-G, with carbon dioxide gas", "C": "Gas metal arc welding with a solid wire fed through a nozzle with an argon blend", "D": "Shielded metal arc welding with a covered electrode and fast freeze flux"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Self-shielded flux cored arc welding, called FCAW-S, which relies on core ingredients): FCAW-S wire contains compounds that decompose in the arc to shield the pool, so no cylinder is needed. FCAW-G and GMAW depend on external gas that wind can blow away."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is setting up a feeder for E71T-1C wire to weld 3/8 inch structural plate in the flat position. The lead hand checks the machine and wants the gun cable connected to the correct terminal so the wire runs with the correct arc characteristics. How should the leads be connected?", "options": {"A": "Gun lead to the positive terminal, so the electrode runs on DCEP polarity", "B": "Gun lead to the negative terminal, so the electrode runs on DCEN polarity", "C": "Gun lead to the positive terminal with the machine switched to AC output", "D": "Gun lead on either terminal since both polarities give the same arc"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gun lead to the positive terminal, so the electrode runs on DCEP polarity): Gas-shielded E71T-1 is designed for DCEP, with the electrode positive, which gives a smooth spray-like arc and good penetration. Reversing polarity makes the arc rough and the weld shallower."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs E71T-8 self-shielded wire on a 1/2 inch plate groove but left the machine set to electrode positive from the previous job. The arc is erratic, spatter is heavy, and the bead is irregular. What correction should the welder make first?", "options": {"A": "Increase the voltage by several volts and slow the travel to stabilize the longer arc", "B": "Increase shielding gas flow to 50 cubic feet per hour at the nozzle", "C": "Shorten the stickout to 1/4 inch so the arc stays closer", "D": "Reverse the leads so the electrode is negative, as the wire maker specifies"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reverse the leads so the electrode is negative, as the wire maker specifies): Self-shielded wires like E71T-8 are formulated for DCEN, and running them on DCEP upsets the core chemistry and arc. Fixing polarity addresses the cause, while gas flow does nothing for a wire with no external shield."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a gas-shielded FCAW job needs to reach into a deep T-joint and lets the stickout grow to about 1-3/4 inches without changing the wire feed speed or voltage. The weld looks rough and the inspector later finds shallow fusion. What did the long stickout most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Lower welding current and shallower penetration, with a rougher arc and more spatter", "B": "Higher welding current and deeper penetration from extra resistance heating", "C": "No change in penetration because voltage alone determines the current", "D": "A narrower bead with improved gas coverage inside the deep joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower welding current and shallower penetration, with a rougher arc and more spatter): Extra stickout adds resistance in the wire, which at a fixed feed speed drops the current actually reaching the pool. Lower current gives shallower fusion, and the long extension also weakens gas coverage."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is making a horizontal fillet on 1/4 inch plate with E71T-1C wire. Slag keeps running ahead of the arc and gets trapped in the toe of the bead. The instructor looks at the gun position. Which change in travel angle best keeps the slag behind the pool?", "options": {"A": "Use a push angle of about 30 degrees so the arc washes slag forward", "B": "Use a drag angle of about 5 to 15 degrees to keep slag trailing", "C": "Hold the gun perpendicular and weave widely to flood slag toward the toes", "D": "Tilt the gun backward 45 degrees and slow down to build up the bead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a drag angle of about 5 to 15 degrees to keep slag trailing): A modest drag angle points the arc force toward the solidified weld and keeps slag behind the puddle. A push angle lets slag run ahead of the arc, where it is overrun and trapped."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is assigned a vertical-up fillet on 3/8 inch plate using 0.045 inch E71T-1C wire. The instructor reminds the group to adjust technique from the flat position settings. Which approach suits E71T-1 wire welded vertical-up? The plate is clean and the machine settings are already reduced.", "options": {"A": "Travel uphill at reduced current, using a stringer or small triangular weave", "B": "Travel downhill at flat position settings with a wide weave and long pauses at both toes", "C": "Travel uphill at the highest current settings with a wide arc and a fast motion", "D": "Travel uphill with the gun perpendicular and a pause at the center"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Travel uphill at reduced current, using a stringer or small triangular weave): Vertical-up work needs reduced current and a controlled motion so the puddle freezes before it sags. A small triangle or stringer keeps the pool small, and going uphill fills against gravity."}, {"id": 349, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a 3/4 inch thick groove weld, a welder finishes the root pass and starts the second pass right away after only a quick brush on the top of the bead. Two passes later, radiography shows dark elongated spots along the weld. What preparation step was skipped?", "options": {"A": "Full slag removal from the bead face and sidewalls with hammer and brush", "B": "A second shielding gas purge of the groove before each new pass", "C": "Preheating the plate to 400 degrees F and holding it there between each of the passes", "D": "Grinding the crown of each pass flush with the surface of the plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Full slag removal from the bead face and sidewalls with hammer and brush): Elongated dark spots on film point to slag trapped between passes. Slag on the bead face and in the sidewall corners must be fully removed, because the next arc may not melt it out."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using E71T-1C wire and a regulator set at 10 cubic feet per hour finds scattered porosity in several fillet welds. The cylinder holds plenty of CO2 and the nozzle looks clean. Which adjustment to the flow rate is most appropriate for this job?", "options": {"A": "Lower the flow to 5 cubic feet per hour to reduce turbulence at the nozzle", "B": "Raise the flow to roughly 35 to 50 cubic feet per hour at the regulator", "C": "Raise the flow to about 100 cubic feet per hour for extra coverage", "D": "Keep the flow at 10 and raise the voltage by 2 volts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the flow to roughly 35 to 50 cubic feet per hour at the regulator): Gas-shielded flux cored welding usually needs about 35 to 50 cfh to blanket the pool. Ten cfh leaves the arc exposed to air, which causes the scattered porosity seen in the welds."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder pulls a spool of E71T-1C from an open box that sat in a damp storage trailer for two weeks. The first few welds show clusters of worm-shaped holes on the surface of the bead. What is the most appropriate action for the welder to take?", "options": {"A": "Raise the wire feed speed and the voltage to burn off the moisture", "B": "Increase the shielding gas flow to 80 cubic feet per hour", "C": "Discard the exposed wire and load a fresh dry spool from sealed packaging", "D": "Switch the leads to DCEN so the arc runs hotter on the wire"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Discard the exposed wire and load a fresh dry spool from sealed packaging): Moisture in the flux core releases hydrogen and water vapor in the arc, producing worm-hole porosity. Extra gas or heat will not remove moisture that is already in the wire, so replacing it is the fix."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A crew is welding a handrail base outdoors with E71T-1C and a 10 mile per hour breeze. Porosity keeps appearing on the windward side of each weld even though the gas flow is set at 40 cubic feet per hour. Which action best addresses the problem?", "options": {"A": "Increase the gas flow well beyond 80 cubic feet per hour at the regulator", "B": "Erect a windscreen around the work or switch to a self-shielded wire such as E71T-8", "C": "Shorten the stickout to 1/4 inch to bring the nozzle nearer the work", "D": "Switch the leads to DCEN and keep using the same E71T-1C wire"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Erect a windscreen around the work or switch to a self-shielded wire such as E71T-8): Wind blows away the gas envelope around the arc, and more flow only adds turbulence. A windscreen restores coverage, and self-shielded wire does not depend on external gas."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A job plan calls for FCAW on structural steel in the vertical-up position, and the shop has four wire types in stock. A welder asks which one is limited to flat and horizontal welding positions and cannot be used for the vertical-up joints. Which wire has that limitation?", "options": {"A": "E71T-1C, a gas-shielded wire classified for use in all welding positions", "B": "E71T-8, a self-shielded wire classified for use in all welding positions", "C": "E71T-11, a self-shielded wire classified for use in all welding positions", "D": "E70T-4, a self-shielded wire classified for the flat and horizontal positions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (E70T-4, a self-shielded wire classified for the flat and horizontal positions): E70T-4 carries a 0 position designator, which limits it to flat and horizontal work, and the 4 identifies its self-shielded usage. The wires with a 1 position designator, including E71T-1, E71T-8 and E71T-11 as listed, are classified for all positions."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop that normally runs E71T-1C with CO2 changes to an E71T-1M wire to cut spatter and keeps using 100 percent CO2 gas from the old cylinder. An inspector asks what concern this combination raises. What is the main concern?", "options": {"A": "The arc will cut out because the M wire cannot carry welding current in CO2 gas", "B": "Weld chemistry and strength can fall outside the classification limits", "C": "The wire will feed erratically because the M designator marks a different diameter", "D": "The weld will pass inspection because all flux cored wire tolerates any gas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weld chemistry and strength can fall outside the classification limits): The M designator means the wire was classified with a mixed argon and CO2 gas. A more oxidizing gas such as pure CO2 burns out alloying elements, which can change strength and toughness from what was tested."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder raises the voltage four volts on a constant voltage machine while running E71T-1C on a 1/2 inch lap joint, hoping to flatten the bead. The weld becomes wide and flat but the toes look ragged and spatter increases. What happened?", "options": {"A": "The wire feed speed rose, giving a narrow tall bead with deeper penetration and less spatter", "B": "The stickout shortened, giving a smoother arc with less spatter at the toes", "C": "The arc lengthened, giving a wider flat bead, more spatter and some undercut risk", "D": "The slag thickened, giving a rounder bead with better fusion at the toes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The arc lengthened, giving a wider flat bead, more spatter and some undercut risk): Voltage controls arc length and bead profile. Too much voltage spreads the bead out, throws more spatter and can cut a groove along the toe where the arc is too long to fill it."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new welder runs a long fillet with E71T-1C at fast travel speed, trying to finish early. The bead is narrow and ropey and the inspector sees the toes are not wetting into the plate. What is the most likely result of traveling too fast?", "options": {"A": "Excess heat input at the toes, leading to a wide bead with deep penetration", "B": "Too little heat at the toes, leading to lack of fusion and a narrow bead", "C": "Extra slag coverage on the bead, leading to a smoother and flatter profile", "D": "Higher shielding gas pressure at the toes, leading to scattered surface porosity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Too little heat at the toes, leading to lack of fusion and a narrow bead): Moving too fast spreads the heat thin along the joint, so the bead piles up narrow and does not melt the plate edges. The toes then show poor wetting and can hide lack of fusion."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder notices porosity forming midway through a day of gas-shielded FCAW. Inspection of the gun shows spatter packed inside the gas nozzle, narrowing the opening around the contact tip. What action should the welder take? The wire is dry and the gas hose is new.", "options": {"A": "Raise the voltage to push the spatter out of the nozzle by arc force", "B": "Clean the spatter from the nozzle and check the contact tip", "C": "Leave the nozzle alone and raise the gas flow above 80 cubic feet per hour", "D": "Replace the wire with a larger diameter to blast through the buildup"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clean the spatter from the nozzle and check the contact tip): Spatter buildup chokes the gas stream and disturbs coverage, producing porosity. Cleaning the nozzle and checking the tip restores a smooth gas flow, which no change in settings can do."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder sets the gas flow at 40 cubic feet per hour but still finds porosity, and the nozzle is clean. The flow meter reads normal at the regulator. What should the welder inspect next in the gas delivery path?", "options": {"A": "The chipping hammer, for wear on its head that leaves slag in the bead", "B": "The hose and fittings for a leak that loses gas before the nozzle", "C": "The wire spool brake, for tension that crushes the flux in the core", "D": "The work clamp, for a poor ground that changes the polarity at the gun"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The hose and fittings for a leak that loses gas before the nozzle): A regulator reading only shows what leaves the cylinder. A cracked hose or loose fitting loses gas along the way, so less reaches the nozzle than the meter claims and porosity results."}, {"id": 359, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is laying a horizontal T-joint fillet with E71T-1C on 3/8 inch plate and wants good tie-in at both toes. The instructor stands behind the welder and checks the gun position. Which gun position is appropriate for this horizontal fillet?", "options": {"A": "About 90 degrees to the lower plate with a strong push angle of 30 degrees", "B": "About 20 degrees from the vertical plate with the gun held directly over the weld", "C": "About 45 degrees between the two plates with a slight drag angle along the joint", "D": "About 70 degrees to the upper plate with a strong drag angle of 40 degrees"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 45 degrees between the two plates with a slight drag angle along the joint): A 45 degree work angle splits the arc between the two plates so both toes fuse, and a slight drag angle keeps slag trailing. A steep push angle lets slag run ahead."}, {"id": 360, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder sees lack of fusion on the sidewall of a vertical-up fillet made with E71T-1C and a weave. Voltage is low and the welder moves quickly across the joint. Which change is most likely to improve fusion at the sides?", "options": {"A": "Lower the voltage and move faster across the joint to cool the puddle", "B": "Raise the voltage slightly and pause briefly at each toe before crossing the joint", "C": "Add a push angle of 30 degrees and lengthen the stickout to 1-1/2 inches", "D": "Replace the weave with a single straight pass at lower wire feed speed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the voltage slightly and pause briefly at each toe before crossing the joint): A short pause at each toe gives the arc time to melt the plate edge, and slightly more voltage widens the bead to reach it. Moving faster at a low setting leaves the sides unfused."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder stops each bead abruptly on a 3/8 inch plate and finds a star-shaped crack at the end of several beads. The inspector asks what technique prevents this. What should the welder do at the end of each bead?", "options": {"A": "Pause at the end of the bead to fill the crater before breaking the arc", "B": "Pull the gun away fast to chill the crater before slag forms over the bead", "C": "Raise the voltage to widen the crater before releasing the trigger", "D": "Increase the stickout during the last half inch to soften the arc"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pause at the end of the bead to fill the crater before breaking the arc): A shrinking, unfilled crater cools fast and cracks from the contraction. Pausing at the end lets the pool fill and the bead taper, and many machines have a crater fill control for this."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder needs to run vertical-up beads on 1/4 inch plate and the stock room has 0.045 inch and 1/16 inch E71T-1C wire. Which choice is better suited for the out of position welding and why? The joint will be inspected by visual and bend testing.", "options": {"A": "The 1/16 inch wire, because the larger diameter melts faster and resists sagging in vertical joints", "B": "The 1/16 inch wire, because the larger diameter requires less skill vertical-up", "C": "Either wire, because both run at the same current and puddle size", "D": "The 0.045 inch wire, because smaller diameters run lower current out of position"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The 0.045 inch wire, because smaller diameters run lower current out of position): Smaller flux cored wire runs at lower amperage, which keeps the puddle small enough to freeze before it sags in vertical or overhead positions. Larger wire carries higher current and produces a heavier pool."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder keeps getting wire jams and burn-back at the contact tip while running 0.045 inch flux cored wire. A check shows the tip installed has a 0.035 inch bore. What is the correct fix for the welder to apply?", "options": {"A": "Increase the drive roll pressure until the wire pushes through the tight tip bore", "B": "Reduce the stickout to 1/4 inch so the tip stays cooler", "C": "Install a contact tip with a bore matched to the 0.045 inch wire diameter", "D": "Switch the polarity so the tip carries less current to the wire"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Install a contact tip with a bore matched to the 0.045 inch wire diameter): A tip bore smaller than the wire makes the wire bind, overheat and fuse to the tip. A matched bore passes the wire smoothly and carries current without binding."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder prepares a joint on structural plate that has heavy oil, loose paint and surface rust within an inch of the edges, then welds it with E71T-1C and sees porosity along the toes. What step before welding would have prevented this?", "options": {"A": "Raising the shielding gas flow to burn the contaminants out of the joint", "B": "Switching to a larger wire diameter that tolerates dirty steel", "C": "Cleaning the oil, paint and rust from the joint area with a grinder or solvent", "D": "Bending the plate edges to make the groove shed the oil"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cleaning the oil, paint and rust from the joint area with a grinder or solvent): Oil, paint and rust break down in the arc and release gas that gets trapped as porosity. Cleaning them from the joint area removes the source, and additional gas cannot compensate for it."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder wants a flatter, wider bead on a groove cap pass with E71T-1C and has the wire feed speed already set for the proper amperage. The bead is currently tall and narrow. Which adjustment fits this goal? The crown is currently tall and narrow.", "options": {"A": "Raise the voltage a little to lengthen the arc and spread the bead wider", "B": "Lower the voltage a little to shorten the arc and spread the bead wider than before", "C": "Lower the wire feed speed a great deal to widen the bead at the toes", "D": "Lengthen the stickout to over 2 inches to flatten the crown"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the voltage a little to lengthen the arc and spread the bead wider): On a constant voltage machine the voltage dial sets arc length and profile, so a modest increase flattens and widens the bead while amperage stays tied to the feed speed. Lowering the voltage would shorten the arc and keep the bead tall, and much lower wire feed or very long stickout works against a wide flat bead."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder complains that the E71T-1C wire feeds erratically and the wire nests behind the drive rolls. The drive roll tension is cranked tight. The wire sheath looks flattened where it passes the rolls. What should the welder do? The spool is new.", "options": {"A": "Tighten the tension further so the wire grips firmly enough to feed", "B": "Back off the drive roll tension until the wire feeds without flattening the sheath", "C": "Replace the tubular wire with a solid wire at the same diameter", "D": "Raise the wire feed speed so the wire moves through before it flattens"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Back off the drive roll tension until the wire feeds without flattening the sheath): Hollow flux cored wire deforms under heavy roll pressure, which causes erratic feeding and wire nests. Lower tension that still grips the wire feeds it without crushing the core."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs self-shielded wire inside a steel tank with a small manway and no ventilation. FCAW-S produces more fume than many other processes. Under ANSI Z49.1, what is the proper way to control the hazard? A supervisor wants the exposure controlled.", "options": {"A": "Open the manway and keep working since the tank volume dilutes the fume", "B": "Use a face shield with dark lens, since it blocks the fume", "C": "Switch to straight argon shielding gas inside the tank to cut the fume", "D": "Provide local exhaust at the work and suitable respiratory protection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provide local exhaust at the work and suitable respiratory protection): Confined spaces need local exhaust ventilation and respiratory protection where fume exposure warrants it. A shield blocks light and spatter but not fume, and argon in a closed tank adds an asphyxiation hazard."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs E71T-8 on DCEN and compares the weld to a previous gas-shielded E71T-1C weld on DCEP. The self-shielded weld shows shallower penetration at the same amperage. What explains this difference? Both welds were made on 3/8 inch plate by the same welder with similar skill.", "options": {"A": "Electrode negative polarity puts more heat into the plate and less into the wire", "B": "Self-shielded wire has no flux, so the arc cannot reach into the joint", "C": "Self-shielded wire uses a lower travel speed, so heat spreads wider at the toes", "D": "Electrode negative polarity puts more heat into the wire and less into the plate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Electrode negative polarity puts more heat into the wire and less into the plate): With the electrode negative, more of the arc heat goes into melting the wire and less goes into the base metal, so penetration is shallower. The welder must watch fusion at the sidewalls."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs E71T-1C on 3/8 inch plate outdoors with the gas set at 45 cubic feet per hour. The cross-section diagram of a sample weld shows a defect marked on the bead. A fan from a nearby bay door was blowing across the joint. What is the most likely cause of the defect?", "options": {"A": "The gun was held at too long a drag angle for the plate thickness being welded", "B": "The gas shield was disturbed by the draft, letting air into the weld pool", "C": "The slag was left on the sidewall from a previous pass", "D": "The root opening was set too narrow for the wire diameter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The gas shield was disturbed by the draft, letting air into the weld pool): Gas pockets in the bead that appear in drafty conditions mean air reached the molten pool. A cross draft breaks up the gas envelope even with correct flow settings, so the fix is a windbreak.", "image": "question_images/q_369.png"}, {"id": 370, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder completes the second pass of a multipass groove weld with E71T-1C and sections the sample. The diagram shows a defect marked in the bead that a bend test would likely open up. The welder says the first pass was only lightly brushed before the second. What most likely caused the defect?", "options": {"A": "Slag left on the first bead was not fully removed before the next pass", "B": "Shielding gas flow was set too high, causing turbulence at the nozzle", "C": "The wire spool absorbed moisture while sitting in the damp crib", "D": "The contact tip bore was too large, so the wire wandered"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slag left on the first bead was not fully removed before the next pass): Dark non-metallic material trapped inside the bead comes from slag that was left between passes. Thorough chipping and brushing, with attention to the sidewall corners, prevents it.", "image": "question_images/q_370.png"}, {"id": 371, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld sample made with E71T-1C on 1/2 inch plate was cut for inspection, and the diagram shows a defect marked along the edge where the bead meets the plate. The welder ran fast at low voltage with a narrow bead. Which change would most likely prevent this defect?", "options": {"A": "Slow the travel speed and raise the voltage so the arc melts the plate edge", "B": "Lengthen the stickout to 1-1/2 inch to increase the heat reaching the plate edge", "C": "Switch to a smaller wire diameter to reduce the heat at the edge", "D": "Raise the shielding gas flow to blanket the plate edge from the air"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow the travel speed and raise the voltage so the arc melts the plate edge): A separation between bead and plate edge means the arc did not melt the base metal. Slower travel and a little more voltage deliver heat to the toe and wet it in.", "image": "question_images/q_371.png"}, {"id": 372, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a horizontal fillet with E71T-1C and sections a sample. The diagram shows a defect marked in the plate at the toe of the bead. The welder used high voltage and traveled quickly without pausing at the toe. What is the most likely cause of the defect?", "options": {"A": "Excessive voltage and fast travel melted the plate edge without filling the groove", "B": "Too much slag trailing in front of the arc along the weld toe", "C": "Moisture in the wire core releasing hydrogen at the plate edge", "D": "Too short a stickout, which overheated the contact tip and starved the edge of filler"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excessive voltage and fast travel melted the plate edge without filling the groove): A groove melted into the plate along the toe and left unfilled is caused by an arc that is too long or a travel that is too fast to fill the melted edge. Lower voltage or a brief toe pause corrects it.", "image": "question_images/q_372.png"}, {"id": 373, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An inspector reviewing a 3/4 inch groove weld made with gas-shielded E71T-1C finds scattered porosity near the start of every bead but none farther along the weld. The wire is dry and flow is 40 cubic feet per hour. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Moisture in the flux core was released only at the start of each bead", "B": "The stickout was too long at the end of each bead after it had settled", "C": "Shielding gas had not yet displaced the air at the nozzle when the arc was struck", "D": "Slag trapped near the end of the previous bead moved into the new puddle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shielding gas had not yet displaced the air at the nozzle when the arc was struck): Porosity concentrated at the arc start with sound weld farther on points to a short-lived gas problem at the start. Adding a short gas preflow or restarting over the previous bead clears the air first."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop swaps a shared feeder between E71T-1C and E71T-11 wire but the operator forgets to change leads. After the swap to E71T-11 the arc is rough, spatter is heavy, and porosity appears. Which finding best explains the symptoms? The rest of the machine settings were left unchanged.", "options": {"A": "The self-shielded wire is running on electrode positive instead of electrode negative", "B": "The self-shielded wire is running without shielding gas, which that wire needs to function", "C": "The self-shielded wire is running on a drooping curve power source", "D": "The self-shielded wire is running at too short a stickout for E71T-11"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The self-shielded wire is running on electrode positive instead of electrode negative): Self-shielded wires are balanced for DCEN. On DCEP the core chemistry works against the arc, so spatter, instability and gas defects follow. Checking polarity at the lug is the first step after any wire swap."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs vertical-up fillets with E71T-1C and a wide weave. Radiography shows slag trapped at the toes where the bead meets plate, and the inspector sees a faint groove along the edge. Which sequence best explains and prevents the defect?", "options": {"A": "Slag filled a toe groove cut by the weave, so pause and clean the groove", "B": "Slag formed from damp wire, so replace the spool and keep the weave unchanged", "C": "Slag formed from low gas flow, so double the flow and widen the weave further", "D": "Slag formed from a long stickout, so shorten it and ignore the toe groove"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slag filled a toe groove cut by the weave, so pause and clean the groove): A toe groove left by a weave without a pause becomes a pocket that holds slag as the next pass covers it. A brief pause at the toes fills the edge, and any groove should be cleaned out before the next pass."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A contractor must weld 2 inch thick plate in all positions at an exposed site with frequent gusts, and wants portable equipment without cylinders. Which choice best fits these conditions while still meeting AWS structural practice? The plate will be welded in the field next month.", "options": {"A": "A gas-shielded wire such as E71T-1C run with a higher flow rate and a wider nozzle", "B": "A self-shielded all-position wire such as E71T-8 under a qualified procedure", "C": "A flat and horizontal self-shielded wire such as E70T-4 in every position", "D": "Any flux cored wire, since the process rules do not depend on position"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A self-shielded all-position wire such as E71T-8 under a qualified procedure): Gusts defeat gas shielding, and the job needs all positions, so an all-position self-shielded wire fits. E70T-4 is limited to flat and horizontal, and any wire used still needs a qualified procedure."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator reports underbead cracking in 1 inch high-strength plate welded with E71T-1C. Records show the wire spool sat open for weeks in a humid bay. Which conclusion is most consistent with the evidence? The inspector sectioned two welds for macro etching and hardness.", "options": {"A": "Excess shielding gas dissolved in the weld metal and caused the cracking", "B": "Moisture in the core raised diffusible hydrogen in the weld metal", "C": "The wire polarity was reversed, putting too little heat into the plate", "D": "The stickout was too long, which lowered carbon in the heat affected zone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Moisture in the core raised diffusible hydrogen in the weld metal): Flux that picks up moisture releases hydrogen into the weld, and hydrogen cracking appears in thick, high-strength steel. Dry storage, dry wire and the procedure's preheat control the risk."}, {"id": 378, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop is considering replacing stick welding with E71T-1C on 3/4 inch vertical groove welds in a covered bay. Which statement best describes the expected outcome if the change is made properly? The bay has a roof and walls but a large door that stays open.", "options": {"A": "Lower deposition rate than stick welding but better wind tolerance for outdoor field work", "B": "Higher deposition and fewer stops, provided drafts cannot disturb the shielding gas", "C": "Equal deposition but no need for slag removal between passes", "D": "Higher deposition because the process works without protecting the gas envelope"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Higher deposition and fewer stops, provided drafts cannot disturb the shielding gas): Continuous wire gives faster deposition and fewer stops than stick electrodes, but gas shielding is easy to disturb. The bay must be sheltered from drafts for the benefits to hold."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder's gas-shielded FCAW wire feeds in surges, the arc flickers, and a bird nest forms behind the drive rolls. The liner is clean, the tip bore matches, and tension is tight. Which action best resolves the cause? The spool is new.", "options": {"A": "Increase tension and use smooth U-groove rolls to hold the wire firmly", "B": "Lower the voltage so the arc draws less wire into the pool at the tip", "C": "Reduce drive roll tension and use knurled rolls sized for the tubular wire", "D": "Switch to straight argon so the wire stops wandering at the tip"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce drive roll tension and use knurled rolls sized for the tubular wire): Surging with a nest behind the rolls means the wire is being crushed or slipping. Lower tension with knurled rolls grips the sheath without deforming it, and the other changes do not touch the feeding problem."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder sets the regulator to 90 cubic feet per hour on a gas-shielded FCAW job, reasoning that more gas must protect the weld better. Porosity worsens. What explains this result? The nozzle is clean and the wire is dry, and the gas hose has no cracks.", "options": {"A": "High flow cools the wire so the flux cannot melt properly inside the arc column", "B": "High flow raises the arc voltage so the wire burns back to the tip", "C": "High flow creates turbulence that pulls surrounding air into the weld pool", "D": "High flow dissolves in the weld pool and forms pores as it cools"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High flow creates turbulence that pulls surrounding air into the weld pool): Gas flow has an effective range. Above it, the stream becomes turbulent and mixes air into the shield, so porosity returns. Matching flow to nozzle size and conditions gives a laminar shield."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using E71T-1C on galvanized angle finds porosity throughout the joint even though the wire is dry and flow is 40 cubic feet per hour. Which cause and fix are the most consistent with the evidence? The joint was brushed lightly before the first pass.", "options": {"A": "Zinc coating absorbs shielding gas, so double the flow rate at the regulator and nozzle", "B": "Zinc coating increases arc voltage, so lower the voltage by several volts", "C": "Zinc coating melts into the wire, so switch to a larger wire diameter", "D": "Zinc coating vaporizes in the arc, so remove the coating from the joint area first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Zinc coating vaporizes in the arc, so remove the coating from the joint area first): Zinc boils well below the melting point of steel, and its vapor is trapped in the pool as porosity. Grinding the coating back from the joint removes the source and cuts fume exposure."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder passes a visual check on the final cap pass but fails radiography with slag lines in the root area of a multipass groove. The welder says each pass was brushed. Which practice most likely left the slag? The welder had trained for three years.", "options": {"A": "Brushing the bead face without chipping slag out of the sidewall corners", "B": "Brushing the bead too long, which pushed slag into the weld metal", "C": "Using too much gas, which blew slag from the surface into the pool", "D": "Chipping every pass too hard, which dislodged the shielding envelope"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brushing the bead face without chipping slag out of the sidewall corners): Slag sticks in the corners where the bead meets the sidewall, and brushing only the face skips it. Chipping and grinding those corners lets the next pass fuse over clean metal."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a winter morning draws wire and plate from a cold outdoor trailer and starts welding immediately in a heated shop. Porosity shows up for the first several welds and then fades. Which explanation fits the pattern? Plate and wire both felt cold to the touch.", "options": {"A": "The warm shop air raised the gas flow rate so turbulence faded as the room cooled", "B": "The regulator needed time before gas flow reached the nozzle at full rate", "C": "The slag from the previous shift had not been chipped off the joint", "D": "Condensation on the cold wire and plate released moisture until they warmed up"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Condensation on the cold wire and plate released moisture until they warmed up): Cold material in a warm room collects condensation, which breaks down in the arc and creates porosity. The effect fades once the plate and wire reach shop temperature, so let them warm and dry first."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder using CO2 from a single cylinder at a high flow rate sees the flow meter fall and then stop mid-weld, and the regulator is frosted. Porosity appears just before the gas stops. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "The regulator froze from rapid CO2 expansion, so use a heater or lower draw", "B": "The cylinder valve was opened too far, so release the valve a half turn", "C": "The nozzle diameter was too large, so install a smaller nozzle for the job", "D": "The wire spool was damp, so replace it and refill with fresh gas"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The regulator froze from rapid CO2 expansion, so use a heater or lower draw): CO2 expanding at high flow chills the regulator and can freeze it shut. A heated regulator or a lower flow manages this, and the sudden porosity is a result of lost shielding as it freezes."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a vertical-up 3/8 inch joint finds the bead sags and slag runs over the puddle. Settings are the same as for flat welding. Which combined change gives the best chance of a sound bead? The wire is dry and the gas is set correctly.", "options": {"A": "Raise current and voltage and use a wide weave with a downward travel to speed it", "B": "Keep current the same and add a strong push angle with a fast pace", "C": "Reduce current and voltage and use a stringer or small weave with an upward travel", "D": "Lower the gas flow to 15 cubic feet per hour and slow the travel speed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce current and voltage and use a stringer or small weave with an upward travel): Flat position settings run a puddle too large for vertical work, so it sags. Lowering current and voltage and keeping the pool small with an upward stringer lets each layer freeze in place."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on a horizontal fillet pushes the gun forward at a 30 degree push angle with E71T-1C. The weld has slag lines at the toes and an uneven profile. The same welder runs clean with a slight drag angle. What does this show?", "options": {"A": "A push angle lets slag run ahead of the arc, where it is overrun and trapped", "B": "A push angle raises the heat so the slag burns out of the weld metal", "C": "A push angle draws the gas away so the slag cannot form at the toes", "D": "A push angle shortens the stickout so the slag layer remains thin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A push angle lets slag run ahead of the arc, where it is overrun and trapped): With a push angle the slag floods ahead of the pool and the arc rolls over it, trapping it at the toes. A slight drag angle keeps the slag behind where it can freeze and peel."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder fails a root bend test on a V-groove with a narrow included angle, and the fracture shows unfused sidewall surfaces along the groove. The welder used straight stringers aimed down the center. Which correction is most appropriate? The test bar was cut from a 3/8 inch plate procedure coupon.", "options": {"A": "Widen the included angle or aim the arc at the sidewalls to fuse each face", "B": "Raise the gas flow to 80 cubic feet per hour so the gas heats the sidewalls", "C": "Lower the voltage so the arc stays shorter at the center of the joint", "D": "Use a larger diameter wire so the bead bridges to both sidewalls"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Widen the included angle or aim the arc at the sidewalls to fuse each face): A narrow groove keeps the arc from reaching the sidewalls, leaving them unfused. Opening the groove or directing the arc toward each face supplies heat where fusion is needed."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder finds a lack of fusion defect at the toe and thinks the machine settings are wrong. The written welding procedure lists voltage, wire feed and travel speed ranges. Which action best matches code practice? The procedure was written and qualified by the shop engineer last year.", "options": {"A": "Raise the settings beyond the listed ranges until the weld looks sound", "B": "Switch to a different wire from the stock room and keep welding", "C": "Lower the settings below the listed ranges until the defect stops forming", "D": "Stay in the procedure ranges and report the problem for review"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay in the procedure ranges and report the problem for review): A welder must work within the qualified procedure. If the ranges produce defects, the supervisor or welding engineer reviews and revises the procedure instead of the welder improvising outside it."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder's flux cored root pass shows a rough ropey bead with a lot of spatter, and the contact tip is nearly welded shut. The stickout is only about 3/8 inch. What does this combination suggest? The welder ran the root at 0.045 inch wire with CO2 shielding.", "options": {"A": "Too long a stickout cooled the wire and made the arc wander at the toes", "B": "Too high a gas flow blew the wire off course at the start of the arc", "C": "A short stickout overheated the tip, causing spatter and burnback", "D": "Too fast a travel speed cooled the tip so the wire stuck at the tip"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A short stickout overheated the tip, causing spatter and burnback): A short extension puts the tip close to the arc, so it overheats and the wire can fuse to it. A stickout around 3/4 inch gives more stable transfer and less spatter."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a vertical weld a welder uses a very wide weave with E71T-1C, pausing only at the middle. The sample shows poor sidewall fusion and trapped slag. Which statement best describes the technique problem? The inspector sectioned the weld and etched the cross-section for review.", "options": {"A": "The weave was too narrow and held heat too long at the center of the bead", "B": "The weave was correct but the gas flow was too low to protect the edges", "C": "The weave was correct but the wire diameter was too large for vertical work", "D": "The weave was too wide with no toe pauses, so edges stayed cold"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weave was too wide with no toe pauses, so edges stayed cold): Wide weaves without toe pauses let the edges cool before the arc returns, so they fuse poorly and trap slag. Narrower motion with a brief hold at each side corrects both defects."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator running FCAW on a thin gauge repair with E71T-11 on DCEN reports burn-through. The welder suspects the amperage is too high. Which adjustment is most consistent with the process characteristics? The plate is 1/8 inch thick and the welder just came off 1/2 inch work.", "options": {"A": "Raise the wire feed speed and travel slower to spread the heat evenly across the joint", "B": "Switch to DCEP so the arc puts more heat into the wire than the plate", "C": "Increase the stickout to about 2 inches so the arc stays hotter", "D": "Lower the wire feed speed and travel faster to cut heat input to the plate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower the wire feed speed and travel faster to cut heat input to the plate): Burn-through on thin plate comes from too much heat input. Lower feed speed means lower current, and faster travel gives the plate less time to absorb heat."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder makes a horizontal fillet with E71T-1C using a very slow travel speed and low voltage. The diagram of the cross-section shows a defect marked at one toe where metal sits on the plate surface. What is the most likely cause of this defect?", "options": {"A": "Travel was too fast and voltage too high, so the arc dug into the plate", "B": "Gas flow was too high, so turbulence carried air into the toe", "C": "Slow travel and low voltage let metal pile up over unfused plate", "D": "The wire was damp, so hydrogen escaped along the edge of the plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Slow travel and low voltage let metal pile up over unfused plate): Metal that rolls over the toe without bonding is caused by slow travel, low voltage or a poor gun angle that lets the puddle spill onto cold plate. Faster travel and correct angle prevent it.", "image": "question_images/q_392.png"}, {"id": 393, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder ends several E71T-1C beads on 3/8 inch plate by releasing the trigger abruptly. Cross-sections of the bead ends show a defect marked at the crater. Which practice would most likely prevent this defect on the next welds? The inspector sectioned the bead ends for macro etching.", "options": {"A": "Increase the shielding gas flow to 80 cubic feet per hour at the end", "B": "Tilt the gun to a strong push angle as the arc is released", "C": "Pause at the bead end to fill the crater before breaking the arc", "D": "Lengthen the stickout at the end so the arc softens before it stops"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pause at the bead end to fill the crater before breaking the arc): A crater cools fast and contracts, and an unfilled crater cracks from the stress. Pausing lets it fill and the crack-prone dip disappears, and a crater fill setting does the same automatically.", "image": "question_images/q_393.png"}, {"id": 394, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder opens a new spool of E71T-1C that was stored in a humid area without its bag. A sectioned sample shows a defect marked in the bead, elongated and running along the weld axis. Which cause best explains the elongated shape of the defect?", "options": {"A": "Slag from the last pass was pushed along the weld axis by arc force", "B": "The root opening was too tight, so the gas could not escape the pool", "C": "Moisture in the flux core released gas that rose through the freezing pool", "D": "The travel speed was too fast, so the arc cooled before the gas escaped"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Moisture in the flux core released gas that rose through the freezing pool): Gas from damp flux rises through the freezing weld and leaves elongated worm-shaped holes. Dry, sealed wire storage prevents them, and discarding exposed wire is the corrective step.", "image": "question_images/q_394.png"}, {"id": 395, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder fills a V-groove with E71T-1C using a root gap that is too tight and low current on the first pass. The diagram of the joint shows a defect marked at the bottom of the groove. Which change is most likely to correct it on the next joint?", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the stickout to over 2 inches to deliver heat to the root", "B": "Lower the voltage and travel faster so the root freezes before it can sag", "C": "Add extra shielding gas to blow the molten pool into the root", "D": "Open the root gap and raise the current so the arc reaches the root"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Open the root gap and raise the current so the arc reaches the root): A root left unfilled shows the arc did not reach the joint bottom. A wider gap, adequate current and a proper root face let the pool penetrate fully through the thickness.", "image": "question_images/q_395.png"}, {"id": 396, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An instructor sets up a gas tungsten arc welding station for first-year students who will practice on one-eighth inch mild steel coupons. Because the student holds the torch by hand and the arc length will drift, the instructor wants amperage to stay nearly steady. Which power source output characteristic is chosen for this process?", "options": {"A": "A constant-voltage flat curve that holds voltage steady while amperage swings widely with arc length changes", "B": "A constant-current drooping volt-ampere curve that holds amperage nearly steady as arc voltage changes", "C": "A rising volt-ampere curve that adds amperage whenever the arc length grows longer", "D": "A pulsed wire-feed output that regulates current by adjusting electrode feed speed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A constant-current drooping volt-ampere curve that holds amperage nearly steady as arc voltage changes): A drooping curve means a small hand movement changes voltage but barely changes current, which keeps the heat on the plate predictable. A flat constant-voltage curve is built for wire-fed processes, where arc length is self-regulated by wire speed."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student asks the instructor which polarity to set for gas tungsten arc welding of three-sixteenths inch mild steel plate with a thoriated electrode. The instructor explains that the choice decides where most of the arc heat lands, and the goal is deep penetration with the least heat loading on the tungsten. Which setting fits?", "options": {"A": "Direct current electrode positive, because a positive electrode places most arc heat into the plate", "B": "DCEN, which sends roughly seventy percent of the arc heat into the plate", "C": "Alternating current with maximum cleaning half cycle, since steel needs oxide removal before welding", "D": "Pulsed electrode positive at low amperage so the tungsten stays cool while the plate melts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (DCEN, which sends roughly seventy percent of the arc heat into the plate): With electrons leaving the tungsten and striking the work, most heat is released at the plate, which melts steel deeply and spares the electrode. Electrode positive reverses this and loads the tungsten with heat instead."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "While cleaning out a drawer, a student finds a tungsten electrode with a red band painted on one end and wants to know what it is. The shop keeps one type of tungsten that is commonly selected for direct current work on steel. Which description matches the red color code under the AWS classification system?", "options": {"A": "Two percent thoriated tungsten, classified EWTh-2, used mostly for direct current electrode negative welding on steel", "B": "Pure tungsten, classified EWP, used mostly for alternating current welding of aluminum and magnesium alloys", "C": "One and a half percent lanthanated tungsten, classified EWLa-1.5, marked red for both polarities", "D": "Zirconiated tungsten, classified EWZr-1, marked red and best suited to direct current steel welding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two percent thoriated tungsten, classified EWTh-2, used mostly for direct current electrode negative welding on steel): Red identifies two percent thoriated tungsten, a classic choice for DCEN steel work. Pure tungsten is marked green, lanthanated types use gold, black or blue, and zirconiated tungsten is brown."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is setting up a transformer-type machine to weld one-eighth inch 6061 aluminum on alternating current with a pure tungsten electrode, and asks how the tip should look after the arc has been running for a short time. The instructor wants the tip shape that gives a stable arc on this process. What is it?", "options": {"A": "A needle sharp point ground to about ten degrees to focus the arc", "B": "A tip split with a hacksaw so current spreads between two small contact points", "C": "A tip bent toward the filler rod so the arc leans into the leading edge", "D": "A rounded ball formed by the arc, which keeps the arc stable on AC"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A rounded ball formed by the arc, which keeps the arc stable on AC): On AC the electrode heats during the positive half cycle, so a pure tungsten tip forms a small ball and holds the arc steady. A needle point on pure tungsten tends to melt and spit, which makes the arc wander."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A new hire grinds a two percent thoriated tungsten on a bench grinder, rolling the electrode across the wheel so the scratches circle the tip. The lead welder stops the work and says the grinding direction itself is wrong. Which direction should tungsten be ground, and why does it matter?", "options": {"A": "Around the circumference, so the marks spread current evenly and widen the arc cone", "B": "Either direction, since the marks on the tip have no effect on arc starting", "C": "Straight into the wheel face only, so the tip stays perfectly flat and square", "D": "Lengthwise, so marks run along the electrode and keep the arc straight"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lengthwise, so marks run along the electrode and keep the arc straight): Marks that run along the length carry current toward the point, giving a steady, directed arc. Marks that circle the tip let the arc wander around the grooves and can make it start unpredictably."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A shop buys two percent thoriated tungsten for steel work, and the safety coordinator is writing a short procedure for sharpening it. The electrodes will be ground several times a day at the same bench. Which statement belongs in that procedure for this tungsten type?", "options": {"A": "A dedicated wheel with dust capture, since thoriated dust is mildly radioactive", "B": "Grind on the shared steel wheel because the dust matches ordinary mild steel dust", "C": "Dip the electrode in water after grinding to neutralize the radioactive coating", "D": "Wear no dust protection because only pure tungsten releases harmful particles when ground"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A dedicated wheel with dust capture, since thoriated dust is mildly radioactive): Thorium is a weak alpha emitter, and the real risk is inhaling grinding dust, so a dedicated wheel with local exhaust is the accepted practice. A shared wheel also spreads contamination to other metals."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student asks why a fabricator sometimes uses helium instead of straight argon when welding thick aluminum with gas tungsten arc welding. The lead welder explains it in terms of what each gas does to the arc. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Helium lowers arc voltage and heat input, which helps control burn-through on thin aluminum sheet", "B": "Helium is heavier than air and blankets the joint better than argon at low flow rates", "C": "Helium cleans the oxide layer faster than argon during every electrode positive half cycle", "D": "Helium raises arc voltage and heat input, which helps penetration on thick aluminum sections"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Helium raises arc voltage and heat input, which helps penetration on thick aluminum sections): Helium has higher ionization energy, so the arc runs hotter and at higher voltage. Argon is the heavier gas and is the one that supplies cleaning action on AC."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welding one-eighth inch mild steel with a three-eighths inch gas cup asks where to set the argon flowmeter. The torch is air cooled, the shop is draft free and there is no gas lens. Which setting is the typical starting point for this setup?", "options": {"A": "About sixty to eighty cubic feet per hour, which gives the widest possible shield", "B": "About fifteen to twenty cubic feet per hour, adjusted slightly for cup size and joint", "C": "About two to four cubic feet per hour, which saves gas on short welds", "D": "A flow in cubic feet per hour that equals the amperage on the machine dial"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About fifteen to twenty cubic feet per hour, adjusted slightly for cup size and joint): Fifteen to twenty cfh covers most standard cups without turbulence. Much higher flow stirs the shield and pulls in air, and a very low flow leaves the pool unprotected."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder gets pinholes in a one-eighth inch mild steel bead and decides to fix them by opening the argon flowmeter to forty-five cubic feet per hour. The pinholes get worse. The gas bottle is full and the regulator checks out. What best explains the result?", "options": {"A": "High flow chills the pool too fast, so higher amperage alone would have cured the pinholes", "B": "Argon moving at high flow ionizes and puts hydrogen into the puddle", "C": "Turbulence from very high flow pulls air into the shield, so flow should be reduced", "D": "The larger flow dilutes the shield unless a larger tungsten electrode is installed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Turbulence from very high flow pulls air into the shield, so flow should be reduced): A stream moving too fast stops being smooth and mixes with surrounding air, so oxygen and nitrogen reach the metal. Matching flow to cup size restores a laminar shield."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder switches on a gas tungsten arc welding machine that has a high-frequency start. The instructor explains that this feature lets the operator begin the arc without dragging the electrode on the plate. What is the main benefit of starting this way?", "options": {"A": "It raises amperage above the dial setting so the arc can burn through surface oxide", "B": "It replaces shielding gas during the first second of welding so preflow is not needed", "C": "It increases filler deposition rate by pre-melting the rod before it enters the pool", "D": "The arc starts without touching the work, so the tungsten and weld stay uncontaminated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The arc starts without touching the work, so the tungsten and weld stay uncontaminated): High-frequency voltage jumps the gap and starts current flow without contact, so no tungsten particles are left in the plate. Scratch starting can transfer tungsten and shorten electrode life."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A technician is welding aluminum on an older transformer-rectifier machine without square-wave output. The arc keeps going out at each current reversal, even though the preflow and cleaning are done correctly. Which high-frequency setting is the proper choice for that machine on alternating current?", "options": {"A": "High frequency off after starting, since the arc relights on its own with no help", "B": "Continuous high frequency, which helps the arc relight at every current reversal", "C": "Start-only high frequency, which only fires while the trigger is first pressed", "D": "Continuous high frequency with the gas turned off, so the arc does not cool"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continuous high frequency, which helps the arc relight at every current reversal): A sine-wave AC arc passes through zero twice per cycle and may extinguish, so continuous high frequency keeps it relighting. Newer inverters with square-wave output rely much less on it."}, {"id": 407, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student needs filler rod for gas tungsten arc welding of one-eighth inch mild steel. The shop stock rack holds several wires, and the student must pick the one that matches carbon steel and is classified in the correct AWS specification. Which should be chosen?", "options": {"A": "ER70S-2, a solid bare wire covered by AWS A5.18 for carbon steel", "B": "ER4043, a solid wire covered by AWS A5.10 with high silicon content", "C": "E7018, a flux coated stick electrode covered by AWS A5.1", "D": "ER5356, a solid wire covered by AWS A5.10 with magnesium for strength"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ER70S-2, a solid bare wire covered by AWS A5.18 for carbon steel): AWS A5.18 covers carbon steel solid wires and rods, and ER70S-2 is a common GTAW choice because of its deoxidizers. The ER4043 and ER5356 wires belong to A5.10 and are aluminum."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is sorting aluminum filler rods in the stock room. Two bins hold similar looking rods, and the instructor says one of them is the five percent silicon type used for fluidity and crack resistance on 6061. Which classification does that description match under AWS A5.10?", "options": {"A": "ER5356, which has about five percent silicon as its main alloying element", "B": "ER4043, which has about five percent silicon as its main alloying element", "C": "ER70S-2, which has about five percent silicon as its main alloying element", "D": "ER1100, which has about five percent silicon as its main alloying element"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (ER4043, which has about five percent silicon as its main alloying element): The 4xxx series is aluminum-silicon, giving 4043 its good flow and reduced hot cracking. The 5356 rod is aluminum-magnesium, 1100 is nearly pure aluminum, and ER70S-2 is a steel wire."}, {"id": 409, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is running a bead on one-eighth inch steel and holds the torch straight up over the joint while feeding rod from the side. The instructor says the arc coverage and visibility could improve. Which torch travel angle is typically recommended for GTAW on plate?", "options": {"A": "A drag angle of forty-five degrees, with the torch trailing behind the molten pool", "B": "A ninety degree angle with no travel tilt, so the arc stays directly over the root", "C": "A push angle of about ten to fifteen degrees, leaning toward the direction of travel", "D": "A push angle of sixty degrees, so the cup almost touches the plate surface"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A push angle of about ten to fifteen degrees, leaning toward the direction of travel): A small push angle sends shielding gas over the metal still to be welded and keeps the electrode pointed at the pool's leading edge. Dragging or steep angles disturb the gas and reduce control of the rod."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student feeds a filler rod by dropping it high through the center of the arc, and the rod end glows and sputters. The instructor demonstrates the correct way to add rod to the pool on a flat butt joint. How should the rod be fed?", "options": {"A": "Drop it through the arc center at a steep angle so it melts first", "B": "Feed it from behind the pool, so the trailing edge freezes with a filled crater", "C": "Hold it two inches off the pool and let droplets fall in from a distance", "D": "Touch it to the pool's leading edge at a low angle, hot end inside the shield"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Touch it to the pool's leading edge at a low angle, hot end inside the shield): Feeding at the pool's leading edge at about fifteen degrees to the plate keeps the rod melting in the pool, not in the arc, and the hot tip stays shielded. Dropping rod through the arc spatters and oxidizes it."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A torch kit includes a gas lens collet body, and the welder asks what it does that a standard collet body does not. The instructor explains how shielding gas flows through it. What is the main advantage of a gas lens?", "options": {"A": "It adds a second shielding gas, helium, automatically to every arc without any added hardware", "B": "Screens smooth gas flow, allowing longer tungsten extension and access to tight joints", "C": "It raises arc voltage so thicker plate can be welded with a smaller power source", "D": "It magnifies the weld pool so the welder can see the molten puddle better"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Screens smooth gas flow, allowing longer tungsten extension and access to tight joints): Fine mesh screens in the lens remove turbulence, so the shield stays coherent farther from the cup. That lets the welder extend the tungsten to reach inside corners without losing protection."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "While adding filler rod to a structural joint on mild steel, a welder accidentally dips the tungsten electrode into the molten pool, and the arc immediately turns rough and erratic. The weld will be inspected by radiography later. What should the welder do next before continuing the joint?", "options": {"A": "Keep welding and let the arc burn the contamination off the tip over the next inch", "B": "Raise amperage to vaporize the fouled end, then drop it back after a few seconds", "C": "Stop, grind off the fouled tip, and clean the weld area", "D": "Wipe the hot tip with a rag and restart on the same spot of the bead"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop, grind off the fouled tip, and clean the weld area): Tungsten dipped in the pool picks up metal and leaves particles in the weld. The tip must be broken or ground back to clean metal, and the weld grinding removes any trapped tungsten before welding resumes."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "After welding a mild steel joint, an inspector finds bright dense flecks inside the bead on the radiograph. The welder admits the tungsten electrode was dipped into the pool twice and the work simply continued each time. Which statement best explains both the defect and the unstable arc that followed those dips?", "options": {"A": "Tungsten is trapped in the weld, and the fouled tip makes the arc wander", "B": "Slag from a rod coating is trapped inside, and a long arc makes it wander", "C": "Hydrogen pores formed from moist rod coating, and a cold plate makes the arc wander", "D": "Excess silicon in the filler formed hard spots, and a heavy tip makes the arc wander"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tungsten is trapped in the weld, and the fouled tip makes the arc wander): A dipped electrode leaves tungsten inclusions that show as dense flecks on radiographs, and the damaged tip disperses the arc. GTAW rod has no flux coating, so slag cannot be the source."}, {"id": 414, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is finishing a bead on one-eighth inch mild steel using a foot pedal. At the end of the joint the pedal is released at full amperage and the arc is broken at once, and a small crack appears in the crater afterward. How should the end of the weld be finished to prevent that crack?", "options": {"A": "Taper current gradually with the pedal while adding rod to fill the crater", "B": "Snap the arc off at full amperage so the crater freezes uniformly with a flat surface", "C": "Pull the torch away quickly while keeping current constant until the arc goes out", "D": "Raise current at the end to melt the crater smooth, then release the pedal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Taper current gradually with the pedal while adding rod to fill the crater): Tapering current while feeding a little rod fills the crater and slows cooling, which reduces shrinkage cracking. Stopping abruptly leaves a deep, hot crater that contracts and cracks."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student releases the pedal and lifts the torch straight away, and the end of the tungsten turns a blue-gray color with a dull surface. The instructor says the post-flow timer was set too short for this amperage. What is the purpose of post-flow shielding gas on a GTAW machine?", "options": {"A": "It protects hot tungsten and cooling weld metal from oxidation after the arc stops", "B": "It purges moisture from the filler rod so the wire is dry before the next weld", "C": "It preheats the base metal near the joint so the next pass tie-in is better", "D": "It lets the preflow time be set to zero so gas is saved at startup"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It protects hot tungsten and cooling weld metal from oxidation after the arc stops): Both the electrode and the last of the weld metal are still hot after the arc stops and oxidize quickly in air. Post-flow keeps argon over them until they cool down."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welding one-eighth inch mild steel holds an arc nearly three times as long as the tungsten diameter, and the bead comes out wide with undercut at the toes and a dull gray coating on the surface. The instructor wants the shielding and bead shape to improve. What change should be made?", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the arc further so heat spreads over a wider area and reduces undercut", "B": "Keep the arc length and use a smaller cup that holds gas near the plate", "C": "Hold the same arc length and use a thinner filler rod than before", "D": "Shorten the arc to about one tungsten diameter to narrow the bead"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shorten the arc to about one tungsten diameter to narrow the bead): A long arc widens the heat zone and lets air reach the pool, producing undercut and oxidation. Keeping the arc near one electrode diameter in length concentrates heat and keeps the shield effective."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder on an inverter machine is welding one-eighth inch aluminum on AC with the balance control left at the factory default. The bead shows black oxide specks and the etched cleaning band beside the weld is very narrow. Which adjustment is the best next step, and what is the tradeoff?", "options": {"A": "Raise the electrode negative percentage to widen cleaning action, which also deepens penetration", "B": "Reduce the electrode negative percentage to widen cleaning action, accepting shallower penetration", "C": "Reduce the frequency setting to its minimum so the cleaning band widens without other change", "D": "Change to direct current electrode negative because it cleans more aggressively than alternating current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the electrode negative percentage to widen cleaning action, accepting shallower penetration): More electrode positive time gives more argon-ion bombardment of the oxide, which widens the cleaning band but puts more heat in the tungsten and less in the plate. Raising electrode negative does the opposite."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student is learning why aluminum can be welded on alternating current but is much harder on direct current electrode negative. The instructor says a surface film gets removed during one half of the AC cycle, and asks the class which half of the cycle carries out that cleaning job and how it works.", "options": {"A": "The electrode negative half cycle, when most arc heat is delivered into the workpiece", "B": "The zero crossing only, when current momentarily stops and the oxide cracks from cooling", "C": "Only during preflow, when the argon first strikes the plate before the arc begins", "D": "The electrode positive half cycle, when argon ions strike and break up the oxide layer"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The electrode positive half cycle, when argon ions strike and break up the oxide layer): Positive argon ions are driven onto the work during the electrode positive half cycle and knock the oxide loose. The electrode negative half cycle provides the penetration."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder asks why aluminum is harder to melt cleanly than its low melting point suggests. The base metal softens near twelve hundred degrees F, yet a surface film keeps its shape on top of the pool and blocks fusion. Which explanation best accounts for this behavior in the weld?", "options": {"A": "The oxide melts below the base metal and floats off automatically once the plate softens", "B": "The oxide melts at the same temperature as the plate, so cleaning is not needed first", "C": "The oxide melts near twelve hundred degrees F but is denser and sinks into the pool", "D": "The oxide melts near thirty-seven hundred degrees F, so it must be broken up or removed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The oxide melts near thirty-seven hundred degrees F, so it must be broken up or removed): Aluminum oxide has a melting point far above aluminum itself, so it stays solid on top of a liquid pool. AC cleaning action and mechanical cleaning are what get rid of it."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student must weld a one-eighth inch 6061 aluminum plate that has been sitting in the shop for several weeks and shows a dull gray film and some fingerprints on the joint edges. The welding will be done on AC with an argon shield. What is the best cleaning method before welding?", "options": {"A": "Scrub with a carbon steel wire wheel also used on mild steel, then weld at once", "B": "Wipe with a lightly oiled rag to stop the oxide from returning before welding", "C": "Skip cleaning, since AC cleaning action removes every contaminant, including oils and grease", "D": "Degrease with solvent, then use a dedicated stainless brush just before welding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Degrease with solvent, then use a dedicated stainless brush just before welding): Solvent removes oil and fingerprints, and a stainless brush used only on aluminum removes oxide without embedding steel particles. Oxide regrows quickly, so brushing is done just before welding."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder extends the tungsten nearly an inch past the gas cup in a standard collet body to reach into a corner joint on mild steel, and the bead turns gray with pinholes. Without changing the flow rate or the shielding gas, what is the best correction for this problem?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the extension or use a gas lens, since long reach lets air in", "B": "Keep the long extension because argon collects in the gap around the exposed tungsten", "C": "Ignore extension length, as it affects arc visibility only and has no effect on shielding", "D": "Lengthen the extension further, since a longer electrode cools better and carries more current"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shorten the extension or use a gas lens, since long reach lets air in): The farther the tungsten sticks out of a standard cup, the farther the shield is from the cup and the more easily air enters. A gas lens maintains a coherent stream to a longer extension."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student asks how amperage is changed during a weld when both hands are busy, one holding the torch and the other feeding the filler rod. The instructor points to a control on the floor beside the machine. Which device is used for this purpose in gas tungsten arc welding?", "options": {"A": "A foot pedal or fingertip control that varies amperage during welding", "B": "A wire feed speed knob on the feeder that changes current through speed", "C": "A flowmeter on the gas regulator that adjusts amperage through gas pressure", "D": "A contact tip in the torch that sets amperage by its bore size"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A foot pedal or fingertip control that varies amperage during welding): A remote pedal or fingertip control lets the welder ramp heat up at the start and down at the end while both hands stay on the work. GTAW has no wire feeder, and gas flow does not set current."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is running two hundred and fifty amperes for long periods on thick aluminum plate using an air-cooled torch, and the handle and cable become too hot to hold safely. The shop has both torch types in the tool crib. What is the best solution to this overheating problem?", "options": {"A": "Triple the argon flow rate so the gas carries the added heat out of the handle", "B": "Use a water-cooled torch; air-cooled types top out near two hundred amperes", "C": "Install a smaller cup so the torch body receives less heat from the arc", "D": "Use a longer arc to move the heat source farther from the torch body"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a water-cooled torch; air-cooled types top out near two hundred amperes): Air-cooled torches rely on shielding gas and body mass for cooling and are rated for lower duty. Water circulation removes far more heat for sustained high current, and extra gas flow causes turbulence."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student accidentally sets the machine to direct current electrode positive while welding a mild steel plate at a normal steel amperage. Within seconds the tungsten tip melts into a ball and a bead of tungsten drops into the pool. What caused this to happen so quickly?", "options": {"A": "Positive polarity places too little heat on the tungsten, so it spits from poor current flow", "B": "Electrode positive loads most arc heat onto the tungsten, which melts at that amperage", "C": "Positive polarity pushes the filler away from the pool and into the electrode", "D": "Positive polarity draws shielding gas ions away from the tip so it oxidizes quickly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Electrode positive loads most arc heat onto the tungsten, which melts at that amperage): Electrons striking the tungsten carry most of the heat to it, so a small electrode can overheat quickly. That is why DCEP is rarely used in GTAW, and only with large electrodes at low current."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder wants an electrode that starts easily at low amperage on thin steel tubing and does not contain thorium. The supplier suggests an gray banded tungsten and the welder asks what it is. Which statement describes this electrode and its main advantage in this kind of work?", "options": {"A": "Pure tungsten, classified EWP, noted for easy low-amperage starts on direct current", "B": "Two percent thoriated tungsten, classified EWTh-2, noted for containing no radioactive element", "C": "Zirconiated tungsten, classified EWZr-1, noted for starting best on direct current steel", "D": "Two percent ceriated tungsten, EWCe-2, noted for easy low-amperage starts on direct current"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Two percent ceriated tungsten, EWCe-2, noted for easy low-amperage starts on direct current): Ceriated tungsten arcs readily at low current and does not contain thorium, which is why it suits thin work. Pure tungsten is green and aimed at AC, and thoriated tungsten is red."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator wants one electrode type that runs well on both direct current steel and alternating current aluminum, and wants to avoid thorium in the shop for safety reasons. The supplier lists several tungsten classifications in the catalog. Which one fits that requirement best for a mixed-metal shop?", "options": {"A": "Pure tungsten, classified EWP, since it carries direct current better than any alloyed type", "B": "Lanthanated tungsten, EWLa-1.5, which runs on AC or DC and has no thorium", "C": "Two percent thoriated tungsten, because it contains no radioactive additions", "D": "Zirconiated tungsten, classified EWZr-1, because it is designed only for direct current steel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lanthanated tungsten, EWLa-1.5, which runs on AC or DC and has no thorium): Lanthanated tungsten holds a stable tip on either polarity and avoids radioactive additives. Thoriated types contain thorium, and zirconiated tungsten is best on AC."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A structural frame made from 5052 aluminum will be anodized after welding, and the customer expects the weld color to blend with the base metal. One welder proposes ER4043 filler and another proposes ER5356. Which filler is the better choice, and what is the reason for choosing it?", "options": {"A": "ER4043, because its silicon turns white after anodizing and matches the base metal", "B": "ER4043, because silicon gives the strongest joint in any aluminum alloy", "C": "ER70S-2, because steel filler gives the best anodized finish on aluminum", "D": "ER5356, because magnesium gives more strength and a closer anodized color"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (ER5356, because magnesium gives more strength and a closer anodized color): Silicon in 4043 turns dark gray when anodized, which contrasts with a 5052 base. A 5356 filler uses magnesium that matches 5052 and anodizes close to the surrounding metal."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "An apprentice uses ER4043 aluminum rod to weld a mild steel bracket on a repair job, and after cooling the joint peels apart with almost no force. The welder says the heat and shielding gas settings were correct for the arc. Which explanation is correct for why the joint failed?", "options": {"A": "Aluminum rod needs more silicon to fuse to steel, so a hotter arc would have worked", "B": "The joint failed because aluminum filler requires alternating current for a steel base", "C": "Aluminum filler will not fuse sound to steel, so a steel rod is needed", "D": "Steel needs a helium preflow before aluminum filler will alloy with it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aluminum filler will not fuse sound to steel, so a steel rod is needed): Steel and aluminum form brittle compounds and do not fuse into a usable weld, so filler must match the base metal. Changing heat, current type or gas does not change this."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a mild steel joint the welder gets gas porosity only in the first inch of every bead, and the rest of the bead is clean and sound. The cup is clean, the rod is dry and the flow rate matches the cup size. Which cause is most likely for the porosity at the start of each bead?", "options": {"A": "Excess post-flow from the last weld left moisture in the cup", "B": "Contaminated rod, which would spread the pores along the entire bead", "C": "Insufficient preflow, so air was still in the cup and hose when the arc started", "D": "An overly large tungsten diameter that causes porosity only at the start"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insufficient preflow, so air was still in the cup and hose when the arc started): Porosity that clears up after the start means air was in the torch when the arc began. Setting preflow so argon fills the cup before arcing fixes it."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder places a pedestal fan directly across the joint to stay cool while welding mild steel, with the argon flow set correctly for the cup, and porosity appears in the bead. The base metal is clean and the hose is sound. How should the problem be fixed on this setup?", "options": {"A": "Double the argon flow rate so the stronger stream overpowers the draft", "B": "Shield the joint from the draft, since a crosswind strips the argon away", "C": "Change to helium, since a lighter gas stays on the joint in a draft", "D": "Lower the amperage so the pool does not give off as much vapor"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shield the joint from the draft, since a crosswind strips the argon away): A moving air stream strips away the thin argon blanket even at normal flow. Windscreens fix the cause, and raising flow adds turbulence that also draws air in."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A purchasing clerk orders shielding gas for the GTAW shop, and the supplier offers several cylinders at different prices. The shop will weld mild steel and stainless steel with tungsten electrodes under inspection. Which gas should be bought for general GTAW work on those metals in this shop?", "options": {"A": "Welding-grade argon of about ninety-nine point nine nine percent purity or better", "B": "Shop compressed air, which is mostly nitrogen and cheaper than welding gas", "C": "One hundred percent carbon dioxide, which gives the deepest penetration on steel", "D": "Argon with twenty-five percent carbon dioxide, as used for short-circuit wire welding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Welding-grade argon of about ninety-nine point nine nine percent purity or better): Pure, dry argon protects tungsten and weld metal. Carbon dioxide and oxygen-bearing mixes attack the tungsten and are made for wire welding."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student removes the helmet while running a low-current GTAW bead on thin mild steel, because there is no visible spatter or smoke coming from the weld. A coworker objects loudly and tells the student to put the helmet back on. What is the best response, based on ANSI Z49.1 practice?", "options": {"A": "Arc light is still intense ultraviolet, so a proper shade lens stays on", "B": "Low amperage arcs give off no harmful light, so clear safety glasses are enough", "C": "Only the filler rod gives off harmful light, so the lens may stay raised", "D": "Spatter is the only eye hazard from this process, so no lens is required"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arc light is still intense ultraviolet, so a proper shade lens stays on): Lack of spatter does not mean lack of radiation. Arc flash burns eyes and skin, and ANSI Z49.1 calls for the right shade lens whenever the arc is struck."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder lengthens the arc by one-sixteenth inch while welding mild steel on a drooping constant-current machine, and a coworker asks what happens to voltage, amperage and heat input as a result. Which statement describes the change that occurs when the arc length increases on this kind of power source?", "options": {"A": "Voltage stays fixed while current rises sharply, which heats the plate much more", "B": "Both voltage and current fall sharply, which cools the pool and stops the weld", "C": "Current falls to zero because the machine cannot adjust for a longer arc", "D": "Voltage rises while current barely changes, so heat input stays similar"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Voltage rises while current barely changes, so heat input stays similar): A drooping curve holds amperage nearly steady, so longer arcs mostly raise voltage. A small current change means the heat input remains reasonably predictable."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder uses a one-sixteenth inch tungsten at high amperage on three-eighths inch mild steel plate, and the tip melts back and spits particles into the pool. The shop stocks several electrode diameters in the same classification. Which change should be made to stop the tip from melting like this?", "options": {"A": "Move to a smaller tungsten, because less diameter carries current more efficiently", "B": "Use a larger tungsten, since the small one is overloaded at that current", "C": "Increase the arc length so heat moves farther away from the tip", "D": "Switch to direct current electrode positive to cool the tungsten tip"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a larger tungsten, since the small one is overloaded at that current): Each tungsten diameter has a current range, and exceeding it melts the tip. A larger electrode handles heat without melting."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student prepares tungsten for direct current work on mild steel and leaves the electrode as a blunt stub with no taper. The instructor wants the tip ground for a stable, focused arc at normal amperage. Which tip preparation is recommended for direct current electrode negative welding?", "options": {"A": "Grind a taper about two to three times the tungsten diameter, leaving a small flat", "B": "Grind a taper only half the tungsten diameter, so the tip remains thick", "C": "Grind a needle ten times longer than the diameter, ending in a sharp point", "D": "Leave the tip as cut, since grinding is optional for direct current work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Grind a taper about two to three times the tungsten diameter, leaving a small flat): A taper in the range of two to three diameters gives a stable focused arc, and a small flat prevents the point from melting away. A needle point breaks off easily, and a blunt tip gives a wandering arc."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student asks how the arc heat is divided between the electrode and the workpiece when welding on direct current electrode negative, because the instructor said that this polarity protects the tungsten. Which statement correctly describes how the heat is divided in the arc for this polarity?", "options": {"A": "About seventy percent of heat enters the work and thirty percent the electrode", "B": "About thirty percent of heat enters the work and about seventy percent the electrode", "C": "Heat divides equally, fifty percent at the work and fifty at the electrode", "D": "All arc heat stays in the electrode, with none reaching the plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About seventy percent of heat enters the work and thirty percent the electrode): Electrons leaving the tungsten deposit most energy where they land, which is the workpiece. That is why DCEN gives deep penetration and a cooler electrode."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A fabricator welds one-sixteenth inch stainless steel sheet and sees heavy warping and discoloration with steady current at the setting that gave good fusion. A coworker suggests using pulsed current on the machine instead. What benefit does pulsing provide on thin sheet like this?", "options": {"A": "It keeps the pool fully molten throughout the weld, which prevents gas from being trapped", "B": "It feeds filler into the arc by pulsing wire, which fills thin joints", "C": "It raises average heat input, so the weld finishes faster and the plate cools sooner", "D": "The pool partly freezes between peaks, so average heat and distortion drop"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The pool partly freezes between peaks, so average heat and distortion drop): Peak current melts, and the lower background current lets the pool shrink and cool, so thin metal receives less total heat. Pulsing does not feed wire."}, {"id": 438, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "On a stainless steel pipe root pass, the inside of the weld shows a gray crystalline sugared surface while the outside of the weld looks bright and clean. The outside shielding gas flow was normal and the filler was clean. What caused the condition on the inside of the pipe?", "options": {"A": "Excess shielding on the outside blew gas into the root and cooled the metal", "B": "The root had no argon back purge, so air oxidized the inside of the joint", "C": "Contaminated filler wire on the outside leaked into the root of the weld", "D": "The tungsten diameter was too large, so the root cooled too slowly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The root had no argon back purge, so air oxidized the inside of the joint): Sugaring is oxidation on the root side when air reaches hot stainless steel. Filling the pipe with argon keeps that surface protected."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A weld on one-eighth inch mild steel has a narrow, stringy bead with poor tie-in at the toes and widely spaced ripples. The amperage and gas flow are correct for the joint and the tungsten is properly ground. What should the welder change to correct this bead shape?", "options": {"A": "Speed up travel even more so the pool has less time to lose heat", "B": "Slow the travel speed, so the pool wets both edges before the torch moves ahead", "C": "Switch to a longer arc so the heat spreads wider beyond the joint", "D": "Reduce the cup size so the gas holds the pool in a narrower shape"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow the travel speed, so the pool wets both edges before the torch moves ahead): A narrow stringy bead with widely spaced ripples signals travel too fast for the heat. Slowing down lets the pool fuse both sides."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder gets intermittent porosity in beads on mild steel, and the cup, flow rate, preflow and work cleanliness are all good. The draft is blocked and the filler is clean. A coworker suggests checking the gas supply connections between the cylinder and the torch. What is the best check to make?", "options": {"A": "Replace the filler rod, since the gas supply cannot cause intermittent porosity", "B": "Increase the post-flow time, since pores form while the arc is off", "C": "Check hose and fittings with leak detection solution, since leaks draw in air", "D": "Turn the flowmeter off and on, so the line can purge itself of air"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check hose and fittings with leak detection solution, since leaks draw in air): A small leak or loose fitting on the supply side can pull air into the hose by suction. Soapy water bubbles reveal the leak location."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder notices the arc wandering on an aluminum joint welded on AC with a pure tungsten electrode that was ground to a sharp point like a steel electrode. The gas, cleaning and balance settings are all normal for the job. Which correction is best for the wandering arc?", "options": {"A": "Let the tip form a small ball, since a ground point will not hold on AC", "B": "Regrind the point sharper, since a sharper point locks the arc on alternating current", "C": "Switch to direct current electrode positive for better arc stability", "D": "Increase gas flow to push the arc onto the aluminum joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Let the tip form a small ball, since a ground point will not hold on AC): Pure tungsten on AC forms a rounded tip that holds the arc. A ground point melts unevenly, so the arc moves around."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder dips filler rod repeatedly and each time pulls the hot rod end out of the gas shield, and the finished weld on mild steel shows oxide contamination along the bead. The tip of the rod is black each time it is pulled out. What happened to cause this?", "options": {"A": "Pulling the hot rod out of the argon oxidized it, carrying oxide into the pool", "B": "The rod picked up moisture from the argon, which then escaped as gas into the pool", "C": "The rod cooled too slowly in argon, which made it brittle and caused cracking", "D": "The rod gave off silicon in the shield, which formed slag on the weld"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pulling the hot rod out of the argon oxidized it, carrying oxide into the pool): Hot filler oxidizes the instant it leaves the gas shield, and that oxide goes into the weld on the next dip. Keeping the end under the cup avoids that."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student practices a GTAW groove weld on one-quarter inch mild steel plate in the position depicted in the diagram. The instructor says this setup gives the welder the most advantage from gravity of any test position. What is the effect of gravity on the molten pool here?", "options": {"A": "Gravity pulls the pool out of the joint, so the pool must stay small and cool", "B": "Gravity has no effect on the pool since surface tension controls the puddle", "C": "Gravity forces the pool to run ahead of the arc, which causes lack of fusion", "D": "Gravity holds the pool in the joint, so larger pools and faster travel work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gravity holds the pool in the joint, so larger pools and faster travel work): With the plate flat and the weld on top, gravity keeps metal in the groove, so heat can be higher and travel faster than in any other position. Gravity does not pull the pool out of a flat groove, run it ahead of the arc, or leave it unaffected, so those statements describe other positions or no real effect.", "image": "question_images/q_443.png"}, {"id": 444, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a GTAW groove weld on one-quarter inch mild steel plate in the position depicted in the diagram. The bead keeps sagging onto the lower plate and the pool does not stay level. Which technique helps most to control the pool in this position?", "options": {"A": "Aim the torch slightly upward and keep the pool small so metal does not sag", "B": "Aim the torch down at the lower plate and run a larger pool", "C": "Use the highest amperage available so the pool remains fluid and flows level", "D": "Hold the torch perfectly flat and use a slow travel speed to keep the pool wide"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aim the torch slightly upward and keep the pool small so metal does not sag): In the horizontal position, gravity pulls the pool down across the groove, so a small pool and slight upward torch aim keep it supported. Aiming down or running very high amperage would flood the pool downward, and a flat torch with slow travel adds heat that makes sagging worse.", "image": "question_images/q_444.png"}, {"id": 445, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welds a GTAW groove on one-quarter inch mild steel plate in the position depicted in the diagram. The molten pool keeps running downward ahead of the arc and the bead looks lumpy. Which approach is typically used to control the pool in this position?", "options": {"A": "Travel downhill with a long arc and large pool, so the pool flows in", "B": "Travel uphill with a long arc and high amperage, so the pool stays wide and fluid", "C": "Travel sideways with a weave wide enough to fill the groove in one pass", "D": "Travel uphill with a short arc and small pool, so each frozen step supports the next"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Travel uphill with a short arc and small pool, so each frozen step supports the next): Going uphill lets each solidified step act as a shelf for the next, and a small pool keeps gravity from running metal down. Downhill travel, a long arc or high amperage would let the pool run ahead of the arc, and a wide sideways weave adds heat the position cannot support.", "image": "question_images/q_445.png"}, {"id": 446, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder runs a GTAW root pass on mild steel plate in the position depicted in the diagram, and molten metal sags out of the joint and hangs below the plate. The amperage was set for flat work. Which change in technique is best for this position?", "options": {"A": "Lower the amperage, shorten the arc, and keep the pool small so metal stays in place", "B": "Raise the amperage and lengthen the arc so more metal flows up into the joint", "C": "Use a large pool with slow travel so gravity can pull the metal into the root", "D": "Switch to direct current electrode positive so the pool is pressed upward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower the amperage, shorten the arc, and keep the pool small so metal stays in place): When the weld is above the welder, gravity pulls the pool downward, so low heat, a short arc and a small pool keep surface tension in control. Higher amperage, a long arc, a large slow pool or a polarity change would add heat or alter penetration without controlling the sagging pool.", "image": "question_images/q_446.png"}, {"id": 447, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welds a GTAW fillet on a mild steel tee joint in the position depicted in the diagram, and the instructor asks how the torch should be held. The goal is equal leg sizes on both plates with good gas coverage ahead of the pool. Which torch placement is typical for this joint?", "options": {"A": "A work angle near forty-five degrees to both plates, with a slight push angle", "B": "A work angle of ninety degrees to the horizontal plate, with a drag travel angle", "C": "A work angle of fifteen degrees toward the vertical plate, with a drag travel angle", "D": "A work angle of seventy degrees toward the horizontal plate, with no travel angle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A work angle near forty-five degrees to both plates, with a slight push angle): A tee fillet needs heat split evenly between the two members, so the torch bisects the joint at about forty-five degrees. A slight push gives gas coverage ahead.", "image": "question_images/q_447.png"}, {"id": 448, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is running a GTAW fillet on a mild steel tee joint in the position depicted in the diagram, and the finished bead is thicker at the bottom with undercut on the vertical plate. The amperage and travel speed are normal. Which adjustment to the arc placement fits this problem?", "options": {"A": "Direct the arc slightly more toward the vertical plate so more metal is added on top", "B": "Reduce travel speed greatly, so the pool becomes larger and flows evenly", "C": "Place the torch at ninety degrees to the vertical plate to build a larger bead", "D": "Aim the arc slightly more at the bottom plate so gravity does not pile metal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Direct the arc slightly more toward the vertical plate so more metal is added on top): Gravity makes the pool sag onto the lower plate and leaves the vertical plate undercut, so directing the arc slightly more toward the vertical plate adds heat and filler there and balances the leg sizes. Aiming at the bottom plate would pile even more metal where the bead is already thick, and slowing down greatly would make the pool larger and sag further.", "image": "question_images/q_448.png"}, {"id": 449, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student welds a GTAW fillet on a mild steel tee joint in the position depicted in the diagram. The molten pool keeps sagging downward and the bead looks uneven with a droopy shape. The instructor asks what technique to use to control the pool in this position.", "options": {"A": "Travel downhill in long strokes with a long arc, so the pool follows gravity", "B": "Travel uphill with a large pool and high heat, so the legs fill in one pass", "C": "Hold the torch at ninety degrees and weave widely across both plates", "D": "Travel uphill in small steps with a short arc, so each frozen step supports the next"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Travel uphill in small steps with a short arc, so each frozen step supports the next): A vertical fillet is supported by freezing metal as the weld climbs, and small pools help. Downhill strokes or a large hot pool let metal sag, and a wide weave at ninety degrees adds more heat than a vertical fillet can support.", "image": "question_images/q_449.png"}, {"id": 450, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student runs a GTAW fillet on a mild steel tee joint in the position depicted in the diagram, and the instructor notes that hot metal drops occasionally fall from the weld pool toward the welder. Which safety precaution is best for work in this position?", "options": {"A": "Wear no extra protection, since GTAW produces no falling sparks or molten metal", "B": "Wear a cap, flame-resistant jacket and ear plugs, since molten drops can fall", "C": "Wear short sleeves for dexterity, since the filler rod is the only hot material", "D": "Tape a cloth over the helmet vents, since the arc lights the face from above"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wear a cap, flame-resistant jacket and ear plugs, since molten drops can fall): Overhead work lets hot metal fall onto the welder, so head and body protection are used, and ear plugs keep drops out of the ears. Skipping protection or wearing short sleeves leaves skin open to falling spatter, and covering helmet vents does not address that hazard.", "image": "question_images/q_450.png"}, {"id": 451, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder passes a plate groove test in the position depicted in the diagram under AWS D1.1 and wants all-position plate certification for groove welds. The test coordinator says one more test position is needed to combine with this one. Which additional test is needed?", "options": {"A": "A one G flat test, since three G plus one G cover all plate groove positions", "B": "A two G horizontal test, since three G plus two G cover all plate groove positions", "C": "A four G overhead test, since three G plus four G cover all plate groove positions", "D": "No other test, because three G alone covers all plate groove positions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A four G overhead test, since three G plus four G cover all plate groove positions): Passing the vertical and overhead plate groove tests together covers flat, horizontal, vertical and overhead groove welds on plate. Vertical alone does not cover overhead.", "image": "question_images/q_451.png"}, {"id": 452, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student reads a weld position designation such as the one in the diagram and asks the instructor what the letter at the end means, since the number was already explained as the test position. Which statement correctly explains the meaning of the letter in these designations?", "options": {"A": "The letter stands for flat position, while the number stands for the number of passes", "B": "The letter stands for fusion weld, while the number stands for the plate thickness", "C": "The letter stands for fabrication, while the number stands for the joint design", "D": "The letter stands for fillet weld, while the number stands for the test position"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The letter stands for fillet weld, while the number stands for the test position): Position designations use a number for the position and a letter for the weld type, with F for fillet and G for groove. The letter does not mean flat, fusion or fabrication; the number gives the position and the letter gives the weld type.", "image": "question_images/q_452.png"}, {"id": 453, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "In the position depicted in the diagram, a welder finds the root of a GTAW pass on mild steel sagging inward and the top bead concave, with metal hanging below the plate. The welder used the same amperage as flat work. Which cause best explains this?", "options": {"A": "Too little heat made the pool freeze, so metal could not reach the root", "B": "Too much argon flow cooled the pool, so it contracted into the root", "C": "Too small a tungsten made a narrow arc, so it overfilled the root", "D": "Too much heat made the pool large, so gravity pulled metal away from the root"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Too much heat made the pool large, so gravity pulled metal away from the root): A large, hot pool in an overhead joint is pulled down by gravity, leaving a concave surface. Lower amperage and quicker travel hold a smaller pool in place.", "image": "question_images/q_453.png"}, {"id": 454, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A student prepares one-quarter inch mild steel plate for a full-penetration GTAW groove weld in the position depicted in the diagram, with no backing strip. The instructor asks which edge preparation is typical for this thickness. Which preparation is the usual choice for this joint?", "options": {"A": "A square edge with no gap, which gives full penetration on quarter inch plate", "B": "A tee-style edge with no bevel and heavy tack welds at ninety degrees", "C": "A ten degree included angle bevel so the weld takes the least filler", "D": "A single V groove near sixty degrees, with a root face and opening"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A single V groove near sixty degrees, with a root face and opening): A sixty degree V with a small root face and opening lets the arc reach the root for full penetration. Square edges without a gap limit penetration at that thickness.", "image": "question_images/q_454.png"}, {"id": 455, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A GTAW bead on mild steel shows the discontinuity marked in the diagram, with small cavities open on the weld surface. The welder used the correct filler and the correct polarity for the job. Which cause is most likely for the marked discontinuity?", "options": {"A": "Excess tungsten in the pool, which is carried to the surface as cavities", "B": "Air entered the shield from low flow, a leak, a draft, or contaminated base metal", "C": "An overly long post-flow time, which pulls cavities into the weld surface", "D": "A filler rod that was too small, which leaves hollow gaps along the weld surface"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Air entered the shield from low flow, a leak, a draft, or contaminated base metal): Gas pores form when oxygen, nitrogen or moisture get into the molten pool. Fixing the shield, the leak or the base metal cleanliness removes them.", "image": "question_images/q_455.png"}, {"id": 456, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A GTAW bead on one-eighth inch mild steel shows the groove along the weld toe marked in the diagram. The welder added very little filler and ran a long arc at high amperage. Which correction is best for the marked discontinuity?", "options": {"A": "Raise amperage and lengthen the arc so the groove melts and fills in", "B": "Speed up travel and remove filler so the toe has less heat to cut away", "C": "Switch to direct current electrode positive so the toes can cool quickly", "D": "Lower amperage, shorten the arc, and pause at the toes to add filler into the groove"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower amperage, shorten the arc, and pause at the toes to add filler into the groove): A groove at the toe appears when the arc melts base metal that filler does not replace. Less heat, a shorter arc and filler at the toes fix it.", "image": "question_images/q_456.png"}, {"id": 457, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder ends a GTAW bead on mild steel plate by releasing the pedal at full amperage, and the end of the weld shows the star pattern marked in the diagram. The rest of the bead is sound. Which finishing technique prevents this marked discontinuity?", "options": {"A": "Taper current with the pedal while adding filler to fill the crater", "B": "Raise current at the end of the weld to melt the crater smooth and flat", "C": "Pull the torch away while holding full current until the arc goes out", "D": "Stop the gas flow first so the crater cools in still air with no gas"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Taper current with the pedal while adding filler to fill the crater): A deep, hot crater shrinks as it cools and cracks. A gradual taper and added filler leave a full crater that cools slowly.", "image": "question_images/q_457.png"}, {"id": 458, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A GTAW bead on one-quarter inch mild steel shows a gap along the sidewall as marked in the diagram, even though the surface of the bead looks smooth and well shaped. The amperage was low and the welder moved quickly. What most likely caused it?", "options": {"A": "Low heat or fast travel left the sidewall unmelted, so the pool did not fuse", "B": "Too much heat melted the sidewall too deeply, which pulled the bead away", "C": "Too long a post-flow time oxidized the sidewall before the pool reached it", "D": "Too large a tungsten diameter changed the polarity of the sidewall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low heat or fast travel left the sidewall unmelted, so the pool did not fuse): A smooth bead can hide unfused sidewalls. Raise heat or slow down, and aim the arc at the sidewall to melt it.", "image": "question_images/q_458.png"}, {"id": 459, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder changes from pure argon to a helium-argon mix to weld half inch aluminum plate and notices the arc is harder to start and runs noticeably hotter at the same amperage setting. The machine and electrode are unchanged. Which explanation fits what the welder observed after the gas change?", "options": {"A": "Helium is heavier than argon, so it blankets the arc and cools the electrode tip", "B": "Helium needs higher voltage to ionize, so starting is harder and heat input rises", "C": "Helium lowers arc voltage, so the arc starts easily but heat input drops", "D": "Helium cleans oxide more aggressively, which lowers the voltage the arc needs to run"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Helium needs higher voltage to ionize, so starting is harder and heat input rises): Helium resists ionizing more than argon, so the arc needs more voltage to start and, at the same current, delivers more heat. That raised voltage is why helium mixes help on thick aluminum."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "level_1_arc_welding_processes", "question": "A welder is burning through the edge of one-sixteenth inch mild steel on a corner joint, even though the gas flow, tungsten condition and travel speed are good. The machine has a foot pedal and the welder has been holding the pedal nearly flat to the floor. Which change would best stop the burn-through?", "options": {"A": "Press the pedal fully to raise amperage so the edge melts quickly and flows together", "B": "Lengthen the arc well past one electrode diameter so heat spreads over the edge", "C": "Use less pedal so amperage drops, and add filler near the edge", "D": "Remove the filler rod entirely so the thin edge has less metal to heat"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use less pedal so amperage drops, and add filler near the edge): Thin edges overheat quickly, so lowering current with the pedal and adding filler to absorb heat prevents holes. Hotter or longer arcs make the burn-through worse."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder in a maintenance shop is asked to cut galvanized steel angle with an oxy-fuel torch. The zinc coating burns off in a white smoke that tastes metallic and leaves a dry throat. Which precaution best fits this job, based on the hazard from the burning coating?", "options": {"A": "Use local exhaust or a fume-rated respirator to avoid breathing zinc oxide from the coating", "B": "Wear a paper dust mask and a face shield, since brief zinc smoke exposure is harmless", "C": "Cut faster with higher pressure so the fumes pass before they can be inhaled", "D": "Pour water on the coating while cutting to bind the zinc into the slag"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use local exhaust or a fume-rated respirator to avoid breathing zinc oxide from the coating): Zinc oxide fumes cause metal fume fever, so ventilation or proper respiratory protection is needed. Speeding up does not remove the coating, and a dust mask does not filter fume."}, {"id": 462, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder cuts a 2 inch plate with a tip sized for 1/4 inch plate. The preheat looks fine, but the cut stalls and the kerf keeps closing with slag. Travel speed is slow and the plate is clean. What is the best explanation for the stall?", "options": {"A": "The plate is too clean, so the jet has no scale to burn in the kerf", "B": "The acetylene pressure is too high and oxidizes the cutting jet before it enters the plate", "C": "The tip orifices cannot deliver enough heat and oxygen volume for the thickness", "D": "The tip is too large and starves the plate of oxygen at the kerf bottom"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The tip orifices cannot deliver enough heat and oxygen volume for the thickness): A small tip gives limited oxygen flow and heat, so the reaction cannot cut through heavy plate. Choosing the tip from the thickness chart prevents it."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "While cutting 1/2 inch plate, a welder hears a shrill hissing from the torch handle and sees the flame burning back inside the equipment instead of at the tip. The flame at the tip is gone. What should the welder do first to stop the situation?", "options": {"A": "Close the torch oxygen valve, then the fuel valve, then the cylinder valves", "B": "Close the fuel valve first, then pull the hose off the regulator to vent it", "C": "Press the cutting oxygen lever to blow the flame out through the tip orifice", "D": "Pinch the fuel hose shut with locking pliers and keep working at reduced speed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Close the torch oxygen valve, then the fuel valve, then the cylinder valves): Squealing with a flame inside the torch is a flashback, so the oxygen supply feeding it is shut first, then the fuel, then the cylinder valves. Opening the cutting lever would feed more oxygen to the burning mixture."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "During air carbon arc gouging the electrode overheats, glows red along its length, and breaks off often. The welder is holding the electrode about ten inches out of the holder to reach a long groove. What is the best corrective step?", "options": {"A": "Increase the extension to improve air cooling along the whole length of the rod", "B": "Reduce the air jet to half pressure so the rod stays hotter during the pass", "C": "Switch from DC electrode positive to DC electrode negative for the same job", "D": "Shorten the electrode extension to about six inches or less so the rod does not overheat"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shorten the electrode extension to about six inches or less so the rod does not overheat): A long extension raises electrical resistance, overheats the rod and weakens it, so it is held to roughly six inches or less. Longer extension makes the problem worse."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "During a hot work safety check at a fabrication shop, an instructor points to the hoses on a cutting outfit and asks how to identify the fuel gas connection by sight and by feel. The student must apply the standard identification used in the United States. Which description fits the fuel gas hose and its fitting?", "options": {"A": "A green hose with a plain nut that has a right-hand thread and brass collar", "B": "A red hose whose grooved nut has a left-hand thread for the fuel fitting", "C": "A black hose with a grooved nut that has a right-hand thread on the regulator side", "D": "A red hose with a plain nut that has a right-hand thread matching the oxygen fitting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A red hose whose grooved nut has a left-hand thread for the fuel fitting): In US practice the fuel gas hose is red and its fittings carry a groove and left-hand threads, while oxygen is green with right-hand threads. The groove and thread direction prevent connecting a hose to the wrong regulator."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a rough, irregular cut face. The operator used a neutral flame, correct speed and a clean tip, but heavy mill scale and rust cover the line of cut on the plate. What should be done first on the next cut from the same stack of plate?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a carburizing flame so the extra carbon burns the scale away", "B": "Clean the scale and rust from the cut line with a wire brush or grinder", "C": "Reduce the oxygen pressure so the scale is not blown out of the kerf", "D": "Add a second preheat pass until the scale glows brighter than the plate itself"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clean the scale and rust from the cut line with a wire brush or grinder): Heavy scale and rust interfere with the preheat and the start of the reaction, producing an uneven face. Cleaning the cut line is the simplest first correction.", "image": "question_images/q_466.png"}, {"id": 467, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows curved drag lines bowing far behind the torch near the bottom of a 1 inch steel plate, with heavy lag at the exit side. Which change in technique most directly reduces the lag on the next cut of this plate?", "options": {"A": "Raise the travel speed so the preheat flame spends less time on the plate", "B": "Slow the travel speed so the oxygen jet can cut cleanly through the full plate thickness", "C": "Shorten the preheat flame so that less heat reaches the lower part of the plate", "D": "Tilt the torch back sharply so the jet points at the plate surface"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow the travel speed so the oxygen jet can cut cleanly through the full plate thickness): Heavy lag means the torch is travelling faster than the jet can penetrate, so slowing down brings the exit closer to the entry. Faster travel increases lag and risks losing the cut.", "image": "question_images/q_467.png"}, {"id": 468, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "An operator cuts 3/8 inch steel and finds the top edge of the cut melted and rounded, with a heavy bead of slag along the underside. Travel speed and tip size match the chart. Which setting is the most likely cause of this edge condition?", "options": {"A": "The preheat flames are set too large for the thickness of the plate", "B": "The cutting oxygen pressure is too low, leaving a layer of unburned iron in the kerf", "C": "The acetylene regulator is set slightly below the working limit for the torch", "D": "The plate is too thin to hold a stable cutting jet through the kerf"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The preheat flames are set too large for the thickness of the plate): Excess preheat heat melts the top edge and rounds it off, and the extra molten metal adds slag to the underside. Low oxygen pressure would give an incomplete cut, not a rounded top edge."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A cutting shop buys lower-purity oxygen to save money. Operators report slower cuts, more slag on the underside, and heavier gas use on the same thickness of carbon steel plate compared with the earlier deliveries from the previous supplier. Which explanation fits these reports?", "options": {"A": "Lower purity cools the preheat flame and keeps the plate from reaching its melting point", "B": "Lower oxygen purity slows the burning of iron and degrades cut quality and speed", "C": "Lower purity raises the kindling temperature of steel above its own melting point", "D": "Lower purity changes only the color of the flame and has no effect on the cut"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower oxygen purity slows the burning of iron and degrades cut quality and speed): Cutting oxygen needs high purity because impurities dilute the reaction that burns iron in the kerf. Poor purity reduces cutting speed and cut quality and increases gas use."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a carbon electrode held at an angle to a steel plate with a jet of air running along the electrode. In air carbon arc gouging, what job does the compressed air jet perform in the process once the arc is struck and a molten pool forms?", "options": {"A": "It shields the arc from the atmosphere like argon does in TIG welding", "B": "It cools the electrode tip so the carbon rod lasts longer in the arc", "C": "It oxidizes the plate so that the steel can burn away along the full groove length", "D": "It blows molten metal out of the groove as the arc melts the base metal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It blows molten metal out of the groove as the arc melts the base metal): The air jet clears molten metal from the groove while the arc supplies the melting heat. Gouging does not rely on burning the steel in oxygen, and the jet is not a shielding gas.", "image": "question_images/q_470.png"}, {"id": 471, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student plans to cut a 2 inch thick steel block and picks the same cutting tip that was used earlier on 1/4 inch plate. The instructor asks for the correct adjustment before the student lights the torch. What should the student do before cutting the block?", "options": {"A": "Keep the small tip and double the length of the acetylene preheat flame", "B": "Keep the small tip and cut faster so the plate does not overheat", "C": "Switch to a larger cutting tip and set a higher oxygen pressure from the manufacturer chart", "D": "Switch to a larger tip but lower the oxygen pressure to match the earlier thin plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Switch to a larger cutting tip and set a higher oxygen pressure from the manufacturer chart): Heavier plate needs a larger cutting orifice and more oxygen volume and pressure, taken from the tip manufacturer's chart. A small tip cannot supply enough heat or oxygen flow to sever thick steel."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A lab torch has a check valve and a flashback arrestor installed at the torch inlet. A student asks why both are used when each seems to guard against a backward flow problem. Which statement describes the difference between them?", "options": {"A": "A check valve stops a flame front, and an arrestor stops only reverse gas flow", "B": "Both do the same job, so one of them can be removed with no added risk", "C": "Check valves stop reverse gas flow, and arrestors stop a backward flame front", "D": "A check valve controls cylinder pressure, and an arrestor controls the hose length"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check valves stop reverse gas flow, and arrestors stop a backward flame front): Check valves block gases from flowing backward and mixing in the hoses, while arrestors quench a burning front that enters the equipment. Each protects against a different part of the same hazard."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows an oxy-fuel cut face with heavy lag on a thick steel plate that an apprentice just cut. The instructor asks about the path of the cutting oxygen stream. Compared with a well-matched cut, which statement about the oxygen stream on this face is correct?", "options": {"A": "The jet enters the bottom first and the top later, reversing the cutting direction", "B": "The jet exits the bottom well behind the point where it entered the top surface", "C": "The jet exits ahead of the torch because the preheat flame outruns it during travel", "D": "The jet stays at the plate surface and fails to penetrate into the thickness"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The jet exits the bottom well behind the point where it entered the top surface): Heavy lag means the jet trails far behind at the exit side, so drag lines curve rearward. The stream still penetrates, which is why the plate gets cut.", "image": "question_images/q_473.png"}, {"id": 474, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student opens a new acetylene cylinder by turning the valve spindle several full turns, then lays the cylinder wrench on the cart across the shop. The instructor stops the work immediately. What is the correct way to open the acetylene cylinder valve and leave it?", "options": {"A": "Open it fully against the back seat and remove the wrench for storage on the cart", "B": "Open it no more than one and one-half turns and leave the wrench on the stem", "C": "Open it only after the oxygen valve and set the regulator to thirty psig first", "D": "Open it one full turn and then close it halfway so pressure builds up slowly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Open it no more than one and one-half turns and leave the wrench on the stem): Limiting the opening to about a turn and a half, preferably less, allows a quick shutoff in an emergency, and a wrench left on the stem saves seconds. A wrench left on the cart would cost time if fire or a leak appeared."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welding instructor inspects the cut face in the diagram, noting slight drag and a clean, square edge with little slag on a piece of 1/2 inch steel plate, checking squareness with a straightedge. Which combination of settings most likely produced this result?", "options": {"A": "An oversized tip, an oxidizing preheat flame, and travel well above the jet rate", "B": "A correctly sized tip, a neutral preheat flame, and steady travel matched to the jet", "C": "A small tip, a carburizing flame, and the inner cone touching the plate surface during travel", "D": "Very high oxygen pressure with the tip held about two inches off the plate surface"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A correctly sized tip, a neutral preheat flame, and steady travel matched to the jet): Clean, square edges come from a correctly sized tip, a neutral flame, and travel that matches the oxygen jet. The other settings would add slag, round the edge, or widen the kerf.", "image": "question_images/q_475.png"}, {"id": 476, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A new torch connection is installed on an oxygen regulator, and the student wants to confirm the fittings do not leak before lighting. The cylinder valve is open, the pressure is on, and the hoses are connected. Which method is the safe way to check for leaks at the fittings?", "options": {"A": "Brush a soap solution over each connection and watch closely for any bubbles forming", "B": "Pass a lit striker slowly along each connection and watch for a flicker in the spark", "C": "Listen at each joint with a stethoscope and accept any faint hiss as normal", "D": "Hold a strip of paper near the fittings and watch for fluttering or movement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brush a soap solution over each connection and watch closely for any bubbles forming): Soapy water bubbles reveal a leak without any ignition source, so it is safe with oxygen and with fuel gas. A flame near a possible fuel leak is the exact hazard the check is meant to prevent."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A maintenance supervisor asks a welder to cut open an old steel drum with an oxy-fuel torch. The drum has no label and smells faintly of solvent when the bung is removed. What is the correct decision about starting the cut?", "options": {"A": "Cut at the seam quickly, since a faint solvent smell burns off in the preheat flame", "B": "Cut it while a coworker sprays the outside with water to absorb the heat", "C": "Cut it only after pressurizing it with oxygen to push out any remaining vapor", "D": "Do not cut until the drum is cleaned, purged and tested free of flammable vapor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not cut until the drum is cleaned, purged and tested free of flammable vapor): Containers that held flammable material can explode when heated, so they are cleaned, purged and verified free of vapor first. Spraying the outside or adding oxygen does not remove vapors from the inside."}, {"id": 478, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student gets ready to use a plasma cutter and wants to check the torch lead by gripping it with a wet glove while the power source is switched on. The instructor stops the student at once. What hazard does this action overlook?", "options": {"A": "Plasma torch circuits carry only low voltage but very high gas pressure in the hose", "B": "Plasma torches become electrically live only after the gas valve has been opened", "C": "Plasma torch circuits carry high open-circuit voltage, often several hundred volts DC", "D": "Plasma torch circuits are isolated from the power source by the plastic gas hose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plasma torch circuits carry high open-circuit voltage, often several hundred volts DC): Plasma systems typically run an open-circuit voltage in the hundreds of volts, so dry gloves and intact leads are needed. Gas pressure in the hose does not isolate the torch from the power source."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A plasma operator cuts 1/2 inch steel and finds a strong bevel along one edge, with the top corner rounded. Consumables are new and travel speed is correct. The torch is being held about a half inch off the plate. Which cause fits best?", "options": {"A": "The work clamp is fastened too close to the starting point of the cut", "B": "The torch standoff is set too high, letting the arc column spread and bevel the edge", "C": "The plasma gas moisture is too low for the nozzle to stay cool", "D": "The workpiece is wired to the negative terminal rather than the positive terminal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The torch standoff is set too high, letting the arc column spread and bevel the edge): A high standoff widens the arc column and bevels the edge and rounds the top corner. Work clamp location and gas dryness do not produce this bevel, and plasma work is normally wired with the electrode negative."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows an irregular, rough cut face on 1/2 inch steel plate. After finishing the cut, the operator found the tip orifices clogged with slag after finishing the cut. Which action best corrects the roughness before the next cut is made?", "options": {"A": "Open the cutting oxygen valve further to blast the slag out during the cut", "B": "Enlarge the orifices with a drill bit so slag no longer sticks inside", "C": "Clean the orifices with a correctly sized tip cleaner and reset the flame", "D": "Move the torch faster so the clogged orifices have less time to affect the cut"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean the orifices with a correctly sized tip cleaner and reset the flame): Partly blocked orifices distort the flame and oxygen stream and roughen the face. A tip cleaner of the right size clears them without enlarging the holes.", "image": "question_images/q_480.png"}, {"id": 481, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student cutting 3/4 inch steel hears a loud pop, the flame goes out, and the torch stays quiet with no hissing inside the handle. The tip had touched the plate just before the pop. Which response is correct before the student attempts to continue the cut?", "options": {"A": "Open the oxygen valve fully to flush the torch handle and keep cutting immediately", "B": "Close only the cylinder valves and leave the torch valves open until the next shift", "C": "Raise the acetylene pressure to prevent another pop and then relight at once", "D": "Close the torch valves, check that the tip is tight and clean, then relight the torch"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Close the torch valves, check that the tip is tight and clean, then relight the torch): This is a backfire, a pop with the flame going out, commonly caused by the tip touching the work or by a dirty or loose tip. The torch can be relit once the valves are closed and the tip is checked."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A plasma cut on 3/8 inch steel leaves thin, hard, tightly bonded strings on the bottom edge that must be ground off. The consumables are new, the standoff height is correct, and the plate is clean and flat on the table. Which cause best explains the dross on this cut?", "options": {"A": "Travel speed was too slow, so the molten metal pooled and fell away as round drops", "B": "Plasma gas flow was too low to keep the arc centered in the kerf during travel", "C": "Travel speed was too fast, so the arc lagged and dragged metal onto the bottom edge", "D": "The nozzle was held in contact with the plate surface during the entire cut"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Travel speed was too fast, so the arc lagged and dragged metal onto the bottom edge): Fast travel leaves high-speed dross, a thin hard string that is hard to remove, and slowing down is the correct response. Rounded beads that knock off easily point to travel that is too slow."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a good oxy-fuel cut face on 1/2 inch plate with slight drag. The operator wants to repeat this quality on the next piece of plate from the same stack. Which travel practice keeps drag lines this small?", "options": {"A": "Pause at each inch of travel so the jet can fully cut each section", "B": "Move the torch as fast as the preheat flame allows without breaking the cut", "C": "Rock the torch forward and back to polish the cut face as it advances", "D": "Move steadily at a speed that keeps the jet exiting close behind where it entered"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Move steadily at a speed that keeps the jet exiting close behind where it entered): Steady travel at a speed matched to the jet lets the oxygen exit close behind where it entered, keeping drag small. Pausing or rocking breaks the smooth pattern and roughens the face.", "image": "question_images/q_483.png"}, {"id": 484, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "In an advanced welding class, the instructor describes a process that strikes an arc between a carbon electrode and the workpiece while a jet of compressed air runs along the electrode. The jet blows the molten pool out of the joint area. What is this process commonly abbreviated as in AWS terminology?", "options": {"A": "PAC, which stands for plasma arc cutting and uses an ionized gas stream", "B": "CAC-A, which stands for air carbon arc cutting and gouging of metal", "C": "OFC-A, which stands for oxy-fuel cutting with an acetylene fuel gas supply", "D": "GTAW-P, which stands for pulsed gas tungsten arc welding with argon shielding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (CAC-A, which stands for air carbon arc cutting and gouging of metal): Air carbon arc cutting, abbreviated CAC-A, melts metal with an arc and blows it away with an air jet. Plasma arc cutting is PAC and uses an ionized gas stream instead of a carbon electrode."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "During a torch maintenance demonstration, the instructor shows a cutting tip with slag partly blocking the small preheat holes around the center bore. Students are asked how to clean those orifices without enlarging them. Which tool is the proper choice for clearing the holes?", "options": {"A": "A tip cleaner file matched to the size of the orifice", "B": "A twist drill bit one size smaller than the orifice and turned by hand", "C": "A steel wire brush pushed in from the tip and twisted several turns", "D": "A pointed center punch tapped lightly into each hole until slag falls out"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A tip cleaner file matched to the size of the orifice): Tip cleaners are made in graduated sizes so the hole is cleared without being reamed oversize. A drill or punch can distort the orifice and spoil the flame and the oxygen stream."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows the cut face of a plate cut by oxy-fuel with a narrow kerf and only slight, nearly straight drag lines on the face. A student must name what drag lines record about the cutting process. What do the drag lines show?", "options": {"A": "How far the preheat flame penetrated into the plate before the cutting oxygen was opened", "B": "The path of the cutting oxygen stream through the plate thickness during the cut", "C": "The rolling grain direction left in the steel at the mill", "D": "The amount of slag left on the plate from the previous torch pass"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The path of the cutting oxygen stream through the plate thickness during the cut): Drag lines trace where the oxygen stream moved from entry to exit, so they show how the jet travelled through the thickness. They are not a record of preheat depth or mill grain.", "image": "question_images/q_486.png"}, {"id": 487, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows air carbon arc gouging on steel plate with a gouge profile. The welder needs to remove a defective weld root before rewelding the joint. Which air supply pressure is typical for this kind of work in a fabrication shop?", "options": {"A": "About 15 psi, matching the pressure limit that applies to acetylene torches", "B": "About 250 psi, taken straight from a high-pressure cylinder manifold line", "C": "About 30 psi, the pressure commonly used for light-duty plasma cutters", "D": "Roughly 80 to 100 psi from a clean, dry compressed air supply"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Roughly 80 to 100 psi from a clean, dry compressed air supply): Gouging commonly runs at about 80 to 100 psi to clear molten metal. Acetylene's 15 psig limit and a low pressure setting would leave metal and carbon in the groove.", "image": "question_images/q_487.png"}, {"id": 488, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A plasma cutter that cut cleanly last week now produces a ragged, wandering cut with a wide kerf on 1/4 inch steel. The operator checks air pressure and the work clamp, and both are normal. Which action is most likely to fix the problem?", "options": {"A": "Inspect and replace the worn electrode and nozzle consumables before cutting again", "B": "Raise the gas pressure well above the manufacturer's stated specification for the torch", "C": "Switch the plasma torch over to electrode positive polarity for a hotter arc", "D": "Wet the plate surface with water to cool the arc column along the cut line"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inspect and replace the worn electrode and nozzle consumables before cutting again): A worn nozzle orifice and a pitted electrode both disturb the arc column, causing a ragged, wide cut. Consumables are replaced first, and plasma torches are not run on electrode positive."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A shop cuts 1/2 inch carbon steel with plasma and notices lumps of metal clinging to the bottom edge of the cut. The lumps are rounded and knock off easily with a chipping hammer. The operator asks which travel condition causes this kind of dross. What is the cause?", "options": {"A": "Travel speed too fast, which drags thin hard strings of metal along the bottom edge", "B": "Gas pressure too low, which leaves an unbroken pilot arc burning across the kerf", "C": "Standoff set too tight, which pushes the arc column back up into the nozzle", "D": "Travel speed too slow, which lets molten metal pool and harden as round beads"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Travel speed too slow, which lets molten metal pool and harden as round beads): Low-speed dross is rounded, forms from metal that sits too long in the kerf, and is easy to remove. High-speed dross appears as thin, tightly bonded strings and calls for slower travel instead."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder performing air carbon arc gouging on steel finds that molten metal stays in the groove and carbon deposits form along the sides. The air regulator reads about 40 psi. Which correction is most appropriate for this problem on the next pass?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the air to about 15 psi so molten metal stays hot in the groove", "B": "Increase the electrode extension past ten inches to push more air through the arc", "C": "Raise the air supply to roughly 80 to 100 psi from a clean dry source", "D": "Switch to alternating current so the carbon deposits burn off along the groove sides"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the air supply to roughly 80 to 100 psi from a clean dry source): A typical gouging air supply is about 80 to 100 psi, which is enough to clear the molten metal behind the arc. Low air leaves metal in the groove and lets carbon pick up on the surface."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "An instructor sees a student light an oxy-fuel torch immediately after connecting the hoses, without opening either torch valve to clear the lines. The torch lights with a loud pop and a brief internal flame. Which practice would have reduced this risk?", "options": {"A": "Open both torch valves at once and light the mixed gas at the tip", "B": "Purge each hose separately for a few seconds so mixed gas is cleared before lighting", "C": "Close the cylinder valves before lighting so the line pressure stays low during ignition", "D": "Rinse the hoses with water first to wash out any trapped gas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Purge each hose separately for a few seconds so mixed gas is cleared before lighting): Purging oxygen and fuel hoses separately removes leftover mixed gas that could ignite inside the hose or torch. Opening both valves together recreates the mixing problem."}, {"id": 492, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "In a cutting lab, students measure the slot width on several steel strips after each torch pass and record the results in their notes. The instructor says the gap left in the plate when a cutting process removes metal has a specific name. What is the term for the width of metal removed by the cut?", "options": {"A": "Kerf, the width of the slot left in the plate where the cutting process removes metal", "B": "Drag, the rearward curve of the lines left on the cut face by the oxygen stream", "C": "Dross, the resolidified material that clings to the bottom edge after the cut", "D": "Bevel, the angle of the cut face measured away from square to the plate surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Kerf, the width of the slot left in the plate where the cutting process removes metal): Kerf is the width of the slot created when the cutting process removes metal. Drag describes the lines on the cut face, dross is solidified material at the bottom edge, and bevel is an angular deviation of the face."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows heavy drag lag on a steel cut where sparks sprayed upward from the top surface instead of dropping out the bottom of the plate. The operator followed a straight layout line with a steady hand. Which condition best explains this face and the spark pattern?", "options": {"A": "Travel speed too fast, or cutting oxygen too low for the thickness of the plate", "B": "Preheat flame too carburizing, leaving a carbon deposit at the kerf entry", "C": "Tip held too close to the plate, so the inner cone touches the metal", "D": "Plate too clean, giving the oxygen jet no scale to burn in the kerf"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Travel speed too fast, or cutting oxygen too low for the thickness of the plate): Sparks shooting upward show the jet is not penetrating the full thickness, which happens when travel outruns the cut or oxygen is low. A carbon-rich flame changes the cut face but not the penetration.", "image": "question_images/q_493.png"}, {"id": 494, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a rough, irregular cut face on 3/8 inch steel. Inspection of the torch setup finds the cutting oxygen pressure well over the chart setting for that thickness, with the correct tip and speed. Which effect of the excess pressure explains the roughness?", "options": {"A": "Extra oxygen creates turbulence in the kerf, widening it and cutting the face unevenly", "B": "Extra oxygen cools the molten iron oxide, which hardens and bonds to the cut face", "C": "Extra oxygen turns the preheat flame into an acetylene-rich carburizing flame at the tip", "D": "Extra oxygen burns away the alloying elements in the surface layer of the plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Extra oxygen creates turbulence in the kerf, widening it and cutting the face unevenly): Oxygen pressure above the chart value causes a turbulent, widened jet that cuts unevenly and roughens the face. The extra gas does not harden the oxide or alter the preheat flame.", "image": "question_images/q_494.png"}, {"id": 495, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A job posting for a fabrication helper lists plasma arc cutting as a required skill. The supervisor wants applicants to understand which materials the process can cut with a transferred arc, since it differs from the torch method taught in the first semester. Which statement correctly describes the materials plasma can cut?", "options": {"A": "Only carbon steel and low alloy steel that form a low melting oxide in the kerf", "B": "Only ferrous metals, since the arc depends on the magnetic pull of iron in the plate", "C": "Any electrically conductive metal, including stainless steel and aluminum", "D": "Nonconductive materials such as glass fiber and ceramic tile when a transferred arc is used"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Any electrically conductive metal, including stainless steel and aluminum): A transferred plasma arc needs the workpiece to carry current, so any conductive metal can be cut. Oxy-fuel is the process limited to steels that burn in oxygen."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A shop foreman reviews the protective devices on each torch with new hires. One small device is fitted at the regulator outlet or the torch inlet and is built to quench a flame front that travels backward into the equipment. The foreman asks the class to name the device that does this job.", "options": {"A": "A pressure relief disc that vents the cylinder when internal pressure climbs too high", "B": "A flashback arrestor that quenches a burning gas front before it reaches the hoses", "C": "An acetone fuse plug that melts and releases gas when the cylinder gets hot", "D": "A cylinder valve cap that shields the valve stem from impact if the cylinder falls"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A flashback arrestor that quenches a burning gas front before it reaches the hoses): A flashback arrestor stops a burning gas front from travelling back toward the hoses, regulator and cylinder. The fuse plug and relief disc protect the cylinder itself from overpressure or heat, not the hoses."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "At the end of a shift, a student finishes cutting and prepares to shut down the oxyacetylene outfit. The instructor asks for the sequence that leaves the whole system safe and depressurized. Which steps are in the correct order for this shutdown?", "options": {"A": "Back out regulator screws, close torch valves, then open the cylinder valves to bleed the hoses", "B": "Close torch valves and coil the hoses, leaving the cylinder valves open overnight", "C": "Close cylinder valves, bleed lines at the torch, back out regulator screws, close torch valves", "D": "Close the oxygen cylinder only, then relight to burn the fuel out of the hoses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Close cylinder valves, bleed lines at the torch, back out regulator screws, close torch valves): Shutting the cylinder valves first and then bleeding the hoses removes trapped gas, and backing out the regulator screws leaves the diaphragms unloaded. Torch valves are closed last so the outfit is left empty and safe."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "Two inspectors compare the cut face in the diagram against a shop's edge-quality criteria for 1/2 inch plate. One says slight drag proves the torch moved too slowly. The other says it reflects well-matched travel. Which assessment is technically accurate?", "options": {"A": "Visible drag means travel was slow, and a perfect face would show no lines", "B": "Drag length tracks preheat flame length, so shorter drag means a longer flame was used", "C": "Drag lines form only when oxygen pressure is excessive and reflect the kerf width", "D": "Small drag reflects matched speed, and large drag comes from travel outrunning the jet"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Small drag reflects matched speed, and large drag comes from travel outrunning the jet): Drag measures how far the jet exits behind its entry, and a small value means travel is close to the best rate. Slow travel does not create long drag, so the first inspector is mistaken.", "image": "question_images/q_498.png"}, {"id": 499, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A shop considers switching from acetylene to propane for cutting steel plate. The foreman notes the propane flame feels cooler and plates take longer to start cutting than with the acetylene outfit they used last term. Which statement about propane compared with acetylene is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Propane burns hotter than acetylene, so preheat is faster and needs less oxygen", "B": "Propane needs no preheat because it carries its own oxidizer inside the cylinder", "C": "Propane flames run cooler, so preheat is slower and the cut uses more oxygen", "D": "Propane can be delivered at thirty psig, so it needs no special tip or hose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Propane flames run cooler, so preheat is slower and the cut uses more oxygen): Acetylene's flame is hotter, so propane preheats slower and consumes more oxygen, and a tip designed for propane is used. Propane's different delivery pressure does not remove the need for a matched tip."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "After finishing a day of oxy-fuel cutting in a fabrication bay, a welder closes the torch valves, caps the cylinders, and leaves without further checks. Hours later a smoldering pallet is found near the cutting table. Which hot work rule applies to this situation?", "options": {"A": "Keep a fire watch for at least thirty minutes and clear nearby combustibles", "B": "Leave the area right after cutting if the torch is closed and the cylinders are capped", "C": "Keep a fire watch for five minutes unless visible sparks reached the floor", "D": "Wet the floor once at the start of the shift and skip any further watching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep a fire watch for at least thirty minutes and clear nearby combustibles): Hot work rules call for removing combustibles within a set distance and keeping a fire watch for at least thirty minutes afterward, since slag can smolder unseen. Closing the torch does not account for hot slag left behind."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "An apprentice finds the threads of an oxygen regulator inlet stiff and applies petroleum jelly to ease the connection onto the cylinder. A coworker objects strongly and removes the regulator. Why is applying petroleum jelly to oxygen fittings a hazard?", "options": {"A": "Petroleum jelly dissolves the brass regulator seat and causes slow leaks over time", "B": "Petroleum jelly raises the oxygen purity and makes the flame hotter than the tip allows", "C": "Petroleum jelly traps moisture inside the regulator and freezes the diaphragm in cold shops", "D": "Oil and grease can ignite violently when exposed to pressurized oxygen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oil and grease can ignite violently when exposed to pressurized oxygen): Pressurized oxygen can ignite hydrocarbons such as oil and grease and cause fire or explosion, so oxygen fittings are kept clean and unlubricated. The issue is combustion, not seat damage or purity."}, {"id": 502, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student is cutting 3/8 inch mild steel plate with an oxyacetylene torch. Before pressing the cutting oxygen lever, the instructor asks the student to confirm the preheat flame setting. A feather of excess acetylene is visible around the inner cones. Which flame adjustment is appropriate for this cut?", "options": {"A": "Add acetylene until the feather doubles in length to give extra heating power", "B": "Add oxygen until the inner cones become short and hissing for faster preheating", "C": "Add oxygen until the feather disappears, leaving a sharply defined neutral flame", "D": "Leave the feather in place because carbon in the flame helps the oxygen jet"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add oxygen until the feather disappears, leaving a sharply defined neutral flame): A neutral preheat flame has distinct inner cones and no feather, and it heats steel without adding carbon or oxidizing the surface. The carburizing feather deposits carbon on the cut face, while an oxidizing flame wastes heat and roughens the start."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A student begins gouging out a defective weld with the air carbon arc process in a busy shop. A coworker nearby complains of a loud, sharp noise and notices sparks and molten metal spraying several feet across the floor. Which protective practice best fits this process?", "options": {"A": "Wear safety glasses with clear lenses, since carbon arcs give off little visible light", "B": "Wear a light cotton shirt, since molten spatter bounces off ordinary cloth easily", "C": "Wear ear protection and flame-resistant leathers, and clear combustibles from the area", "D": "Stand directly behind the electrode so the air blast pushes the sparks away from you"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wear ear protection and flame-resistant leathers, and clear combustibles from the area): Air carbon arc gouging is very loud and throws molten metal, so ear protection, flame-resistant clothing and a cleared work area are needed. A dark filter lens is also required, not clear lenses."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "While practicing with an oxyacetylene torch on a strip of 1/4 inch steel, a student hears a sharp pop, the flame goes out, and the torch stays quiet with no hissing afterward. The student asks the instructor what to call this event. Which term names a brief pop in which the flame extinguishes and the torch can be relit?", "options": {"A": "Flashback, a sustained flame that burns inside the torch with a shrill squeal", "B": "Flame lag, a trailing of the oxygen stream behind the torch travel direction", "C": "Reverse purge, a hose cleaning step done before the torch is first lit", "D": "Backfire, a momentary pop that puts the flame out at the tip of the torch"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Backfire, a momentary pop that puts the flame out at the tip of the torch): A backfire is a brief pop with the flame going out, and the torch can usually be relit after the cause is corrected. Flashback is different because the flame keeps burning inside the torch or hoses with a squeal."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a U-groove profile gouged into steel plate with a carbon electrode. A fabricator needs this profile on a thick butt joint before welding. Which electrode and polarity are typical for DC gouging of carbon steel in this kind of work?", "options": {"A": "A bare graphite rod on direct current electrode negative with the air supply on", "B": "A copper-coated rod on alternating current with the air supply switched off", "C": "A thoriated tungsten electrode on direct current electrode positive with argon shielding", "D": "A copper-coated carbon electrode on direct current electrode positive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A copper-coated carbon electrode on direct current electrode positive): Copper-coated DC carbon electrodes are normally run electrode positive for gouging steel. Tungsten electrodes belong to arc welding, not air carbon arc gouging.", "image": "question_images/q_505.png"}, {"id": 506, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder gouges a defective weld with carbon arc and finds the groove too shallow and wide while holding the electrode nearly parallel to the plate. The travel pace is brisk. Which adjustment deepens and narrows the groove on the next pass?", "options": {"A": "Lower the electrode angle toward the plate and speed up the travel across the joint", "B": "Reduce the air pressure to 20 psi so the arc stays hotter in the groove", "C": "Lengthen the electrode extension to ten inches and keep the same angle and travel", "D": "Increase the electrode angle toward the plate and slow the travel speed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase the electrode angle toward the plate and slow the travel speed): A steeper electrode and slower travel concentrate heat in one spot, cutting deeper and narrower. A shallow angle spreads the arc across the surface and gives a wide shallow groove."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows heavy lag on a 3/4 inch plate cut. A new technician suggests raising the cutting oxygen pressure far higher to cure it. Why is that a poor first step compared with adjusting the travel speed along the cut?", "options": {"A": "Excess pressure widens the kerf and adds turbulence without fixing the speed mismatch", "B": "Higher pressure cools the plate through the larger gas volume and halts the cut", "C": "Oxygen pressure bears no relation to kerf width on thicker plate, so it cannot help", "D": "Higher pressure shortens the drag lines but wastes gas, which breaks code rules for cutting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excess pressure widens the kerf and adds turbulence without fixing the speed mismatch): Lag comes from travel faster than the jet can handle, so travel speed is the first variable to change. Extra pressure widens the kerf and creates turbulence instead of correcting the mismatch.", "image": "question_images/q_507.png"}, {"id": 508, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "An instructor explains that oxy-fuel cutting cannot start until the steel reaches a specific condition. The preheat flames raise a small spot on the plate edge until the cutting oxygen lever can be pressed. What does the steel look like when it is ready to cut?", "options": {"A": "Dull gray with a thin oxide film just beginning to form along the plate edge", "B": "White hot and dripping from the edge, marking the full melting point of the plate", "C": "Dark straw color with blue tints, which signals the start of oxide coloring", "D": "Bright cherry red, near the kindling temperature of steel in a pure oxygen stream"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bright cherry red, near the kindling temperature of steel in a pure oxygen stream): Steel ignites in a pure oxygen stream when it reaches a bright cherry red near 1,600 to 1,800 degrees F. Melting the plate is not the goal, since the cut relies on burning the iron rather than melting it."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder shuts off the torch and notices the regulator low pressure gauge keeps creeping upward while the torch valves are closed. The cylinder valve is open and the hose connections are tight. What does this reading show about the condition of the regulator?", "options": {"A": "The torch tip is clogged and the pressure will settle within a few minutes", "B": "The cylinder is nearly empty and the gauge readings are drifting upward as it empties", "C": "The regulator seat is leaking and the regulator should be taken out of service", "D": "The hose is too long and the gas inside it is warming and expanding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The regulator seat is leaking and the regulator should be taken out of service): A gauge creeping up with the torch closed shows gas leaking past the seat, which can overpressure the downstream equipment. The regulator is removed from service for repair, not simply watched."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "The diagram shows a U-groove prepared by air carbon arc gouging on carbon steel plate that will be welded next. The groove surface has a thin dark carbon-rich layer left by the arc. What should be done to the groove before welding begins?", "options": {"A": "Grind to bright metal to remove carbon-rich residue that could cause porosity", "B": "Weld over the layer directly, since the arc will burn the carbon away in the pool", "C": "Quench the groove in water to harden the residue so it can be wire brushed", "D": "Apply a coat of oil to the groove so the carbon does not oxidize"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Grind to bright metal to remove carbon-rich residue that could cause porosity): Gouging leaves a carbon-rich surface that can cause weld porosity or cracking, so grinding to bright metal is needed. Welding over it or quenching does not remove the carbon.", "image": "question_images/q_510.png"}, {"id": 511, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A shop has an oxy-fuel torch on one bench and a plasma cutter on another. The job list includes 3/4 inch carbon steel and 1/4 inch aluminum plate. The foreman asks which statement about the capability of the two processes is accurate for planning the work?", "options": {"A": "Plasma can cut both materials, while oxy-fuel can cut only the carbon steel", "B": "Oxy-fuel can cut both materials, while plasma can cut only the aluminum plate", "C": "Both processes can cut either material with the same gas and the same settings", "D": "Neither process can cut aluminum without first heating the plate well above one thousand degrees F"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Plasma can cut both materials, while oxy-fuel can cut only the carbon steel): Plasma melts any conductive metal, so it handles steel and aluminum. Oxy-fuel needs a metal whose oxide melts below the base metal, which aluminum does not provide."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder must pierce a hole in the middle of a 1/2 inch steel plate with an oxy-fuel torch rather than starting at an edge. Molten slag keeps blowing back onto the nozzle and spraying the face shield. Which technique reduces the blowback during the pierce?", "options": {"A": "Preheat the spot, tilt the torch slightly, and open the cutting oxygen gradually", "B": "Hold the torch square to the plate and open the cutting oxygen lever at full flow", "C": "Lay the tip flat on the plate so slag cannot flow back up the nozzle", "D": "Start with the preheat flame turned off to keep the plate surface cooler during the pierce"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Preheat the spot, tilt the torch slightly, and open the cutting oxygen gradually): Tilting the torch slightly and bringing it upright as the pierce breaks through lets slag blow away from the nozzle. Pressing the lever fully at once or touching the nozzle to the plate invites blowback and backfires."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A new apprentice sets up an oxyacetylene cutting outfit in the school lab and asks the instructor about the regulator delivery limit for the fuel gas. Free acetylene can decompose violently when pressurized too far, so a ceiling applies at the torch hose. What working pressure is the upper limit for acetylene?", "options": {"A": "30 psig, the same ceiling that applies to propane and other liquefied fuel gases", "B": "15 psig, because acetylene can decompose at higher delivery pressures", "C": "5 psig, the point where flame speed starts to exceed gas velocity inside the cutting tip", "D": "25 psig, the pressure at which acetone starts to boil out of the acetylene cylinder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (15 psig, because acetylene can decompose at higher delivery pressures): Acetylene is unstable as a free gas above about 15 psig, which is why regulator delivery is limited there. Propane and similar liquefied gases do not share this decomposition hazard, so their limit does not apply."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder cuts a thick plate of medium-carbon steel with an oxy-fuel torch at room temperature. The cut edge is very hard and cracks appear along it afterward. Which measure would best reduce this problem on the next cut of the same plate?", "options": {"A": "Cool the plate with water immediately so the edge stays soft and ductile", "B": "Use a longer carburizing flame to add carbon and soften the cut edge", "C": "Preheat the whole plate before cutting so the cut edge cools more slowly", "D": "Cut faster with a smaller tip so that less heat enters the plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Preheat the whole plate before cutting so the cut edge cools more slowly): Medium and high carbon steels harden at a flame-cut edge when it cools quickly, so preheating the plate slows cooling and reduces cracking. Water quenching speeds the cooling and makes it worse."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A fabricator tries to cut 3/8 inch stainless steel plate with an oxyacetylene torch. The torch heats the plate and the oxygen lever is pressed, but a crust forms and the cut stalls inside the kerf. The supervisor asks for the technical reason. What explains the failure?", "options": {"A": "Stainless steel conducts heat away too slowly for the preheat flame to raise it to kindling", "B": "The oxygen jet cools stainless below its kindling point as it passes through the kerf", "C": "Chromium oxide melts above the base metal temperature and blocks the burning reaction", "D": "Stainless steel contains no iron, so the cutting reaction has nothing to burn"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chromium oxide melts above the base metal temperature and blocks the burning reaction): Chromium oxides melt well above the base metal, so they shield it and prevent a clean exothermic cut. Stainless does contain iron, and plasma arc cutting avoids the problem because it melts the metal rather than burning it."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A safety officer inspects the gas storage area in a welding lab and finds an acetylene cylinder lying on its side near the wall. The officer asks the class how acetylene cylinders must be stored and used. What is the correct position for the cylinder?", "options": {"A": "Lying flat on a rack, so the porous filler stays evenly wetted by acetone", "B": "Tilted about thirty degrees, so condensed water drains away from the valve", "C": "Inverted with the valve down, so liquid acetylene feeds directly into the regulator", "D": "Upright and secured, so the acetone inside stays in the cylinder rather than the hose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Upright and secured, so the acetone inside stays in the cylinder rather than the hose): Acetylene is dissolved in acetone held in a porous mass, so the cylinder stays upright to keep liquid acetone out of the regulator and torch. A cylinder that was laid down should stand upright for a while before use."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A shop runs a large heating tip on a single acetylene cylinder for several hours. Frost forms on the cylinder and the flame becomes weak and smelly with a yellow tint. The supervisor asks which draw-rate rule was ignored. What is the rule for pulling acetylene from one cylinder?", "options": {"A": "Draw no more than half of the cylinder contents per hour at room temperature", "B": "Draw acetylene only after the cylinder has warmed well above one hundred twenty degrees F", "C": "Draw no more than about one-seventh of the cylinder contents per hour of continuous use", "D": "Draw until the cylinder gauge reads zero, then change it for a fresh cylinder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Draw no more than about one-seventh of the cylinder contents per hour of continuous use): Drawing acetylene too fast pulls acetone out of the cylinder and can ruin the flame. The usual limit is about one-seventh of the contents each hour, so heavy users switch to a manifold."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder prepares to back-gouge a root pass in carbon steel plate using DC equipment and a copper-coated carbon electrode. The instructor asks for the polarity normally selected for this work. Which setting is typical for gouging carbon steel with this electrode?", "options": {"A": "Direct current electrode negative, using the copper-coated carbon electrode with no air supply", "B": "Direct current electrode positive, using the copper-coated carbon electrode", "C": "Alternating current with the air supply switched off and a bare graphite rod", "D": "Direct current electrode positive, using a thoriated tungsten electrode with argon shielding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Direct current electrode positive, using the copper-coated carbon electrode): Copper-coated carbon electrodes are normally run DCEP for gouging steel, and the coating improves current carrying and electrode life. Tungsten and argon belong to arc welding processes, not air carbon arc gouging."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "A welder feels an acetylene cylinder becoming warm to the touch with no flame nearby, and the surface keeps heating while the valve is closed. The welder fears internal decomposition. Which response is most appropriate in this situation for the welder and nearby workers?", "options": {"A": "Close the valve if safe, move it outside, cool it with water, and call for help", "B": "Carry it back to the storage cage and check it again after an hour has passed", "C": "Open the valve fully to vent the pressure into the closed shop room briefly", "D": "Seal the cylinder in a metal cabinet to starve the internal reaction of any air supply"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Close the valve if safe, move it outside, cool it with water, and call for help): A heating acetylene cylinder can be decomposing internally and may explode, so it is moved to a safe outdoor place, cooled with water and reported to emergency services. Venting indoors or sealing it only raises the danger."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "thermal_cutting_processes", "question": "Two apprentices hear a loud pop during a cut on 3/8 inch plate. One says the flame went out, the torch is quiet and cool, and it was only a backfire. The other says the flame is burning inside the handle with a squeal and calls it a flashback. What is the key difference?", "options": {"A": "A backfire burns inside the hoses, while a flashback only pops at the tip", "B": "A backfire extinguishes the flame, while a flashback keeps burning inside the torch", "C": "A flashback is silent and cool, while a backfire squeals and overheats the torch handle", "D": "A flashback relights itself after each pop, while a backfire needs a new striker spark"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A backfire extinguishes the flame, while a flashback keeps burning inside the torch): Backfire is a brief pop that puts the flame out, and flashback is a sustained internal flame with a shrill hiss that can reach the hoses. Mistaking the second for the first leaves a burning hose in the operator's hands."}, {"id": 521, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fabricator reads a plate edge at 5/8 inch on the tape and must record it in a decimal-inch fit-up table for a 3/8 inch carbon steel plate assembly before tacking begins. Layout sheets in this shop use decimals only. What decimal value belongs in the table for the 5/8 inch reading?", "options": {"A": "The reading converts to 0.85 inch, because the numerator and denominator are joined.", "B": "The reading converts to 0.58 inch, because the fraction is rounded down to hundredths.", "C": "The reading converts to 0.625 inch, which is five divided by eight.", "D": "The reading converts to 0.5625 inch, which is the decimal for nine sixteenths."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The reading converts to 0.625 inch, which is five divided by eight.): Dividing 5 by 8 gives 0.625. The 0.5625 value belongs to 9/16, a neighboring tape mark that is easy to confuse with 5/8 when reading quickly."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "An apprentice is told a bracket hole must be drilled 3/4 inch from the plate edge, but the drawing for the export customer is dimensioned in millimeters. Using 25.4 millimeters per inch, which value should the apprentice mark on the plate for the 3/4 inch dimension?", "options": {"A": "Mark the hole center at 33.87 millimeters from the edge of the plate.", "B": "Mark the hole center at 1.905 millimeters from the edge of the plate.", "C": "Mark the hole center at 19.5 millimeters from the edge of the plate.", "D": "Mark the hole center at 19.05 millimeters from the edge of the plate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mark the hole center at 19.05 millimeters from the edge of the plate.): Multiplying 0.75 by 25.4 gives 19.05 millimeters. Dividing 25.4 by 0.75 inverts the operation and overstates the distance, while 1.905 misplaces the decimal point."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A weld shop tracks rejected parts for a quarterly report. Out of 250 structural plates inspected this quarter, the shop recorded a 4 percent scrap rate after visual testing and sorting by the quality lead. How many plates were scrapped during the quarter according to that percentage?", "options": {"A": "Sixty two plates were scrapped, found by dividing 250 by 4.", "B": "One plate was scrapped, found by dividing 4 by 250 and rounding up.", "C": "Ten plates were scrapped, found by multiplying 250 by 0.04.", "D": "One hundred plates were scrapped, found by multiplying 250 by 0.4."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ten plates were scrapped, found by multiplying 250 by 0.04.): Four percent means 0.04, and 250 times 0.04 equals 10. Dividing by 4 or using 0.4 treats the percentage with the wrong decimal placement."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder is laying out a fillet weld that runs along the full edge of a rectangular base plate measuring 24 inches by 18 inches, and the foreman needs the total edge length for ordering wire. What is the perimeter of this rectangular base plate in inches?", "options": {"A": "The perimeter is 432 inches, found by multiplying the length and the width.", "B": "The perimeter is 42 inches, found by adding length and width once.", "C": "The perimeter is 66 inches, found by adding three of the four sides.", "D": "The perimeter is 84 inches, found by doubling the sum of both sides."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The perimeter is 84 inches, found by doubling the sum of both sides.): Perimeter equals 2 × (24 + 18) = 84 inches. The 432 figure is the area, and 42 results from forgetting to double the sum."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A coating crew will paint one face of a steel gusset that measures 12 inches by 9 inches, and the supervisor wants the face area to estimate how much primer the crew should open. What is the area of this rectangular gusset face in square inches?", "options": {"A": "The face area equals 42 square inches from doubling the sum of the sides.", "B": "The face area equals 21 square inches from adding the two sides together.", "C": "The face area equals 54 square inches from halving the product of the sides.", "D": "The face area equals 108 square inches from multiplying twelve by nine."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The face area equals 108 square inches from multiplying twelve by nine.): Area of a rectangle is length times width, and 12 × 9 = 108 square inches. The other values come from perimeter or sum errors, which are different measurements."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fitter is building an open steel test box that measures 10 inches long, 6 inches wide and 4 inches deep, and needs the interior space it will hold for a fluid leak test. How many cubic inches of volume does the box contain?", "options": {"A": "The box holds 248 cubic inches, found by totaling the surface area of all faces.", "B": "The box holds 240 cubic inches, found by multiplying all three dimensions.", "C": "The box holds 120 cubic inches, found by halving the final product.", "D": "The box holds 20 cubic inches, found by adding the three dimensions."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The box holds 240 cubic inches, found by multiplying all three dimensions.): Volume is length × width × depth, so 10 × 6 × 4 = 240 cubic inches. The 248 value is the total surface area, a different quantity measured in square inches."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A machinist measures a bushing wall at 0.4375 inch on the digital caliper and the traveler sheet asks for measurements rounded to the nearest hundredth of an inch. Which rounded value should be written on the sheet for this caliper reading of 0.4375 inch?", "options": {"A": "Write 0.44 inch, because the thousandths digit of seven rounds the hundredths place up.", "B": "Write 0.43 inch, because the extra digits are dropped without rounding.", "C": "Write 0.45 inch, because the five in the last place rounds upward twice.", "D": "Write 0.40 inch, because the traveler sheet only needs the tenths place."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Write 0.44 inch, because the thousandths digit of seven rounds the hundredths place up.): The digit after the hundredths place is 7, which is five or more, so 0.43 rounds up to 0.44. Truncating leaves 0.43, and rounding twice produces 0.45, which overshoots."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop mixes a shielding blend where the ratio of argon to carbon dioxide is 3 to 4 by volume in a test lab, and a technician has measured 9 units of argon so far. How many units of carbon dioxide match those 9 units of argon at the same ratio?", "options": {"A": "Twelve units of carbon dioxide, because both parts scale by a factor of three.", "B": "Thirteen units of carbon dioxide, because the difference between the parts is added.", "C": "Ten units of carbon dioxide, because the gap of one is kept constant.", "D": "Thirty six units of carbon dioxide, because the two numbers are multiplied."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Twelve units of carbon dioxide, because both parts scale by a factor of three.): Going from 3 to 9 multiplies by 3, so 4 becomes 12 to hold the ratio. Adding a fixed difference does not keep a ratio, which depends on multiplication."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop saws four equal pieces of 17 3/4 inches from a 72 inch bar using a blade that removes 1/8 inch per cut. Three cuts separate the four pieces, and the last piece ends at the bar end. How much material remains as scrap after cutting?", "options": {"A": "Five eighths of an inch remains after the pieces and three kerfs are removed.", "B": "One inch remains, because the saw kerf losses are left out of the sum.", "C": "One half inch remains, because four kerf cuts were counted instead of three.", "D": "Three eighths of an inch remains, because the kerf total was subtracted twice."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Five eighths of an inch remains after the pieces and three kerfs are removed.): Four pieces total 71 inches and three kerfs total 3/8 inch, so 72 minus 71 3/8 leaves 5/8 inch. Skipping the kerf gives a full inch, and counting four cuts gives 1/2."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A pipe fitter lays out a bracket from three segments measured on a tape: 2 3/16 inches, 1 7/8 inches and 3/4 inch. All values are in sixteenths on the tape, and the overall length is needed to cut a flat bar blank. What is the combined length of the three segments?", "options": {"A": "The total is 4 11/16 inches, because one eighth was converted incorrectly.", "B": "The total is 5 1/16 inches, because the numerators were added over the denominators.", "C": "The total is 4 15/16 inches, because three quarters was read as 14/16.", "D": "The total is 4 13/16 inches after converting each piece to sixteenths."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The total is 4 13/16 inches after converting each piece to sixteenths.): In sixteenths the pieces are 35, 30 and 12, totaling 77, which is 4 13/16. Adding denominators together or misconverting one piece shifts the total off by a sixteenth or more."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder has a 7 1/4 inch long flat bar and needs to trim a 3 9/16 inch section off one end for a gusset. The remaining piece will be tack welded into a fixture. How long is the remaining piece after the 3 9/16 inch section is removed?", "options": {"A": "The remaining piece measures 4 5/16 inches, because the fractions were subtracted in reverse order.", "B": "The remaining piece measures 3 5/16 inches, because the borrowed inch was left out.", "C": "The remaining piece measures 3 13/16 inches, because quarters were converted to eighths wrongly.", "D": "The remaining piece measures 3 11/16 inches after borrowing one inch to subtract."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The remaining piece measures 3 11/16 inches after borrowing one inch to subtract.): Rewrite 1/4 as 4/16, borrow 1 inch to make 20/16, and 20/16 minus 9/16 is 11/16, with 6 minus 3 giving 3. Reversing the subtraction of the fractions gives 5/16 and a wrong whole number."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fabricator ordering plate from a metric mill must state the thickness in millimeters, and the print calls for 1 1/2 inch plate for the base of a column. Using 25.4 millimeters per inch, what plate thickness in millimeters should the order list for this 1 1/2 inch requirement?", "options": {"A": "Order 16.9 millimeter plate, found by dividing 25.4 by the 1.5 value.", "B": "Order 3.81 millimeter plate, found by shifting the decimal point one place wrongly.", "C": "Order 40.6 millimeter plate, found by converting 1.6 inches in place of 1.5.", "D": "Order 38.1 millimeter plate, found by multiplying 1.5 by the 25.4 factor."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Order 38.1 millimeter plate, found by multiplying 1.5 by the 25.4 factor.): Thickness in millimeters equals 1.5 × 25.4 = 38.1. Dividing by 1.5 inverts the conversion, and shifting the decimal makes a plate ten times too thin."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing from overseas specifies 50 millimeter plate for a pressure vessel support, and the shop stock list only shows plate in inches to the nearest hundredth. Using 25.4 millimeters per inch, which stock thickness best matches the 50 millimeter requirement on the drawing?", "options": {"A": "Choose plate of about 0.51 inches, found by dividing 25.4 by 50.", "B": "Choose plate of about 1,270 inches, found by multiplying 50 by 25.4.", "C": "Choose plate of about 1.97 inches, found by dividing 50 by 25.4.", "D": "Choose plate of about 2.54 inches, found by multiplying 0.1 by 25.4."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Choose plate of about 1.97 inches, found by dividing 50 by 25.4.): Millimeters convert to inches by dividing by 25.4, so 50 ÷ 25.4 = 1.97 inches. Multiplying instead produces an absurd 1,270, and dividing the factor by 50 inverts the ratio."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop foreman needs 38 pounds of welding wire to deposit on a job and adds 12 percent for spatter and stub waste when placing the order. The supplier sells wire by the pound. How many pounds should the order list after adding the allowance to the 38 pound requirement?", "options": {"A": "Order 4.56 pounds, found by calculating only the allowance without the base amount.", "B": "Order 42.56 pounds, found by multiplying 38 by 1.12 to add the allowance.", "C": "Order 50 pounds, found by adding 12 pounds to the base amount.", "D": "Order 43.18 pounds, found by dividing 38 by 0.88 to remove the allowance."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Order 42.56 pounds, found by multiplying 38 by 1.12 to add the allowance.): Adding 12 percent means multiplying by 1.12, giving 42.56 pounds. The 4.56 figure is just the allowance, and adding a flat 12 pounds confuses percent with a count."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "During a bolt audit, an inspector rejects 6 of the 150 structural bolts pulled from a bin because of thread damage, and the report asks for the rejection rate as a percentage. What percentage of the inspected bolts were rejected in this audit sample?", "options": {"A": "Four percent were rejected, found by dividing 6 by 150 and multiplying by 100.", "B": "Twenty five percent were rejected, found by dividing 150 by 6.", "C": "Six percent were rejected, found by reading the rejected count as the rate.", "D": "Zero point zero four percent were rejected, found by skipping the final multiplication."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Four percent were rejected, found by dividing 6 by 150 and multiplying by 100.): Rate equals part divided by whole, so 6 ÷ 150 = 0.04, which is 4 percent. Inverting the division gives 25, and omitting the ×100 step leaves the decimal 0.04, which would wrongly be read as 0.04 percent."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A GMAW technician uses a mix of argon to carbon dioxide at a 3 to 1 ratio by volume and is filling a blend tank with 60 cubic feet in total. How many cubic feet of argon belong in the tank to keep the specified 3 to 1 ratio?", "options": {"A": "Add 20 cubic feet of argon, because the total was divided by the ratio of three.", "B": "Add 45 cubic feet of argon, because argon makes three of every four parts.", "C": "Add 15 cubic feet of argon, because that is the amount for one part.", "D": "Add 30 cubic feet of argon, because the two gases split the total evenly."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add 45 cubic feet of argon, because argon makes three of every four parts.): The mix has 4 parts total, so argon is 3/4 of 60, which equals 45 cubic feet. Dividing by 3 gives 20, which has no basis in the ratio, and a 30 split ignores the ratio."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A site drawing is printed at a scale of 1/4 inch equals 1 foot, and the welder measures a steel beam on the drawing at 3 1/2 inches. Using the scale, what is the actual length of the beam in feet shown by that 3 1/2 inch measurement?", "options": {"A": "The beam is 0.875 feet long, because 3.5 was multiplied by 0.25.", "B": "The beam is 14 feet long, because 3.5 divided by 0.25 gives 14.", "C": "The beam is 7 feet long, because 3.5 was doubled for the scale.", "D": "The beam is 28 feet long, because the scale was read as 1/8 inch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The beam is 14 feet long, because 3.5 divided by 0.25 gives 14.): Each 1/4 inch on the drawing is one foot, so 3.5 inches holds 14 quarter inch units. Multiplying by 0.25 shrinks the number instead of scaling it up."}, {"id": 538, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A flat steel panel measuring 96 inches by 60 inches will be seal welded around its full outer edge to a frame, and the estimator needs the weld length in inches to price the job. What total weld length is needed to go around the entire outer edge?", "options": {"A": "The weld runs 312 inches, which is twice the sum of the two sides.", "B": "The weld runs 156 inches, which is the sum of the sides only once.", "C": "The weld runs 5,760 inches, which is the product of the two sides.", "D": "The weld runs 216 inches, which is three of the four sides added."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The weld runs 312 inches, which is twice the sum of the two sides.): Going around a rectangle means 2 × (96 + 60) = 312 inches, or 26 feet. The 5,760 value is the panel area in square inches, which does not measure edge length."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fabrication crew will abrasive blast both faces of a flat steel sheet that measures 4 feet by 8 feet before coating. The coating contractor bills by the square foot of surface treated. How many square feet of surface will be blasted across both faces?", "options": {"A": "Sixty four square feet, which is twice the area of one 4 by 8 face.", "B": "Thirty two square feet, which counts only one face of the sheet.", "C": "Twenty four square feet, which is the perimeter of the sheet in feet.", "D": "One hundred twenty eight square feet, which doubles the total a second time."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sixty four square feet, which is twice the area of one 4 by 8 face.): One face is 4 × 8 = 32 square feet, and two faces make 64. The perimeter of 24 feet is a length, not an area, so it cannot price surface treatment."}, {"id": 540, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A pressure test uses an open steel trough measuring 36 inches long, 12 inches wide and 8 inches deep, and the test lead wants the capacity in US gallons using 231 cubic inches per gallon. About how many gallons will the full trough hold?", "options": {"A": "The trough holds about 7.5 gallons, found by dividing the volume by 462.", "B": "The trough holds about 30.0 gallons, found by dividing the volume by 115.", "C": "The trough holds about 15.0 gallons, found by dividing 3,456 by 231.", "D": "The trough holds about 3,456 gallons, found by skipping the unit conversion."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The trough holds about 15.0 gallons, found by dividing 3,456 by 231.): Volume is 36 × 12 × 8 = 3,456 cubic inches, and dividing by 231 gives about 14.96 gallons. Leaving out the division reports cubic inches as if they were gallons."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A 4 inch inside diameter pipe run is 10 feet long and will be flushed with cleaning solution before welding, so the lead needs its internal volume in cubic inches. Using 3.1416 for pi, what is the volume of this pipe run?", "options": {"A": "About 1,508 cubic inches, using pi times the radius squared times 120 inches.", "B": "About 6,032 cubic inches, using the diameter squared in place of the radius.", "C": "About 754 cubic inches, using only 60 inches as the run length.", "D": "About 480 cubic inches, using the radius squared without the pi factor."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 1,508 cubic inches, using pi times the radius squared times 120 inches.): Radius is 2 inches and length is 120 inches, so V = π × 2² × 120 ≈ 1,508 cubic inches. Squaring the diameter makes the answer four times too large."}, {"id": 542, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A cut list reads 7.45 inches, and the saw stop on this shop's bandsaw is marked only in sixteenths of an inch. A fitter wants the nearest sixteenth mark to the listed length. Which fractional reading best matches 7.45 inches on the saw stop?", "options": {"A": "Set the stop at 7 7/16 inches, since 7.45 lies closest to 7.4375.", "B": "Set the stop at 7 1/2 inches, since the tenths digit is read as a half.", "C": "Set the stop at 7 3/8 inches, since the decimal is truncated to eighths.", "D": "Set the stop at 7 9/16 inches, since the value rounds up a mark."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set the stop at 7 7/16 inches, since 7.45 lies closest to 7.4375.): Multiplying 0.45 by 16 gives 7.2 sixteenths, which rounds to 7, so 7 7/16 inches. The 7 1/2 mark is 0.0500 away, farther than the 0.0125 error at 7/16."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A digital readout shows a gap of 0.8125 inch, but the fitter works from a tape marked only in sixteenths and needs the fractional equivalent to cut a spacer. Which fraction on the tape equals the 0.8125 inch reading shown on the readout?", "options": {"A": "The spacer should measure 4/5 inch, because the readout is rounded to the tenths.", "B": "The spacer should measure 7/8 inch, because the value is rounded to the next eighth.", "C": "The spacer should measure 11/16 inch, because 0.8125 is mistaken for 0.6875.", "D": "The spacer should measure 13/16 inch, because 0.8125 times 16 equals 13."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The spacer should measure 13/16 inch, because 0.8125 times 16 equals 13.): Multiply the decimal by 16 to count sixteenths: 0.8125 × 16 = 13. A tape reader who rounds to eighths loses the exact 1/16 detail and overcuts the spacer."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A design change reduces a base plate thickness from 0.500 inch to 0.375 inch to save weight on a skid frame, and the engineer's note asks for the percent decrease in thickness. What percent decrease does the change represent against the original plate thickness?", "options": {"A": "A 25 percent decrease, found by dividing the 0.125 inch change by 0.500.", "B": "A 33 percent decrease, found by dividing the change by the new thickness.", "C": "A 12.5 percent decrease, found by reading the change in eighths as percent.", "D": "A 37.5 percent decrease, found by using the new thickness as a percentage."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A 25 percent decrease, found by dividing the 0.125 inch change by 0.500.): Percent change uses the original value as the base, so 0.125 ÷ 0.500 = 0.25. Dividing by the new thickness inflates the result to 33 percent."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop measured that 12 feet of a fillet weld on a rail section consumed 3 pounds of wire, and a new rail needs 50 feet of the same weld at the same settings. How many pounds of wire should the welder pull for the 50 foot run?", "options": {"A": "Pull 200 pounds, since the feet per pound ratio was applied in reverse.", "B": "Pull 16.7 pounds, since one third of the length was used as pounds.", "C": "Pull 41 pounds, since the extra footage was added to the original pounds.", "D": "Pull 12.5 pounds, since wire use scales in direct proportion to weld length."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pull 12.5 pounds, since wire use scales in direct proportion to weld length.): The ratio is 3 pounds per 12 feet, so 50 feet needs 50 × 3 ÷ 12 = 12.5 pounds. Adding the extra footage to the pounds mixes two different units."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A 6 inch outside diameter pipe nipple will be seal welded around its full circumference to a flange face, and the estimator needs the bead length per joint. Using 3.1416 for pi, about how long is the weld bead around the pipe in inches?", "options": {"A": "The bead is about 28.27 inches, found by using pi times the radius squared.", "B": "The bead is about 37.70 inches, found by multiplying pi by twice the diameter.", "C": "The bead is about 9.42 inches, found by multiplying pi by the radius.", "D": "The bead is about 18.85 inches, found by multiplying pi by the 6 inch diameter."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The bead is about 18.85 inches, found by multiplying pi by the 6 inch diameter.): Circumference equals π × diameter, so 3.1416 × 6 ≈ 18.85 inches. The 28.27 figure is a circle's area, and 9.42 uses the radius where the diameter belongs."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A print requires a minimum plate thickness of 1/2 inch for a lifting lug, and the supplier delivers plate stamped 12 millimeters thick. The receiving inspector must decide whether this plate meets the minimum. Using 25.4 millimeters per inch, which statement is correct about the delivered plate?", "options": {"A": "It falls short, because 12 millimeters is about 0.472 inch, under the half inch minimum.", "B": "It meets the minimum, because 12 is a larger number than the 1/2 requirement.", "C": "It falls short, because 12 millimeters is only about 0.305 inch in thickness.", "D": "It meets the minimum, because 12 millimeters rounds up to 12.7 millimeters."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It falls short, because 12 millimeters is about 0.472 inch, under the half inch minimum.): Half an inch is 12.7 millimeters, and 12 ÷ 25.4 gives 0.472 inch, so the plate is thin by 0.7 millimeter. Comparing 12 to 1/2 ignores that the two numbers use different units."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "Three bars of 4 5/8 inches, 6 3/16 inches and 2 1/4 inches must be set end to end in a machine bed opening that is exactly 13 inches wide, with no cutting allowed. What does the total of the bars show about the fit in the opening?", "options": {"A": "The stack is 12 15/16 inches, so it fits with a sixteenth to spare.", "B": "The stack is 13 1/16 inches, so it overruns the opening by one sixteenth.", "C": "The stack is 13 1/8 inches, so it overruns the opening by an eighth.", "D": "The stack is 13 3/8 inches, so it overruns the opening by three eighths."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The stack is 13 1/16 inches, so it overruns the opening by one sixteenth.): In sixteenths the bars are 74, 99 and 36, which total 209, or 13 1/16 inches. A one sixteenth carry error is enough to turn a failure into an apparent fit."}, {"id": 549, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A procurement lead must deposit 57 pounds of weld metal on a pressure frame, and the process has a 95 percent deposit efficiency, meaning 95 percent of purchased wire ends up in the joint. How many pounds of wire must be purchased to deposit 57 pounds?", "options": {"A": "Purchase 60 pounds, found by dividing the 57 pounds needed by 0.95.", "B": "Purchase 59.85 pounds, found by adding 5 percent to the 57 pounds.", "C": "Purchase 54.15 pounds, found by taking 95 percent of the 57 pounds.", "D": "Purchase 57 pounds, found by assuming all wire becomes deposited weld metal."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Purchase 60 pounds, found by dividing the 57 pounds needed by 0.95.): Efficiency is deposited divided by purchased, so purchased equals 57 ÷ 0.95 = 60 pounds. Adding 5 percent to the deposit understates the need, because the 5 percent loss applies to the purchased amount."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "An engineer upgrades a fillet weld from a 1/4 inch leg to a 3/8 inch leg, and the estimator knows the cross section of an equal leg fillet scales with the square of the leg size. How much more filler per inch is needed for the larger weld?", "options": {"A": "About 2.25 times as much, since the leg ratio of 1.5 is squared.", "B": "About 1.5 times as much, since filler scales directly with the leg size.", "C": "About 3.0 times as much, since the two leg sizes are added together.", "D": "About 1.25 times as much, since only the 1/8 inch increase is counted."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 2.25 times as much, since the leg ratio of 1.5 is squared.): A fillet area is proportional to leg squared, so (3/8 ÷ 1/4)² = 1.5² = 2.25. Treating filler as proportional to leg length understates the cost of larger welds."}, {"id": 551, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A coating spec for a blasted skid base lists a surface of 2,160 square inches for a plate assembly, but the paint supplier sells coverage by the square foot. Which value correctly converts the plate assembly surface so the quote matches the spec?", "options": {"A": "Use 180 square feet, because the conversion factor of 12 was applied.", "B": "Use 25,920 square feet, because the area was multiplied by 12 twice.", "C": "Use 15 square feet, because 144 square inches make up one square foot.", "D": "Use 216 square feet, because the decimal point was moved one place only."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use 15 square feet, because 144 square inches make up one square foot.): A square foot is 12 × 12 = 144 square inches, so 2,160 ÷ 144 = 15. Dividing by only 12 forgets that area conversions square the linear factor."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welded fluid test tank measures 2 feet by 1 foot by 1 1/2 feet inside, and the written test procedure records capacity in cubic inches. Which value gives the capacity of the tank in cubic inches for the test record?", "options": {"A": "The tank holds 36 cubic inches, since the 3 cubic feet were multiplied by 12.", "B": "The tank holds 432 cubic inches, since the 3 cubic feet were multiplied by 144.", "C": "The tank holds 5,184 cubic inches, since a cubic foot has 1,728 cubic inches.", "D": "The tank holds 3 cubic inches, since the unit label was simply changed."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The tank holds 5,184 cubic inches, since a cubic foot has 1,728 cubic inches.): The volume is 3 cubic feet, and each cubic foot is 12 × 12 × 12 = 1,728 cubic inches, giving 5,184. Using 12 or 144 applies a length or area factor to a volume."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fitter rounds twelve spacer lengths from 2.46 inches up to 2.50 inches each for convenience on the bandsaw, and the pieces will be stacked in a fixture with a limited opening. How much extra stack length does the rounding add in total?", "options": {"A": "The stack grows by 0.04 inch, since only a single spacer is affected.", "B": "The stack grows by 0.24 inch, since half of the spacers are rounded.", "C": "The stack grows by 4.8 inches, since the decimal point is placed one place off.", "D": "The stack grows by 0.48 inch, since each of the twelve pieces gains 0.04 inch."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The stack grows by 0.48 inch, since each of the twelve pieces gains 0.04 inch.): Each spacer is 0.04 inch long, and twelve of them add 12 × 0.04 = 0.48 inch. Small rounding errors on repeated parts add up and can jam a fixture."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A tank builder compares a 2 inch outside diameter nozzle with a 4 inch outside diameter nozzle, both sized by diameter only, and wants to know how the solid cross section area of the larger one compares with the smaller. How many times larger is the cross section area?", "options": {"A": "Two times larger, because area changes in step with the diameter.", "B": "Eight times larger, because the diameter is cubed to find the area.", "C": "Three times larger, because the diameters differ by two inches.", "D": "Four times larger, because area depends on the square of the diameter."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Four times larger, because area depends on the square of the diameter.): Area is proportional to diameter squared, so doubling the diameter gives 2² = 4 times the area. Circumference only doubles, which is why a weld length and a metal area scale differently."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A frame is built from metric drawings as a rectangle 2.5 meters by 1.2 meters, and the inch based tape in the shop is used to check the total edge length of the rectangle. Using 0.0254 meter per inch, about how many inches is the full perimeter?", "options": {"A": "About 291.3 inches, from a perimeter of 7.4 meters divided by 0.0254.", "B": "About 145.7 inches, from only half of the perimeter being converted.", "C": "About 29.1 inches, from the decimal point being placed one place off.", "D": "About 7,400 inches, from the meters being read as millimeters then used."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 291.3 inches, from a perimeter of 7.4 meters divided by 0.0254.): The perimeter is 2 × (2.5 + 1.2) = 7.4 meters, and dividing by 0.0254 gives about 291.3 inches. Converting only half of the edge or misplacing a decimal point changes the answer by a large factor."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A part is drawn at a 1 to 8 reduction, meaning every inch on the paper represents 8 inches on the real part, and the welder measures a feature at 5.25 inches on the drawing. What is the actual length of that feature on the part?", "options": {"A": "The feature is 0.66 inch long, found by dividing the drawing value by 8.", "B": "The feature is 42 inches long, found by multiplying the drawing value by 8.", "C": "The feature is 13.25 inches long, found by adding 8 to the drawing value.", "D": "The feature is 84 inches long, found by multiplying the value by 16."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The feature is 42 inches long, found by multiplying the drawing value by 8.): A reduction scale means the real part is larger, so 5.25 × 8 = 42 inches. Dividing by 8 reverses the direction of the scale and shrinks the feature instead."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A mill certificate lists a plate nominal thickness of 0.375 inch with a tolerance of minus 10 percent, and the inspector reads 0.340 inch on a micrometer at the plate edge of the delivered lot. Based on those numbers, does the plate meet the lower tolerance limit?", "options": {"A": "No, because the lower limit works out to 0.3650 inch for the plate.", "B": "Yes, because the lower limit is 0.3375 inch and the reading sits above it.", "C": "No, because the lower limit works out to 0.3500 inch for the plate.", "D": "No, because the tolerance allows only 5 percent, giving a limit of 0.3563 inch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because the lower limit is 0.3375 inch and the reading sits above it.): Ten percent of 0.375 is 0.0375, so the lower limit is 0.3375 inch, and 0.340 is above it. Limits of 0.350 or 0.365 come from subtracting a flat amount instead of a percentage."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing calls for a 3/4 inch bolt in a hole that the shop drilled to 19 millimeters because it was the nearest metric bit on hand, and the inspector needs to judge whether the bolt can pass through. What does the conversion show?", "options": {"A": "The hole is about 0.775 inch, so the bolt passes with some clearance.", "B": "The hole is about 0.75 inch, so the bolt passes with a snug fit.", "C": "The hole is about 0.748 inch, so the 0.750 inch bolt will not pass.", "D": "The hole is about 0.48 inch, so the bolt is far too large to enter."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The hole is about 0.748 inch, so the 0.750 inch bolt will not pass.): Dividing 19 by 25.4 gives 0.748 inch, which is 0.002 inch smaller than the bolt, so it will not pass. Rounding the hole to 0.75 hides a difference that matters on a clearance fit."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A 6 inch outside diameter pipe 20 feet long needs its entire outer surface blasted, and the contractor bills by the square foot. Using 3.1416 for pi and the lateral surface formula of pi times diameter times length, about how many square feet will be billed?", "options": {"A": "About 377 square feet, because the length stayed in feet but the diameter in inches.", "B": "About 31.4 square feet, after converting 20 feet to 240 inches and dividing by 144.", "C": "About 15.7 square feet, because the radius was used in place of the diameter.", "D": "About 62.8 square feet, because the area was counted for both sides of the pipe."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 31.4 square feet, after converting 20 feet to 240 inches and dividing by 144.): The surface is π × 6 × 240 ≈ 4,524 square inches, and dividing by 144 gives about 31.4 square feet. Mixing feet and inches in one product gives a number that is neither unit."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A stack of seven plates each 5/16 inch thick must fit in a clamp gap of 2 1/4 inches, and the fixture needs at least 1/16 inch of clearance to slide the plates in. Which result is correct for the seven plate stack in the clamp gap?", "options": {"A": "The stack is 2 3/16 inches, leaving exactly the 1/16 inch of clearance needed.", "B": "The stack is 2 5/16 inches, so it overfills the gap by 1/16 inch.", "C": "The stack is 1 7/8 inches, so it leaves 3/8 inch of clearance.", "D": "The stack is 2 1/2 inches, so it overfills the gap by 1/4 inch."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The stack is 2 3/16 inches, leaving exactly the 1/16 inch of clearance needed.): Seven plates of 5/16 inch total 35/16 inch, which is 2 3/16 inches, and 2 1/4 minus 2 3/16 leaves 1/16 inch. Counting six or eight plates produces the other totals."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welding engineer hands a shop supervisor a procedure sheet that lists volts, amps and travel speed in inches per minute and asks for the arc energy delivered to each inch of joint. Using the standard heat input equation with the 60 and 1000 constants, which unit does the answer carry when expressed correctly?", "options": {"A": "Kilojoules per inch, because the 60 converts minutes to seconds and 1000 converts joules.", "B": "Joules per square inch, because the equation divides energy by the plate surface area covered.", "C": "Volts per inch, because travel speed divides directly into the arc voltage reading.", "D": "Watts per minute, because amps and volts multiply to power and time cancels out."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Kilojoules per inch, because the 60 converts minutes to seconds and 1000 converts joules.): Volts times amps gives watts, which is joules per second. Multiplying by 60 turns the minute-based travel speed into seconds, and dividing by 1000 reports kilojoules per inch. The other units ignore that the result is energy delivered per length of joint."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fabricator must estimate weld metal for hundreds of feet of equal-leg fillet welds on structural tubing and starts by treating the cross-section as a right triangle with two equal legs. Which expression gives the area of that triangular profile for a leg length L in inches?", "options": {"A": "The leg length multiplied by itself, since both legs span the full square corner.", "B": "The leg length multiplied by the throat length, taking the throat as the triangle height.", "C": "Three quarters of the leg length multiplied by itself, allowing for convex weld face.", "D": "Half of the leg length multiplied by itself, as for any triangle with a square corner."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Half of the leg length multiplied by itself, as for any triangle with a square corner.): A fillet profile is a right triangle whose base and height are both the leg size, so its area is one half of L squared. Using the full L squared doubles the estimate, and leg times throat does not describe any side pair of this triangle."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "On a drawing, an equal-leg flat-face fillet weld is dimensioned by leg size, but the design strength check uses effective throat. A welder with a 3/8 inch leg wants the quick multiplier that converts leg size to theoretical throat for this flat profile. Which multiplier applies?", "options": {"A": "About 1.414, since the face length is the root two multiple of the leg.", "B": "About 0.500, since the throat is half the leg for an equal-leg profile.", "C": "About 0.866, since the throat follows a 30 degree triangle relationship.", "D": "About 0.707, since the throat is the perpendicular distance from the corner to the face."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 0.707, since the throat is the perpendicular distance from the corner to the face.): The throat is the shortest distance from the root to the weld face, which for a 45 degree right triangle equals the leg times 0.707. The 1.414 figure is the face length, not the throat, and 0.866 belongs to a 30-60-90 triangle."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A pipe fitter prepares a butt joint on carbon steel pipe using a 37.5 degree bevel on each pipe end, a common standard prep for pipe welding. When the two beveled ends are brought together, what included groove angle results at the joint?", "options": {"A": "Thirty-seven and a half degrees, because the groove shares one bevel angle.", "B": "Fifty-two and a half degrees, because the bevel angles are subtracted from ninety.", "C": "Seventy-five degrees, because the two bevel angles add together across the groove.", "D": "Sixty degrees, because pipe groove included angles are set equal to plate groove angles."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Seventy-five degrees, because the two bevel angles add together across the groove.): The included angle of a groove is the sum of the bevels on both members, so two 37.5 degree bevels make a 75 degree groove. A 60 degree included angle would need 30 degree bevels, which is the common plate prep rather than the pipe prep."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A fitter lays out a two-piece 90 degree mitered elbow from straight carbon steel pipe, cutting two identical segments that meet at a single mitered joint. What angle should each segment's end be cut from square so the joint turns the line exactly 90 degrees?", "options": {"A": "Ninety degrees, because each pipe end must be cut at the full turn angle.", "B": "Thirty degrees, because the cut angle is one third of the total turn.", "C": "Forty-five degrees, because the single joint turns the line and each cut takes half.", "D": "Sixty degrees, because pipe miters are laid out on a six-sided pattern."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Forty-five degrees, because the single joint turns the line and each cut takes half.): At a miter joint, the two cut faces share the turn equally, so each cut is half of the turn the joint produces. One joint turning 90 degrees therefore needs two 45 degree cuts, which together bend the run a full quarter turn."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "Pipe is ordered by nominal pipe size, a label that does not directly equal any measured dimension for sizes through 12 inch. A weld inspector holds a caliper to a 4 NPS pipe end of any schedule. What outside diameter does the caliper read?", "options": {"A": "About 4.500 inches, the fixed outside diameter for that nominal size at every wall thickness.", "B": "About 4.026 inches, the inside diameter of the standard schedule 40 wall.", "C": "Exactly 4.000 inches, since the nominal size is the outside measurement.", "D": "About 5.563 inches, the outside diameter of the next larger nominal size."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 4.500 inches, the fixed outside diameter for that nominal size at every wall thickness.): For NPS 4 the outside diameter is fixed at 4.500 inches, and only the wall thickness changes with schedule. The 4.026 inch figure is the schedule 40 inside diameter, and 5.563 inches belongs to NPS 5."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welding supervisor compares GMAW and SMAW costs and the purchasing sheet lists deposition efficiency for each process next to the cost per pound. A trainee asks what the percentage actually represents when a spool or box of filler metal is purchased and used up on a job.", "options": {"A": "Weld metal deposited divided by the base metal weight of the joined plates.", "B": "Arc-on time divided by total time on the clock during the shift.", "C": "Heat delivered to the joint divided by the electrical energy drawn from the machine.", "D": "Weld metal weight deposited divided by the weight of filler purchased and consumed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weld metal weight deposited divided by the weight of filler purchased and consumed.): Deposition efficiency compares the filler that actually ends up in the weld with the filler that was bought, with the difference lost as spatter, stubs, slag or fume. Arc-on time is the operating factor, and heat delivered to the joint is arc efficiency."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "Before estimating the weight of weld metal in a carbon steel groove weld, a trainee converts the joint volume in cubic inches to pounds. Weld deposits of mild steel have nearly the same density as the plate. Which density value should be used for this conversion?", "options": {"A": "About 0.098 pounds per cubic inch, the usual figure for aluminum alloys.", "B": "About 0.323 pounds per cubic inch, the usual figure for copper alloys.", "C": "About 0.284 pounds per cubic inch, the usual figure for carbon steel.", "D": "About 0.0284 pounds per cubic inch, the steel figure scaled for weld porosity."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 0.284 pounds per cubic inch, the usual figure for carbon steel.): Carbon and low alloy steel weigh about 0.284 pound per cubic inch, so weld volume multiplied by 0.284 gives pounds. The 0.098 value is aluminum and 0.323 is copper, and no porosity scaling shifts the decimal place."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A structural shop welds an equal-leg fillet with a 3/8 inch leg size on a W-shape beam flange to a base plate. The estimator wants the cross-sectional area of the weld profile before multiplying by length, treating the face as flat. What area results?", "options": {"A": "Roughly 0.141 square inch, found as the leg multiplied by itself.", "B": "Roughly 0.265 square inch, found by multiplying the leg by 0.707.", "C": "Roughly 0.035 square inch, found as a quarter of the leg squared.", "D": "Roughly 0.070 square inch, found as half of the leg squared."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Roughly 0.070 square inch, found as half of the leg squared.): The area of an equal-leg fillet is one half of the leg squared, so 0.375 squared is 0.1406 and half of that is 0.0703 square inch. Multiplying the leg by 0.707 gives the throat length, which is a distance and not an area."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A tank fabricator quotes continuous 1/4 inch equal-leg fillet welds along a baffle plate and needs the weld metal volume in one foot of that weld, with a flat face assumed. The estimator already knows the profile area is half the leg squared.", "options": {"A": "About 0.750 cubic inch, from 0.0625 square inch multiplied by twelve inches.", "B": "About 0.031 cubic inch, from the profile area carried over for one inch of length.", "C": "About 4.500 cubic inches, from 0.375 square inch multiplied by twelve inches.", "D": "About 0.375 cubic inch, from 0.03125 square inch multiplied by twelve inches."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 0.375 cubic inch, from 0.03125 square inch multiplied by twelve inches.): Half of 0.25 squared is 0.03125 square inch, and a foot of weld is twelve inches long, so the volume is 0.375 cubic inch. Dropping the one half doubles the answer, and skipping the length multiplier leaves only the area."}, {"id": 571, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A boiler shop deposits a 3/8 inch equal-leg fillet using carbon steel filler. For a weight-based filler order the estimator needs pounds of weld metal in one foot of that weld, using 0.284 pound per cubic inch and a flat face.", "options": {"A": "About 0.48 pound per foot, from 1.69 cubic inches of steel.", "B": "About 0.020 pound per foot, from the profile area without the length factor.", "C": "About 0.24 pound per foot, from 0.844 cubic inch of steel.", "D": "About 0.84 pound per foot, from the cubic inches taken as pounds directly."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 0.24 pound per foot, from 0.844 cubic inch of steel.): Area is half of 0.375 squared, or 0.0703 square inch, and a foot holds 0.844 cubic inch. Multiplying by 0.284 gives 0.24 pound. Leaving out the density treats volume as weight, and using the full leg squared doubles the result."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A contractor plans 120 feet of 3/8 inch equal-leg fillet welds with flux-cored wire on a machine skid frame. Weld metal weighs 0.24 pound per foot, and the wire deposits at 90 percent efficiency. How much wire should be ordered to finish this run?", "options": {"A": "About 26 pounds, from 28.8 pounds of weld metal multiplied by 0.90.", "B": "About 29 pounds, from the weld metal weight alone with losses ignored.", "C": "About 32 pounds, from 28.8 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.90.", "D": "About 58 pounds, from doubling the weld metal weight to cover spatter and stubs."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 32 pounds, from 28.8 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.90.): The weld holds 120 times 0.24, or 28.8 pounds. Since only 90 percent of the wire reaches the joint, the purchase is 28.8 divided by 0.90, or 32 pounds. Multiplying by 0.90 would order less wire than ends up in the weld."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop will weld 200 feet of 1/4 inch equal-leg fillet using solid wire GMAW. Weld metal weighs 0.1065 pound per foot and deposition efficiency for the wire is 95 percent. Calculate the quantity of wire to consume for the job.", "options": {"A": "About 22.4 pounds, from 21.3 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.95.", "B": "About 21.3 pounds, from the weld metal weight with no loss allowance.", "C": "About 20.2 pounds, from 21.3 pounds of weld metal multiplied by 0.95.", "D": "About 44.8 pounds, from doubling the answer for spatter allowance and spool tail ends."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 22.4 pounds, from 21.3 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.95.): Two hundred feet at 0.1065 pound per foot is 21.3 pounds in the joint. Wire consumed equals deposited weight divided by efficiency, so 21.3 divided by 0.95 is 22.4 pounds. Multiplying by 0.95 would suggest fewer pounds were consumed than were deposited."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder runs 0.035 inch solid wire at a wire feed speed of 300 inches per minute. A reference chart lists the wire weight as 0.00027 pound per inch. Assuming all wire melts into the joint, what hourly weight of filler is fed to the arc?", "options": {"A": "About 0.08 pounds per hour, from the per-minute figure without a time conversion.", "B": "About 4.9 pounds per hour, from 0.081 pound per minute multiplied by sixty.", "C": "About 8.1 pounds per hour, from 0.081 multiplied by one hundred.", "D": "About 81 pounds per hour, from wire weight times feed speed times one thousand."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 4.9 pounds per hour, from 0.081 pound per minute multiplied by sixty.): Feed speed times weight per inch gives 300 times 0.00027, or 0.081 pound per minute. Over sixty minutes that is 4.86 pounds, about 4.9. The smaller choice stops at the per-minute number, and the larger ones use an invented multiplier."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A pipe welder uses 0.045 inch solid wire at 350 inches per minute for fill passes. The wire chart lists 0.00045 pound per inch. Ignoring spatter, what is the amount of filler fed into the arc during one hour of continuous arcing at this setting?", "options": {"A": "About 5.7 pounds, from the weight per inch listed for thinner 0.035 wire.", "B": "About 0.16 pounds, from the weight fed in a single minute of arcing.", "C": "About 9.5 pounds, from 0.1575 pound per minute multiplied by sixty minutes.", "D": "About 94.5 pounds, from the per-minute weight multiplied by six hundred minutes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 9.5 pounds, from 0.1575 pound per minute multiplied by sixty minutes.): At 350 inches per minute and 0.00045 pound per inch, wire feeds at 0.1575 pound per minute, which is 9.45 pounds per hour. The 5.7 figure comes from using the weight of a thinner wire, so the wire diameter in the chart matters."}, {"id": 576, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A procedure requires 6 pounds per hour of 0.035 inch solid wire deposited with negligible losses. The wire weighs 0.00027 pound per inch. What wire feed speed in inches per minute should the welder set to meet that hourly rate?", "options": {"A": "About 22,000 inches per minute, from hourly weight divided by weight per inch.", "B": "About 370 inches per minute, from 0.10 pound per minute divided by 0.00027.", "C": "About 740 inches per minute, from doubling the figure to cover spatter.", "D": "About 185 inches per minute, from halving the hourly rate before dividing."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 370 inches per minute, from 0.10 pound per minute divided by 0.00027.): Six pounds per hour is 0.10 pound per minute, and dividing by 0.00027 pound per inch gives about 370 inches per minute. Skipping the hour-to-minute conversion produces an answer that is sixty times too large."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder runs flux-cored wire at 24 volts and 220 amps and travels at 10 inches per minute on a plate. The procedure qualification record lists heat input in kilojoules per inch. Using volts times amps times 60 divided by travel speed times 1000, determine the heat input.", "options": {"A": "About 0.53 kJ per inch, from 5,280 divided by 10,000.", "B": "About 316.8 kJ per inch, from 316,800 divided by 1,000 alone.", "C": "About 63.4 kJ per inch, from halving the travel speed in the formula.", "D": "About 31.7 kJ per inch, from 316,800 divided by 10,000."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 31.7 kJ per inch, from 316,800 divided by 10,000.): Volts times amps is 5,280 watts, and multiplying by 60 gives 316,800. Dividing by travel speed times 1000, which is 10,000, gives 31.68 kJ per inch. Leaving out the factor of 60 shrinks the answer by sixty."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder runs GMAW at 22 volts and 180 amps and moves at 12 inches per minute along a joint. Find the arc heat input in kilojoules per inch with the standard equation, since the procedure specification for this welded bracket lists the result as an essential variable.", "options": {"A": "About 0.33 kJ per inch, from 3,960 divided by 12,000.", "B": "About 39.6 kJ per inch, from halving the travel speed in the formula.", "C": "About 237.6 kJ per inch, from dividing by one thousand only.", "D": "About 19.8 kJ per inch, from 237,600 divided by 12,000."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 19.8 kJ per inch, from 237,600 divided by 12,000.): Twenty-two volts times 180 amps is 3,960 watts. Multiplying by 60 gives 237,600, and dividing by 12 times 1000 gives 19.8 kJ per inch. The 0.33 value omits the 60 that converts minutes to seconds."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A procedure for a quenched and tempered steel plate caps heat input at 45 kJ per inch. The welder uses 28 volts and 250 amps. What is the slowest travel speed in inches per minute that still keeps within the cap?", "options": {"A": "About 9.3 inches per minute, from 420,000 divided by 45,000.", "B": "About 18.7 inches per minute, from doubling the answer after dividing.", "C": "About 4.7 inches per minute, from halving the quotient of the same figures.", "D": "About 0.16 inches per minute, from dropping the factor of sixty entirely."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 9.3 inches per minute, from 420,000 divided by 45,000.): Rearranging the heat input equation gives travel speed equal to volts times amps times 60 divided by heat input times 1000. That is 7,000 times 60 over 45,000, or 9.33 inches per minute, and any slower travel exceeds the cap."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "On a flat plate, a welder deposits a 15 inch long stringer bead in 75 seconds with the gun moving steadily. The procedure sheet requires the travel speed in inches per minute for the heat input calculation. What travel speed was used?", "options": {"A": "Five inches per minute, from the seconds required per inch of bead.", "B": "1,125 inches per minute, from multiplying the length by the seconds.", "C": "Twelve inches per minute, from 15 inches divided by 1.25 minutes.", "D": "0.2 inch per minute, from the speed per second labeled as per minute."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Twelve inches per minute, from 15 inches divided by 1.25 minutes.): Seventy-five seconds is 1.25 minutes, and 15 inches in 1.25 minutes is 12 inches per minute. The value of 0.2 is the correct speed per second, but heat input needs minutes, and 5 is a time per inch rather than a speed."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "Two 3/4 inch plates are each beveled 30 degrees, leaving a 1/8 inch root face on each, and are set with a 1/8 inch root opening for a single-V groove weld. What is the groove width at the top surface?", "options": {"A": "About 0.72 inch, from two bevel widths with the root opening left out.", "B": "About 0.85 inch, from two bevel widths of 0.361 plus the root opening.", "C": "About 0.99 inch, from using full plate thickness instead of bevel depth.", "D": "About 0.49 inch, from a single bevel width plus the root opening."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 0.85 inch, from two bevel widths of 0.361 plus the root opening.): Bevel depth is 0.75 minus the 0.125 root face, or 0.625 inch, and the horizontal run is 0.625 times tan 30 degrees, or 0.361 inch per plate. Two runs plus the 0.125 root opening give 0.847 inch at the top."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop welds a full-penetration girth joint on 6 inch nominal pipe size carbon steel with an outside diameter of 6.625 inches. For planning arc time the estimator needs the distance the torch travels around the joint. What circumference results?", "options": {"A": "About 19.1 inches, from pi multiplied by the 6.065 inch inside diameter.", "B": "About 20.8 inches, from pi multiplied by the outside diameter.", "C": "About 18.8 inches, from pi multiplied by the nominal size of six.", "D": "About 41.6 inches, from two times pi multiplied by the outside diameter."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 20.8 inches, from pi multiplied by the outside diameter.): Circumference is pi times diameter, and the pipe's real outside diameter is 6.625 inches, giving 20.8 inches. Using the nominal size understates the distance, and doubling pi ignores that the diameter, not the radius, is being used."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder completes a pass on a pipe girth joint with a 20.8 inch circumference. The pass travels at an average of 5 inches per minute around the pipe. How long does one pass take to travel once around the joint?", "options": {"A": "About 104 minutes, from multiplying circumference by travel speed.", "B": "About 3.8 minutes, from using the six inch nominal size with pi.", "C": "About 0.24 minute, from five divided by the circumference.", "D": "About 4.2 minutes, from 20.8 inches divided by 5 inches per minute."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 4.2 minutes, from 20.8 inches divided by 5 inches per minute.): Time equals distance divided by speed, so 20.8 inches at 5 inches per minute takes 4.16 minutes. Multiplying the two numbers has no physical meaning here, and inverting the ratio gives a speed per inch rather than a time."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing calls for an effective throat of 1/4 inch on an equal-leg flat fillet, and the shop cuts leg sizes in sixteenth inch increments on its gauges for structural bracket work. What is the smallest standard leg size that provides at least the required throat?", "options": {"A": "Three eighths inch, because 0.25 divided by 0.707 is about 0.354 and rounds up.", "B": "Five sixteenths inch, because a leg is 25 percent longer than the throat.", "C": "One quarter inch, because leg and throat are the same for flat profiles.", "D": "One half inch, because leg size must be double the required throat."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Three eighths inch, because 0.25 divided by 0.707 is about 0.354 and rounds up.): Throat equals 0.707 times leg, so the leg is the throat divided by 0.707, or 0.354 inch. The next sixteenth increment at or above that is 3/8 inch (0.375), since 5/16 inch (0.3125) would give a throat of only 0.221 inch."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A repair job needs 50 pounds of deposited weld metal and the shop will use shielded metal arc electrodes with a 65 percent deposition efficiency from the supplier's data sheet. How many pounds of electrodes must be purchased to cover the entire job?", "options": {"A": "About 77 pounds, from 50 divided by 0.65.", "B": "About 33 pounds, from 50 multiplied by 0.65.", "C": "About 58 pounds, from 50 increased by 15 percent for stubs.", "D": "About 65 pounds, from taking the efficiency percentage as a weight."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 77 pounds, from 50 divided by 0.65.): Only 65 percent of the electrode weight ends up in the weld, so purchased weight equals deposited weight divided by 0.65, which is 76.9 pounds. Multiplying by 0.65 would buy less electrode than the weld itself needs."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A pipe fitter cuts a three-piece 90 degree mitered elbow from carbon steel pipe, with two joints that each turn the line by the same amount. At what angle from square is each miter cut made on the segment ends?", "options": {"A": "Twenty-two and a half degrees, since each joint turns 45 and the cut is half.", "B": "Forty-five degrees, since each joint turns the line by that amount.", "C": "Thirty degrees, since ninety divided by three pieces gives the cut.", "D": "Fifteen degrees, since ninety divided by six cut faces gives the cut."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Twenty-two and a half degrees, since each joint turns 45 and the cut is half.): Two joints share the 90 degree turn, so each turns the line 45 degrees. A miter cut is half the joint turn, giving 22.5 degrees. The 45 degree answer applies to a two-piece elbow, which has only one joint."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "An apprentice calculates heat input for 24 volts, 220 amps and 10 inches per minute and reports 0.528 kJ per inch on the traveler. The supervisor knows this is far too low for a GMAW procedure. What error did the apprentice most likely make in applying the heat input equation?", "options": {"A": "A decimal slipped when entering amps as 22 instead of 220, cutting the answer tenfold.", "B": "The 1000 divisor was applied in the numerator instead of the denominator of the fraction.", "C": "Travel speed was treated as seconds per inch, which reverses its effect on the answer.", "D": "The factor of 60 was left out, so the minutes were not converted to seconds."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The factor of 60 was left out, so the minutes were not converted to seconds.): Volts times amps divided by 10 times 1000 gives exactly 0.528, so the 60 is the missing piece. The true value is 31.68 kJ per inch. The decimal-slip and inverted-speed ideas would produce 3.17 and other values, not 0.528."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder on quenched and tempered steel keeps voltage and current fixed but slows travel speed from 12 inches per minute to 6 inches per minute to fill a wide joint. The procedure sets a heat input ceiling for the base metal. What happens as a result of the slower travel?", "options": {"A": "Heat input doubles, the joint cools more slowly, and the heat affected zone grows wider.", "B": "Heat input halves, the joint cools faster, and the heat affected zone narrows.", "C": "Heat input stays unchanged because volts and amps alone fix the energy per inch.", "D": "Heat input doubles, but cooling rate and heat affected zone width stay unchanged."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heat input doubles, the joint cools more slowly, and the heat affected zone grows wider.): Travel speed sits in the denominator of the heat input equation, so halving it doubles the energy per inch. More energy per inch slows cooling and widens the heat affected zone, which can push a quenched and tempered steel past its limit."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A procedure for high strength steel limits heat input to 40 kJ per inch per pass. A welder sets 26 volts and 280 amps and travels at 10 inches per minute. Which conclusion is supported by the standard heat input calculation?", "options": {"A": "Heat input is 26.2 kJ per inch, well within the cap, so no change is needed.", "B": "Heat input is 43.7 kJ per inch, over the cap, so travel must exceed 10.9 ipm.", "C": "Heat input is 43.7 kJ per inch, acceptable because the cap applies to root passes.", "D": "Heat input is 72.8 kJ per inch, so travel speed must be doubled to comply."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heat input is 43.7 kJ per inch, over the cap, so travel must exceed 10.9 ipm.): Twenty-six volts times 280 amps times 60 divided by 10,000 gives 43.68 kJ per inch, above 40. The slowest compliant speed is 436,800 divided by 40,000, or 10.92 inches per minute. The cap as stated applies to every pass."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing calls for 1/4 inch equal-leg fillets on 100 feet of seam, but the welder habitually makes 5/16 inch fillets to be safe. Using weld metal at 0.284 pound per cubic inch and a flat face, how much extra metal does this habit add on the job?", "options": {"A": "About 2.7 pounds extra, roughly 25 percent more weld metal than specified.", "B": "About 6.0 pounds extra, roughly 56 percent more weld metal than specified.", "C": "About 12 pounds extra, roughly 112 percent more weld metal than specified.", "D": "About 1.0 pound extra, roughly 10 percent more weld metal than specified."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 6.0 pounds extra, roughly 56 percent more weld metal than specified.): A 1/4 inch fillet holds 10.65 pounds over 100 feet and a 5/16 inch fillet holds 16.64 pounds. Area scales with the square of the leg, so the ratio is 1.5625, which is 56 percent more and about 6.0 pounds of extra filler."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder is told to run 1/4 inch fillets but runs 1/2 inch fillets along the same long seam on a heavy structural frame in the flat position. By what factor does weld metal volume per foot increase over the specified size?", "options": {"A": "Two times, because area changes in direct proportion to the leg size.", "B": "Three times, because the throat grows by a factor of 1.5.", "C": "Eight times, because volume depends on the cube of the leg size.", "D": "Four times, because area depends on the square of the leg size."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Four times, because area depends on the square of the leg size.): Fillet area is half the leg squared, so doubling the leg multiplies area by four, from 0.0313 to 0.125 square inch. Length is unchanged, so volume per foot also rises fourfold, not eightfold."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A job needs 50 pounds of weld metal deposited. Option one is shielded metal arc electrodes at 65 percent deposition efficiency, option two is flux-cored wire at 85 percent. How many more pounds of filler must the shop buy for the first option than the second?", "options": {"A": "About 10 pounds more, from the 20 point efficiency gap applied to 50 pounds.", "B": "About 18 pounds more, from 76.9 pounds against 58.8 pounds purchased.", "C": "About 32 pounds more, from 50 pounds multiplied by the lower efficiency.", "D": "About 25 pounds more, from half of the 50 pound requirement."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 18 pounds more, from 76.9 pounds against 58.8 pounds purchased.): Electrodes needed are 50 divided by 0.65, or 76.9 pounds, while the wire needs 50 divided by 0.85, or 58.8 pounds. The difference is about 18 pounds. Applying the efficiency gap directly to 50 pounds ignores that losses are a share of what is bought."}, {"id": 593, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop welds 200 feet of 1/4 inch fillet weighing 0.1065 pound per foot with a process that deposits 8 pounds per hour while the arc is on. The welder's operating factor is 40 percent. How many clock hours should be planned for the job?", "options": {"A": "About 2.7 hours, from the arc time with no allowance for downtime.", "B": "About 1.1 hours, from multiplying the arc hours by the operating factor.", "C": "About 6.7 hours, from 2.66 arc hours divided by the 0.40 operating factor.", "D": "About 4.4 hours, from dividing the arc hours by a 60 percent factor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 6.7 hours, from 2.66 arc hours divided by the 0.40 operating factor.): The weld metal is 21.3 pounds, which takes 21.3 divided by 8, or 2.66 hours of arc time. Since the arc runs only 40 percent of the clock, planned hours are 2.66 divided by 0.40, about 6.7. Multiplying by 0.40 would shorten the schedule."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder switches from 0.035 inch solid wire to 0.045 inch solid wire without changing the 300 inches per minute wire feed speed on a GMAW machine. How does the weight of wire fed per hour compare after the change?", "options": {"A": "It rises about 65 percent, because wire cross-section grows with the square of diameter.", "B": "It rises about 29 percent, because weight grows in direct proportion to diameter.", "C": "It stays the same, because the feed speed in inches per minute is unchanged.", "D": "It rises about 112 percent, because weight grows with the cube of diameter."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It rises about 65 percent, because wire cross-section grows with the square of diameter.): Wire weight per inch follows cross-sectional area, which varies with diameter squared. The ratio 0.045 over 0.035, squared, is about 1.65, so the same feed speed delivers roughly 65 percent more metal. Feed speed in inches does not fix weight."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A welder travels 0.2 inch per second while running 25 volts and 200 amps on a mild steel plate. The procedure sheet requires heat input in kilojoules per inch using travel speed in inches per minute. What heat input results?", "options": {"A": "About 1,500 kJ per inch, after entering 0.2 directly as the travel speed.", "B": "About 0.42 kJ per inch, after dividing the correct answer by sixty again.", "C": "About 300 kJ per inch, after leaving travel speed out of the fraction.", "D": "About 25 kJ per inch, after converting the travel speed to 12 inches per minute."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 25 kJ per inch, after converting the travel speed to 12 inches per minute.): The equation expects inches per minute, and 0.2 inch per second is 12 inches per minute. Then 5,000 times 60 divided by 12,000 gives 25 kJ per inch. Entering 0.2 unconverted inflates the result sixty-fold to 1,500."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "An estimator sizes girth weld length for a run of 8 inch nominal pipe joints by multiplying pi by 8. The pipe's true outside diameter is 8.625 inches. What is wrong with this method and how large is the per joint error?", "options": {"A": "The method is correct, since nominal size equals outside diameter for 8 inch pipe.", "B": "The inside diameter should be used, so each joint estimate is about 2 inches long.", "C": "Nominal size is not the outside diameter, so each joint is about 2 inches short.", "D": "Wall thickness should be added once, so each joint is about 1 inch short."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nominal size is not the outside diameter, so each joint is about 2 inches short.): Pi times 8.625 is 27.1 inches, while pi times 8 is only 25.1 inches, so the estimate runs about 2 inches or 7 percent low per joint. Nominal size matches outside diameter only for pipe 14 inches and larger."}, {"id": 597, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing calls for a 45 degree included angle single-V groove in 1/2 inch plate, no root face, and a 1/8 inch root opening. The fitter bevels both plates equally. What bevel per plate and groove width at the top surface are needed?", "options": {"A": "45 degrees per plate, giving about 1.13 inches of opening at the top surface.", "B": "22.5 degrees per plate, giving about 0.54 inch of opening at the top surface.", "C": "22.5 degrees per plate, giving about 0.41 inch of opening at the top surface.", "D": "11.25 degrees per plate, giving about 0.32 inch of opening at the top surface."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (22.5 degrees per plate, giving about 0.54 inch of opening at the top surface.): Half of 45 degrees is 22.5 degrees per plate. The horizontal run per plate is 0.5 times tan 22.5 degrees, or 0.207 inch, so two runs plus the 0.125 root opening give 0.539 inch. Leaving out the root opening gives 0.41."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A drawing calls for a 5/16 inch leg on an equal-leg fillet, but a welder reads the gauge as 5/16 inch effective throat and builds it that way. What is the approximate leg size built and how does the weld area compare to the specified fillet?", "options": {"A": "Leg about 7/16 inch, and the weld area is about double the specified area.", "B": "Leg about 7/16 inch, and the weld area is about 41 percent more than specified.", "C": "Leg 5/16 inch, so weld area matches because leg and throat are equal for flat fillets.", "D": "Leg about 7/32 inch, and the weld area is half the specified area."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Leg about 7/16 inch, and the weld area is about double the specified area.): A 5/16 inch throat means a leg of 0.3125 divided by 0.707, or 0.442 inch, close to 7/16. Area scales with the leg squared, so the ratio is (1.414) squared, which is 2.0. The 41 percent figure is the leg increase, not the area increase."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A shop weighed wire for a trial: 25 pounds were fed to the arc and 23.5 pounds ended up in the test welds. The job is 400 feet of 5/16 inch fillet weighing 0.1664 pound per foot. How much wire should be bought for the production run?", "options": {"A": "About 71 pounds, from 66.6 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.94.", "B": "About 67 pounds, from the weld metal weight alone with no losses added.", "C": "About 63 pounds, from multiplying the weld metal weight by the 0.94 efficiency.", "D": "About 133 pounds, from doubling the weld metal weight."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 71 pounds, from 66.6 pounds of weld metal divided by 0.94.): The trial shows 23.5 divided by 25, or 94 percent deposition efficiency. The job needs 400 times 0.1664, or 66.6 pounds in the weld, so wire to buy is 66.6 divided by 0.94, about 70.8 pounds."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "trade_math", "question": "A GMAW procedure runs 24 volts, 220 amps and 10 inches per minute. The engineer applies an arc efficiency factor of 0.85 to report net heat input delivered to the plate. What net heat input in kilojoules per inch should appear on the record?", "options": {"A": "About 31.7 kJ per inch, since efficiency factors apply to gross input only.", "B": "About 37.3 kJ per inch, from 31.7 divided by the 0.85 efficiency.", "C": "About 26.9 kJ per inch, from 31.7 multiplied by the 0.85 efficiency.", "D": "About 0.45 kJ per inch, from omitting the factor of 60 and applying 0.85."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 26.9 kJ per inch, from 31.7 multiplied by the 0.85 efficiency.): Gross heat input is 24 times 220 times 60 divided by 10,000, or 31.68 kJ per inch. Net input is the share that reaches the plate, so multiply by 0.85 to get 26.9. Dividing by the efficiency would claim more heat than the arc produced."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding instructor is reviewing steel classifications with a first-year class before a lab on plate fabrication. The students are asked to sort samples by carbon content using the common shop categories for plain carbon steel. Which carbon range is generally assigned to low carbon steel, often called mild steel, used for structural shapes such as angle and plate?", "options": {"A": "Between 0.30 and 0.60 percent carbon, giving a balance of strength and ductility", "B": "Between 0.60 and 1.00 percent carbon, producing very high hardness and limited ductility", "C": "Between 1.00 and 2.00 percent carbon, a range reserved for cast iron products", "D": "Up to roughly 0.30 percent carbon, which gives good ductility and easy weldability"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Up to roughly 0.30 percent carbon, which gives good ductility and easy weldability): Low carbon steel holds about 0.30 percent carbon or less, so it stays ductile and welds without special heat treatment in most thicknesses. Medium carbon steel starts above that figure, which is why the 0.30 to 0.60 range does not describe plain structural plate."}, {"id": 602, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "During a metallurgy lesson on the iron-carbon system, an instructor holds a magnet to a sample of plain steel at room temperature and then describes the crystal structures present. Students must name the phase that is body-centered cubic, soft, magnetic and dissolves very little carbon in room-temperature steel. Which phase fits that description?", "options": {"A": "Austenite, the face-centered cubic phase that dissolves a large amount of carbon at high temperature", "B": "Ferrite, the soft body-centered cubic phase that holds very little dissolved carbon", "C": "Martensite, the hard body-centered tetragonal phase formed by rapid cooling", "D": "Cementite, the iron carbide compound that is extremely hard and brittle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ferrite, the soft body-centered cubic phase that holds very little dissolved carbon): Ferrite is body-centered cubic and can hold only a tiny fraction of a percent of carbon, which keeps it soft and magnetic. Austenite is face-centered cubic and nonmagnetic, and in plain steel it exists only at elevated temperature."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding technology student studies what happens to plain carbon steel as it is heated above its upper critical temperature near the weld. The instructor explains that the crystal structure rearranges into a phase that is face-centered cubic, nonmagnetic, and capable of dissolving much more carbon. What is the name of this high-temperature phase?", "options": {"A": "Austenite, which forms on heating and can dissolve far more carbon than ferrite", "B": "Pearlite, a layered mixture of ferrite and cementite that forms during slow cooling", "C": "Ferrite, the body-centered cubic phase that is stable only below the critical temperature", "D": "Tempered martensite, a tough structure created by reheating hardened steel to moderate temperatures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Austenite, which forms on heating and can dissolve far more carbon than ferrite): Heating steel above its critical range converts the structure to austenite, a face-centered cubic lattice that takes carbon into solution. How that austenite later cools determines whether ferrite, pearlite or martensite forms."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A weld inspector tests the heat-affected zone of a medium carbon steel joint that cooled quickly and finds very high hardness readings. The report states the zone contains a phase formed when austenite cools too fast for carbon to escape the lattice. Which phase is hard and brittle with a body-centered tetragonal structure?", "options": {"A": "Ferrite, a soft structure that forms when austenite is cooled very slowly", "B": "Pearlite, a layered structure that gives steel moderate hardness and good ductility", "C": "Austenite, a face-centered cubic structure that is stable and soft at room temperature", "D": "Martensite, a supersaturated structure that is hard and brittle until it is tempered"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Martensite, a supersaturated structure that is hard and brittle until it is tempered): Fast cooling traps carbon inside a distorted lattice, producing martensite, which is very hard and has low toughness. Ferrite and pearlite form only when cooling is slow enough for carbon to diffuse."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding procedure sheet for a structural joint includes a note about the heat-affected zone. A new apprentice asks the foreman what that term refers to on a finished groove weld in plate, since it is not the weld metal itself and it is not the untouched plate either. How should the heat-affected zone be defined?", "options": {"A": "Filler metal that was melted and mixed with the base metal in the joint", "B": "Base metal next to the weld that did not melt but changed properties from heat", "C": "The slag-covered surface layer that forms on top of the finished weld bead", "D": "Any area of the plate that was marked by a temperature-indicating crayon before welding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Base metal next to the weld that did not melt but changed properties from heat): The heat-affected zone is solid base metal whose microstructure and properties were altered by the welding heat without being melted. The molten and re-solidified region is the weld metal, which is a separate zone."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A mechanical testing lab reports results for a tensile specimen cut from a welded plate, listing yield strength, tensile strength and elongation. A student wants to know which of the three values marks the stress where the steel begins to deform permanently and will not return to its original length after unloading. Which property is that?", "options": {"A": "Tensile strength, the highest stress the specimen carries before it fractures", "B": "Yield strength, the stress at which permanent plastic deformation begins", "C": "Elongation, the percentage increase in gauge length measured after the specimen breaks", "D": "Hardness, the resistance of the surface to indentation by a standard indenter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yield strength, the stress at which permanent plastic deformation begins): Below the yield strength steel springs back when unloaded; above it, some stretch stays permanently. Tensile strength is the later peak stress, and elongation is a ductility measure taken after fracture."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabrication shop builds a pressure-bearing support frame that will operate outdoors in winter, so the engineer specifies impact testing of the weld metal and heat-affected zone. The test breaks a notched bar with a swinging pendulum and records the absorbed energy. Which mechanical property does a Charpy V-notch test measure?", "options": {"A": "Hardness, the resistance to surface indentation under a fixed test load", "B": "Ductility, the percentage of stretch a bar achieves before it separates", "C": "Fatigue strength, the stress a part survives through millions of repeated load cycles", "D": "Toughness, the ability to absorb energy and resist fracture when struck"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Toughness, the ability to absorb energy and resist fracture when struck): The Charpy test records how many foot-pounds of energy a notched bar absorbs while breaking, which is a direct indicator of toughness. Hardness, ductility and fatigue strength are measured by different tests."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop receives a mixed bin of stainless steel offcuts labeled 304, 316, 410 and 430. The supervisor wants new helpers to know which family the 300 series belongs to before they pick filler metals. Which description correctly identifies the 300 series stainless steels, such as 304 and 316, used in food equipment?", "options": {"A": "Austenitic grades containing chromium and nickel, generally nonmagnetic when annealed", "B": "Martensitic grades containing chromium only, which are hardenable by heat treatment", "C": "Ferritic grades that are strongly magnetic, with little nickel added to the chemistry", "D": "Precipitation-hardening grades that gain strength after a low-temperature aging cycle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Austenitic grades containing chromium and nickel, generally nonmagnetic when annealed): The 300 series are austenitic stainless steels built on chromium plus nickel, which keeps the structure face-centered cubic and nonmagnetic in the annealed state. The 400 series splits into ferritic and martensitic families."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is told to preheat a thick section of medium carbon steel plate before tacking and welding a repair. A trainee asks the instructor for the main reason preheat is applied to the joint area before arc initiation, since the weld will be molten anyway. What is the primary purpose of preheating?", "options": {"A": "To melt the base metal deeper so the filler wire fuses with the plate", "B": "To slow cooling so the heat-affected zone forms a less hard microstructure", "C": "To burn off mill scale so slag cannot form beneath the weld bead", "D": "To raise the yield strength of the plate before the weld metal is deposited"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To slow cooling so the heat-affected zone forms a less hard microstructure): Preheat lowers the temperature difference between the weld and the plate, which slows cooling. Slower cooling reduces martensite formation and lets hydrogen diffuse out, cutting the risk of cracking."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A materials engineer reviewing a mill test report for a structural plate calculates a number called carbon equivalent, which combines carbon with alloying elements such as manganese, chromium and nickel. The weld shop will use the value when choosing preheat. What does a higher carbon equivalent indicate about the steel's welding behavior?", "options": {"A": "Lower strength, which means the steel can be welded with no special precautions or heat control", "B": "Better ductility, which allows welding at faster travel speeds with less heat", "C": "Higher resistance to corrosion, which removes the need for postweld cleaning", "D": "Greater hardenability and higher risk of cracking in the heat-affected zone without preheat"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Greater hardenability and higher risk of cracking in the heat-affected zone without preheat): Carbon equivalent expresses how strongly the chemistry promotes hard martensite on cooling. The higher the number, the more preheat and control the steel typically needs to avoid heat-affected zone cracking."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A quality technician needs to check the hardness of a hardened tool steel weld repair and a piece of soft mild steel plate in the same afternoon. She asks which common hardness method uses a diamond cone penetrator and a C scale for hard steels. Which method is she referring to?", "options": {"A": "The Rockwell test, which reads the depth of indentation under a major load", "B": "The Charpy test, which reads the energy absorbed by a notched bar when struck", "C": "The Brinell test, which measures the diameter of an impression left by a hardened ball", "D": "The guided bend test, which judges cracks on the surface of a bent specimen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Rockwell test, which reads the depth of indentation under a major load): The Rockwell C scale pairs a diamond cone indenter with a heavy load and reports hardness from indentation depth. Brinell uses a ball and measures a diameter, while Charpy and bend tests do not measure hardness."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding class cuts and etches a cross-section of a single-pass weld on thick steel plate and examines the grain pattern under magnification. The instructor points out that grains near the fusion line look much larger than those in the base metal farther away. What condition most directly produces coarse grains in the heat-affected zone?", "options": {"A": "Rapid quenching of the plate in water immediately after welding finishes", "B": "Cold working the plate before it was cut and beveled for welding", "C": "Prolonged exposure to very high temperature next to the fusion boundary", "D": "Using a filler metal with a higher tensile strength than the base metal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prolonged exposure to very high temperature next to the fusion boundary): Grains grow when steel sits near its melting temperature, so the band closest to the fusion line, which sees the highest peak heat for the longest time, becomes coarsest. Quenching tends to refine rather than coarsen the grains."}, {"id": 613, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A mill test report for a plate lists 0.20 percent carbon, 1.20 percent manganese, 0.10 chromium, 0.05 molybdenum, 0.02 vanadium, 0.20 nickel and 0.15 copper. The shop uses the IIW carbon equivalent formula to rate weldability. Which value results from C + Mn/6 + (Cr + Mo + V)/5 + (Ni + Cu)/15?", "options": {"A": "Approximately 0.40 percent, found by adding only the carbon and manganese terms together", "B": "Approximately 0.78 percent, found by adding nickel and copper without dividing by fifteen", "C": "Approximately 1.92 percent, found by simply adding every listed element together", "D": "Approximately 0.46 percent, found by dividing each group by its own factor and summing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Approximately 0.46 percent, found by dividing each group by its own factor and summing): Carbon contributes 0.20, manganese 1.20 ÷ 6 = 0.20, the chromium, molybdenum and vanadium group 0.17 ÷ 5 = 0.034, and nickel plus copper 0.35 ÷ 15 = 0.023, for a total near 0.457. Leaving out or skipping a divisor gives the other values."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop is qualifying a procedure on a plate heat whose certificate shows 0.25 percent carbon and 1.50 percent manganese, with all other alloying elements at trace levels. The welding engineer applies a simplified carbon equivalent of carbon plus manganese divided by six. What value should she record for this heat of steel?", "options": {"A": "1.00, because manganese is divided by two before being added to the carbon", "B": "1.75, because carbon and manganese are simply added together", "C": "0.50, because manganese contributes 0.25 once divided by six and carbon adds 0.25", "D": "0.25, because only the manganese term is divided by six and carbon is ignored"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0.50, because manganese contributes 0.25 once divided by six and carbon adds 0.25): Manganese counts as 1.50 ÷ 6 = 0.25, and the carbon adds 0.25, so the total is 0.50. The other answers come from using the wrong divisor or dropping one of the two terms."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is joining 2 inch thick plate of medium carbon steel using low-hydrogen SMAW electrodes on a cold morning. Cracks appear in the heat-affected zone hours after welding. The inspector suspects hydrogen-assisted cracking. Which action would most reduce the risk of that cracking on the repair weld?", "options": {"A": "Increase travel speed to put less heat into the joint and finish sooner", "B": "Apply the specified preheat and hold interpass temperature so hydrogen can diffuse away", "C": "Quench the finished joint with water to lock in a fine grain structure", "D": "Grind the weld surface smooth before it cools to remove trapped gas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply the specified preheat and hold interpass temperature so hydrogen can diffuse away): Preheat and controlled interpass temperature slow cooling and give hydrogen time to leave the joint, which attacks two of the three causes of cracking. Faster travel and quenching speed cooling and make the heat-affected zone harder."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "Two plates of the same medium carbon steel are welded with identical current, voltage and travel speed. One plate is 1/4 inch thick and the other is 2 inches thick, both at room temperature. The welder notices the heavy plate shows much harder heat-affected zone readings. What explains the difference between the two joints?", "options": {"A": "The thick plate absorbs more carbon from the arc atmosphere during welding", "B": "The thin plate reaches a higher peak temperature that softens the structure", "C": "The thick plate has a lower carbon equivalent because it was rolled hotter", "D": "The thick plate pulls heat away faster, so the zone cools more quickly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The thick plate pulls heat away faster, so the zone cools more quickly): A thick plate conducts heat away from the weld in more directions, raising the cooling rate. Faster cooling favors martensite, so the heat-affected zone ends up harder than in the thin plate."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding inspector checks a GMAW record for a groove weld on alloy steel plate showing 24 volts, 200 amps and a travel speed of 8 inches per minute. The procedure limits heat input to protect toughness. Using volts times amps times 60, divided by travel speed in inches per minute, then divided by 1000, what is the heat input?", "options": {"A": "36 kJ per inch, calculated as 288,000 divided by 8 and then by 1000", "B": "4.8 kJ per inch, found by multiplying only volts and amps and dividing by 1000", "C": "0.6 kJ per inch, found by dividing 4,800 by travel speed and then 1000", "D": "288 kJ per inch, found by skipping the division by travel speed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (36 kJ per inch, calculated as 288,000 divided by 8 and then by 1000): 24 × 200 × 60 = 288,000; dividing by 8 inches per minute gives 36,000 joules per inch, which is 36 kJ per inch. Dropping the factor of 60 or the travel speed produces the other figures."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A tensile specimen machined from weld metal has a reduced section cross-sectional area of 0.200 square inches. During the test, the machine records a yield load of 11,600 pounds and a maximum load of 14,000 pounds before fracture. The lab technician must report the ultimate tensile strength in psi. What value is reported?", "options": {"A": "70,000 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by the original area", "B": "58,000 psi, found by dividing the yield load by the original area", "C": "2,800 psi, found by multiplying the maximum load by the original area", "D": "12,000 psi, found by dividing the gap between yield and maximum loads by the area"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (70,000 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by the original area): Tensile strength is maximum load divided by original area: 14,000 ÷ 0.200 = 70,000 psi. The yield load divided by the area gives 58,000 psi, which is the yield strength, not the tensile strength."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A guided test on a welded tensile bar uses a 2 inch gauge length marked before pulling. After the bar fractures, the technician fits the pieces together and measures the distance between the marks at 2.5 inches. The procedure requires reporting ductility as percent elongation. What percent elongation should the report show?", "options": {"A": "20 percent, found by dividing the 0.5 inch increase by the final length", "B": "125 percent, found by dividing the final length by the original length", "C": "25 percent, since the gauge length grew by 0.5 inch on a 2 inch base", "D": "50 percent, found by multiplying the 0.5 inch increase by one hundred"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (25 percent, since the gauge length grew by 0.5 inch on a 2 inch base): Percent elongation is the increase in gauge length divided by the original gauge length: 0.5 ÷ 2 = 0.25, or 25 percent. Dividing by the final length or by one instead gives the other values."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A round tensile bar for a procedure qualification has an original cross-sectional area of 0.200 square inches. After fracture, the necked-down area at the break measures 0.140 square inches. The lab sheet asks for reduction of area, another measure of ductility. Which value should be recorded?", "options": {"A": "70 percent, found by dividing the final area by the original area", "B": "43 percent, found by dividing the area lost by the final area", "C": "30 percent, found by dividing the area lost by the original area", "D": "6 percent, found by reading the 0.06 area lost as a whole number"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (30 percent, found by dividing the area lost by the original area): The area lost is 0.200 minus 0.140 = 0.060 square inches, and 0.060 ÷ 0.200 = 0.30, or 30 percent. Using the final area or the wrong base gives the other results."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator welds a thick 304 stainless tank for a brewery and later finds corrosion along bands next to the welds after a few months in service. The metallurgist says chromium carbides formed in the heat-affected zone in the 800 to 1500 degree F range. Which material choice would best prevent that problem on the next tank?", "options": {"A": "Use 410 martensitic stainless, which resists corrosion better after rapid cooling", "B": "Use 304 with a higher preheat, which keeps chromium in solution", "C": "Use 304L, whose low carbon content limits carbide formation during welding", "D": "Specify 430 ferritic stainless, which contains more nickel than the 300 series"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use 304L, whose low carbon content limits carbide formation during welding): Sensitization happens when carbon ties up chromium as carbides at grain boundaries, so a low-carbon grade such as 304L has too little carbon to do much damage. Added preheat would hold the metal longer in the sensitizing range."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder moves from 1/8 inch carbon steel sheet to 1/8 inch 304 stainless sheet and uses the same joint design and heat. The stainless panel warps far more than the carbon steel did. Which property combination of austenitic stainless steel mainly explains this larger distortion?", "options": {"A": "Higher thermal expansion together with lower thermal conductivity than carbon steel", "B": "Lower thermal expansion together with higher thermal conductivity than carbon steel", "C": "A lower melting range that makes the sheet soften across its whole width", "D": "A body-centered cubic structure that shrinks unevenly as the weld freezes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Higher thermal expansion together with lower thermal conductivity than carbon steel): Austenitic stainless expands roughly half again as much as carbon steel and conducts heat poorly, so heat stays concentrated near the weld and shrinkage forces are larger. That combination is why it distorts so readily."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A helper in a fabrication shop finds an unmarked stainless bar in the annealed condition and needs a quick sorting check before choosing a filler metal. A hand magnet sticks firmly to the bar. Based on that result, which stainless family is most likely, as opposed to the usual 304 or 316?", "options": {"A": "An annealed austenitic grade such as 316, which is strongly magnetic in that state", "B": "A ferritic or martensitic grade such as 430 or 410, both of which are magnetic", "C": "A 304L grade, since low carbon makes austenitic stainless attract magnets", "D": "A high-nickel austenitic grade such as 310, because extra nickel makes the structure magnetic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A ferritic or martensitic grade such as 430 or 410, both of which are magnetic): Annealed austenitic grades are essentially nonmagnetic, while ferritic and martensitic stainless steels attract a magnet. A strong pull therefore points toward the 400 series rather than the 300 series."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A procedure for 1-1/2 inch thick plate with a carbon equivalent near 0.50 is being drafted. The welding engineer must decide whether to call for preheat before welding with low-hydrogen electrodes. Compared with a 0.30 carbon equivalent steel of the same thickness, what should the engineer expect for the 0.50 steel?", "options": {"A": "A lower preheat requirement because higher alloy content slows the steel's cooling", "B": "A higher preheat requirement because the more hardenable steel forms martensite when cooled quickly", "C": "The same preheat requirement because thickness alone controls cooling and cracking risk", "D": "No preheat requirement because low-hydrogen electrodes remove hydrogen from the plate itself"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A higher preheat requirement because the more hardenable steel forms martensite when cooled quickly): A higher carbon equivalent means the heat-affected zone forms martensite more easily, so more preheat is needed to slow cooling. Low-hydrogen electrodes reduce hydrogen from the weld but cannot cure a hard microstructure."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "While welding a quenched and tempered alloy plate in multiple passes, the welder finds the plate has cooled below the specified minimum between passes but also notes the procedure lists a maximum interpass temperature. Which reason best explains why a maximum interpass temperature is specified for this type of steel?", "options": {"A": "To keep the plate from cooling below the minimum before the next pass is deposited", "B": "To avoid excessive heat buildup that coarsens grains and lowers strength and toughness", "C": "To allow hydrogen to enter the weld pool while the plate remains hot", "D": "To keep the filler metal from alloying with the base metal during fusion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To avoid excessive heat buildup that coarsens grains and lowers strength and toughness): Quenched and tempered steels get their properties from a controlled heat treatment, and letting the plate run too hot between passes can undo it by growing grains and softening the structure. The cap protects strength and toughness."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder must verify that a 1 inch carbon steel plate has reached a 300 degree F preheat before striking an arc. He has a temperature-indicating crayon rated 300 degrees F. After touching the plate near the joint, the mark remains a dry chalk line instead of melting. What does that result mean?", "options": {"A": "The plate has exceeded 300 degrees F, so welding can begin immediately", "B": "The crayon has expired, so a different brand must be used for testing", "C": "The crayon was applied too lightly, so the result cannot say anything about temperature", "D": "The plate has not yet reached 300 degrees F, so heating must continue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The plate has not yet reached 300 degrees F, so heating must continue): A temperature-indicating crayon melts and smears only when the surface reaches its rated temperature. A mark that stays dry means the plate is still below that value and needs more heat."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop welds 4130 chromium-molybdenum tubing for a racing frame, and the supervisor warns that this alloy steel hardens in the heat-affected zone more readily than mild steel. Which pair of alloying elements gives 4130 its added hardenability and strength compared with plain low carbon steel?", "options": {"A": "Nickel and copper, which lower hardenability so the steel welds like mild steel", "B": "Sulfur and phosphorus, which improve strength and make the metal easier to machine", "C": "Silicon and aluminum, which remove carbon from the steel during the refining process", "D": "Chromium and molybdenum, which raise hardenability and strength at elevated temperature"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chromium and molybdenum, which raise hardenability and strength at elevated temperature): The 4130 designation identifies a chromium-molybdenum steel with about 0.30 percent carbon. Those two elements increase hardenability, which is why preheat and controlled cooling matter more than on mild steel."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A farm equipment repair involves welding a broken axle made of 1045 medium carbon steel, which contains about 0.45 percent carbon. The welder has low-hydrogen electrodes, a propane torch and an insulating blanket. What procedure best prevents cracking in the heat-affected zone on this repair?", "options": {"A": "Weld at room temperature with a cellulosic electrode and cool the axle in water", "B": "Weld cold with a high-current setting so the joint reaches heat quickly", "C": "Preheat only the filler metal and cool the axle quickly with compressed air", "D": "Preheat the axle, use low-hydrogen electrodes, and let it cool slowly in a blanket"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Preheat the axle, use low-hydrogen electrodes, and let it cool slowly in a blanket): Medium carbon steel hardens readily, so the plan must slow cooling and limit hydrogen. Preheating the part, using low-hydrogen filler and wrapping it for slow cooling does both."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A procedure qualification coupon is cut from a groove weld and bent around a plunger in a jig so that the weld face is on the outside of the bend. Cracks longer than the code limit would indicate a problem. Which property does the guided bend test primarily evaluate in the weld and heat-affected zone?", "options": {"A": "Hardness of the heat-affected zone measured by diamond indentation depth", "B": "Impact toughness at low temperature measured by pendulum energy", "C": "Ductility and soundness of the fusion zone under plastic bending", "D": "Fatigue life under repeated cyclic loading measured in millions of cycles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ductility and soundness of the fusion zone under plastic bending): A guided bend forces the weld to stretch plastically, so cracks or open flaws expose poor ductility or lack of fusion. It does not measure hardness, impact energy or fatigue life."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "In the school storage room, an instructor hands the class three unidentified steel bars and a bench grinder to identify the bars by their sparks. Bar one gives few long straw-colored sparks with little branching, while bar three gives a bushy burst with many branching sparks. What does the bushy, branching spark stream suggest about bar three?", "options": {"A": "It has a lower carbon content than the bar giving few long sparks", "B": "It has a higher carbon content than the bar giving long straight sparks", "C": "It is austenitic stainless steel, because that family gives the heaviest branching", "D": "It was hardened by quenching, because spark patterns reflect heat treatment history rather than carbon"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It has a higher carbon content than the bar giving long straight sparks): In a spark test, more carbon makes the spark stream burst into more branches, while mild steel gives long, mostly unbranched sparks. The pattern is a rough indicator of carbon content, not heat treatment."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A weld inspector investigating delayed cracking in a thick high-strength steel weld lists the conditions needed for hydrogen-induced cracking to occur. She reminds the crew that removing one of them prevents the problem. Which set correctly lists the three conditions required together for hydrogen cracking?", "options": {"A": "Excess oxygen, a soft ferritic microstructure, and compressive stress across the weld", "B": "Diffusible hydrogen, a fine-grained austenitic structure, and high ambient humidity in the shop environment", "C": "Surface rust, a high interpass temperature, and a very slow travel speed", "D": "Diffusible hydrogen, a hard susceptible microstructure, and sufficient tensile stress in the joint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffusible hydrogen, a hard susceptible microstructure, and sufficient tensile stress in the joint): Hydrogen cracking needs hydrogen in the metal, a brittle structure such as martensite, and tensile stress to open the crack. Preheat, dry electrodes and lower restraint each remove one of those three."}, {"id": 632, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is multipass welding 316 stainless plate and notices the plate becoming too hot to touch between passes. The procedure lists a maximum interpass temperature. For austenitic stainless steel, which result does keeping the interpass temperature low help to limit?", "options": {"A": "Hardening by martensite formation in the heat-affected zone of the plate", "B": "Carbide precipitation and distortion caused by prolonged heat in the sensitizing range", "C": "Hydrogen absorption that would otherwise produce delayed cracking in the weld after it cools", "D": "Loss of magnetism that would otherwise make the weld hard to inspect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Carbide precipitation and distortion caused by prolonged heat in the sensitizing range): Austenitic stainless loses corrosion resistance when it dwells in the 800 to 1500 degree F range, and it also distorts more as heat builds up. A low interpass cap limits both problems."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is handed a box of E7018 electrodes for a structural repair on A36 plate and asked to explain the classification to a new helper. The helper must state what the first two digits, 70, tell the welder about the deposited weld metal's mechanical properties. What do they indicate?", "options": {"A": "Minimum yield strength of 70,000 psi after postweld heat treatment", "B": "Maximum hardness of 70 on the Rockwell B scale for the deposit", "C": "Required preheat temperature of 70 degrees F before the electrode is used", "D": "Minimum tensile strength of 70,000 psi in the as-welded deposit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Minimum tensile strength of 70,000 psi in the as-welded deposit): In AWS A5.1 classifications, the first digits give the minimum tensile strength in thousands of psi, so E70 means 70,000 psi. The yield strength for E7018 is lower, at 58,000 psi minimum."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A project engineer must choose between a ferritic steel and a 304 stainless for piping that carries a cryogenic liquid at very low temperature. Both materials meet strength needs, but fracture risk is the concern. Which statement best explains why austenitic stainless is often chosen for cryogenic service?", "options": {"A": "Its body-centered cubic structure becomes more ductile as temperature falls", "B": "Its higher carbon content raises hardness enough to stop crack growth", "C": "Its face-centered cubic structure stays tough at very low temperatures", "D": "Its magnetic nature lets it absorb impact energy through domain movement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Its face-centered cubic structure stays tough at very low temperatures): Face-centered cubic metals have no sharp ductile-to-brittle transition, so austenitic stainless keeps its toughness in the cold. Ferritic steels, with their body-centered cubic lattice, turn brittle as temperature drops."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "After welding a repair on medium carbon steel without preheat, an inspector measures the heat-affected zone with a portable hardness tester and records readings far above the base metal. The zone cooled quickly from welding temperature. What microstructure most likely explains these high hardness readings?", "options": {"A": "Ferrite formed because rapid cooling gave carbon time to settle out of the lattice", "B": "Coarse pearlite formed because the zone cooled slowly through the critical range", "C": "Spheroidized cementite formed because the zone stayed hot for many hours", "D": "Martensite formed when austenite cooled too fast for carbon to leave the lattice"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Martensite formed when austenite cooled too fast for carbon to leave the lattice): Without preheat, the quick cooling traps carbon in the lattice and produces hard martensite. Ferrite and pearlite need slow cooling, and spheroidized cementite needs long holding at moderate heat."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A hardened weld repair on a medium carbon shaft shows excellent hardness but cracks when lightly tapped with a hammer. The metallurgist recommends a moderate-temperature reheating below the critical range after hardening. What is the main effect of tempering this martensitic structure?", "options": {"A": "It converts the martensite back to austenite so grains can refine on cooling", "B": "It reduces hardness slightly while greatly improving toughness and cracking resistance", "C": "It increases hardness further by forming more carbide at the grain boundaries", "D": "It drives the carbon out of the steel surface so later hardening cannot occur"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It reduces hardness slightly while greatly improving toughness and cracking resistance): Tempering lets some trapped carbon form fine carbides, relieving internal stress. Hardness drops modestly, but toughness improves a great deal, which is the purpose of the treatment."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A designer compares two structural plates. Plate X is A36 with a minimum yield of 36 ksi, and plate Y is a high-strength grade with a minimum yield of 50 ksi. A welder asks which plate begins to stretch permanently at the lower applied stress under a gradually increasing load.", "options": {"A": "Plate Y, because higher strength steel gives up its elastic range sooner", "B": "Both plates, because yield point is set by modulus and not by grade", "C": "Neither plate, because permanent deformation starts at the tensile strength value", "D": "Plate X, because it reaches permanent deformation at 36 ksi before plate Y does"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plate X, because it reaches permanent deformation at 36 ksi before plate Y does): Yield strength is the stress where permanent deformation starts, so the plate with the lower value yields first. Plate X at 36 ksi yields before plate Y at 50 ksi."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is about to weld 1/2 inch A36 plate outdoors where the steel temperature reads 20 degrees F and frost covers the joint. Under typical AWS D1.1 practice, which action should the welder take before striking an arc on this plate?", "options": {"A": "Warm the plate and remove the frost, bringing it to at least 70 degrees F", "B": "Weld immediately with higher amperage so the arc heat melts the frost away", "C": "Wipe the frost off and weld at the existing temperature on this thin plate", "D": "Switch to a cellulosic electrode, which tolerates moisture and low plate temperature"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Warm the plate and remove the frost, bringing it to at least 70 degrees F): AWS D1.1 requires base metal below 32 degrees F to be warmed to at least 70 degrees F before welding. Frost adds moisture, which is also a hydrogen source, so it must be removed."}, {"id": 639, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder on a toughness-critical joint in low alloy steel is asked to reduce coarse grain growth in the heat-affected zone without making the weld cool dangerously fast. The qualified procedure allows adjustment of travel speed, amperage and bead technique within limits. Which adjustment best narrows the coarse-grained region?", "options": {"A": "Use wide weave beads at slow travel speed to spread the heat evenly", "B": "Raise amperage and slow travel speed so the pool stays molten longer", "C": "Use stringer beads at a moderate travel speed to lower heat input per pass", "D": "Hold the arc on each bead end to preheat the next pass"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use stringer beads at a moderate travel speed to lower heat input per pass): Coarse grains come from high heat input and long time at peak temperature. Stringer beads at a moderate speed reduce heat per inch and shorten that exposure, while weaves and slow travel increase it."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "Weld cracks appear in the heat-affected zone of a 2 inch thick plate a day after welding, running parallel to the weld toe without reaching the weld metal. Electrodes were low-hydrogen and stored properly, but the plate was welded at room temperature and the carbon equivalent is 0.48. Which cause best fits the evidence?", "options": {"A": "Hard martensite in the heat-affected zone cracked under residual stress with hydrogen present", "B": "Lack of fusion at the root left a slag inclusion that opened under stress", "C": "Excessive preheat grew the grains until they separated under shrinkage stress", "D": "Hot cracking in the weld metal from sulfur segregation formed during solidification of the pool"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hard martensite in the heat-affected zone cracked under residual stress with hydrogen present): Delayed cracks in the heat-affected zone of thick, high carbon equivalent plate welded cold point to martensite, residual stress and hydrogen. No preheat was used, and hot cracking would appear in the weld metal rather than the base metal."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop must weld 1 inch plate of two steels: Steel P with 0.18 percent carbon and 0.90 percent manganese, and Steel Q with 0.35 percent carbon and 1.40 percent manganese. Using C + Mn/6, which steel would the welding engineer expect to need more careful preheat control, and why?", "options": {"A": "Steel Q, since its carbon equivalent near 0.58 is well above Steel P near 0.33", "B": "Steel P, since its carbon equivalent of 0.33 exceeds the 0.58 of Steel Q", "C": "Neither steel, since both carbon equivalents fall under the limit needing preheat", "D": "Both equally, since only plate thickness sets the preheat requirement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Steel Q, since its carbon equivalent near 0.58 is well above Steel P near 0.33): Steel P works out to 0.18 + 0.15 = 0.33, while Steel Q is 0.35 + 0.23 = 0.58. The higher value means more hardenability and a greater need for preheat control, even at equal thickness."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder argues that a high-strength steel is automatically hard to weld, while a coworker says weldability depends on hardenability from carbon and alloy content. They compare a 70 ksi low carbon microalloyed steel with a carbon equivalent of 0.35 and a 50 ksi medium carbon steel with a carbon equivalent of 0.55. Whose claim does the data best support?", "options": {"A": "The welder, since the 70 ksi steel is stronger and therefore more crack-prone", "B": "The welder, since yield strength directly sets the preheat temperature of any steel", "C": "Neither, since carbon equivalent describes ductility rather than cracking tendency", "D": "The coworker, since the 50 ksi steel has the higher carbon equivalent and a harder zone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The coworker, since the 50 ksi steel has the higher carbon equivalent and a harder zone): Weldability tracks hardenability, which carbon equivalent measures, not the strength number alone. The lower strength steel has the higher carbon equivalent and will be the one more prone to heat-affected zone cracking."}, {"id": 643, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder preheats a 3/4 inch plate of A36 to 600 degrees F before welding, far above the procedure value of 150 degrees F, believing more heat is safer. After welding, the joint shows lower toughness. Which explanation best accounts for the drop in toughness?", "options": {"A": "Rapid cooling formed martensite throughout the plate during the high preheat", "B": "The extra heat added carbon from the surrounding air into the weld metal", "C": "The high preheat trapped hydrogen permanently inside the weld metal", "D": "Excess heat and slow cooling coarsened the grains in the heat-affected zone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Excess heat and slow cooling coarsened the grains in the heat-affected zone): Preheat well above the specified level slows cooling too much and holds the steel near grain-growth temperatures, producing coarse grains and lower toughness. High preheat encourages hydrogen to escape and does not cause martensite."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "Two groove welds with identical heat input are made on the same steel: a 3/8 inch plate and a 3 inch plate. A metallurgist predicts the thick plate will cool faster at the weld, and a student asks why thickness matters more than the arc settings. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Thick plate has lower thermal conductivity, which traps heat near the arc", "B": "Heat spreads in three dimensions in thick plate, so it leaves the weld faster", "C": "Thick plate absorbs the arc's energy and releases it only after welding", "D": "Thin plate loses heat by radiation faster, so it cools more rapidly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heat spreads in three dimensions in thick plate, so it leaves the weld faster): A thick plate carries heat away through its depth as well as its width, so the weld zone cools more quickly than in thin plate, where heat flow is mostly two-dimensional. That faster cooling raises hardening risk."}, {"id": 645, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A repair crew welds a 4 inch thick, highly restrained steel casting section with a carbon equivalent of 0.45. Their procedure for unrestrained 1/2 inch plate of the same steel called for no preheat. Which change to the plan is most justified for the heavy restrained section?", "options": {"A": "Keep no preheat because identical steel chemistry means identical welding requirements for both sections", "B": "Add preheat and interpass control because thickness and restraint raise cooling rate and stress", "C": "Use a smaller electrode and travel faster to reduce the heat in the section", "D": "Quench each pass with water to stop grain growth between layers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add preheat and interpass control because thickness and restraint raise cooling rate and stress): Thick sections cool faster and restraint builds residual stress, both of which raise cracking risk even when the chemistry is unchanged. Preheat and interpass control address cooling rate directly, whereas quenching makes it worse."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A student writes four notes on a quiz about the iron-carbon system. The instructor asks which note correctly states what happens when plain 0.40 percent carbon steel in the austenite range cools very slowly to room temperature. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "It transforms entirely to martensite, a hard structure with very little ductility", "B": "It stays austenite, which is stable and nonmagnetic at room temperature", "C": "It transforms to a mixture of ferrite and pearlite, a soft and ductile structure", "D": "It becomes cementite, the only phase stable below the critical temperature"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It transforms to a mixture of ferrite and pearlite, a soft and ductile structure): Slow cooling gives carbon time to diffuse, so austenite breaks down into ferrite and pearlite. Martensite requires fast cooling, and plain carbon steel does not hold austenite at room temperature."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A designer needs a steel for a crane hook weld repair and asks the metallurgist about trade-offs. Cold working and heat treatment can raise strength, but the metallurgist warns that gains in one property usually cost another. Which general trade-off is correct for carbon steels?", "options": {"A": "Higher strength and hardness usually come with greater ductility and toughness", "B": "Higher ductility usually comes with greater hardness and higher yield strength", "C": "Higher toughness usually comes with greater hardness and lower impact absorption", "D": "Higher strength and hardness usually come with reduced ductility and toughness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Higher strength and hardness usually come with reduced ductility and toughness): Processes that raise strength and hardness, such as quenching or cold work, generally make the steel less able to stretch or absorb impact. That is why hard heat-affected zones crack more easily."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A structural engineer states that switching from A36 plate to a heat-treated alloy steel with a yield near 100 ksi will not change how much the member elastically deflects under the same load and geometry. A welder doubts this because the alloy is so much stronger. Which property supports the engineer's claim?", "options": {"A": "Modulus of elasticity rises in direct proportion to yield strength in alloy steels", "B": "Alloy steels carry stress elastically up to their ultimate tensile strength", "C": "Modulus of elasticity is about 29 million psi for essentially all carbon and alloy steels", "D": "Elastic deflection depends mainly on carbon content, which both plates share"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Modulus of elasticity is about 29 million psi for essentially all carbon and alloy steels): Stiffness is set by the modulus of elasticity, which is nearly the same for every steel. A higher yield strength lets the member carry more load before yielding, but it does not make the steel stiffer."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "Charpy tests on a weld's heat-affected zone give 45 ft-lb at 70 degrees F but only 8 ft-lb at minus 20 degrees F. A supervisor wants to use the weld in a cold-climate bridge member. What does this data most strongly indicate about the ferritic steel's behavior?", "options": {"A": "It gains toughness as temperature falls, so the cold test was invalid", "B": "It has excess ductility, which is why the cold energy value dropped", "C": "It has a ductile-to-brittle transition and loses toughness as temperature falls", "D": "It is austenitic, since austenitic steels lose toughness quickly in the cold"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It has a ductile-to-brittle transition and loses toughness as temperature falls): A large drop in absorbed energy at low temperature is the signature of the ductile-to-brittle transition in body-centered cubic ferritic steels. Austenitic steels keep their toughness in the cold, so the claim that austenitic steels turn brittle in the cold is backwards."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder checks preheat on a 2 inch plate by touching the crayon to the groove face immediately after pulling the torch away, and the mark melts. The inspector rejects the check and asks that the plate be soaked and measured away from the heated spot. What is the inspector's reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Crayons give accurate readings on weld metal but not on base metal", "B": "The surface runs hotter than the core just after heating, so the reading overstates preheat", "C": "The torch flame leaves carbon on the surface that melts the crayon at a false reading", "D": "Preheat must be measured after the first pass has been deposited"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The surface runs hotter than the core just after heating, so the reading overstates preheat): Right after a torch is removed, the surface is hotter than the interior of a thick plate. A short soak lets the temperature equalize through the thickness so the reading reflects the whole joint."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator welds a thick 316L stainless vessel with very high heat input and long interpass dwell. After welding, a corrosion test shows no intergranular attack, yet a coworker claims the low-carbon grade makes temperature control unnecessary. What is the most accurate response to the coworker?", "options": {"A": "He is right, because low carbon eliminates carbide, distortion and hot cracking concerns", "B": "He is right, because the L grades cannot form chromium carbides under any welding conditions", "C": "Low carbon delays carbide formation but excess heat still risks distortion and other problems", "D": "He is wrong, because low-carbon grades sensitize faster than standard grades"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low carbon delays carbide formation but excess heat still risks distortion and other problems): The L grades resist sensitization because little carbon is available, but heat control still matters because stainless distorts heavily and can suffer other damage from excess heat. Low carbon does not make the grade immune to every welding problem."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A lab reports Rockwell C 52 in the heat-affected zone of a quenched and tempered plate weld, versus Rockwell C 28 in the base metal. The welding engineer must decide what the readings imply about the procedure. Which conclusion is best supported by the numbers?", "options": {"A": "The zone is much harder, so cooling was too fast or preheat was too low", "B": "The zone is softer, so cooling was too slow or heat input was too high", "C": "The zone shows normal tempering, so the readings confirm the weld is tough", "D": "The test is invalid, since Rockwell C cannot be used on weld joints"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The zone is much harder, so cooling was too fast or preheat was too low): A heat-affected zone far harder than the base metal signals martensite from fast cooling, pointing to low preheat or low heat input. High hardness also lowers toughness, so the readings do not confirm a tough weld."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A student heats a plain 0.40 percent carbon steel bar well above its upper critical temperature in a furnace and holds a magnet near it, expecting the bar to behave like it did at room temperature. Which result should the instructor tell the student to expect, and why?", "options": {"A": "The magnet will stick harder, because carbon moves into solution and adds magnetism", "B": "The magnet will stick the same, because crystal structure does not affect magnetism", "C": "The magnet will not stick, because the hot steel has become nonmagnetic austenite", "D": "The magnet will stick to the core only, because the surface scale blocks the field"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The magnet will not stick, because the hot steel has become nonmagnetic austenite): Above the critical range the steel transforms to face-centered cubic austenite, which is nonmagnetic. Magnetism returns when the bar cools back into ferrite and pearlite."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "An inspector finds a 5/8 inch A36 fillet weld made in one pass with 40 percent more heat input than the procedure allows, and the toughness test fails. Hardness readings are normal. Which mechanism is most consistent with these results rather than hydrogen cracking?", "options": {"A": "Coarse grain growth in the heat-affected zone from excessive heat input", "B": "Martensite formation in the heat-affected zone from rapid quench cooling", "C": "Sensitization of chromium carbides at the grain boundaries of the base metal", "D": "Underbead hydrogen cracking driven by high hardness and residual stress"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Coarse grain growth in the heat-affected zone from excessive heat input): Excess heat input with normal hardness points to coarsened grains, which lower toughness without raising hardness. Martensite and hydrogen cracking would show elevated hardness, and sensitization applies to stainless steel."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "On a multipass groove weld in 1-1/2 inch alloy plate, the crew lets the joint cool to 80 degrees F overnight, but the procedure calls for a 250 degree F minimum preheat and interpass. Cracking is later found. Which welder action on restart would have best followed the procedure and avoided the cracking?", "options": {"A": "Reheat the joint to at least 250 degrees F before depositing the next pass", "B": "Weld the next pass quickly at higher amperage to reheat the cold joint", "C": "Skip the reheat since passes already deposited act as preheat for new ones", "D": "Wipe the joint with water to check for dryness, then continue welding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reheat the joint to at least 250 degrees F before depositing the next pass): The minimum interpass temperature applies every time welding resumes, so a cold joint must be brought back up before the next pass. Welding hot on a cold joint cools just as fast and invites cracking."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A maintenance worker plans to weld a broken file made of high carbon steel, about 1.0 percent carbon, using a general-purpose mild steel electrode and no preheat because the break looks clean. A senior welder says the repair will likely fail. Which reason best supports the senior welder's warning?", "options": {"A": "High carbon steel forms brittle martensite readily and cracks next to the weld", "B": "High carbon steel melts at a lower temperature, so the file collapses under heat", "C": "Mild steel electrodes cannot carry current into a high carbon steel base metal", "D": "High carbon steel is magnetic, which deflects the arc away from the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High carbon steel forms brittle martensite readily and cracks next to the weld): At about 1.0 percent carbon the heat-affected zone forms hard, brittle martensite on cooling, so cracks are likely without preheat and slow cooling. Electrode choice and magnetism are not the cause of the failure."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A repair shop must weld an exhaust component made of 409 or 430 ferritic stainless and wants to avoid heavy grain growth in the weld zone, which can make ferritic grades brittle. A helper suggests treating it like 304 and using high heat to get good penetration. Which advice best reflects ferritic stainless behavior?", "options": {"A": "Raise heat input because ferritic grades refine their grains when heated slowly", "B": "Preheat well above 1000 degrees F because ferritic grades harden fully on cooling like martensite", "C": "Treat it like 304, since ferritic and austenitic grades share the same grain behavior", "D": "Limit heat input because ferritic grades grow coarse grains and lose toughness when overheated"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Limit heat input because ferritic grades grow coarse grains and lose toughness when overheated): Ferritic stainless steels have no phase change to refine grains, so grains that grow coarse in the heat-affected zone stay coarse and reduce toughness. Keeping heat input low limits that growth."}, {"id": 658, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A transverse tensile specimen from a groove weld on A36 plate breaks in the base metal well away from the weld at 66,000 psi, above the 58,000 psi minimum tensile strength of the plate. The inspector must decide what this result shows. Which conclusion is correct about the weld joint for that test?", "options": {"A": "The weld joint failed, because the break did not occur in the weld metal", "B": "The test is invalid, because breaks must occur in the heat-affected zone", "C": "The weld is stronger than the base metal by exactly 8,000 psi", "D": "The weld joint is at least as strong as the base metal, so the test passes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weld joint is at least as strong as the base metal, so the test passes): When a transverse specimen fails in the base metal at a stress above the required minimum, the weld joint carried the load without failing first. The 8,000 psi difference only compares the test value to the minimum, not weld to plate."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A quenched and tempered steel such as A514 needs a minimum preheat to prevent cracking, but the supplier also caps heat input to protect strength. A welder believes both rules do the same job. How do the two controls actually differ in purpose?", "options": {"A": "Both controls prevent hydrogen cracking only, so they can be traded freely", "B": "Preheat protects the tempered strength, while the heat input cap limits hydrogen cracking in the weld", "C": "Preheat limits hardening and hydrogen cracking, while the heat input cap protects the tempered strength", "D": "Preheat raises the base metal's yield strength, while the cap lowers its hardness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Preheat limits hardening and hydrogen cracking, while the heat input cap protects the tempered strength): The preheat slows cooling to avoid hard martensite and let hydrogen escape, whereas the heat input cap stops the weld from overheating and softening the heat-treated base metal. They guard against opposite problems, so one cannot replace the other."}, {"id": 660, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "Two identical medium carbon steel weld samples are heated to 1600 degrees F, then one is cooled slowly in a furnace and the other is quenched in oil. The slow sample measures 20 Rockwell C points softer. Which pairing correctly links each cooling method with its resulting microstructure?", "options": {"A": "Furnace cooling gives martensite, while oil quenching gives soft ferrite and pearlite", "B": "Both samples give martensite, but the slow sample holds more retained austenite", "C": "Furnace cooling gives ferrite and pearlite, while oil quenching gives hard martensite", "D": "Furnace cooling gives cementite alone, while oil quenching produces coarse pearlite"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Furnace cooling gives ferrite and pearlite, while oil quenching gives hard martensite): Slow cooling lets carbon diffuse, producing soft ferrite and pearlite, while a quench traps carbon and forms hard martensite. That structural difference accounts for the 20 point hardness gap."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator is preparing a thick carbon steel pressure vessel nozzle weld, made with E7018 electrodes under heavy restraint, for the furnace. Before loading, the foreman asks the apprentice what the main purpose of the stress relief heat treatment is for this particular weldment. What is that purpose?", "options": {"A": "Raise the tensile strength of the weld metal well above the electrode classification minimum", "B": "Relieve residual stress in the weld and heat affected zone and temper hard microstructures", "C": "Refine the weld metal grain size by recrystallizing the whole plate above its upper critical temperature", "D": "Restore the corrosion resistance that was lost when chromium carbides formed along the grain boundaries"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Relieve residual stress in the weld and heat affected zone and temper hard microstructures): Stress relief holds the weldment below the lower critical temperature so residual stress relaxes and hard zones temper. It does not refine grains, which is what normalizing does, and it does not add strength."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A 2 inch thick carbon steel weldment is going into a furnace for stress relief under a typical structural code rule of one hour per inch of thickness with a fifteen minute minimum. The inspector asks the furnace operator how long the weldment must be held at the soak temperature.", "options": {"A": "Fifteen minutes, because the fifteen minute minimum governs weldments under three inches thick", "B": "Two hours at soak temperature, counted once the full thickness reaches that temperature", "C": "Four hours, since the holding time doubles for every inch beyond the first inch", "D": "One hour, because the rule counts only the weld throat rather than the plate thickness"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Two hours at soak temperature, counted once the full thickness reaches that temperature): One hour per inch of thickness applied to 2 inches gives 2 hours, and the clock starts when the full section is at temperature. The fifteen minute figure is only a floor for thin sections."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A furnace operator wants to save shift time by opening the furnace door at the end of the soak and letting a large welded frame cool in the open shop. The weld inspector stops the operator and points to the cooling rate limits in the procedure. What is the reason for a controlled cooling rate?", "options": {"A": "Rapid cooling from soak temperature would make the weld metal absorb atmospheric hydrogen from the furnace", "B": "Cooling faster than the code rate would raise the lower critical temperature of the steel", "C": "Controlled cooling is needed only so the furnace thermocouples stay within their calibration tolerance", "D": "Uneven fast cooling creates new thermal stresses and distortion that undo the benefit of treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uneven fast cooling creates new thermal stresses and distortion that undo the benefit of treatment): Thick and thin sections cool at different speeds, so quick cooling sets up thermal gradients that regenerate residual stress. The rate limit protects the stress relief that the soak achieved."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop supervisor proposes stress relieving a welded Type 304 stainless steel tank at 1100°F for several hours, using the same cycle that works on the shop's carbon steel tanks, to reduce distortion risk. A senior welding inspector objects strongly. What is the main metallurgical concern with this cycle for the stainless tank?", "options": {"A": "The cycle would convert the austenite to martensite and leave the tank hard and brittle", "B": "Time in the sensitization range forms grain boundary chromium carbides and cuts corrosion resistance", "C": "The austenitic grains would grow rapidly at that temperature and cause a large drop in toughness", "D": "Stainless steel loses its nonmagnetic property permanently once it is held above 1000°F for an hour"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Time in the sensitization range forms grain boundary chromium carbides and cuts corrosion resistance): A carbon steel stress relief temperature sits inside the 800°F to 1500°F band where 304 sensitizes. Chromium carbides form on grain boundaries and the adjacent metal loses its corrosion resistance."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A procedure writer is choosing a stress relief temperature for a quenched and tempered steel weldment. The mill tempered this plate at 1150°F. A colleague suggests a 1250°F stress relief to get the largest possible stress reduction. What is the problem with the colleague's suggestion?", "options": {"A": "That temperature sits above the lower critical point and would form fresh martensite during cooling", "B": "Higher soak temperatures reduce stress relief effectiveness because residual stress increases with temperature", "C": "Going above the plate's tempering temperature can soften it and drop strength below the specified minimum", "D": "The furnace would oxidize the surface so heavily that the plate would fall below minimum thickness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Going above the plate's tempering temperature can soften it and drop strength below the specified minimum): Stress relief on quenched and tempered plate has to stay under the temperature used to temper it, or the plate loses the strength the heat treatment gave it. 1250°F is below the lower critical point, so new martensite is not the issue."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding engineer lists the benefits of postweld heat treatment on a hardenable low alloy steel weldment, beyond relieving residual stress. A student asks what the treatment does to the hard heat affected zone next to the weld, which formed a martensitic structure during welding. What does the treatment do there?", "options": {"A": "Closes small porosity and slag inclusions by diffusion at the soak temperature", "B": "Tempers the hard brittle martensite so toughness and ductility improve", "C": "Raises the carbon content of the heat affected zone for greater hardness and wear resistance", "D": "Restores the original mill rolled grain structure of the base metal outside the weld"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tempers the hard brittle martensite so toughness and ductility improve): Soaking below the lower critical temperature tempers martensite, trading some hardness for toughness. Porosity and slag are not removed by heat treatment, and the carbon content does not change."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A crew is stress relieving a girth weld on 12 inch carbon steel pipe using electric resistance heating blankets. The inspector reviews the setup and wants the heated band to extend well past the weld toes, with insulation wrapped beyond the heated band. What is the reason for that arrangement?", "options": {"A": "It lets the blankets run at lower power so the thermocouples can be placed farther away", "B": "A wider band burns off surface scale so inspectors can see the weld toes", "C": "It preheats the next joint along the pipe run so welding can continue immediately", "D": "It softens the temperature gradient so cooler nearby metal adds no new stress"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It softens the temperature gradient so cooler nearby metal adds no new stress): A steep temperature gradient at the edge of a local heat treatment creates fresh thermal stress. A wide heated band with insulation beyond it spreads the gradient over a longer length of pipe."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "After welding the full thickness of two 1/2 inch plates with a single V groove from one side only, the plates are bent so the edges away from the joint tilt upward toward the groove side of the weld. A trainee is asked to name the type of distortion that occurred.", "options": {"A": "Angular distortion, caused by more shrinkage at the wide groove face than at the root", "B": "Longitudinal bowing, caused by shrinkage along the length of the weld in a thin member", "C": "Transverse shrinkage that pulls the two plates closer together across the root opening", "D": "Buckling caused by compressive residual stress in thin regions away from the weld"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Angular distortion, caused by more shrinkage at the wide groove face than at the root): The wide top of a single V holds more weld metal and shrinks more than the root, which rotates the plates toward the weld face. That rotation is angular distortion."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop welds 1 inch thick plate with a single V groove and sees severe angular distortion on every part. The engineer allows a joint redesign for the next order. Which change to the joint and sequence best reduces angular distortion on the same plate thickness?", "options": {"A": "Use a double V groove, alternating passes on both sides to balance shrinkage", "B": "Use a wider root opening and larger included angle so weld metal shrinks freely", "C": "Switch to a larger electrode and run fewer, heavier passes from the same side", "D": "Preheat only the groove faces on the open side so they shrink less than the root"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a double V groove, alternating passes on both sides to balance shrinkage): A double V cuts the weld volume and lets passes on opposite sides cancel each other's rotation. A wider groove adds weld metal, which increases shrinkage rather than reducing it."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is joining a 10 foot long stiffener to thin plate with a continuous fillet weld and wants to limit longitudinal bowing. The foreman says to deposit short segments, each welded in the direction opposite to the overall travel direction. What is this technique called and why does it help?", "options": {"A": "Skip welding; segments are placed at random spacing so the joint ends cool before the middle", "B": "Cascade welding; layers are built in stepped lengths so the root pass cools before filling", "C": "Stringer sequencing; narrow beads are laid straight so the heat affected zone stays small and straight", "D": "Backstep welding; short segments distribute the heat so shrinkage is spread along the joint length"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Backstep welding; short segments distribute the heat so shrinkage is spread along the joint length): In backstep welding each segment is deposited against the overall direction, so the heat and shrinkage are spread along the joint. Skip and cascade sequences are real techniques but do not describe this procedure."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding instructor is explaining residual stress in a restrained carbon steel butt joint in the as welded condition with no heat treatment applied. A student asks roughly how large the longitudinal residual tensile stress in the weld metal can become. What does the instructor tell the class?", "options": {"A": "About one tenth of the tensile strength because the base metal absorbs most of the stress", "B": "Approximately the yield strength of the weld metal in the direction along the weld", "C": "Near zero because the molten weld metal relaxes as it solidifies and cools", "D": "Roughly double the yield strength because shrinkage adds to the load stress"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Approximately the yield strength of the weld metal in the direction along the weld): Weld metal shrinks as it cools while the surrounding cold metal resists, so it stretches until it yields. The residual tensile stress therefore settles near yield strength."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is asked to reduce residual stress in a multipass groove weld in thick mild steel by light peening with a rounded tool. The procedure writer must decide which passes to peen. Which practice is appropriate for this weld?", "options": {"A": "Peen the root pass heavily to cold work it, and skip the intermediate passes", "B": "Peen only the final cap pass with a sharp chisel to close surface cracks", "C": "Peen intermediate passes while still warm, but leave root and cap passes unpeened", "D": "Peen every pass after it has cooled to room temperature for the best stress relief effect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Peen intermediate passes while still warm, but leave root and cap passes unpeened): Light peening of warm intermediate passes stretches the weld metal and offsets shrinkage. The root can crack under peening and the cap should stay unpeened so that peening does not hide surface defects from inspection."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator clamps a 3/4 inch steel plate rigidly to a heavy strongback to eliminate distortion before welding a long groove joint with E7018. After the clamps are released the next morning, transverse cracks appear across the weld. What is the most likely explanation for the cracking?", "options": {"A": "The strongback was a different alloy, so thermal expansion differences pulled the weld apart when unclamped", "B": "Clamping trapped hydrogen in the plate surface layers directly beneath the clamp faces", "C": "Rigid restraint blocked shrinkage, trading distortion for residual tensile stress above weld capacity", "D": "Clamping cooled the plate too fast, creating porosity that later grew into transverse cracks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rigid restraint blocked shrinkage, trading distortion for residual tensile stress above weld capacity): A joint that cannot move cannot relieve shrinkage, so the stress stays in the weld as residual tension and can exceed the weld's ability to carry it. Heavy restraint reduces distortion at the price of higher cracking risk."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder will fillet weld a flange to the end of a thin wall tube. Past jobs show the flange pulls toward the weld and ends up leaning inward. To counter this, the fitter sets the flange slightly off square in the opposite direction before tacking. What is this technique called?", "options": {"A": "Tack bridging, which ties both parts to a fixed plate until welding is finished", "B": "Presetting, which positions the parts opposite to the expected shrinkage movement", "C": "Backing up, which adds a permanent strip behind the joint to control shrinkage", "D": "Stress staggering, which offsets each pass to randomize the direction of residual stress"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Presetting, which positions the parts opposite to the expected shrinkage movement): Presetting, also called prebending, builds the expected shrinkage into the setup so the part pulls into position as the weld cools. It relies on past results for the same joint."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder runs a vertical up pass using 24 V, 220 A and a travel speed of 10 inches per minute. The procedure limits heat input to 35 kJ per inch to control distortion. Using heat input in kJ per inch equal to volts × amps × 60 ÷ (travel speed × 1000), what is the result?", "options": {"A": "About 21.1 kJ per inch, which is well under the 35 kJ per inch limit", "B": "About 31.7 kJ per inch, which is within the 35 kJ per inch limit", "C": "About 52.8 kJ per inch, which exceeds the 35 kJ per inch limit", "D": "About 316.8 kJ per inch, which far exceeds the 35 kJ per inch limit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 31.7 kJ per inch, which is within the 35 kJ per inch limit): 24 × 220 = 5,280 W; × 60 = 316,800; ÷ (10 × 1,000) = 31.68 kJ per inch, which is under 35. Leaving out the travel speed division gives the 316.8 figure."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder tells the foreman that 304 stainless sheet enclosures warp far more than identical mild steel enclosures welded with the same heat input and joint design on the same table. The foreman wants the physical explanation. Which pair of properties of austenitic stainless steel explains the extra distortion?", "options": {"A": "Lower thermal expansion and higher thermal conductivity, so heat spreads quickly into the cooler plate", "B": "Higher yield strength and higher conductivity, so shrinkage forces build faster across thin sections", "C": "Lower melting point and lower density, so the sheet pulls out of shape during cooling", "D": "Higher thermal expansion and lower thermal conductivity, so heat stays concentrated and expands more"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Higher thermal expansion and lower thermal conductivity, so heat stays concentrated and expands more): Austenitic stainless expands about half again as much as carbon steel and conducts heat poorly, so the heated zone stays hot and large. Both properties increase distortion on thin sheet."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "An inspector finds underbead cracking in the heat affected zone of a high strength steel weld two days after welding, with no visible defects when the weld was first inspected. The metallurgist says three conditions must exist together for this cracking to occur. Which set is correct?", "options": {"A": "Excess oxygen, a coarse ferrite grain structure, and compressive stress at the weld root", "B": "Nitrogen contamination, a fully austenitic structure, and slow cooling through the sensitization range", "C": "Sulfur segregation, a low melting point film, and high heat input at the crater", "D": "Diffusible hydrogen, a hard susceptible microstructure, and tensile residual stress in the joint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffusible hydrogen, a hard susceptible microstructure, and tensile residual stress in the joint): Hydrogen cracking needs hydrogen in the metal, hardened martensitic microstructure, and tensile stress together. Removing any one of them, usually with preheat and low hydrogen practice, prevents it."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A quality manager explains why a code requires a waiting period after welding thick high strength steel before final nondestructive testing is released. The explanation refers to when hydrogen induced cracks usually form relative to cooling temperature and time. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Cracks form above 2000°F while the weld metal is still partly liquid beneath the slag", "B": "Cracks form within seconds of arc extinction while the crater is still glowing", "C": "Cracks form only while the steel is held at preheat temperature during welding", "D": "Cracks form near ambient temperature, often hours or days after the weld has cooled"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cracks form near ambient temperature, often hours or days after the weld has cooled): Hydrogen cracking is delayed because hydrogen needs time to diffuse to stress concentrations in the hard zone. The waiting period lets such cracks show up before the final inspection."}, {"id": 679, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder opens a hermetically sealed can of E7018 electrodes for a job under AWS D1.1 and wants to keep the electrodes protected from moisture pickup between uses. The welder will carry only a few electrodes at a time. Where should the remaining electrodes be kept?", "options": {"A": "In an unheated toolbox with the lid closed to block humidity from the shop", "B": "In a heated holding oven at 250°F or higher until they are needed", "C": "In the open can near the work so they stay at ambient temperature", "D": "Wrapped in plastic inside a refrigerator below 40°F"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In a heated holding oven at 250°F or higher until they are needed): Low hydrogen coverings absorb moisture from humid air, so opened electrodes go into a holding oven at the minimum temperature the code specifies. A cold or unheated box does not stop moisture pickup."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "E7018 electrodes were left out on a humid shop floor for a full shift, well beyond the code exposure limit. The quality manager does not want to scrap the cartons if reconditioning is permitted by the code. What treatment is typically used to recondition low hydrogen electrodes?", "options": {"A": "Bake at 700°F to 800°F for at least one hour, then store in a holding oven", "B": "Dry them at 250°F in the holding oven for two hours, then use immediately", "C": "Dip them in solvent and wipe them dry before returning them to the can", "D": "Bake at 1000°F to 1200°F for thirty minutes to burn off the coating moisture"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bake at 700°F to 800°F for at least one hour, then store in a holding oven): Moisture absorbed into the covering must be baked out at 700°F to 800°F, well above holding oven temperature. A much hotter bake would damage the covering."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A plate has the chemistry C 0.20, Mn 1.20, Cr 0.10, Mo 0.05, V 0.02, Ni 0.10 and Cu 0.15 percent. An engineer uses the IIW formula CE = C + Mn/6 + (Cr + Mo + V)/5 + (Ni + Cu)/15 to estimate cracking risk. What carbon equivalent results?", "options": {"A": "About 0.25 percent, which indicates low cracking susceptibility from leaving out manganese", "B": "About 0.45 percent, a moderate level that usually calls for some preheat", "C": "About 0.49 percent, which indicates a slightly higher cracking susceptibility", "D": "About 0.82 percent, which indicates very high cracking susceptibility"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 0.45 percent, a moderate level that usually calls for some preheat): 0.20 + 1.20/6 = 0.40; (0.10 + 0.05 + 0.02)/5 = 0.034; (0.10 + 0.15)/15 = 0.017. The total is 0.451. Adding the alloy elements without dividing by their factors inflates the answer."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "An engineer checks a mill test report for a 2 inch thick restrained joint: C 0.25, Mn 1.50, Cr 0.20, Mo 0.10, V 0.00, Ni 0.30 and Cu 0.15 percent. Using the IIW carbon equivalent formula, which conclusion about preheat and procedure fits the result?", "options": {"A": "CE is about 0.41, so no preheat is needed for a restrained thick joint", "B": "CE is about 0.59, so this steel needs substantial preheat and low hydrogen practice", "C": "CE is about 0.50, so a 100°F preheat is adequate for thick joints that are restrained", "D": "CE is about 0.74, so the steel cannot be welded and needs replacing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (CE is about 0.59, so this steel needs substantial preheat and low hydrogen practice): 0.25 + 1.50/6 = 0.50; (0.20 + 0.10 + 0)/5 = 0.06; (0.30 + 0.15)/15 = 0.03. The sum is 0.59. High carbon equivalent steel is weldable but needs preheat, low hydrogen consumables and attention to restraint."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder preheats a 2 inch thick plate before tacking a restrained groove joint, heating only a narrow strip about 1 inch each side of the joint. The inspector measures the temperature at the edge of the strip and rejects the setup. What does AWS D1.1 require for the heated zone?", "options": {"A": "Preheat a band of one half the plate thickness on either side of the groove", "B": "Preheat to a distance equal to the plate thickness, but not less than 3 inches", "C": "Preheat only the groove faces themselves, since heat conducts through the plate anyway", "D": "Preheat out to 12 inches from the joint, because the code sets one fixed distance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Preheat to a distance equal to the plate thickness, but not less than 3 inches): D1.1 requires the minimum preheat in all directions from the weld for a distance at least equal to the thickest part, and not under 3 inches. For this plate that is 3 inches."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A trainee asks why preheating a hardenable steel plate reduces the risk of hydrogen cracking, when the weld will cool back to room temperature anyway. The instructor explains the effect on the cooling rate of the joint and the behavior of hydrogen in the steel. What is the explanation?", "options": {"A": "Slower cooling limits martensite and gives hydrogen time to diffuse out of the joint", "B": "It raises the carbon equivalent of the steel so the heat affected zone hardens less", "C": "It melts the surface oxide so the arc sheds hydrogen into the slag faster", "D": "It expands the plate so the weld shrinks less and keeps no residual stress after cooling"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slower cooling limits martensite and gives hydrogen time to diffuse out of the joint): Preheat slows cooling through the transformation range, which gives a softer microstructure, and it keeps the joint warm long enough for hydrogen to escape. Preheat does not change the carbon equivalent of the steel or melt surface oxide, so its benefit comes from slower cooling and hydrogen escape."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "An engineer reviews a purchase order calling for E7018-H4R electrodes for a crack sensitive steel repair on a thick restrained joint. A new buyer asks what the H4 designator tells the welder about the electrode. Which answer is correct for this AWS electrode designation?", "options": {"A": "Moisture in the covering does not exceed four percent by weight after baking", "B": "Diffusible hydrogen in the weld deposit stays at or below four milliliters per 100 grams", "C": "The electrode can be exposed to air for four hours before it needs rebaking", "D": "The deposit has a minimum Charpy impact value of four foot pounds at the test temperature"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diffusible hydrogen in the weld deposit stays at or below four milliliters per 100 grams): The H designator gives maximum diffusible hydrogen in milliliters per 100 grams of deposited metal, so H4 is a very low hydrogen electrode. The R means moisture resistant covering."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder repairing an oil pipeline support uses E6010 on a high carbon equivalent steel and later finds toe cracks growing in the heat affected zone. The inspector blames the electrode type. Which change would reduce the hydrogen cracking risk in the repair?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a low hydrogen electrode such as E7018 with proper storage and preheat", "B": "Switch to E6011 because its covering carries less moisture than E6010", "C": "Use E7024 since its heavy iron powder coating shields the weld more fully", "D": "Use a smaller E6010 electrode and travel faster to cut the time hydrogen can enter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Switch to a low hydrogen electrode such as E7018 with proper storage and preheat): Cellulosic coverings such as E6010 release a lot of hydrogen into the arc. A low hydrogen electrode, kept dry and used with preheat, removes the main hydrogen source in this repair."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "On a rainy site, a welder prepares a joint on 1 inch A572 Grade 50 plate. Moisture and mill oil are visible on the groove faces. The welder plans to weld with E7018. What step best reduces hydrogen risk before an arc is struck?", "options": {"A": "Weld through the moisture because the arc heat will burn it off before the pool solidifies", "B": "Dry and clean the groove faces, remove oil, and preheat above the dew point as specified", "C": "Switch to a cellulosic electrode, since it tolerates moisture better than a low hydrogen type", "D": "Spray a thick coat of anti spatter compound to seal the surface from water"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dry and clean the groove faces, remove oil, and preheat above the dew point as specified): Moisture and oil break down in the arc and put hydrogen into the weld pool. Drying and cleaning the joint and preheating it keeps the low hydrogen electrode from being undermined by the base metal."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A stainless fabricator hears the term sensitization while reviewing a failed 304 tank weld that suffered intergranular corrosion in the heat affected zone. The metallurgist uses the word to describe what happened to the metal. What does sensitization mean here?", "options": {"A": "Delta ferrite forms in the weld and makes the stainless steel magnetic and brittle", "B": "Nitrogen enters the weld pool from the air and forms hard nitrides along the weld surface", "C": "Chromium carbides form at grain boundaries and leave nearby grains short of chromium", "D": "The passive oxide film thickens and becomes too stable to protect the grain faces"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chromium carbides form at grain boundaries and leave nearby grains short of chromium): In sensitized stainless the carbon ties up chromium as carbides at the grain boundaries. The depleted zones next to them can no longer form a protective film, so intergranular corrosion follows."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding procedure for 304 stainless tubing in a food plant limits the time the heat affected zone spends in one temperature band to reduce intergranular corrosion risk. The inspector asks the welder to name the critical sensitization range for the common austenitic grades. Which range is correct?", "options": {"A": "Approximately 200°F to 500°F, where hydrogen becomes mobile in the heat affected zone", "B": "Approximately 800°F to 1500°F, where chromium carbides form along grain boundaries", "C": "Approximately 1600°F to 1800°F, where martensite begins forming in austenitic grades", "D": "Approximately 2000°F to 2300°F, where delta ferrite dissolves into the austenite matrix"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Approximately 800°F to 1500°F, where chromium carbides form along grain boundaries): Sensitization happens when stainless dwells between roughly 800°F and 1500°F long enough for carbides to form. Keeping heat input and interpass temperature low shortens that dwell."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is building a process vessel from 304L stainless steel that will not be solution annealed after welding. The drawing needs intergranular corrosion resistance to be preserved in the weld. Which filler metal pairs best with the 304L base metal?", "options": {"A": "ER316L, because its molybdenum is needed whenever the vessel contains water", "B": "ER410, because its martensitic structure gives the highest weld strength for vessels", "C": "ER347, because its higher carbon content resists chromium carbide formation", "D": "ER308L, whose low carbon content limits chromium carbide formation in the weld"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (ER308L, whose low carbon content limits chromium carbide formation in the weld): 308L is the standard match for 304L, and its low carbon keeps carbides from forming in an as welded joint. 347 resists sensitization through niobium, not through higher carbon."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator must weld 1/4 inch A36 carbon steel to 304 stainless steel for a bracket. The welder asks which filler avoids hard brittle martensite in the diluted weld metal. Which filler classification is normally selected for this dissimilar joint?", "options": {"A": "ER308L, because it matches the stainless side and dilution has little effect", "B": "ER70S-6, because it matches the carbon steel and bonds to the stainless easily", "C": "ER316L, because molybdenum prevents martensite forming from carbon steel dilution", "D": "ER309L, whose extra chromium and nickel offset dilution from the steel"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (ER309L, whose extra chromium and nickel offset dilution from the steel): Carbon steel dilutes the weld and pulls chromium and nickel down. 309L starts with extra of both, so the diluted deposit remains austenitic instead of turning martensitic."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welding engineer specifies 308L filler with a ferrite number of 5 to 10 for an austenitic stainless weld. A new technician asks why a little ferrite is wanted when the base metal is fully austenitic. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Ferrite makes the weld nonmagnetic so magnetic particle inspection can be skipped later", "B": "Ferrite raises the weld's corrosion resistance above that of the fully austenitic base metal", "C": "Ferrite increases ductility at cryogenic temperatures better than austenite does", "D": "Small ferrite amounts absorb impurities at solidification and lower the hot cracking risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Small ferrite amounts absorb impurities at solidification and lower the hot cracking risk): A fully austenitic weld tends to crack as it solidifies because impurities concentrate at the boundaries. A few percent ferrite dissolves them and so protects against solidification cracking."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder runs the root pass on 316L stainless pipe with GTAW, using argon only on the torch side, and finds a rough, dark gray, sugared surface inside the pipe at the root. The pipe interior was not purged before welding. What corrective practice prevents this problem on the next joint?", "options": {"A": "Raise the amperage so the root cools faster and oxide has less time to form", "B": "Use a carbon steel wire brush on the root to remove the dark scale", "C": "Purge the pipe interior with argon until oxygen is very low, before and during welding", "D": "Switch to pure carbon dioxide inside the pipe to shield the root from air"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Purge the pipe interior with argon until oxygen is very low, before and during welding): Hot stainless exposed to air at the root oxidizes into a crusty sugared surface. An argon backing purge keeps oxygen away while the metal is hot."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A shop uses the same wire brush on carbon steel and on stainless steel pipe at one bench. Weeks after fabrication, the stainless pipe shows scattered rust speckles near the welds and the owner calls for an explanation. What is the likely cause, and what is the fix for future work?", "options": {"A": "Chloride in the shielding gas deposited rust; replace the argon with an argon helium mix", "B": "The filler was too low in chromium; switch from 308L to 309L filler", "C": "Embedded iron from the brush contaminated the surface; use a dedicated stainless brush", "D": "Interpass temperature was too low; keep the weld above 600°F between passes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Embedded iron from the brush contaminated the surface; use a dedicated stainless brush): Carbon steel particles left in the surface rust and break the passive film. Dedicated stainless tools, kept only for stainless, avoid the contamination."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is multipass welding 3/8 inch 316L stainless plate for a food process line. The procedure limits interpass temperature to 350°F, but a fabricator wants to skip the limit to save time. What is the main risk of exceeding the interpass limit on this austenitic grade?", "options": {"A": "Longer time in the sensitization range and greater distortion and hot cracking tendency", "B": "Hydrogen cracking, since austenitic steels harden into martensite when they cool slowly", "C": "Loss of the passive film because the heat boils away the chromium on the surface", "D": "Excess ferrite in the weld metal, which leaves the joint magnetic and brittle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Longer time in the sensitization range and greater distortion and hot cracking tendency): Austenitic stainless holds heat because of low conductivity. A hot interpass keeps the zone near the sensitization range longer and increases distortion and cracking."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welded tank section made from Type 321 stainless operates continuously at about 1000°F, and a young designer asks why 321 was chosen over 304 for service in the sensitization range. A senior engineer gives the metallurgical reason. Which explanation is accurate for this stabilized grade?", "options": {"A": "Titanium raises the melting point so the grain boundaries stay solid during service heating", "B": "Titanium forms a thicker passive layer that blocks sensitization from the outside surface", "C": "Titanium ties up carbon as carbides, so chromium stays in solution at grain boundaries", "D": "Titanium converts the austenite to ferrite, which does not sensitize in this range"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Titanium ties up carbon as carbides, so chromium stays in solution at grain boundaries): Titanium bonds with carbon in preference to chromium, so no chromium carbide forms and the grain boundaries keep their corrosion resistance. Niobium does the same job in type 347."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A new welder cleans a 6061 plate with solvent and starts GTAW, but the weld is dull with poor fusion and inclusions. The instructor says the oxide film on aluminum is the main problem. Which property of that oxide makes it a welding problem?", "options": {"A": "Its melting point is far above that of the aluminum base metal beneath it", "B": "It is magnetic and deflects the arc away from the joint centerline", "C": "It is an electrical insulator but melts at a lower temperature than the aluminum", "D": "It reacts with argon to form a hard layer on the weld surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Its melting point is far above that of the aluminum base metal beneath it): Aluminum oxide melts near 3700°F while the metal below melts near 1200°F, so the film stays solid in the pool and causes poor fusion and inclusions unless removed. The oxide is not magnetic, it does not react with argon to form a hard layer, and it melts far above the base metal rather than below it."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is preparing 1/4 inch 5052 aluminum plate for GMAW. The plate has light oil from machining and a visible oxide film. The shop asks for a cleaning sequence that removes both oil and oxide before welding begins. Which sequence is correct for this plate?", "options": {"A": "Brush with a carbon steel brush first, then wipe with solvent and weld the next day", "B": "Degrease with solvent first, then use a dedicated stainless brush just before welding", "C": "Grind the joint with a standard grinding wheel and weld immediately without a solvent wipe", "D": "Wipe the plate with water and a cloth, then store it overnight to dry"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Degrease with solvent first, then use a dedicated stainless brush just before welding): Solvent removes oil first so the brush does not drive it into the metal, and brushing right before welding limits the time the oxide has to reform. Carbon steel brushes contaminate aluminum."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder is GTAW welding aluminum with AC and a trainee asks about the half of the cycle in which the electrode is positive. The instructor says that half cycle has a specific benefit for aluminum. What does it do?", "options": {"A": "Delivers most of the heat into the base metal for deep penetration", "B": "Breaks up the surface oxide film, giving the cleaning action", "C": "Cools the tungsten between pulses so the point stays sharp", "D": "Stabilizes the arc by raising the voltage across the gap"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Breaks up the surface oxide film, giving the cleaning action): Electrode positive current removes the oxide film, while electrode negative gives the penetration. AC combines both so aluminum can be welded cleanly."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A fabricator welds 6061-T6 aluminum frames that will be anodized after welding, and the customer wants the weld color to match the base metal as closely as possible. The shop has both silicon and magnesium alloy wire on the shelf. Which filler is the better choice for color match?", "options": {"A": "4043, since its silicon content gives a bright white weld after anodizing", "B": "ER1100, because pure aluminum has no alloying elements to change the color", "C": "5356, since its magnesium content anodizes to a color closer to the 6061 base metal", "D": "ER4047, whose high silicon content is the standard choice for anodized parts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (5356, since its magnesium content anodizes to a color closer to the 6061 base metal): Silicon in 4043 and 4047 turns the weld a dark gray after anodizing, while 5356 stays much closer to the 6061 color. Strength and crack resistance still need to be checked for the application."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder lays an autogenous GTAW bead, without filler wire, on 6061-T6 aluminum plate and finds a crack running down the centerline of the bead. The shop lead tells the welder to add filler wire and rerun it. Which reasoning supports adding 4043 filler for this alloy?", "options": {"A": "Filler adds enough copper to raise the weld melting point above that of the base", "B": "Silicon rich filler moves the weld chemistry out of the crack range and improves fluidity", "C": "Filler cools the pool below the oxide formation temperature so cracks cannot start", "D": "Filler adds hydrogen free shielding that blocks porosity, which is the real cause of cracking"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Silicon rich filler moves the weld chemistry out of the crack range and improves fluidity): 6061 cracks when melted on its own because of its composition. Silicon rich 4043 changes the weld chemistry and gives a fluid pool that fills as it solidifies."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A tank trailer builder wants to use 5356 filler on 5083 aluminum for a vessel that will see continuous service near 200°F in a heated cargo. An experienced welding engineer advises against it and asks for a different filler or alloy. What is the engineer's reason for the advice?", "options": {"A": "5356 softens and loses most of its shear strength once the vessel passes 120°F in service", "B": "The filler's silicon would form brittle eutectic films that melt below 200°F in service", "C": "High magnesium alloys can crack from stress corrosion in prolonged service above about 150°F", "D": "Heat above 150°F makes the oxide layer thicker and impossible to remove with brushing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High magnesium alloys can crack from stress corrosion in prolonged service above about 150°F): Aluminum alloys with more than about 3 percent magnesium can sensitize to stress corrosion cracking when held above roughly 150°F for long periods, so such service calls for a different filler or alloy. The other reasons describe strength loss, brittle silicon films or oxide growth, none of which is the stress corrosion cracking problem in warm service."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A GMAW welder produces scattered fine porosity through aluminum welds on 5052 sheet in a damp shop. The wire spool had been left unwrapped on the machine for several weeks over the summer. What is the most likely cause of the porosity, and what correction fits it?", "options": {"A": "Hydrogen from moisture on the wire; store wire sealed and clean the plate", "B": "Nitrogen from air in the argon; switch to a helium shielding gas alone", "C": "Too much current; lower the amperage to cut the weld pool temperature in half", "D": "Wire too soft; change to a larger diameter wire for stiffer feeding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydrogen from moisture on the wire; store wire sealed and clean the plate): Aluminum pools dissolve hydrogen readily and release it as pores on freezing. Moisture on the wire surface is a common source, so sealed storage and clean material are the fix."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder runs 1/2 inch 6061 aluminum plate with the same amperage and travel speed used on mild steel of that thickness and finds poor fusion at the start of every bead, even on clean plate. Which property of aluminum best explains the lack of fusion at the start of the bead?", "options": {"A": "High thermal conductivity pulls heat away from the arc, so thick plate may need preheat", "B": "Low melting point makes the pool collapse into the root before the plate can fuse", "C": "High electrical resistance wastes arc energy as heat in the electrode wire", "D": "Low density lets the arc force push the pool away from the joint line"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High thermal conductivity pulls heat away from the arc, so thick plate may need preheat): Aluminum conducts heat several times better than steel, so the plate drains the arc's heat. Thick sections need more current, faster travel to stay ahead of the puddle, or a modest preheat."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A repair welder is asked to weld a cracked gray cast iron pump housing on a customer's job site. The shop foreman warns that gray iron cracks very easily during and after welding. Which property of gray cast iron makes it prone to cracking from weld shrinkage strain?", "options": {"A": "A very low melting point that makes the casting collapse before the weld can solidify", "B": "A high sulfur content that forms low melting films along grain boundaries in the casting", "C": "Very low ductility, with flake graphite that cannot absorb weld shrinkage strain", "D": "A thick oxide skin like aluminum that blocks fusion unless brushed away first"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Very low ductility, with flake graphite that cannot absorb weld shrinkage strain): Gray iron has almost no ductility, and its flake graphite cannot stretch to absorb weld shrinkage, so the heat affected zone cracks readily. A low melting point, high sulfur or an aluminum like oxide skin are not what makes gray iron crack from shrinkage strain."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder repairs a crack in a gray iron engine block without preheating, using a nickel based covered electrode. The welder asks about technique that limits cracking in the casting beside the weld. Which technique is recommended for this cold repair of the casting?", "options": {"A": "Run one continuous long bead at high amperage so the casting heats up evenly", "B": "Use a wide weave at high amperage to melt out the graphite flakes", "C": "Quench each short bead in water right after welding to lock in its shape", "D": "Deposit short beads, peen each one while hot, and let the casting cool before continuing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Deposit short beads, peen each one while hot, and let the casting cool before continuing): Short, peened beads at low heat input keep the casting cool and relieve shrinkage stress, which suits cold welding with nickel. Quenching or heavy heat input would crack the brittle iron."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A repair shop receives an unidentified metal part for welding, and a technician makes a spark test on a bench grinding wheel before selecting a filler. The technician says the spark pattern points to gray cast iron. Which spark pattern is typical for gray cast iron?", "options": {"A": "Long bright white sparks with many starbursts, carried far from the wheel", "B": "Short dull red sparks with little branching, staying close to the grinding wheel", "C": "Long straw colored streaks with few breaks, ending in a small arrow tip", "D": "A bright orange glow at the wheel contact point with no visible sparks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Short dull red sparks with little branching, staying close to the grinding wheel): Gray iron gives short dull red sparks with little branching because the carbon is bound up as graphite flakes. Long bright starburst sparks suggest high carbon steel."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A mechanic must weld a broken ductile iron suspension component and wants to know how it differs from the gray iron castings the shop usually repairs. How does the graphite structure of ductile iron differ from gray iron, and what does that mean for weldability?", "options": {"A": "Graphite exists as round nodules, giving better ductility and weldability than flake gray iron", "B": "Graphite is absent, so it welds like low carbon steel with ordinary electrodes", "C": "Graphite exists as sharp flakes joined in networks, which makes it tougher than steel", "D": "Carbon is combined as iron carbide throughout, so it needs no preheat when welded"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Graphite exists as round nodules, giving better ductility and weldability than flake gray iron): Ductile iron has rounded graphite nodules that do not act as crack starters, so it tolerates strain better than gray iron. It still needs proper procedure because carbon pickup can form hard zones."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A joint connects carbon steel to a low alloy steel with higher hardenability, and both are the same thickness. The WPS writer must pick the filler metal and preheat for this dissimilar joint under an ordinary code. Which approach is normally used for the filler and the preheat?", "options": {"A": "Select filler matching the stronger alloy and skip preheat because the carbon steel dilutes it", "B": "Select filler for the weaker side's strength, and preheat to suit the more hardenable steel", "C": "Select a stainless filler so it bridges between the two with no concern for preheat", "D": "Select filler by the thicker section only and preheat to the thinner section's needs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Select filler for the weaker side's strength, and preheat to suit the more hardenable steel): The weld cannot be stronger than needed for the lower strength side, while the hardenable steel sets the cracking risk and therefore the preheat. Choosing filler by the stronger side or by thickness alone ignores that preheat follows the hardenable steel."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A boiler header joint between carbon steel and austenitic stainless steel will see many heating and cooling cycles over years of service. The designer picks a nickel base filler such as ERNiCr-3 instead of 309L for the joint. What reason justifies choosing the nickel filler?", "options": {"A": "Nickel alloy melts at a lower temperature, so it prevents dilution with the carbon steel", "B": "Its expansion lies between the two metals, reducing thermal fatigue stress and carbon migration", "C": "Nickel filler is magnetic, which allows magnetic particle inspection of the whole joint", "D": "Nickel alloy filler has higher carbon content, which strengthens the stainless fusion boundary"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Its expansion lies between the two metals, reducing thermal fatigue stress and carbon migration): In cyclic high temperature service, the expansion mismatch of 309L against carbon steel can fatigue the fusion line. A nickel alloy sits between the two expansions and resists carbon migration."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder deposits 308L filler directly onto mild steel for a build up and finds hard cracked martensitic zones in the first layer of the weld. The welder asks why the stainless filler did not give a tough deposit. What caused this problem in the first layer?", "options": {"A": "Excess ferrite from the 308L filler became brittle when it cooled slowly through 1000°F", "B": "Dilution from the carbon steel dropped alloy content low enough for martensite to form", "C": "Carbon steel cannot bond with stainless weld metal, so cracks formed at the interface", "D": "Sigma phase formed because the carbon steel supplied extra chromium to the pool"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dilution from the carbon steel dropped alloy content low enough for martensite to form): Mixing mild steel into 308L lowers the chromium and nickel percentages of the deposit, so the weld hardens as martensite. 309L is used because it holds up better under that dilution."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder must join a gray cast iron bracket to a mild steel plate for a farm repair, and the shop asks which filler is most suitable for the dissimilar joint. Carbon will dissolve from the cast iron into the weld pool. Which filler should the welder choose?", "options": {"A": "A nickel iron electrode such as ENiFe-CI, which tolerates carbon pickup from the cast iron", "B": "E7018 low hydrogen electrode, since it matches the steel and tolerates carbon pickup", "C": "ER308L, since the stainless deposit resists cracking from carbon migration", "D": "E6010 cellulosic electrode, since its deep penetration fuses graphite flakes into the weld"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A nickel iron electrode such as ENiFe-CI, which tolerates carbon pickup from the cast iron): Nickel and nickel iron deposits stay soft and dissolve carbon without forming hard carbides, so they suit cast iron. An E7018 deposit would pick up carbon and turn hard and brittle."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A designer wants to arc weld an aluminum bracket directly to carbon steel plate using ER4043 filler, with no transition piece. An experienced welding engineer says the joint will not hold up in service. What is the main metallurgical reason for the failure of this joint?", "options": {"A": "The oxide layer on aluminum is absorbed by the steel and cannot be removed", "B": "The large melting point gap makes the steel vaporize before the aluminum fills the groove", "C": "Brittle iron aluminum intermetallic compounds form at the interface and crack under stress", "D": "Carbon from the steel dissolves into the aluminum pool and forms hard graphite flakes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Brittle iron aluminum intermetallic compounds form at the interface and crack under stress): Molten aluminum on steel forms brittle iron aluminum intermetallics at the interface. Such joints use mechanical fasteners, explosion bonded transition pieces or friction processes instead."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder reads the electrode classification E7018 on a box for a structural steel job and asks the lead what the first two digits stand for. The lead points to the AWS A5.1 specification. Which meaning is correct for the first two digits of this classification?", "options": {"A": "Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi", "B": "Maximum carbon content of the deposited weld metal is 0.70 percent", "C": "Welding current required for the electrode is 70 amperes at one eighth inch", "D": "Minimum yield strength of the base metal that the electrode may weld is 70 ksi"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Minimum tensile strength of the deposited weld metal is 70,000 psi): In an E70XX classification the digits after E give minimum tensile strength in thousands of psi, so 70 means 70,000 psi. The digits do not give carbon content, current or base metal yield strength; they state the tensile strength of the deposited weld metal."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder needs a structural electrode for flat and horizontal fillet welds only, with a heavy iron powder covering for a high deposition rate on thick plate in a shop. The foreman asks for the AWS A5.1 classification. Which classification fits this description?", "options": {"A": "E7024, a heavy iron powder electrode limited to flat and horizontal fillet positions", "B": "E6010, a cellulosic electrode for deep penetration in all positions on DC electrode positive", "C": "E7018, a low hydrogen electrode limited to flat position welding on thick plate", "D": "E6013, a rutile electrode intended for vertical down welding on thin sheet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (E7024, a heavy iron powder electrode limited to flat and horizontal fillet positions): E7024 has a thick iron powder covering that gives high deposition but restricts it to flat and horizontal work. E7018 is a low hydrogen electrode usable in all positions."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "An engineer proposes E9018 electrodes instead of E7018 for fillet welds on 50 ksi structural steel, reasoning that stronger weld metal means a stronger joint. A senior inspector disagrees with the choice. What is the best reason to avoid the stronger filler in this case?", "options": {"A": "Overmatched weld metal adds hardness and crack risk, and the code rarely needs it for fillets", "B": "E9018 deposits are lower in deposition rate so the joint cannot reach full size", "C": "E9018 loses tensile strength once the weld cools through 1000°F", "D": "E9018 has no AWS classification and cannot be used on structural steel"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Overmatched weld metal adds hardness and crack risk, and the code rarely needs it for fillets): Fillet welds in structural steel are normally sized with E70 electrodes. Higher strength filler raises hardness and cracking risk and adds cost with little design benefit."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A welder finds a crack running along the centerline of a deep narrow weld bead, discovered right after the weld solidified. The weld was made on carbon steel with a single heavy pass using a high current. Which type of cracking is this crack on the centerline of the bead?", "options": {"A": "Hydrogen cracking caused by hydrogen collecting in the hard heat affected zone", "B": "Lamellar tearing caused by weak nonmetallic stringers in the rolled plate", "C": "Reheat cracking caused by stress relaxation during postweld heat treatment", "D": "Solidification cracking caused by low melting films being pulled apart as the weld shrinks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Solidification cracking caused by low melting films being pulled apart as the weld shrinks): A centerline crack formed during freezing is solidification cracking. The last liquid, rich in low melting impurities, is pulled apart by shrinkage across the narrow weld."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A submerged arc welder on thick plate has centerline cracks in deep narrow passes with a width to depth ratio well below one. The procedure allows changes to current, voltage and travel speed within qualified limits. Which change best reduces the centerline cracking in these passes?", "options": {"A": "Raise current and travel speed to deepen penetration and narrow the bead further", "B": "Lower the arc voltage and increase stickout to deepen the bead profile", "C": "Increase arc voltage to widen the bead and reduce current to lessen penetration depth", "D": "Increase travel speed alone to chill the pool faster before shrinkage stresses build"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase arc voltage to widen the bead and reduce current to lessen penetration depth): Cracking drops when the bead is wider than it is deep, so impurities are not trapped at the center. Higher voltage widens the bead and lower current reduces penetration."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "At the end of a GTAW pass on 304 stainless, small star shaped cracks appear in the crater. The welder shuts off the arc abruptly and lifts the torch away at the end of the weld, as a habit. What technique prevents this kind of crater cracking at the end of a weld?", "options": {"A": "Fill the crater by pausing or tapering current with a downslope before ending the arc", "B": "Whip the torch away at full current so the crater cools rapidly in air", "C": "Add extra cold filler wire to the crater after the arc is out", "D": "Stop the weld abruptly at high current and grind the crater flat afterward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fill the crater by pausing or tapering current with a downslope before ending the arc): An abrupt stop leaves a deep concave crater that freezes under tension, which is a solidification crack. Filling it and tapering the current leaves a convex crater that resists cracking."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "welding_metallurgy", "question": "A lot of free machining steel with high sulfur shows centerline cracking when welded with solid wire in a robotic cell, even with good bead shape. The metallurgist says the sulfur content is the main concern. Which explanation fits what is happening in the weld metal?", "options": {"A": "Sulfur raises the melting point of the weld metal, so it freezes before shrinkage ends", "B": "Sulfur combines with hydrogen to form gas pockets that crack the weld centerline", "C": "Sulfur forms low melting iron sulfide films at grain boundaries that tear as the weld shrinks", "D": "Sulfur hardens the heat affected zone into martensite that cracks after cooling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sulfur forms low melting iron sulfide films at grain boundaries that tear as the weld shrinks): Iron sulfide melts at a much lower temperature than the surrounding steel, so thin liquid films stay between grains while the weld shrinks and are torn open. Free machining steel is therefore poor for welding."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welding inspector starts a shift on a structural steel fabrication job governed by AWS D1.1. Before any arc is struck, the inspector reviews joint fit-up, base metal identification, and the preheat setup against the welding procedure. Which phase of visual inspection does this activity represent, and why is it performed first?", "options": {"A": "Inspection during welding, since interpass conditions are judged once the first pass has cooled", "B": "Inspection before welding, since fit-up and material errors cost least to fix before metal is deposited", "C": "Inspection after welding, since dimensional checks need a finished bead to compare against tolerances", "D": "Final acceptance inspection, since the code groups every check into one end of job review"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inspection before welding, since fit-up and material errors cost least to fix before metal is deposited): Pre-weld checks cover fit-up, root opening, cleanliness, material markings, and equipment setup. Errors found here cost minutes, while the same errors found afterward can mean gouging out finished weld metal."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "On a pipe shop floor, an inspector holds a fillet weld gauge against a completed tee joint to compare the weld with the size called for on the drawing. The gauge is a set of steel leaves with convex and concave shapes cut into them. What does this gauge primarily measure?", "options": {"A": "Undercut depth along the toe, read directly from a sliding scale on the gauge", "B": "Fillet leg size, with the right leaf also showing whether the face is convex or concave", "C": "Root penetration of the joint, read by pushing a thin feeler pin through the weld throat", "D": "Hi-lo misalignment between the two plates, measured at the weld root before any welding starts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fillet leg size, with the right leaf also showing whether the face is convex or concave): Fillet gauges check leg length and help judge face profile. They do not read undercut depth or root penetration, which need other tools or other test methods."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator needs to check the bevel angle on a groove joint in 1 inch plate before welding begins, and later the reinforcement height and fillet size after welding. The inspector reaches for one adjustable gauge with a sliding scale. Which gauge is this inspector most likely holding?", "options": {"A": "An ultrasonic thickness gauge, which reads plate wall thickness along the bevel face", "B": "A magnetic particle yoke, which shows indications along the toe between its legs", "C": "A bridge cam gauge, which reads bevel angle before welding and reinforcement or fillet size afterward", "D": "A contact pyrometer, which reads interpass temperature at the weld face during the second pass"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A bridge cam gauge, which reads bevel angle before welding and reinforcement or fillet size afterward): The bridge cam gauge combines several visual checks, including bevel angle in preparation and reinforcement or fillet size after welding. It cannot find surface cracks or read temperature."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "During a visual inspection course, an instructor asks students to separate the terms discontinuity and defect as AWS uses them. A student writes an answer on the board, and the class decides whether it matches accepted usage. Which statement correctly separates the two terms?", "options": {"A": "A discontinuity is a flaw found by nondestructive testing, while a defect is found by sight", "B": "A discontinuity is an interruption in the weld, becoming a defect past the code limits", "C": "A defect is a flaw inside the weld, while a discontinuity only reaches the surface", "D": "A discontinuity needs repair when found, while a defect may stay with an owner waiver"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A discontinuity is an interruption in the weld, becoming a defect past the code limits): Welds contain some discontinuities. A discontinuity becomes a rejectable defect only when it exceeds the acceptance criteria of the governing code, so the code and contract decide."}, {"id": 725, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector measures a finished GMAW fillet on a 1/2 inch plate tee joint. The drawing calls for a 3/8 inch fillet. A fillet gauge shows 1/4 inch legs along a 4 inch stretch of an otherwise full length weld, and the rest of the weld measures full size. What action fits the inspector's role?", "options": {"A": "Accept the weld, since a fillet within 1/16 inch of size passes on statically loaded work", "B": "Reject that stretch as undersized, record its location, and have the welder add weld metal", "C": "Reject the entire weld and require full removal, since a short leg leaves no usable strength", "D": "Accept the weld after confirming the vertical leg is full size, since one leg sets throat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reject that stretch as undersized, record its location, and have the welder add weld metal): An undersize fillet in one area is fixed by adding weld metal there. The inspector records the finding and reinspects. Removing the whole weld is not needed for a local shortfall."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector checks a groove weld on 1 inch plate with a bridge cam gauge. Reinforcement at the center of the cap reads 1/8 inch. The drawing and the governing code allow a maximum of 1/8 inch reinforcement for this thickness. How should the inspector record the cap reinforcement?", "options": {"A": "Acceptable, since the measured height does not exceed the maximum the code allows", "B": "Rejectable, since reinforcement above flush leaves a stress riser on statically loaded joints", "C": "Rejectable, since the cap on plate over 3/4 inch must stand at least 3/16 inch high", "D": "Acceptable after grinding the cap flush, since reinforcement is not permitted on groove welds"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acceptable, since the measured height does not exceed the maximum the code allows): A value equal to the maximum passes. Reinforcement limits are written as maximum heights, and flush is not required unless the contract says so."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector watches a welder finish the root pass on a single V groove in A36 plate using E7018 electrodes. Before the second pass starts, the inspector examines the cooled root bead and the groove sidewalls. Which inspection step belongs at this interpass stage of the job?", "options": {"A": "Measure final reinforcement height on the root bead, since it sets the cap profile", "B": "Check the root bead for cracks and porosity and confirm slag is cleaned from the sidewalls", "C": "Verify final weld size with a fillet gauge, since the root pass fixes the leg length", "D": "Stamp the welder's identification beside the joint, since the root pass completes the weld"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Check the root bead for cracks and porosity and confirm slag is cleaned from the sidewalls): Interpass visual checks catch cracks, porosity, and trapped slag while they are still reachable. Final size and profile checks come after welding, once the cap is finished."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A shop inspector examines a finished butt weld on 3/8 inch plate under good light. The cap is smooth, but the toe on one side shows a shallow groove along 2 inches of its length. The inspector measures its depth at 0.020 inch, and the code limit is 1/32 inch. What is the correct call?", "options": {"A": "Rejectable, since measurable undercut on a butt weld must be repaired with added weld metal", "B": "Acceptable, since 0.020 inch is less than the 1/32 inch (0.031 inch) limit", "C": "Rejectable, since undercut length beyond 1 inch is not permitted under the code at any depth", "D": "Acceptable only if the groove is ground blended, since undercut cannot stay as welded"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acceptable, since 0.020 inch is less than the 1/32 inch (0.031 inch) limit): Undercut is judged by depth and length against the code limit. 1/32 inch equals 0.031 inch, so 0.020 inch is inside the limit. Compare the measured value to the actual contract numbers."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A pipe welder reports that flux cored wire on an outdoor job keeps leaving scattered surface holes. The inspector notes a steady 15 mph breeze across the joint, the gas flow at the cylinder reads normal, and the nozzle is clean. Which cause should be addressed first?", "options": {"A": "Slag left from the previous pass, which releases gas bubbles into the next bead", "B": "Travel speed that is too slow, which lets the puddle lose its protection as it cools", "C": "Wind disturbing the shielding gas at the arc, so the joint needs a windbreak", "D": "A bevel angle that is too wide, which traps shielding gas under the root face"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wind disturbing the shielding gas at the arc, so the joint needs a windbreak): Wind strips shielding gas away from the arc, letting air reach the puddle and form porosity. A windbreak or a self shielded wire addresses it. Slag and bevel angle do not scatter pores this way."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder finishes a cap pass with the bead rolling over the plate surface at the toe instead of blending into it. The inspector sees bead metal sitting on top of the plate with no fusion beneath it. Which instruction addresses the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Increase preheat on the plate edge so the toe melts away before the bead arrives", "B": "Lengthen the arc and weave faster, which flattens the bead and pushes metal over the toe", "C": "Switch to a smaller electrode and slow the travel so more metal builds in the center", "D": "Reduce current or travel faster, and aim the electrode at the toe so the puddle fuses"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce current or travel faster, and aim the electrode at the toe so the puddle fuses): Overlap forms when molten metal flows onto cold base metal without fusing, from slow travel, excess current, or poor electrode angle. Adjusting those settings and aiming at the toe fixes it."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "During final visual inspection of a 5/8 inch groove weld, the inspector finds a surface breaking crack running across the weld at a right angle to the axis. The governing code does not permit cracks of any size. What should the inspector direct?", "options": {"A": "Accept the weld, since a crack shorter than 1/2 inch stays within normal code limits", "B": "Reject the weld but allow a second hotter pass over the crack to close it", "C": "Reject the weld and require the crack plus sound metal past each end to be removed", "D": "Reject the weld but let the welder run a hotter pass to melt the crack out"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reject the weld and require the crack plus sound metal past each end to be removed): Cracks are rejectable at any size. The repair means excavating the crack and a margin of sound metal beyond its ends before rewelding. Welding over a crack leaves it in place."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A quality inspector must check misalignment between two pipe ends before the root pass on a 6 inch Schedule 40 butt joint. A hi-lo gauge is selected for the job. What does the hi-lo gauge measure, and at what stage does the inspector use it?", "options": {"A": "The offset between the inside surfaces of the mating pipe ends, checked during fit-up", "B": "The depth of root concavity inside the pipe, checked once the weld has cooled", "C": "The size of the outside fillet legs, checked after the cap pass is complete", "D": "The width of the root gap between the pipe ends, checked after tacks are removed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The offset between the inside surfaces of the mating pipe ends, checked during fit-up): A hi-lo gauge reads the vertical offset between mating parts at fit-up. That offset affects root penetration and fatigue behavior. Root gap width needs a different gauge."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector reviews a fillet weld on a 3/8 inch tee joint. The face is strongly convex, and the toes meet the plate at sharp angles. The leg sizes match the drawing, and several welds on the job show the same bead shape. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the welds, since a convex fillet carries more load and so passes under the code", "B": "Require removal of the welds, since convex shape means leg size cannot be trusted", "C": "Ignore the profile, since leg size alone decides acceptance on fillet welds", "D": "Check the code profile limits and flag the welds whose toe angles exceed them"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Check the code profile limits and flag the welds whose toe angles exceed them): Heavy convexity with steep toe angles concentrates stress and may exceed the code profile limit. The inspector compares the measured shape with the criteria instead of assuming extra metal is better."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector on a bridge job finds slag stuck in a groove along one sidewall, visible after chipping, in a multipass E7018 weld. The welder used a stringer sequence. Which practice should the welder adopt on later passes to prevent this?", "options": {"A": "Raise the arc length so the arc blows slag out of the groove", "B": "Clean slag fully between passes and grind deep notches in the sidewall before the next bead", "C": "Skip interpass cleaning and raise current so the next pass remelts the slag left", "D": "Use a wider weave so the next bead sits above the slag and seals the groove"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clean slag fully between passes and grind deep notches in the sidewall before the next bead): Slag trapped between passes will not reliably melt out. It must be removed mechanically, and deep notches are ground out. Higher current or a longer arc does not clear it."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder repairs a lack of fusion flaw found on the sidewall of a V groove in 1 inch plate. The inspector asks which change in technique best prevents the same flaw on the next joint using the same procedure. Which answer is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use a longer arc so heat spreads wider and softens the sidewall before the metal arrives", "B": "Aim the arc at the sidewall, hold a correct angle, and pause at the toe", "C": "Raise travel speed and lower current so less heat reaches the wall and distortion stays small", "D": "Narrow the groove angle so the walls sit closer and the puddle bridges the gap"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aim the arc at the sidewall, hold a correct angle, and pause at the toe): Lack of fusion occurs when the arc does not melt the sidewall. Aiming at the wall, holding proper angle, and pausing at the toes gives fusion. Lower heat and longer arcs make it worse."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector reviews a pipe root pass on an open root V groove with a 3/32 inch gap. The bead shows open sections with no weld metal reaching the back side along a 3 inch stretch. Which diagnosis and fix are most appropriate for this condition?", "options": {"A": "Porosity in the root, treated by raising shielding gas flow and welding over the pores again", "B": "Slag inclusion, treated by raising voltage and welding the next pass over the area", "C": "Incomplete penetration, treated by grinding out the area and rewelding with proper gap, current, and technique", "D": "Overlap in the root, treated by lowering amperage and brushing the bead surface clean before capping"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incomplete penetration, treated by grinding out the area and rewelding with proper gap, current, and technique): A root pass that does not reach the back side has not penetrated the joint. The fix is removing the affected area and rewelding with enough heat and a correct gap. Welding over it buries the flaw."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "Fine cracks appear in the base metal beside a weld a day after a welder joined thick high carbon steel plate without preheat on a cold morning. The inspector must name the likely cause and recommend a sound preventive measure. Which answer fits best?", "options": {"A": "Slag inclusion from poor cleaning, prevented by faster travel and a wider weave", "B": "Hydrogen cracking in the heat affected zone, prevented by preheat, low hydrogen electrodes, and dry storage", "C": "Undercut from heavy current, prevented by a shorter arc and lower heat input across the joint", "D": "Porosity from lost shielding gas, prevented by higher gas flow and a clean nozzle before welding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hydrogen cracking in the heat affected zone, prevented by preheat, low hydrogen electrodes, and dry storage): Cracks in the heat affected zone appearing hours later point to hydrogen cracking, which needs hydrogen, hardness, and stress. Preheat and low hydrogen practice counter it."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A shop welds 1/4 inch aluminum with GMAW, and the finished beads show scattered pores through the cross section. The surface was brushed, but the plate sat in a damp storage yard for weeks. Which action best targets the likely cause of the porosity?", "options": {"A": "Increase travel speed so the puddle freezes before gas can form and stay inside the bead", "B": "Lower the wire feed speed so the bead builds more slowly at the center", "C": "Switch to straight carbon dioxide shielding gas, which gives deeper penetration on thin aluminum plate", "D": "Remove oxide and moisture from the joint area and clean the filler wire before welding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove oxide and moisture from the joint area and clean the filler wire before welding): Moisture and oxide on aluminum release hydrogen into the puddle and form pores. Cleaning the joint and wire removes the source. Carbon dioxide shielding is not used on aluminum."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector prepares to visually examine a finished weld on a structural beam using a flashlight and a small mirror. Under typical AWS visual examination practice, which lighting and viewing approach supports a reliable result on the weld and the adjacent base metal?", "options": {"A": "Use dim light to cast shadows, hold the eye within two inches, and look straight down", "B": "Use adequate light, a workable distance and angle, and look along toes, cap, and base metal", "C": "Work in bright direct sun glare, which shows cracks better than steady shop lighting does", "D": "Examine the weld center, since the toes and base metal fall outside visual testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use adequate light, a workable distance and angle, and look along toes, cap, and base metal): Good visual testing needs enough light, a suitable distance and angle, and a look at the toes and adjacent base metal. Poor light or a narrow focus on the cap misses surface breaks."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder completes a 5/16 inch fillet on a lap joint where the top plate edge is 3/8 inch thick. Along the plate edge, the arc has melted away part of the top corner. The inspector must decide how to judge weld size on this joint. What is correct?", "options": {"A": "Measure the remaining leg actually present and compare it with the size specified", "B": "Treat the fillet as normal shape and accept it without measuring the leg", "C": "Treat the melted corner as overlap on the top plate and reject the weld outright", "D": "Order ultrasonic testing of the root before any size decision is made on the weld"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Measure the remaining leg actually present and compare it with the size specified): Melting away the plate edge leaves a smaller effective leg. The inspector measures what is actually there and compares it with the required size. Shape alone does not accept a fillet."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A pressure piping radiograph shows a line of aligned pores in the root, and the inspector finds the pores sit only where the welder restarted the electrode. Several joints show the same pattern. Which cause best fits this pattern of porosity?", "options": {"A": "Gas or moisture entrapped at restarts, from damp electrodes or poor start technique", "B": "Excess travel speed along the joint, which would scatter pores", "C": "Heavy mill scale on the plate, which would form clusters across the whole weld", "D": "Excess current throughout the pass, which would appear as undercut at the toes instead of pores"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gas or moisture entrapped at restarts, from damp electrodes or poor start technique): Pores clustered at restarts point to a restart problem such as damp electrodes or poor start technique. A cause acting along the whole joint would give pores along the entire length."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator disputes the rejection of a fillet weld. The inspector found 1/32 inch of undercut, and the contract cites AWS D1.1 for statically loaded structures. The inspector must respond with reasoning that rests on the contract. Which reasoning is best?", "options": {"A": "The weld fails because undercut is a defect under every AWS code at any measured depth", "B": "The weld passes because the inspector may waive limits when the fabricator objects to the finding", "C": "The weld fails because the fabricator must file a written deviation for each discontinuity", "D": "The weld fails only if measured undercut exceeds the D1.1 limit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weld fails only if measured undercut exceeds the D1.1 limit): A discontinuity becomes a defect when it exceeds the acceptance criteria in the governing code. The inspector measures and compares, and does not reject by category or waive limits on request."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A weld procedure record shows a 1/4 inch fillet. In production, a welder makes a fillet whose legs gauge at 1/4 inch, but the face is concave and the throat measures only 0.15 inch. The specified minimum throat is about 0.18 inch. What concern does a careful inspector raise?", "options": {"A": "The leg size is wrong and must increase, since throat size has no bearing on strength", "B": "The profile is ideal and needs no check, since concavity adds throat area to the joint", "C": "The weld fails for undercut, since a concave fillet has undercut at both toes", "D": "The concave face leaves a smaller throat than the leg suggests, so strength may fall short"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The concave face leaves a smaller throat than the leg suggests, so strength may fall short): Fillet strength depends on throat. A concave face with the same legs gives a smaller throat than a flat or convex one, so the inspector checks the effective throat against the specified value."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector finds repeated slag inclusions in vertical up welds made with E7018 by the same welder, and each time the welder cleans and repairs them. A review shows a tight stringer technique with no pause at the sidewalls. What is the best root cause finding?", "options": {"A": "The electrodes are the wrong class, since E7018 produces slag in vertical up work", "B": "Poor sidewall manipulation traps slag in a cold lap area, so coaching is the lasting fix", "C": "The plate preheat is too high, which freezes slag into the groove sidewalls during each pass", "D": "The current is too low, which has no link to slag movement in the puddle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Poor sidewall manipulation traps slag in a cold lap area, so coaching is the lasting fix): Repeated slag with the same technique points to the welder's manipulation, not chance. Fixing the cause through technique and coaching stops the repeat, while repair alone does not."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A visual report lists cracks radiating in a star shape at the end of a bead where the welder broke the arc abruptly on a thick plate. The inspector must name the condition and state the required response under a code that bans cracks. What follows?", "options": {"A": "A longitudinal HAZ crack from hydrogen, which is acceptable if it stays short in length", "B": "Normal shrinkage at the bead end, which is acceptable when the opening is under 1/8 inch", "C": "Undercut at the toe, which is repaired by grinding without rewelding the end of the bead", "D": "A crater crack from an unfilled crater, which must be removed and repaired"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A crater crack from an unfilled crater, which must be removed and repaired): A star shaped crack in the crater is a crater crack, caused by breaking the arc without filling the crater. Cracks are rejectable, and repair means excavation and rewelding."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A procedure for a bridge job requires the inspector to verify weld size after welding. The fillet gauge shows a 3/8 inch leg, but the inspector also notes a 3/16 inch gap between plates at the root. What is the proper way to interpret weld size on this joint?", "options": {"A": "The gap has no effect, since weld size equals the measured leg for any root opening", "B": "The effective size may need reduction for the gap, so apply the code root opening rule", "C": "The gap raises the effective size, since the extra space allows deeper penetration into the root", "D": "The gap makes the weld unacceptable, since fillets permit no root opening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The effective size may need reduction for the gap, so apply the code root opening rule): Codes commonly require leg size to be increased by the root opening, or the effective throat to be reduced. The inspector applies the code rule instead of ignoring the gap."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fillet weld passes visual inspection of the cap, but a later bend test shows lack of fusion at the root. The inspector discovers the visual inspection covered only the finished cap. What lesson does this event teach about the limits of visual testing?", "options": {"A": "Visual testing sees only surface conditions, so in process checks and other tests find hidden flaws", "B": "Visual testing finds root defects when lighting is good, so the bend test result was wrong", "C": "Visual testing replaces other tests when a certified inspector signs the report on the finished weld", "D": "Visual testing is useful before welding, so after welding it adds no value"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Visual testing sees only surface conditions, so in process checks and other tests find hidden flaws): Visual testing reveals only what is visible. Root fusion problems may sit below the surface, so in process inspection and volumetric or destructive tests confirm what the surface hides."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector measures a groove weld and finds reinforcement at 3/16 inch where the code maximum is 1/8 inch, along one 6 inch section only. The remaining weld on the plate measures 3/32 inch. How should the inspector handle the 6 inch section?", "options": {"A": "Accept the section, since the average over the whole weld is within the code limit", "B": "Reject the whole plate, since one high spot condemns the joint beyond any possible repair", "C": "Reject that section and require grinding or blending to within the limit, then reinspect", "D": "Accept the section after adding weld metal to raise the reinforcement evenly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reject that section and require grinding or blending to within the limit, then reinspect): Limits apply at each location, not as an average. Excess reinforcement is corrected by grinding or blending and then reinspected. Adding more metal would worsen the condition."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welding supervisor says a small amount of porosity is acceptable on a statically loaded structure, while the inspector says it should be rejected. Both need a basis to settle the question. Which source of authority decides which of them is right?", "options": {"A": "The welder's experience level, since a veteran welder sets the porosity limit on the job", "B": "The inspector's personal judgment, since an inspector may apply tighter limits than the contract", "C": "The acceptance criteria in the governing code or contract, since both opinions are measured against it", "D": "The supervisor's schedule, since production needs set the acceptable porosity on a given day"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The acceptance criteria in the governing code or contract, since both opinions are measured against it): Acceptance is defined by the governing code or contract. Neither schedule pressure nor personal preference overrides it, so both parties check the document and measure."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator reports a weld that the welder says looks fine, with a smooth cap, yet a surface exam finds a groove melted into the base metal along the toe, 0.040 inch deep, where the code limit is 1/32 inch. Which finding and disposition are correct?", "options": {"A": "Undercut beyond the limit, so it is repaired by filling with weld metal and reinspecting", "B": "Overlap within limits, so it is accepted after the toe is painted to prevent corrosion", "C": "Porosity near the surface, so it is accepted since the cap looks smooth", "D": "Lack of fusion in the cap, so the entire weld is removed and replaced"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Undercut beyond the limit, so it is repaired by filling with weld metal and reinspecting): A groove melted into the base metal at the toe is undercut. At 0.040 inch it exceeds 1/32 inch (0.031 inch), so it is repaired with a stringer bead and reinspected."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A trainee asks why weld size and profile are checked after welding rather than only before welding starts. The instructor explains what the finished weld shows that the preparation stage does not. Which statement best describes what post weld visual inspection confirms on a completed joint?", "options": {"A": "Final preheat temperature and the electrode class used for the root pass on the joint", "B": "Joint fit-up, root opening, and bevel angle before any weld metal has been deposited in it", "C": "Finished size, profile, and surface condition, such as cracks, undercut, overlap, and cap porosity", "D": "Interior slag lines and root fusion on a closed pipe joint,"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Finished size, profile, and surface condition, such as cracks, undercut, overlap, and cap porosity): After welding, the inspector checks size, profile, and visible surface flaws such as cracks, undercut, and overlap. Fit-up is checked before welding, and interior flaws need other test methods."}, {"id": 752, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector examines a 3/8 inch fillet on a tee joint and notes that the toes blend smoothly into the plate and the face is slightly convex, with legs of 3/8 inch measured by gauge on both members. No surface breaks appear. Which disposition is correct?", "options": {"A": "Rejectable, since a convex face indicates overlap at the toes on any fillet of this size", "B": "Rejectable, since the legs must exceed the drawing size by 1/16 inch", "C": "Acceptable only after radiography confirms that fusion reaches the root of the joint on both members", "D": "Acceptable for size and profile, since the legs match the drawing and the toes blend smoothly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acceptable for size and profile, since the legs match the drawing and the toes blend smoothly): A fillet with correct legs, smooth toes, and a mild convex face meets typical visual criteria. Radiography is not required for routine acceptance of a fillet that passes visual inspection."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder asks how to tell the difference between porosity and slag at the surface of a stringer bead. The inspector points to a hole with a smooth rounded wall and no dark residue. Which discontinuity does this surface feature indicate?", "options": {"A": "Slag inclusion, since slag leaves smooth rounded cavities with a clean wall and no residue", "B": "Lack of fusion, since unfused areas appear as small round holes along the cap", "C": "Overlap, since overlapping metal leaves round cavities along the toe", "D": "Porosity, since a smooth rounded cavity with no residue comes from trapped gas"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Porosity, since a smooth rounded cavity with no residue comes from trapped gas): Gas pores are rounded cavities with smooth walls. Slag appears as nonmetallic residue and is usually irregular or elongated. Overlap and lack of fusion have different shapes and locations."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector finds several welds with excess reinforcement and sharp toe transitions on a fatigue loaded detail. The welder points out that the welds are strong enough in static tests. How should the inspector weigh the finding against the governing requirements?", "options": {"A": "Accept the welds, since passing static tests means the profile cannot matter under any loading", "B": "Reject the welds only if the welder agrees that the profile is poor", "C": "Compare the profile with the code criteria for the loading type, since fatigue limits are tighter", "D": "Waive the profile limit, since extra metal at the toe adds fatigue life to the detail"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Compare the profile with the code criteria for the loading type, since fatigue limits are tighter): Fatigue performance depends on toe shape and reinforcement, so loading type affects acceptance limits. Static strength does not show fatigue behavior, so the inspector applies the code criteria."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "On a pipe job, an inspector finds a joint where the cap shows slight sagging on the underside and the weld toe shows no undercut. Radiography later reports unfused areas at the root. The welder maintains the root was welded correctly. Which review step is most valuable?", "options": {"A": "Re-gauge the cap reinforcement, since cap height predicts root fusion on a pressure pipe joint", "B": "Review the root pass records, gap, and current, since unfused roots trace to setup and technique", "C": "Blame the radiographer, since root fusion cannot be shown by radiography on an open root joint", "D": "Grind the cap flush and accept the joint, since the root cannot be reached after welding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Review the root pass records, gap, and current, since unfused roots trace to setup and technique): Unfused root areas trace to joint setup, heat, and technique during the root pass. Reviewing those records finds the cause. Cap height says little about root fusion, and radiography does show root conditions."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector examines the weld cross section shown from a GMAW fillet on carbon steel plate in a fabrication shop. Round cavities appear near the cap surface of the bead. Which of these is a common cause of this kind of discontinuity during welding?", "options": {"A": "A travel speed that is too slow, which piles up metal ahead of the arc", "B": "A root opening that is too wide, which lets the puddle fall through the joint", "C": "Contaminated base metal or lost shielding gas coverage at the arc", "D": "A bevel angle that is too small, which keeps the cap from reaching the toes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contaminated base metal or lost shielding gas coverage at the arc): Rounded cavities form when gas is trapped in the freezing puddle. Dirty base metal, moisture, or poor gas coverage supplies that gas. Travel speed and joint geometry affect other discontinuities.", "image": "question_images/q_756.png"}, {"id": 757, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A shop welds 3/8 inch plate with E7018 electrodes pulled from an open box that sat on the bench for three days. The diagram shows the pattern found in the cap of one weld. What corrective action addresses the likely cause for the next batch of welds?", "options": {"A": "Raise amperage by 20 amps so the puddle stays molten longer before freezing", "B": "Weave the electrode wider on each pass so the pores float out of the puddle", "C": "Use a longer arc length so gas escapes before the bead freezes", "D": "Store electrodes in a holding oven and issue only a small amount at a time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Store electrodes in a holding oven and issue only a small amount at a time): Low hydrogen electrodes absorb moisture from the air, and moisture leaves pores in the weld. Proper oven storage prevents it. Arc length and weave width do not remove absorbed moisture.", "image": "question_images/q_757.png"}, {"id": 758, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector sees the porosity pattern shown in the diagram in the cap of a 1/2 inch groove weld. The contract specifies AWS D1.1 for statically loaded structures. The pores are small and few in number. Which decision best reflects correct inspection practice?", "options": {"A": "Reject the joint on sight, since porosity of any kind exceeds the code limits", "B": "Accept the joint on sight, since porosity does not affect statically loaded structures", "C": "Defer to the welder, since pore size is judged by the person who made the weld", "D": "Measure the pores and compare size and spacing with the code criteria before deciding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Measure the pores and compare size and spacing with the code criteria before deciding): Codes permit limited porosity within stated size and spacing limits. The inspector measures and compares with the criteria rather than rejecting or accepting by appearance.", "image": "question_images/q_758.png"}, {"id": 759, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A visual inspector studies the diagram of a fillet weld that shows a groove melted into the base metal along the toe of a tee joint in carbon steel. The inspector wants to explain the usual cause to a trainee. Which welding practice most commonly causes this discontinuity?", "options": {"A": "Low current, which leaves the bead sitting on the cold plate surface", "B": "A root gap that is too narrow, which blocks the puddle from the root", "C": "Electrodes stored in a damp area, which release hydrogen into the base metal", "D": "Excessive current or travel speed, or an arc held too long at the toe"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Excessive current or travel speed, or an arc held too long at the toe): Undercut is a groove melted into the base metal and not refilled. Too much heat, speed, or an arc dwelling at the toe causes it. Low current causes overlap, and damp electrodes cause porosity or cracks.", "image": "question_images/q_759.png"}, {"id": 760, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder produces the toe condition shown in the diagram on a horizontal fillet in 3/8 inch plate. The inspector measures its depth at 1/16 inch, and the code limit for this joint is 1/32 inch. What should the inspector direct the welder to do?", "options": {"A": "Clean the groove, deposit a small stringer to fill it, then request reinspection", "B": "Leave the groove as welded and note it on the report, since the depth is close", "C": "Grind the whole weld flush with the plate to remove the groove with the bead", "D": "Paint over the groove so exposure does not cause corrosion in service"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clean the groove, deposit a small stringer to fill it, then request reinspection): Undercut deeper than the limit is repaired by filling the groove with weld metal in a small stringer and reinspecting. Grinding the weld away removes the joint, and painting hides the problem.", "image": "question_images/q_760.png"}, {"id": 761, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fillet weld on a 1/2 inch plate tee joint has the profile shown in the diagram, with weld metal lying on the plate beside the toe. The welder says the weld looks strong and wants it accepted. What does the inspector explain about this condition?", "options": {"A": "The metal rests on unfused plate and forms a notch, so the overlap must be removed", "B": "The extra metal adds strength at the toe, so the joint can be accepted", "C": "The condition affects appearance only and is ignored by the structural codes in common use today", "D": "The metal fused to the plate underneath, so only the bead color needs fixing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The metal rests on unfused plate and forms a notch, so the overlap must be removed): Overlap is weld metal that has flowed onto the plate without fusing. It creates a sharp notch and a hidden gap, so it is removed by grinding or gouging and rewelded if needed.", "image": "question_images/q_761.png"}, {"id": 762, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector finds the overlap shown in the diagram on several vertical up fillet welds, all made by the same welder and all on the same side of the joint. Which change would a careful inspector recommend after reviewing the welder's technique?", "options": {"A": "Raise travel speed sharply so the puddle has no time to flow over the toe", "B": "Use a longer arc and a wider weave so metal spreads further onto the plate surface", "C": "Open the root gap so metal drops through instead of flowing onto the toe", "D": "Lower the current slightly, pause at the toe, and aim so the puddle fuses"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower the current slightly, pause at the toe, and aim so the puddle fuses): Overlap on one side suggests poor electrode angle or too much metal for the heat. Lower current, a pause at the toe, and proper aim let the puddle wet and fuse the plate. A longer arc makes the spread worse.", "image": "question_images/q_762.png"}, {"id": 763, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a gap between the weld bead and the plate sidewall in a groove joint on 1/2 inch plate. A student is asked which of these is a recognized cause of this discontinuity during welding. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Too little heat input or an electrode angle that misses the sidewall", "B": "Too much heat, which melts away the sidewall and leaves a groove at the toe", "C": "Moist electrodes, which release hydrogen and form tiny pores in the bead", "D": "Fast cooling of the weld, which forms cracks along the centerline of the cap"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Too little heat input or an electrode angle that misses the sidewall): Lack of fusion means weld metal did not bond to the base metal or a prior bead. Low heat or an angle that misses the wall are typical causes. Excess heat leads to undercut instead.", "image": "question_images/q_763.png"}, {"id": 764, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder uses short circuit GMAW on 3/8 inch plate in a fabrication shop, and the diagram shows fusion missing along the sidewall of the joint. The inspector checks the machine and finds the voltage set low and the travel speed fast. Which fix addresses the cause?", "options": {"A": "Lower wire feed further and travel faster so the puddle stays small and cool", "B": "Use a smaller bevel and shorter stickout so the arc stays narrow", "C": "Raise voltage and wire feed to suit the joint and slow travel to melt the wall", "D": "Add flux to the plate surface so the sidewall melts more easily"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise voltage and wire feed to suit the joint and slow travel to melt the wall): Short circuit transfer runs cool, and low voltage with fast travel leaves sidewalls unfused. Raising the settings and slowing travel puts enough heat into the wall. A smaller puddle adds to the problem.", "image": "question_images/q_764.png"}, {"id": 765, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a lack of fusion indication found by a surface exam on a code weld. The cap passed bead appearance checks. An inspector must state the finding and disposition in the report. Which statement is the best description?", "options": {"A": "A cosmetic variation noted for information, since the bead surface looks smooth", "B": "An acceptable flaw if shorter than the plate thickness, since short flaws are tolerated", "C": "A rejectable flaw to be removed by grinding or gouging, rewelded, and inspected again", "D": "A base metal laminate that is the plate supplier's problem, not the welder's"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A rejectable flaw to be removed by grinding or gouging, rewelded, and inspected again): Lack of fusion is a planar flaw that common codes reject. It must be removed and the area rewelded. A smooth bead does not indicate sound fusion beneath it.", "image": "question_images/q_765.png"}, {"id": 766, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "In the diagram of a single V groove weld on pipe, the root gap remains open with no weld metal through the joint thickness. A trainee must give the correct term for this discontinuity before the inspector explains the cause. Which term applies?", "options": {"A": "Excessive reinforcement, where the cap stands too high above the surface of the plate", "B": "Burn through, where the molten pool falls out of the root and leaves a hole", "C": "Arc strike, where the electrode touches the plate outside the joint area and leaves a mark", "D": "Incomplete joint penetration, where weld metal fails to reach the root of the joint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Incomplete joint penetration, where weld metal fails to reach the root of the joint): Incomplete joint penetration means weld metal did not extend through the thickness to the root. Burn through is the opposite, with metal falling out of the joint.", "image": "question_images/q_766.png"}, {"id": 767, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder makes the root pass on a 1/4 inch butt joint with a tight root face and a 1/16 inch gap using low current. The diagram shows the unfilled root found afterward. Which change gives better penetration on the next joint?", "options": {"A": "Lower the current further and close the gap so the puddle stays small and controlled", "B": "Raise the current or open the root gap so the arc reaches the back", "C": "Add a second cap pass over the top so weld metal sinks", "D": "Use a larger electrode and a faster travel speed on the root pass"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the current or open the root gap so the arc reaches the back): A tight gap and low current keep the arc from reaching the root. More current or a wider gap lets the arc melt through. A cap pass does not push metal down into an unfused root.", "image": "question_images/q_767.png"}, {"id": 768, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a root condition on a pressure piping weld that cannot be seen from outside after welding. A colleague says visual inspection of the cap proves the joint is sound. How should the inspector respond to this claim?", "options": {"A": "Agree, since a well formed cap means the root is fully penetrated beneath it", "B": "Disagree, since root condition on a closed joint needs in process checks or radiography", "C": "Agree, since root passes matter on joints that carry fatigue loading rather than static loads", "D": "Disagree, since tensile tests on a coupon are the only way to judge a root"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Disagree, since root condition on a closed joint needs in process checks or radiography): A good cap says little about a hidden root. In process inspection of the root pass and volumetric testing find lack of penetration. A coupon test does not prove each production weld.", "image": "question_images/q_768.png"}, {"id": 769, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a line breaking through the weld surface across the bead on a structural joint in plate. An apprentice asks how the typical visual acceptance table for statically loaded structures treats this discontinuity. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "It is accepted if shorter than 1/4 inch, since short cracks are within normal limits", "B": "It is accepted if it does not reach the toe, since interior cracks are tolerated", "C": "It is accepted after painting, since a coating stops the crack growing in service", "D": "It is rejected, since cracks are not permitted in the weld or heat affected zone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is rejected, since cracks are not permitted in the weld or heat affected zone): Codes do not accept cracks of any size because they can grow under load. The weld is rejected, and the crack is removed with a margin before rewelding.", "image": "question_images/q_769.png"}, {"id": 770, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector marks the crack shown in the diagram on a code weld in 1/2 inch plate, and the welder asks how to repair it. The crack ends have been located and marked on the plate with a scribe. Which sequence meets good repair practice?", "options": {"A": "Weld a thin bead over the crack to seal it, then grind the cap flush", "B": "Drill a small hole at each end and leave the crack", "C": "Remove the crack and sound metal past each end, verify by penetrant, then reweld", "D": "Heat the area to a dull red so the crack closes up before rewelding the joint"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the crack and sound metal past each end, verify by penetrant, then reweld): A crack must be fully excavated and verified gone before rewelding. Welding over it or drilling stop holes in a code weld leaves the flaw in the joint. Heating does not close a crack.", "image": "question_images/q_770.png"}, {"id": 771, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder runs a single pass fillet in stainless steel, and the diagram shows a crack that formed while the weld was still cooling from solidification. The inspector reviews the work. Which cause is typical for this type of cracking?", "options": {"A": "Hydrogen from damp electrodes, which cracks the plate hours after the weld has cooled down", "B": "A cold arc that fails to melt the plate and leaves the bead sitting", "C": "A long arc that blows shielding gas away from the puddle during the pass", "D": "A narrow deep bead under high restraint, with a sulfur rich puddle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A narrow deep bead under high restraint, with a sulfur rich puddle): Hot cracks form at high temperature during solidification and link to narrow deep beads, restraint, and impurities such as sulfur. Hydrogen cracks appear later in the heat affected zone.", "image": "question_images/q_771.png"}, {"id": 772, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a hot crack in a thick restrained fillet weld in stainless plate. The inspector reviews options with the welding engineer to prevent a repeat on production joints next month. Which preventive measure is best supported by the cause of this crack?", "options": {"A": "Increase travel speed to make the bead deeper and narrower so it freezes sooner", "B": "Use a wider, flatter bead with a lower depth to width ratio and less restraint", "C": "Switch to electrodes kept in an open container so moisture lowers the risk of cracking", "D": "Quench the weld in water right after welding so the crack stays closed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use a wider, flatter bead with a lower depth to width ratio and less restraint): Hot cracking is encouraged by narrow deep beads and high restraint. A wider flatter bead and lower restraint reduce it. Faster travel deepens the bead, and quenching adds stress.", "image": "question_images/q_772.png"}, {"id": 773, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a crack running along the center of a weld bead in a groove joint. The welder says it formed because the plate was damp, and the inspector reviews the evidence on the joint. Which interpretation is most consistent with centerline cracking?", "options": {"A": "Solidification stress in a deep narrow bead, so bead shape and restraint need review", "B": "Hydrogen from damp plate, so drying the plate alone is enough to cure the problem", "C": "Slag trapped in the bead, so more thorough chipping removes the cause of the crack", "D": "Undercut at the toes, so reducing current at the toes removes the center crack"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Solidification stress in a deep narrow bead, so bead shape and restraint need review): Centerline cracks run along the weld axis where the last liquid freezes. They come from bead shape and restraint, not damp plate. Drying plate helps against hydrogen cracks in the base metal.", "image": "question_images/q_773.png"}, {"id": 774, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a crack in the base metal next to the weld, running through the heat affected zone of a steel plate joint. A trainee asks which mechanism is the most common cause of this crack in steel. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Hydrogen assisted cracking in hardened steel under residual stress", "B": "Gas trapped in the puddle, which forms round pores along the bead", "C": "Slag trapped between passes, which leaves dark elongated pockets", "D": "Arc blow, which pushes the puddle away from the joint during welding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydrogen assisted cracking in hardened steel under residual stress): Cracks in the heat affected zone of steel typically need hydrogen, a hard structure, and stress. Preheat and low hydrogen practice reduce the risk. Pores and slag are separate discontinuities.", "image": "question_images/q_774.png"}, {"id": 775, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator finds the heat affected zone crack shown in the diagram on thick alloy steel plate welded at ambient temperature on a cold morning with no preheat applied to the joint. Which procedural change is most appropriate for the next joint?", "options": {"A": "Weld faster at higher current to shorten the time the plate spends hot during the pass", "B": "Apply the specified preheat, use dry low hydrogen electrodes, and control the cooling", "C": "Use a cellulosic electrode for extra penetration and skip preheat on the thick alloy plate", "D": "Chill the plate with cold water before welding to harden the heat affected zone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply the specified preheat, use dry low hydrogen electrodes, and control the cooling): Preheat slows cooling, dry low hydrogen electrodes limit hydrogen, and controlled cooling lets hydrogen escape. A cellulosic electrode adds hydrogen, and chilling the plate raises cracking risk.", "image": "question_images/q_775.png"}, {"id": 776, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows small cracks radiating in a star pattern at the end of a weld bead on a plate joint. An apprentice asks which technique prevents this discontinuity at the end of a pass. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Snap the electrode away quickly so the crater cools and freezes shut with no void left", "B": "Raise current at the end of the pass so the crater remelts the surrounding plate", "C": "Lengthen the arc at the end so shielding gas spreads over the crater before it cools", "D": "Fill the crater by pausing briefly or reversing slightly before breaking the arc"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fill the crater by pausing briefly or reversing slightly before breaking the arc): A crater left unfilled shrinks as it freezes and cracks. Pausing or reversing to fill it prevents that. Snapping the arc away leaves the crater hollow.", "image": "question_images/q_776.png"}, {"id": 777, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A GTAW operator finishes a root pass on stainless pipe, and the diagram shows cracking in the crater at the end of the bead. The inspector asks which welding machine feature would help prevent it on future passes. Which feature best addresses this?", "options": {"A": "A current downslope that tapers amperage gradually at the end of the weld", "B": "A higher starting current that gives a hotter first spot at the arc start", "C": "A shorter postflow time that stops shielding gas the moment the arc ends at the pipe", "D": "A larger tungsten that widens the arc during the final seconds"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A current downslope that tapers amperage gradually at the end of the weld): Downslope reduces current gradually so the crater fills and cools without a sudden void. Starting current and tungsten size do not control how the crater freezes.", "image": "question_images/q_777.png"}, {"id": 778, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows a dark nonmetallic pocket buried in a multipass SMAW weld in 1 inch plate. The inspector wants to give the welder a working practice that prevents this discontinuity in later passes. Which practice does this most directly?", "options": {"A": "Raise amperage on later passes so the slag floats out to the surface of the pool", "B": "Remove slag from each pass and grind rough or undercut areas before the next", "C": "Use a shorter arc and faster travel so the puddle freezes", "D": "Add flux powder to the groove so the slag stays on top of the puddle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove slag from each pass and grind rough or undercut areas before the next): Slag left from a prior pass or caught in a notch gets trapped by the next bead. Cleaning each pass and grinding notches removes it. Higher amperage cannot reliably melt out slag buried in the groove.", "image": "question_images/q_778.png"}, {"id": 779, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector reviews the slag inclusion shown in the diagram. Radiography shows elongated dark lines between passes in a groove weld, while the welder insists the visual inspection of the cap was clean. What is the best conclusion about visual testing?", "options": {"A": "The inclusion sits below the surface, so only in process checks or radiography reveal it", "B": "The inclusion would show at the surface, so the radiograph must have been misread", "C": "The inclusion is harmless, since slag is not weld metal and cannot carry load", "D": "The inclusion proves the electrode was wrong, since a good cap pass shows slag lines"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The inclusion sits below the surface, so only in process checks or radiography reveal it): Slag buried between passes cannot be seen on a finished cap. Checking each pass during welding or using radiography reveals it. Cap appearance says little about interior slag.", "image": "question_images/q_779.png"}, {"id": 780, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "The diagram shows elongated gas cavities lying along the weld axis in a fillet weld on primed steel plate. The primer was not removed from the joint area before welding began. Which cause is most likely, and which fix applies?", "options": {"A": "Slag left from a previous pass, so chip and brush the finished bead more carefully", "B": "Gas released by primer or contamination at the joint, so clean it before welding", "C": "Excess reinforcement, so grind the cap flat to let the trapped gas escape from the weld", "D": "Slow travel on the final pass, so increase speed to thin the bead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gas released by primer or contamination at the joint, so clean it before welding): Elongated gas cavities along the axis link to gas released from surface contaminants such as primer or oil in the joint. Cleaning the joint prevents them. Grinding the cap does not remove interior cavities.", "image": "question_images/q_780.png"}, {"id": 781, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A new shop apprentice is learning liquid penetrant testing on a stainless steel pipe weld at a fabrication shop. The instructor asks the apprentice to describe the order of the main steps so that the inspection follows the written procedure and nothing gets skipped. Which sequence lists the steps in the proper order?", "options": {"A": "Apply penetrant, apply developer, allow dwell time, clean the surface, remove excess penetrant, then inspect.", "B": "Clean, apply penetrant, allow dwell, remove excess penetrant, apply developer, then inspect and evaluate.", "C": "Clean the surface, apply developer, apply penetrant, remove excess, allow dwell time, then inspect the weld.", "D": "Allow dwell time, clean the surface, apply penetrant, remove excess, apply developer, then inspect the weld."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clean, apply penetrant, allow dwell, remove excess penetrant, apply developer, then inspect and evaluate.): Penetrant testing starts with a clean, dry surface, then penetrant goes on and sits for the dwell period before the excess is removed. Developer comes last because it pulls the trapped penetrant back out where it can be seen. Putting developer on before penetrant would give nothing to draw out."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "During a welding inspection class, a student asks what kind of discontinuities liquid penetrant testing can find on a finished aluminum fillet weld. The instructor wants a precise statement of the method's limits, tied to how the penetrant actually gets into a flaw and how it is later revealed on the weld surface. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "It finds buried discontinuities up to about one inch below the surface of the weld metal.", "B": "It finds only surface-open discontinuities, because penetrant must enter the opening by capillary action.", "C": "It finds internal porosity and slag deep inside the weld as well as surface cracks.", "D": "It finds surface and subsurface flaws in ferromagnetic metals using a magnetized part and iron particles."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It finds only surface-open discontinuities, because penetrant must enter the opening by capillary action.): Penetrant has to flow into a flaw, so the flaw must break the surface. Buried porosity and slag are not reached by the liquid. A magnetized part with iron particles describes a different method that needs a ferromagnetic part."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A shop inspector must check carbon steel plate welds and also a separate batch of 6061 aluminum brackets for surface cracks. The supervisor reminds the inspector that one common surface method depends on magnetic fields set up inside the part under test. On which materials can magnetic particle testing be used?", "options": {"A": "Any metal, including aluminum and austenitic stainless steel, as long as the surface is clean.", "B": "Nonmagnetic alloys only, since a magnetic field would disturb particle movement near the flaw.", "C": "Ferromagnetic materials such as carbon steel, since the part must carry a magnetic field.", "D": "Thin sheet metal under one eighth inch only, since thick sections absorb the magnetizing current."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ferromagnetic materials such as carbon steel, since the part must carry a magnetic field.): Particles collect at a flaw only where a magnetic leakage field exists, so the part has to be ferromagnetic. Aluminum and austenitic stainless are not, so penetrant testing is used on them. Thickness is not the limiting factor."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A technician in a pressure vessel shop explains ultrasonic testing to a new hire who has only ever seen radiographic film. The technician sets a probe on a coupled steel surface and points to a screen showing pulses and returning echoes from the plate. What does the instrument use to locate internal flaws in a weld?", "options": {"A": "Gamma rays passing through the weld and exposing film on the opposite side of the joint.", "B": "Leakage fields from a magnetized weld that gather fine iron particles at each internal flaw.", "C": "Colored dye drawn out of surface cracks by a developer coating and viewed under white light.", "D": "High-frequency sound pulses that reflect from flaws and return to the probe as signals."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (High-frequency sound pulses that reflect from flaws and return to the probe as signals.): Ultrasonic testing sends sound into the metal, and any reflector such as a crack or lack of fusion sends an echo back. The echo position and height are read on the display. Film, magnetic particles and dye belong to other methods."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiographer exposes a groove weld on one inch thick steel plate and develops the film for the reviewer. The reviewer points to a small device with wires or drilled holes lying beside the weld image on the source side. What does the image quality indicator, also called a penetrameter, demonstrate?", "options": {"A": "The technique is sensitive enough to reveal small discontinuities of a specified size.", "B": "The weld metal matches the filler classification listed on the welding procedure specification.", "C": "The film was stored at a temperature suitable to hold radiation exposure readings accurately.", "D": "The weld reinforcement height falls inside the limits written in the governing code document."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The technique is sensitive enough to reveal small discontinuities of a specified size.): The image quality indicator has known wire diameters or hole sizes, so seeing the required one proves the setup could show a flaw of that size. It says nothing about filler metal, film storage or reinforcement height."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder's 3/8 in plate groove coupon is cut into guided bend specimens at the lab. The technician labels one specimen for a face bend and explains to the welder how it will be loaded in the jig. In a face bend, which surface ends up stretched on the outside of the bend?", "options": {"A": "The root of the weld, opposite the finishing passes, ends up on the convex surface.", "B": "The face of the weld, finished from the top, ends up on the convex surface.", "C": "The side edge of the weld, where the plate was cut, takes the stretching load.", "D": "Neither surface, because the weld sits on the neutral axis in the middle of the bend."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The face of the weld, finished from the top, ends up on the convex surface.): In a face bend the face is placed in tension on the outside of the curve, so surface flaws and lack of fusion near it open up. A root bend puts the root on the outside instead."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator welds low temperature service pressure vessel plates, and the procedure qualification record calls for Charpy V-notch testing at a stated temperature. A new inspector asks what property the test actually measures from the broken specimens. What does a Charpy V-notch impact test determine?", "options": {"A": "The maximum tensile load per square inch the weld carries before the joint separates.", "B": "The energy a notched specimen absorbs while breaking, showing the weld's toughness.", "C": "The hardness of the heat affected zone, read from an indentation made by a diamond point.", "D": "The ability of the weld to bend around a fixed radius without cracking on its surface."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The energy a notched specimen absorbs while breaking, showing the weld's toughness.): A swinging hammer breaks a notched bar and the energy absorbed, in foot-pounds, shows how tough the metal is at that temperature. Tensile strength, hardness and bendability are separate properties checked by other tests."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "After a procedure qualification, a lab cuts a cross section from a fillet weld on structural steel, grinds it smooth, and swabs it with an acid solution before viewing it under low magnification. The welder wonders why the surface was etched. What is a macroetch test mainly used to reveal?", "options": {"A": "The chemical analysis of the weld metal and the carbon content of the base plate.", "B": "Fusion, penetration, weld size, and the weld and heat affected zone profile in cross section.", "C": "The impact toughness of the weld measured at the temperature stated in the qualification record.", "D": "The fine grain boundaries and phases of the metal, which need several hundred times magnification."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fusion, penetration, weld size, and the weld and heat affected zone profile in cross section.): Etching a polished cross section makes the weld metal, fusion line and heat affected zone stand out, so size, penetration and fusion can be judged. Chemistry, toughness and fine microstructure need other tests."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder performing a fillet weld test on two pieces of 1/4 in plate is told the specimen will be loaded until it fractures through the weld, and then the broken surface will be examined closely. What is the fillet weld break test designed to show about the weld?", "options": {"A": "Soundness of the weld interior, including root fusion and any porosity or slag.", "B": "Ultimate tensile strength of the weld metal expressed in pounds per square inch.", "C": "Ability of the weld to withstand repeated cyclic loads without developing cracks over time.", "D": "Resistance of the weld to corrosion after exposure to salt spray for a specified duration."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Soundness of the weld interior, including root fusion and any porosity or slag.): Breaking the fillet exposes the inside of the weld, where an inspector can see root fusion, gas holes and trapped slag. It gives no tensile figure, fatigue life or corrosion data."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "During a code class discussion of inspection terms, the instructor writes two words on the board, discontinuity and defect, and asks the students to explain how they differ. One welder says they mean the same thing and are used interchangeably. Which statement correctly separates the two terms?", "options": {"A": "A discontinuity is any weld interruption, and it becomes a defect when it exceeds acceptance limits.", "B": "A defect is any weld interruption, and it becomes a discontinuity when it exceeds acceptance limits.", "C": "A discontinuity is found only by destructive testing, while a defect is found by visual examination.", "D": "A discontinuity must be repaired when found, while a defect is a minor flaw codes permit."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A discontinuity is any weld interruption, and it becomes a defect when it exceeds acceptance limits.): Codes treat a discontinuity as an interruption in the normal structure, which may be acceptable. Only when it exceeds the acceptance criteria is it called a defect and rejected. The reverse definition swaps the terms."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector scans a butt weld with an angle beam ultrasonic probe and notices the screen shows no signal when the probe is lifted slightly from the steel surface. A trainee asks why a gel or oil is spread on the plate before scanning. What is the purpose of the couplant in ultrasonic testing?", "options": {"A": "It cools the probe so the crystal does not overheat while scanning thick weld joints.", "B": "It lubricates the surface so the probe can slide smoothly over weld ripples without catching.", "C": "It fills the air gap between probe and part so sound can pass into the metal.", "D": "It reveals surface cracks by drawing out a visible dye that is held within the gel."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It fills the air gap between probe and part so sound can pass into the metal.): Sound does not cross an air gap, so a liquid or gel couplant links the probe to the steel. Without it the signal drops out, as the trainee saw when the probe lifted. Cooling and lubrication are side effects only."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A quality technician reviews a procedure for fluorescent liquid penetrant examination of titanium tubing welds. The procedure requires a darkened inspection booth for the final step. What additional equipment makes the indications visible to the inspector working in that booth?", "options": {"A": "A bright white light held at a low angle that casts shadows beside each indication.", "B": "A magnetic yoke that draws glowing particles toward the leakage fields at the cracks.", "C": "An ultraviolet black light that makes the retained penetrant glow at the indications.", "D": "An ultrasonic probe that vibrates the penetrant out of each crack for easy viewing."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An ultraviolet black light that makes the retained penetrant glow at the indications.): Fluorescent penetrant contains a dye that glows under ultraviolet light, which is why the booth is dark. White light shows visible dye only, and a yoke or probe belongs to other methods."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder applies visible red dye penetrant to a 1/2 in carbon steel fillet weld and wipes it off after about thirty seconds because the shift is ending. The written procedure specifies a longer dwell time for this material. What is the most likely result of cutting the dwell time short?", "options": {"A": "Penetrant will etch the base metal and leave stains that look like cracks under the developer.", "B": "Developer will not adhere to the surface, which prevents any indication from forming on the weld.", "C": "Penetrant will dry into the cracks and make the surface unusable for later retesting.", "D": "Penetrant may not fully enter tight cracks, so fine surface discontinuities could go undetected."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Penetrant may not fully enter tight cracks, so fine surface discontinuities could go undetected.): Penetrant moves into a tight crack slowly by capillary action, and the dwell period gives it that time. A short dwell leaves narrow flaws partly filled, so little bleeds out and the flaw can pass unseen."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "Removing excess penetrant from a stainless steel pipe weld with the solvent removable method, a helper sprays cleaner directly onto the weld surface and scrubs it. The lead inspector stops the work and states the correct technique for this method. What should the helper do instead?", "options": {"A": "Rinse the weld with a low pressure water spray and dry it with warm compressed air.", "B": "Soak the surface with solvent so the penetrant is lifted from every crack before wiping.", "C": "Wipe with a lint-free cloth, then one lightly dampened with solvent, without flooding the surface.", "D": "Wipe once with a dry cloth and let the remaining penetrant evaporate for several minutes."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wipe with a lint-free cloth, then one lightly dampened with solvent, without flooding the surface.): With the solvent removable method the surface is wiped, then wiped with a dampened cloth. Flooding with solvent can wash penetrant out of real flaws. A water rinse belongs to the water washable method."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder grinds an aluminum weld flush using a wire wheel and a disc, and the inspector applies penetrant and finds no indications on a joint that later cracked in a bend test. The inspector suspects the surface preparation. What is the most probable cause of the missed cracks?", "options": {"A": "Aluminum is magnetic enough to hold the penetrant out of the fine cracks along the toe.", "B": "Wire brushing left the surface too clean, so the penetrant could not form an adequate film.", "C": "Penetrant reacts with the aluminum oxide layer and neutralizes itself before reaching the cracks.", "D": "Grinding smeared soft metal over the crack openings, sealing them so penetrant could not enter."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grinding smeared soft metal over the crack openings, sealing them so penetrant could not enter.): Soft metals like aluminum smear under grinding and wire brushing, closing surface openings. Penetrant needs open flaws, so procedures call for etching or careful cleaning after mechanical work. Aluminum is not magnetic."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector uses a yoke to magnetize a carbon steel fillet weld and is looking for transverse cracks running across the weld bead. The yoke legs are placed on either side of the weld, so the field runs across the bead. A coworker says the setup is wrong. What change would improve detection of these cracks?", "options": {"A": "Raise the current so the field strength increases and the cracks line up with the flux.", "B": "Set the yoke legs along the weld axis so the field crosses the cracks squarely.", "C": "Switch to a dry powder of larger particle size so fine cracks collect more of it.", "D": "Keep the yoke where it sits, since field direction has little effect on which cracks show."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Set the yoke legs along the weld axis so the field crosses the cracks squarely.): A flaw shows best when the magnetic field crosses it at close to ninety degrees. A field running across the bead is parallel to transverse cracks, so they leak little flux. Turning the yoke to follow the weld corrects this."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A pipefitter asks a certified inspector to check an austenitic 304 stainless steel pipe weld for surface cracks using magnetic particle testing because the shop yoke is close by. The inspector declines and offers a different method. Which method is the better choice for this weld?", "options": {"A": "Liquid penetrant testing, because austenitic stainless is not magnetic and cannot hold the particles.", "B": "Eddy current testing, because it detects only deep internal slag in nonmagnetic welds.", "C": "Dry magnetic particle testing with a stronger yoke to overcome the weak magnetism of the pipe.", "D": "Visual examination alone, since surface cracks cannot form in austenitic stainless steel welds."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Liquid penetrant testing, because austenitic stainless is not magnetic and cannot hold the particles.): Austenitic stainless steel does not support a useful magnetic field, so magnetic particle testing is not suitable. Penetrant testing finds surface-open cracks in nonmagnetic metal. A stronger yoke cannot fix this."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A weld inspector must examine a 1 in thick carbon steel groove weld for internal lack of fusion along the bevel face. The weld reinforcement is still in place, so a probe cannot sit flat on top of the weld. Which approach lets the inspector scan the weld volume from the plate surface?", "options": {"A": "An angle beam probe moved on the plate beside the weld, sending sound into it diagonally.", "B": "A straight beam probe placed on the weld crown, sending sound straight down through the reinforcement.", "C": "A magnetic yoke placed across the weld crown with fluorescent particles sprayed on top.", "D": "Dye penetrant brushed over the weld crown and left to dwell for thirty minutes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An angle beam probe moved on the plate beside the weld, sending sound into it diagonally.): Angle beam probes send sound diagonally, so they can reach the weld and bevel faces from the flat plate beside it. Straight beam from the crown is blocked by the uneven surface, and penetrant or particles only find surface flaws."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiographer reviewing film of a 3/8 in pipe weld sees several small, dark, round spots scattered along the weld image, along with one long, dark, irregular streak located inside the weld width. Which pair of discontinuities do these indications most likely represent?", "options": {"A": "Tungsten inclusions for the round spots, and overlap for the irregular dark streak.", "B": "Lack of fusion for the round spots, and arc strikes for the irregular streak.", "C": "Porosity for the round spots, and slag inclusion for the irregular dark streak.", "D": "Cracks for the round spots, and excess reinforcement for the irregular streak."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Porosity for the round spots, and slag inclusion for the irregular dark streak.): Gas pores appear as dark round spots, while slag appears as dark irregular shapes. Tungsten is denser and shows lighter, and overlap and arc strikes are surface conditions that this film would not display this way."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "On a radiograph of a steel plate weld, one part of the weld image looks lighter than the surrounding weld. The radiographer must decide what kind of condition could account for the lighter area on the film. Which condition would most likely produce a lighter area?", "options": {"A": "A void in the weld, because radiation passes through gas easily and darkens the film.", "B": "A crack running parallel to the beam, because it absorbs radiation along the entire weld length.", "C": "Extra metal thickness, such as excess reinforcement or a tungsten inclusion, absorbing more of the radiation.", "D": "Underfill at the face, because thinner metal lets less radiation reach the film beneath."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Extra metal thickness, such as excess reinforcement or a tungsten inclusion, absorbing more of the radiation.): Film turns darker where more radiation passes through, such as pores, and lighter where more metal absorbs it, such as extra thickness or a denser inclusion. Underfill and voids both darken the film."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A reduced section tensile specimen cut from a groove weld coupon measures 0.500 in thick and 1.000 in wide at the narrowest section. The testing machine records a maximum load of 32,500 lb before fracture. What is the ultimate tensile strength of the specimen?", "options": {"A": "32,500 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by twice the cross-sectional area.", "B": "65,000 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by the 0.500 in² cross-sectional area.", "C": "130,000 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by half the cross-sectional area.", "D": "16,250 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by four times the cross-sectional area."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (65,000 psi, found by dividing the maximum load by the 0.500 in² cross-sectional area.): Area is 0.500 in × 1.000 in = 0.500 in². Stress is load divided by area, so 32,500 ÷ 0.500 = 65,000 psi. Rechecking: 65,000 × 0.500 = 32,500. The other values come from using the wrong area."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A reduced section tensile specimen measures 0.750 in wide by 0.375 in thick at its narrowest section and breaks in the base metal at 20,250 lb. The base metal specification requires a minimum tensile strength of 70,000 psi. Does the specimen meet the minimum requirement?", "options": {"A": "Yes, the stress is 72,000 psi, so it exceeds the 70,000 psi minimum.", "B": "No, the stress is 54,000 psi, which falls below the minimum value for the base metal.", "C": "No, the stress is 27,000 psi, which falls below the minimum value for the base metal.", "D": "No, a base metal break means the weld was not loaded, so the test is void."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, the stress is 72,000 psi, so it exceeds the 70,000 psi minimum.): Area is 0.750 × 0.375 = 0.28125 in². Stress is 20,250 ÷ 0.28125 = 72,000 psi, and 72,000 × 0.28125 = 20,250 confirms it. That clears 70,000 psi, and a base metal break shows the weld was stronger than the plate."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder's 3/8 in groove coupon is being cut into bend specimens. The technician removes the weld reinforcement and then rounds the long edges on the tension side. Why must the reinforcement be removed flush before a guided face bend or root bend test is run?", "options": {"A": "Reinforcement would stick to the die and prevent the plunger from reaching the full depth.", "B": "Reinforcement would add weight that changes the load reading shown on the testing machine.", "C": "Raised reinforcement would stiffen the bend and hide how the weld metal itself behaves under strain.", "D": "Reinforcement would be softer than the base plate and bend before the fusion line does."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raised reinforcement would stiffen the bend and hide how the weld metal itself behaves under strain.): Extra weld metal on the surface changes how the specimen bends and shields the fusion line from strain. Machining it flush lets the test judge the weld the same way every time."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "On a root bend specimen from a 3/8 in V-groove coupon with the backing removed, a 3/16 in open crack appears on the convex surface after bending. The governing structural code allows discontinuities up to 1/8 in measured in any direction on that surface. What is the result for this coupon?", "options": {"A": "Accepted, because cracks are measured only on the concave side of a bent specimen.", "B": "Rejected, because the crack on the convex surface exceeds the 1/8 in limit in the code.", "C": "Accepted, because the code limit applies only to cracks that run through the full thickness.", "D": "Rejected, because the specimen bent past the center line of the die before the crack formed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rejected, because the crack on the convex surface exceeds the 1/8 in limit in the code.): The acceptance limit is read on the convex surface, where the tension opens flaws. A 3/16 in crack is larger than 1/8 in, since 3/16 equals 0.1875 in. Bending past the die center line is not a rejection reason."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "After a fillet weld break test on 3/8 in plate, the fractured surface shows a dark band of unfused metal along most of the root and several round holes near the center. The code requires complete fusion at the root. What is the proper conclusion for this welder's test?", "options": {"A": "The test passes, since the weld broke through the throat instead of the base metal.", "B": "The test passes if the leg size meets the minimum, because fracture surfaces are advisory only.", "C": "The test is inconclusive and must be repeated with a macroetch, since breaks cannot show fusion.", "D": "The test fails, since the fracture shows incomplete fusion at the root and visible porosity."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The test fails, since the fracture shows incomplete fusion at the root and visible porosity.): A fillet break exposes root fusion and gas holes directly. Unfused metal at the root and visible porosity break the code requirement. Leg size does not excuse lack of fusion."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A lab runs Charpy V-notch specimens from a procedure qualification weld at 0°F, then repeats the test at -40°F on three more specimens cut from the same weld. The absorbed energy readings at -40°F come out much lower. What does this change in results indicate?", "options": {"A": "The weld metal becomes stronger in tension, so the specimens simply stop absorbing impact energy.", "B": "The testing machine hammer lost speed, so the lower readings are a calibration problem only.", "C": "The notch was machined in the wrong location, so the readings are invalid for both temperatures.", "D": "The weld metal turns less tough and more brittle as temperature drops toward transition."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The weld metal turns less tough and more brittle as temperature drops toward transition.): Many steels lose toughness as they get colder and move from ductile to brittle fracture, so absorbed energy falls. This is exactly why Charpy tests are run at the minimum service temperature."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A macroetch of a double fillet cross section on 1/4 in plate shows complete fusion at the root and both toes, but the legs measure 3/16 in against a required 1/4 in leg size. How should the inspector report this specimen?", "options": {"A": "It passes, since good fusion at the root and toes is the only thing macroetching evaluates.", "B": "It fails for lack of fusion, as the etched line shows incomplete bonding at the root.", "C": "It passes, since leg size is verified only with a fillet gauge on the production weld.", "D": "Fusion is acceptable, but the 3/16 in legs are smaller than required, so it fails."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fusion is acceptable, but the 3/16 in legs are smaller than required, so it fails.): A macroetch shows size as well as fusion, and 3/16 in is less than 1/4 in. Good fusion alone does not make an undersized weld acceptable."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator completes a groove weld on ASTM A514 quenched and tempered steel in the afternoon and wants to run ultrasonic and magnetic particle testing before leaving. The inspection lead points to the structural welding code timing rule for this steel. When may final NDT acceptance testing begin?", "options": {"A": "Immediately after the final pass, because delayed cracking is only a concern for stainless steel.", "B": "After one hour, once the weld surface is cool enough for the couplant or particles.", "C": "After at least 48 hours at ambient temperature, so any delayed cracking has time to appear.", "D": "After the weld has been stress relieved in a furnace at a temperature of 1,100°F."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (After at least 48 hours at ambient temperature, so any delayed cracking has time to appear.): Quenched and tempered high-strength steels can crack hours after welding as hydrogen diffuses, so AWS D1.1 requires a 48 hour wait before acceptance NDT. Early testing could pass a weld that cracks later."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiographer is shooting pipe welds in a shop bay using an iridium-192 source, and the area is roped off. A welder walks toward the rope to retrieve a tool. Which action best limits the exposure of workers near the gamma radiography site?", "options": {"A": "Rely on the film cassette to absorb radiation before it reaches workers beside the pipe.", "B": "Keep a barricade and distance from the source, since dose rate falls with distance.", "C": "Hand out thin lead aprons, since gamma rays from iridium are stopped by a few millimeters.", "D": "Shorten the exposure by using a stronger source placed nearer the weld."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keep a barricade and distance from the source, since dose rate falls with distance.): Radiation intensity falls off with the square of distance, so barricades and distance protect nearby workers. A film cassette and thin aprons do not stop gamma rays from iridium-192, and a stronger source adds dose."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A technician applies a thick, uneven coat of white developer during a visible dye penetrant test on a steel weld, and the surface looks nearly opaque. A bleed-out that should appear at a known crack is hard to see. What is the likely effect of an over-applied developer coat?", "options": {"A": "It dissolves the penetrant from inside the crack, which prevents bleed-out from forming.", "B": "It causes the penetrant to run across the weld and creates false cracks along the toe.", "C": "It strengthens contrast so every indication appears larger than the real size of the flaw.", "D": "It can mask small indications by hiding fine bleed-out beneath a layer that is too heavy."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It can mask small indications by hiding fine bleed-out beneath a layer that is too heavy.): Developer should be a thin, even film. A thick coat can bury small bleed-out and hide it, so the flaw may be missed. It does not dissolve the penetrant or invent cracks."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A pressure piping contractor needs to find buried slag and lack of fusion in a 1.5 in thick carbon steel groove weld on a production pipe. Surface methods have already passed the weld. Which method is best suited to detect these internal flaws?", "options": {"A": "Liquid penetrant testing, since a long dwell time lets penetrant migrate to buried flaws.", "B": "Visual testing with magnification, since slag shows through the weld metal when lit well.", "C": "Magnetic particle testing with a wet bath, since particles collect at flaws throughout the thickness.", "D": "Ultrasonic or radiographic testing, since both can detect discontinuities located inside the weld."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ultrasonic or radiographic testing, since both can detect discontinuities located inside the weld.): Only volumetric methods such as ultrasonic and radiographic testing see inside the weld. Penetrant and visual methods need surface openings, and magnetic particle testing finds surface and slightly subsurface flaws."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder completes a 3/8 in V-groove coupon on a steel backing bar for a root bend test during qualification. Before bend specimens are cut, the instructor tells the welder what must happen to the backing bar. What preparation is needed for the root bend specimen?", "options": {"A": "Leave the backing bar attached so it supports the specimen during bending.", "B": "Remove the backing and grind the root surface flush with the base plate surface.", "C": "Weld the backing to the plate permanently so that the root stays in compression.", "D": "Replace the backing with a thicker bar before the specimen is placed in the jig."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the backing and grind the root surface flush with the base plate surface.): A root bend must stress the weld root itself, so the backing is removed and the root ground flush. A bar left in place would stiffen the specimen and hide root flaws."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welding instructor cuts bend specimens from a qualification coupon with a plasma torch, leaving rough kerf edges on the tension side corners. The test lab says the specimens are not ready for testing as prepared. What additional step is needed on the cut edges?", "options": {"A": "Quench the cut edges in water so the heat affected metal hardens uniformly before bending.", "B": "Weld a thin bead along each cut edge to fill the gouges before testing.", "C": "Polish the edges to a mirror finish using fine abrasive paper of at least 600 grit.", "D": "Machine or grind the cut surfaces smooth and round the tension side edges slightly."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Machine or grind the cut surfaces smooth and round the tension side edges slightly.): Rough thermal-cut edges can start cracks that are unrelated to the weld. Specimen surfaces are machined or ground smooth and the corners rounded, with grinding marks running lengthwise."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "While scanning with a straight beam probe on a 3/4 in plate beside a weld, an inspector sees a full-height signal at about half the plate thickness over a wide area, and the back wall echo drops away in the same area. What does this most likely represent?", "options": {"A": "Normal sound reflecting from the weld root because the probe sits directly above the weld.", "B": "Surface porosity in the weld crown that scatters the sound toward the opposite plate face.", "C": "A lamination in the plate that reflects the sound before it can reach the back surface.", "D": "Poor couplant on the surface, which would also remove the initial pulse from the screen."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A lamination in the plate that reflects the sound before it can reach the back surface.): A flat reflector at mid-thickness that blocks the back wall is typical of a lamination. Poor couplant would weaken all signals, including the initial pulse, and the probe is not on the weld."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fabricator welding a pressure vessel under ASME Section IX and a structural bridge component under AWS D1.5 must decide how to judge discontinuities found in each product during final inspection. Which statement describes how weld acceptance is determined for each?", "options": {"A": "Each weld is judged against the acceptance criteria of the code governing that product.", "B": "All welds are judged against a single universal set of limits published by one standards body.", "C": "Acceptance rests on the inspector's own experience about what looks sound on the weld surface.", "D": "The welder decides acceptance after comparing the finished weld with a photograph of a good weld."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Each weld is judged against the acceptance criteria of the code governing that product.): Each code sets its own limits, so a flaw acceptable in one product can be rejected in another. The inspector applies the limits that govern the job rather than personal judgment."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector rejects a 6 in long crack in a structural steel groove weld found by magnetic particle testing. The welder gouges out the weld area and plans to reweld right away. What must be confirmed before the repair weld is deposited?", "options": {"A": "The excavation is filled with flux cored wire, since it burns through any remaining crack tips.", "B": "The gouged area has been quenched in water so the groove cools down quickly.", "C": "The excavation has been ground to a knife edge so the new weld wets out.", "D": "The crack was fully removed, verified by magnetic particle or penetrant testing of the excavation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The crack was fully removed, verified by magnetic particle or penetrant testing of the excavation.): Welding over a crack that was not entirely removed would carry it into the repair. Codes call for confirming removal with MT or PT before rewelding. Quenching or a knife edge does not remove a crack."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder's face bend specimen cut from a groove weld on 3/8 inch plate is placed in the guided bend jig for an AWS qualification test. The instructor checks the setup before the plunger loads it. Where should the weld be positioned relative to the die opening so the test is valid?", "options": {"A": "Centered over the die opening so the plunger bends the weld and heat affected zones.", "B": "Off to one side so the plunger loads only the base metal.", "C": "Clamped against a die shoulder so the bend starts at the fusion line.", "D": "Placed at one end of the specimen so the plunger bends the weld last."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Centered over the die opening so the plunger bends the weld and heat affected zones.): The test is meant to strain the weld and the heat affected zones, so the weld sits at the center under the plunger. Anywhere else, the bend would miss the area being qualified."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder qualifies on 1 in thick plate for a structural job, and the shop cuts side bend specimens transverse across the weld instead of face and root bends, because the plate is thick. Why are side bend specimens preferred on thick plate?", "options": {"A": "They load only the weld face, so thick plate does not need its reinforcement removed.", "B": "They avoid the need for a guided bend jig, since the specimen bends by hand.", "C": "They measure impact toughness of the thick plate at the lowest temperature of service.", "D": "They test the full weld thickness for fusion defects without excessive bending strain at the surface."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They test the full weld thickness for fusion defects without excessive bending strain at the surface.): On thick plate, face and root bends would need very high force and strain, and they only stress one side. A side bend puts the whole weld cross section on the outside surface, so fusion flaws at any depth show."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A procedure qualification record on 1 in plate requires Charpy tests of both the weld metal and the heat affected zone at a stated temperature. The lab prepares the notched specimens. Where is the notch located on each type of specimen?", "options": {"A": "At the plate surface for both types, because toughness is tested only on the outside skin.", "B": "Across the full weld width for both, so the notch spans weld and base metal evenly.", "C": "At the root for weld metal specimens, and at the plate edge for heat affected specimens.", "D": "Weld centerline for weld metal, and near the fusion line for heat affected zone specimens."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weld centerline for weld metal, and near the fusion line for heat affected zone specimens.): Each specimen is notched in the region whose toughness is being measured, so the weld centerline is used for weld metal and the fusion line area for the heat affected zone. Notching at the plate surface, across the whole weld width or at the root would not sample the weld metal or the heat affected zone separately."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A welder's 3/8 in groove coupon passes visual and radiographic examination but fails two guided bend specimens, each showing a 1/4 in crack on the convex surface along the fusion line. The shop lead blames the film. Which conclusion best fits all of this evidence?", "options": {"A": "The film is wrong, because radiography is more sensitive than bend tests for fusion flaws.", "B": "The base plate is too hard, because bend cracks at fusion lines come from plate chemistry.", "C": "Sidewall lack of fusion likely exists as a planar flaw the film missed due to orientation.", "D": "The jig radius was too tight, so the cracks are artifacts and the weld should pass."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sidewall lack of fusion likely exists as a planar flaw the film missed due to orientation.): Planar flaws like sidewall lack of fusion can be missed on film when they are not aligned with the beam, while a bend test opens them directly. Two matching failures at the fusion line point to a real flaw."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A tight fatigue crack is suspected at the toe of a fillet weld on a painted carbon steel bridge member. The inspector wants the quickest reliable method for a surface crack on this ferromagnetic steel. Which choice is most defensible?", "options": {"A": "Magnetic particle testing after local paint removal, as it finds tight surface cracks.", "B": "Radiography, because tight cracks lying parallel to the surface show clearly on the film.", "C": "Straight beam ultrasonic testing from the top surface, because it images toe cracks directly.", "D": "Macroetch of the member in place, because etching can be done without cutting the steel."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Magnetic particle testing after local paint removal, as it finds tight surface cracks.): Magnetic particle testing is fast and sensitive for tight surface cracks in ferromagnetic steel once coating that could hide them is removed. Cracks parallel to the film are hard to see, and a macroetch needs a cut section."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector performs a water washable penetrant test on an aluminum weld repair and sees red background across much of the surface, hiding possible indications. The procedure was followed in every step except the rinsing, which was rushed. What is the best corrective action?", "options": {"A": "Apply a second coat of developer over the red background so contrast is restored.", "B": "Lengthen the dwell time so the penetrant settles into the surface roughness evenly.", "C": "Reclean the part and repeat the test with more thorough excess penetrant removal.", "D": "Switch to magnetic particle testing since the red background shows the metal is magnetic."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reclean the part and repeat the test with more thorough excess penetrant removal.): Heavy background means excess penetrant was left on the surface, so the part must be cleaned and the test redone with better removal. More developer or dwell would make the background worse, and aluminum is not magnetic."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "During wet fluorescent magnetic particle testing of a steel weld at a sharp fillet toe, particles gather in a broad fuzzy band along the toe, but no sharp line forms. After the part is demagnetized and remagnetized at lower current, the band disappears. How should the first indication be classified?", "options": {"A": "A relevant crack indication that closed up when current was reduced, so the toe is rejected.", "B": "A nonrelevant indication from toe geometry and strong field, since it vanished at lower current.", "C": "A relevant lack of fusion indication, since it only shows when the field is strong.", "D": "A bath contamination indication, which is recorded as a rejectable flaw until the bath is replaced."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A nonrelevant indication from toe geometry and strong field, since it vanished at lower current.): Sharp geometry like a fillet toe can leak flux and hold particles when the field is strong. A true crack would still show at lower current, so this is nonrelevant, not a rejectable flaw."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiograph taken straight through a groove weld shows no indications, yet an ultrasonic scan later reports a planar flaw along the bevel face tilted about 30° from the beam path. What best explains why the film may have missed it?", "options": {"A": "Film is insensitive to volumetric flaws, so only ultrasonic testing can show porosity.", "B": "Gamma sources cannot penetrate steel thicker than half an inch, so the film showed the surface.", "C": "Planar flaws show on film mainly when the beam runs nearly parallel to them.", "D": "Ultrasonic reflection from a flat face produces false signals, so the report is unreliable on bevels."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Planar flaws show on film mainly when the beam runs nearly parallel to them.): A thin planar flaw adds little thickness difference when the beam crosses it at an angle, so film shows it faintly or not in the image. Ultrasonic sound reflects well from planar surfaces, which makes it a strong complement to radiography."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A reduced section tensile specimen from a procedure qualification breaks in the weld metal at 66,000 psi, and the fracture shows slag inclusions. The base metal minimum tensile strength is 70,000 psi. What does this result mean for the qualification?", "options": {"A": "It passes, because the weld stress is within ten percent of the base metal minimum.", "B": "It passes, because breaks in the weld are expected and only base metal breaks are rejected.", "C": "It is inconclusive, because slag inclusions void the test and a new specimen must be cut.", "D": "It fails, because a weld break under the minimum tensile strength means a weak joint."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It fails, because a weld break under the minimum tensile strength means a weak joint.): A specimen that breaks in the weld must reach at least the specified minimum tensile strength, and 66,000 is below 70,000. Being close does not count, and slag is the likely reason the weld is weak."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "Three Charpy specimens cut from one weld give 35, 28, and 18 ft-lbf at the test temperature. The specification requires an average of at least 27 ft-lbf with no single value below 20 ft-lbf. How should the inspector evaluate these results?", "options": {"A": "Pass, since the 27 ft-lbf average meets the specified minimum average energy.", "B": "Fail, since the 27 ft-lbf average passes but one specimen is below the single-value limit.", "C": "Fail, since the average of 25 ft-lbf is below the required minimum average.", "D": "Pass, since two of the three specimens are above the single-value limit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fail, since the 27 ft-lbf average passes but one specimen is below the single-value limit.): The average is (35 + 28 + 18) ÷ 3 = 81 ÷ 3 = 27 ft-lbf, which meets the average requirement. But 18 is under the 20 ft-lbf single-value floor, so the set fails. Rechecking: 27 × 3 = 81."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiograph of a pressure pipe weld shows a single round porosity indication 1/16 in in diameter. The governing code permits isolated porosity up to 3/32 in. The foreman wants it repaired to be safe. What action matches proper practice?", "options": {"A": "Accept the weld as is, because the indication is within the code's stated acceptance limit.", "B": "Reject the weld, since porosity of any size is a defect in pressure piping.", "C": "Repair it, because the inspector may tighten the limit when a foreman requests it.", "D": "Cut out the entire joint, since porosity shows the welder used the wrong shielding gas."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accept the weld as is, because the indication is within the code's stated acceptance limit.): Since 1/16 in is smaller than 3/32 in, the indication is a discontinuity within the limit and not a defect. Repairing an acceptable weld adds heat and risk, and inspectors apply the code, not a request."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An engineer must choose one volumetric method for a 4 in thick carbon steel butt weld in a fabrication yard where gamma radiation cannot be used. Planar lack of fusion is the main concern for this joint. Which choice is most defensible?", "options": {"A": "Radiography with X-rays, because thick sections give the sharpest film image of planar flaws.", "B": "Ultrasonic testing, because it is sensitive to planar flaws in thick sections and uses no radiation.", "C": "Magnetic particle testing with a yoke, because it reveals planar flaws through the full thickness.", "D": "Penetrant testing with a long dwell, because it travels through small root gaps in thick welds."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ultrasonic testing, because it is sensitive to planar flaws in thick sections and uses no radiation.): Ultrasonic sound travels well through thick steel and reflects from planar flaws, with no radiation hazard. Radiography loses sensitivity in thick sections, and penetrant and magnetic particle testing are surface methods."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A crew applies standard penetrant to a weld at about 20°F outdoors without checking the procedure temperature limits, and the test shows no indications. A leak is found later in service at that same weld. Which lesson does this case illustrate?", "options": {"A": "Penetrant works better when cold, so the missing indications mean the leak formed later.", "B": "Cold parts need a qualified procedure or warming, since cold thickens penetrant and slows flaw entry.", "C": "Cold surfaces form frost that makes developer unnecessary, so the missing step caused the miss.", "D": "Developer works only above 100°F, so the cold weld did not draw penetrant from the crack."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cold parts need a qualified procedure or warming, since cold thickens penetrant and slows flaw entry.): Standard penetrant is qualified for a limited temperature range, roughly 50°F to 125°F. Below that the liquid gets thicker and enters flaws slowly, so a clean result on a cold part cannot be trusted."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A fillet weld macroetch looks good in cross section with fusion at both legs, but a fillet break test on the same weld shows slag along much of its length. A supervisor says the macroetch proves the weld is sound. How should the discrepancy be understood?", "options": {"A": "The macroetch shows one cross section, so slag elsewhere along the weld can be missed.", "B": "The fillet break is unreliable, because breaking the weld distorts the internal condition it exposes.", "C": "Macroetching is more sensitive than breaking, so the slag was introduced during the break.", "D": "Slag does not count in fillet break tests, so the macroetch result should govern."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The macroetch shows one cross section, so slag elsewhere along the weld can be missed.): A macroetch samples a single cut plane, while a break exposes a long length of the weld. Slag elsewhere along the joint would show only in the break, so the weld is rejected."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A root bend specimen from a 3/8 in V-groove coupon cracks open at the root with a long fissure, while the face bend cut from the same coupon passes without any cracks. Which weld condition best explains this pair of results?", "options": {"A": "Excess reinforcement on the face, since it concentrates tension on the face side.", "B": "Porosity near the cap, since cap flaws open only during the root bend.", "C": "Poor shielding on the final pass, since root bends test only the last bead.", "D": "Incomplete root penetration or lack of fusion, which opens on the convex side."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Incomplete root penetration or lack of fusion, which opens on the convex side.): The root bend puts the root in tension, so a root flaw opens there. The face bend stresses the face instead, which can pass while the root is defective, so the flaw lies at the root."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector sees an ultrasonic signal at a steady position and constant amplitude all the way around a pipe butt weld that has root reinforcement and slight internal mismatch. What should the inspector consider before rejecting the weld on the strength of that signal?", "options": {"A": "Whether the probe frequency should be doubled, since steady signals show electronic noise only.", "B": "Whether the signal is a reflection from the root contour, checked by rescanning from another angle.", "C": "Whether the weld is acceptable, because signals of uniform amplitude around a pipe indicate sound metal.", "D": "Whether the couplant is too thin, since root signals appear only when couplant is plentiful."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Whether the signal is a reflection from the root contour, checked by rescanning from another angle.): Root contour and mismatch reflect sound consistently around a pipe, so a steady signal may be geometry and not a flaw. Skilled operators check the profile and rescan from other angles before judging."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A procedure assigns a long dwell time to a tight fatigue crack in aluminum but a shorter time for wide porosity in a casting weld repair. A trainee asks the reason for the difference. Which reasoning supports the longer dwell for the tight crack?", "options": {"A": "Aluminum absorbs penetrant into the base metal, so more time compensates for what is lost.", "B": "Tight cracks fill slowly by capillary action, so they need longer dwell than wide, open discontinuities.", "C": "Wider openings hold more penetrant and so need longer to drain before developer goes on.", "D": "Dwell time depends only on part temperature, not on the type of discontinuity being sought."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tight cracks fill slowly by capillary action, so they need longer dwell than wide, open discontinuities.): The narrower the opening, the slower the penetrant fills it, so tight cracks need longer. Wide pores fill fast. Dwell is set by material, penetrant and expected flaw type."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A bend specimen shows a 3/16 in crack at a corner of the convex surface that traces to a visible slag inclusion, with no other cracks. The code allows corner cracks up to 1/4 in unless they come from slag or other fusion-type discontinuities, which are held to 1/8 in. How is the specimen judged?", "options": {"A": "Rejected, because a corner crack tied to slag is held to the stricter 1/8 in limit.", "B": "Accepted, because any corner crack under 1/4 in meets the limit.", "C": "Accepted, because slag cracks are judged only by the radiographic film.", "D": "Rejected, because every corner crack fails the bend test."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rejected, because a corner crack tied to slag is held to the stricter 1/8 in limit.): The crack is tied to slag, so the 1/8 in limit applies and 3/16 in (0.1875 in) is larger than 0.125 in. A corner crack not tied to slag could have been up to 1/4 in."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An ultrasonic report lists lack of fusion in a groove weld at a spot where the surface magnetic particle test showed nothing and the visual inspection was clean. The crew argues the two results conflict. How should these results be reconciled?", "options": {"A": "The UT report is wrong, because clean surface MT rules out internal lack of fusion.", "B": "The MT result is wrong, because it should detect every internal flaw near the surface.", "C": "Both can be valid, since MT finds surface flaws and UT finds buried ones.", "D": "Both are invalid until a macroetch is done, since the methods must agree to count."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Both can be valid, since MT finds surface flaws and UT finds buried ones.): The two methods look at different volumes. A buried flaw that does not break the surface will not show with MT or visual examination, so the UT finding stands and is compared with the code limits."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "On a radiograph of a pressure pipe weld, the essential wire of the image quality indicator cannot be seen, although the film appears clear and well exposed to the reviewer. The shop wants the weld released today. What should the radiographer conclude?", "options": {"A": "The technique lacks the required sensitivity, so the film is rejected and the shot repeated.", "B": "The weld has no flaws, since a clear film means every discontinuity would have been visible.", "C": "The film is acceptable, since the indicator only identifies the part and weld number.", "D": "The indicator was the wrong thickness, so the weld is rejected without a retake being offered."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The technique lacks the required sensitivity, so the film is rejected and the shot repeated.): If the required wire is not visible the technique cannot be shown to reveal flaws of the specified size. A clear look does not prove sensitivity, so the film is repeated with a better technique."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A penetrant test passes an aluminum pipe weld, but a later radiograph of that pipe shows scattered internal porosity in the same weld. A manager claims the penetrant result was done wrong and wants the inspector disciplined. How should this difference be explained?", "options": {"A": "Radiography is oversensitive and records grain patterns that are not real porosity.", "B": "Penetrant works poorly on aluminum, so the first test must have been performed incorrectly.", "C": "Porosity forms in the weld only after the penetrant test, from moisture absorbed while cooling.", "D": "Penetrant testing reveals only flaws open to the surface, so internal porosity went undetected."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Penetrant testing reveals only flaws open to the surface, so internal porosity went undetected.): Penetrant can only enter openings at the surface, so buried pores are invisible to it. Radiography sees density changes through the metal, so the two results do not conflict."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A contractor needs to learn whether weld metal is tough enough at -50°F and whether the welded joint carries its full design strength, so the engineer must specify two laboratory tests for the procedure qualification of a new steel weld. Which pair of tests answers these two questions?", "options": {"A": "Face bend for toughness at that temperature, and macroetch for the joint strength.", "B": "Fillet break for toughness at that temperature, and Charpy for the joint strength.", "C": "Macroetch for toughness at that temperature, and root bend for the joint strength.", "D": "Charpy V-notch for toughness at that temperature, and reduced section tensile for joint strength."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Charpy V-notch for toughness at that temperature, and reduced section tensile for joint strength.): Charpy testing measures impact energy at a chosen temperature, and a tensile test measures how much load the joint carries. Bend, break and etch tests judge soundness, not toughness or strength figures."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "An inspector performing fluorescent penetrant testing in a booth sees only faint glows at a known crack. A reading shows white light in the booth is far above the procedure's limit, though the ultraviolet lamp output is correct. What is the best correction?", "options": {"A": "Raise the ultraviolet intensity above the limit, so the faint indication glows brighter.", "B": "Remove the developer, since fluorescence works best without any developer film.", "C": "Apply a second coat of penetrant after the first dwell so the glow strengthens.", "D": "Reduce ambient white light to the procedure limit, since bright light dims the glow."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce ambient white light to the procedure limit, since bright light dims the glow.): Fluorescent indications are judged in a dark booth because white light reduces contrast. Lowering room light to the limit restores visibility. Exceeding lamp limits or changing steps is not the fix."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "welding_inspection_and_testing", "question": "A radiograph of a structural weld shows a thin crack-like line about 1/4 in long. The structural code does not permit cracks of any length in welds. The foreman says the line is too small to matter. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Reject and repair the weld, because structural codes treat cracks as rejectable whatever their length.", "B": "Accept the weld, because discontinuities this small fall below the film's usable detection threshold.", "C": "Accept the weld, because cracks matter only when they are longer than the weld throat.", "D": "Reshoot with a different source, since film density alone decides whether cracks are accepted."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reject and repair the weld, because structural codes treat cracks as rejectable whatever their length.): Cracks are planar flaws that can grow under load, so structural codes reject them at any length. A visible indication that is clearly crack-like is already above the detection limit."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabrication shop owner is explaining to a new hire how welding documents differ in role. The owner points out that AWS D1.1 is a document covering rules for one particular application, and that it becomes mandatory only when a contract or a law adopts it. Which term best describes AWS D1.1?", "options": {"A": "A specification, meaning a detailed description of one filler metal's chemistry and mechanical properties", "B": "A code, meaning rules for a specific application made binding by contract or law", "C": "A procedure, meaning step by step instructions a welder follows on one joint", "D": "A standard drawing, meaning a pictorial layout of one fabricated part's dimensions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A code, meaning rules for a specific application made binding by contract or law): A code covers a whole type of work and gains force when a contract or law adopts it. A specification describes a material or product, and a procedure gives direction for a particular joint."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A purchasing clerk is sorting documents for a fabrication shop. One document is AWS A5.18, which spells out the chemical composition, mechanical properties and packaging requirements for carbon steel solid wire and metal cored electrodes. Which category of welding document does it belong to?", "options": {"A": "A code, because it governs how an entire structure must be designed, built and inspected", "B": "A procedure, because it lists the amperage and travel speed for each joint", "C": "A qualification record, because it documents the results of a test weld", "D": "A specification, because it defines the required properties of a specific product"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A specification, because it defines the required properties of a specific product): Filler metal documents such as A5.18 define what a product must be, which makes them specifications. Codes govern the work itself, and procedures and records are shop documents."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A student asks which welding code a fabricator would normally use for building frames and similar structures made of carbon and low alloy steel. The instructor writes four document names on the board and asks the class to choose the one whose title and scope match this type of work.", "options": {"A": "AWS D1.1, the Structural Welding Code for steel buildings and similar structures", "B": "AWS D1.5, the Bridge Welding Code covering highway bridge steel members", "C": "AWS D17.1, the specification for fusion welding of aerospace applications", "D": "API 1104, the standard for welding of pipelines and related facilities"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (AWS D1.1, the Structural Welding Code for steel buildings and similar structures): AWS D1.1 is titled Structural Welding Code Steel and is the usual governing document for building frames. D1.5 targets highway bridges, D17.1 aerospace work and API 1104 pipelines."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "During shop orientation, a supervisor hands a welder a one page sheet that lists the process, base metal, filler metal, preheat, position, current range and travel speed to be used for a specific joint. The welder asks what this sheet is correctly called, so the term can be used on the shop floor.", "options": {"A": "A procedure qualification record, a record of the test weld and its results", "B": "A welder performance qualification record, proof that one person passed a skill test", "C": "A weld map, a drawing that numbers every joint on the fabricated assembly", "D": "A welding procedure specification, written direction for production welds"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A welding procedure specification, written direction for production welds): The WPS is the written direction the welder follows in production. The PQR is the test evidence behind it, and the welder's own skill test is recorded separately."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A quality manager files a document containing the actual welding variables used on a test coupon, along with the tensile and bend test results from the testing laboratory. This record is later used to support one or more written procedures. A new inspector asks what the document is called.", "options": {"A": "A welding procedure specification, the written instructions given to welders in production", "B": "A welder performance qualification record, documenting one person's skill test", "C": "A visual inspection checklist, recording the weld profiles found acceptable in production", "D": "A procedure qualification record, abbreviated PQR, documenting the test and its results"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A procedure qualification record, abbreviated PQR, documenting the test and its results): A PQR records what was actually done on the test coupon and how it tested. A WPS is written from that evidence, so the two documents are related but not interchangeable."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor wants to use a prequalified WPS under AWS D1.1 and is reviewing which arc welding processes the code allows without qualification testing. The estimator lists four possibilities for a structural steel job and asks which one is accepted as a prequalified process when the other requirements are met.", "options": {"A": "Oxyfuel gas welding, since it is a long established process for steel", "B": "Submerged arc welding, when all other prequalified requirements are met", "C": "Plasma arc welding, since it concentrates heat in a narrow zone", "D": "Gas metal arc welding using the short circuit transfer mode"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Submerged arc welding, when all other prequalified requirements are met): D1.1 prequalifies SMAW, SAW, FCAW and GMAW other than short circuit transfer. Oxyfuel, plasma and short circuit GMAW must be qualified by test."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A boiler fabricator needs the document that sets the rules for qualifying welding procedures, welders and welding operators for pressure containing equipment such as boilers and vessels. The quality manager looks for the part of the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code that covers these qualification rules.", "options": {"A": "ASME Section IX, covering qualification of welding and brazing procedures and personnel", "B": "ASME Section II, which lists the material specifications for ferrous and nonferrous metals", "C": "ASME Section V, which describes nondestructive examination methods and procedures", "D": "ASME Section VIII, which gives the design rules for pressure vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ASME Section IX, covering qualification of welding and brazing procedures and personnel): Section IX is the qualification section of the ASME code. The other sections cover materials, examination methods and vessel design."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welding instructor labels four test plates with position designations. One plate is to be welded with its groove in the flat position, with the plate lying level and the weld deposited from above. Which AWS designation identifies a groove weld made in this flat position?", "options": {"A": "2G, the horizontal position for groove welds on plate", "B": "1G, the flat position for groove welds made on plate", "C": "3G, the vertical position for groove welds on plate", "D": "4G, the overhead position for groove welds on plate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1G, the flat position for groove welds made on plate): The number identifies the position and the letter identifies the weld type, so 1G is a flat groove weld. The higher numbers describe horizontal, vertical and overhead groove welds."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder passes a D1.1 groove weld test on plate in the 3G vertical position using SMAW. The foreman wants to assign the welder to a groove weld on a beam splice in the flat position with the same process. Is the assignment covered by this qualification, and why?", "options": {"A": "Yes, since 3G qualifies the welder for groove welds in flat, horizontal and vertical positions", "B": "No, because a vertical test only qualifies the welder to weld in the vertical position", "C": "No, because flat position welds need a separate qualification test at that position", "D": "Yes, but only after the welder also passes an overhead groove test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, since 3G qualifies the welder for groove welds in flat, horizontal and vertical positions): A 3G groove test qualifies the flat, horizontal and vertical positions for the same process. The foreman does not need a separate flat test."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder completes a groove weld test on pipe fixed at a 45 degree angle, the 6G position, under AWS D1.1 and passes all required bend tests. The shop planner asks which groove weld positions this single test qualifies the welder to perform, within the other limits of the qualification.", "options": {"A": "All positions for groove welds, within the other limits of the qualification", "B": "Flat and horizontal positions only, since the pipe does not rotate during the test", "C": "Only the 45 degree inclined position that was actually used in the test", "D": "Flat and vertical positions only, because the overhead position needs its own test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (All positions for groove welds, within the other limits of the qualification): The inclined fixed pipe test passes through flat, vertical and overhead orientations as the welder goes around the joint. For that reason 6G is the most inclusive groove test."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop holds a PQR for a groove weld made with gas metal arc welding. A project engineer proposes switching the same joint to flux cored arc welding and simply editing the existing WPS to show the new process. Under ASME Section IX, what must be done before welding production joints?", "options": {"A": "Only the WPS header needs revising since process is a nonessential variable on a groove joint", "B": "The welder must pass a new guided bend test but the PQR stays valid", "C": "A new PQR must be run because changing the welding process is an essential variable", "D": "The inspector must approve the edit and then the existing PQR may be reused"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A new PQR must be run because changing the welding process is an essential variable): A process change alters the weld metal and heat flow, so it is an essential variable. The WPS needs fresh PQR support for the new process."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A WPS under ASME Section IX shows a change in groove design as a nonessential variable for the process in use. The fabricator wants to alter the groove angle on production joints. What may the fabricator do without welding a new test coupon for this change?", "options": {"A": "Revise the WPS to the new groove detail, since no new PQR is needed", "B": "Weld a new test coupon because every change on a WPS needs a new PQR", "C": "Obtain a new welder performance qualification for every welder on the job", "D": "Request a revised code edition before any joint dimension may be altered"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Revise the WPS to the new groove detail, since no new PQR is needed): Nonessential variables can be changed on the WPS without requalifying, provided the WPS is revised to show the change. Only essential variable changes force new test coupons."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A project requires notch toughness testing of the weld metal, so the ASME Section IX WPS must address a category of variables that matters only when impact testing is specified. The fabricator wants to raise heat input above the qualified value. Which variable category now applies to this change?", "options": {"A": "Nonessential variables, which can be revised in the WPS without any retesting", "B": "Essential variables only, which are unrelated to the toughness requirement of the job", "C": "Supplementary essential variables, which apply when toughness testing is specified", "D": "Informational variables, which appear on the form only for the inspector's reference"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supplementary essential variables, which apply when toughness testing is specified): Supplementary essential variables are added to the essential list when toughness testing is required. Heat input is a typical one, so exceeding the qualified value needs requalification."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder passed a D1.1 qualification test on SMAW groove welds in January, then spent seven months working only as a fitter without using any arc welding process. The supervisor wants to assign the welder to SMAW production welds. What does the code require before that assignment?", "options": {"A": "No action, because passed qualifications stay valid until the code edition changes", "B": "Requalification, since the welder did not use the process for more than six months", "C": "Only a visual test of a single fillet weld, since the groove test remains current", "D": "A new written exam on weld symbols, with no welding test required"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Requalification, since the welder did not use the process for more than six months): Qualification stays effective only while the welder keeps using the process, and a gap of six months or more ends it. The welder must be tested again."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor follows a prequalified WPS under AWS D1.1 for a fillet weld on structural steel using flux cored arc welding. A new hire has not been tested under the code. The contractor believes prequalified status removes every testing need. What is the accurate position on welder testing?", "options": {"A": "The welder is exempt, since a prequalified WPS also carries the qualification of the whole crew", "B": "The welder needs testing only if the joint is a groove weld rather than fillet", "C": "Testing is required only when the engineer specifically asks for impact testing", "D": "The welder still needs qualification, since prequalification covers procedures, not people"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The welder still needs qualification, since prequalification covers procedures, not people): Prequalification waives procedure testing, not welder testing. Every welder on code work must hold a current qualification for the process and position."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabricator ran one successful procedure qualification test on carbon steel plate with SMAW. The engineer plans to write several WPSs for different thicknesses and positions within the limits allowed by that test. Can one PQR support more than one WPS under ASME Section IX, and why?", "options": {"A": "No, because each WPS needs its own unique PQR covering exactly one thickness", "B": "Yes, but only when all the WPSs list the identical welding position", "C": "No, because a PQR is valid only for the welder who ran the test", "D": "Yes, since a single PQR can support multiple WPSs that stay within its qualified ranges"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yes, since a single PQR can support multiple WPSs that stay within its qualified ranges): A PQR supports every WPS whose variables fall inside the ranges it qualifies. A PQR records the procedure, not the person, so it is not tied to one welder."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A lab report for a procedure qualification test shows reduced section tensile specimens broke in the base metal above the minimum specified tensile strength, and the guided bend specimens showed no cracks. A trainee asks what the guided bend tests were mainly checking in the weld. Which answer is best?", "options": {"A": "Ultimate tensile strength of the weld, measured by the load needed to bend it", "B": "Hardness of the heat affected zone, measured across the bent surface", "C": "Soundness and ductility of the weld and heat affected zone, shown by bending", "D": "Chemical composition of the deposited metal, confirmed by the bend radius"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Soundness and ductility of the weld and heat affected zone, shown by bending): Bend tests reveal fusion defects and lack of ductility by stretching the outer surface. Strength is measured by the tensile test instead."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A WPS lists base metals by P-number. A welding engineer wants to explain to apprentices what the P-number grouping accomplishes under ASME Section IX, so that fewer separate procedure qualifications are needed for similar materials. What does a P-number represent?", "options": {"A": "A grouping of filler metals by their usability characteristics", "B": "A grouping of weld deposit chemical compositions used for analysis", "C": "A grouping of base metals with similar weldability and metallurgical properties", "D": "A numbering of welder test positions used on qualification plates"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A grouping of base metals with similar weldability and metallurgical properties): P-numbers group base metals so a qualification on one can cover others in the group. Filler metals use F-numbers and weld deposit analysis uses A-numbers."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder tests in the 2G position on plate groove under D1.1 and passes. The job requires a groove weld in the horizontal position, and a later job requires one in the flat position. A supervisor must decide how far this one test reaches. Which statement applies?", "options": {"A": "The test covers horizontal welds only, so the flat job needs its own separate test", "B": "The test covers horizontal and vertical welds but not flat welds", "C": "The test covers every position because horizontal is the most difficult one", "D": "The test qualifies horizontal and flat groove welds on plate, covering both jobs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The test qualifies horizontal and flat groove welds on plate, covering both jobs): A 2G groove test qualifies the horizontal and flat positions. It does not reach vertical or overhead work."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A QC clerk files a record showing that a named welder passed a test, with the process, position, filler metal and thickness ranges recorded along with the test results. Under ASME Section IX, which record is this, and what does it document about the welder?", "options": {"A": "A welder performance qualification record, abbreviated WPQ, for that specific welder", "B": "A procedure qualification record, abbreviated PQR, for the shop's procedure", "C": "A welding procedure specification, abbreviated WPS, issued for production use", "D": "A nondestructive examination procedure, abbreviated NDE, issued for inspectors"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A welder performance qualification record, abbreviated WPQ, for that specific welder): The WPQ records one person's test and the ranges it qualifies. The PQR belongs to the procedure rather than a person."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contract for a structural steel building requires welding to AWS D1.1, 2020 edition, but the shop supervisor has only the newest edition on the shelf. A weld detail differs slightly between the two editions. Which document governs this job?", "options": {"A": "The 2020 edition, since the contract names it as the governing document", "B": "The newest edition, since it supersedes older versions on every active project", "C": "Whichever edition has the less demanding requirement for the joint concerned", "D": "The edition the welder was originally qualified under, whatever year it was"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 2020 edition, since the contract names it as the governing document): The edition cited in the contract controls the work, even if a newer one exists. Changing editions needs a contract change agreed by the parties."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A WPS review comment from the inspector notes that a provision in the code uses the word shall for preheat verification, while another provision uses should for a layout suggestion. The apprentice asks how to treat these two words. Which interpretation matches AWS code language?", "options": {"A": "Both words are mandatory, and should only appears where the engineer is less certain", "B": "Shall marks a mandatory requirement, while should marks a recommendation only", "C": "Shall is advisory guidance and should is the mandatory requirement", "D": "Neither word has any bearing on compliance, since only the drawings are enforceable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shall marks a mandatory requirement, while should marks a recommendation only): In AWS codes shall is mandatory, should is recommended and may is optional. Treating a should as a requirement or a shall as advice causes errors in compliance."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabricator receives a job to weld thin stair framing made from 16 gauge sheet steel, about 1/16 inch thick. The buyer asks whether AWS D1.1 applies. A senior welder recalls that the code has a lower limit on base metal thickness. Which statement fits?", "options": {"A": "D1.1 does not apply because the metal is under its 1/8 inch minimum thickness", "B": "D1.1 covers it because the code applies to every thickness of carbon steel", "C": "D1.1 covers it only if the sheet is galvanized before welding", "D": "D1.1 covers it because 16 gauge meets the code's minimum thickness of 1/16 inch"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (D1.1 does not apply because the metal is under its 1/8 inch minimum thickness): D1.1 applies to structural steel 1/8 inch and thicker. Thinner sheet falls under a different code such as the sheet steel structural welding code."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop uses a prequalified WPS for a complete joint penetration groove weld, but the fit up crew opens the root opening well beyond the tolerance shown in the code's prequalified joint details. A QA technician reviews the paperwork. What is the consequence for the prequalified status?", "options": {"A": "Prequalified status stays valid because only the process matters, not joint details", "B": "Prequalified status stays valid if the welder compensates with extra filler passes", "C": "The joint is no longer prequalified and needs qualification by test before use", "D": "Prequalified status ends only if the inspector rejects the finished weld visually"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The joint is no longer prequalified and needs qualification by test before use): Prequalification depends on staying within the code's joint details and tolerances. Outside those limits the WPS must be supported by a test like any other."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A pipe welder wants a test that shows ability to weld pipe held with its axis horizontal and fixed during the test, so the weld progresses around the whole circumference while the pipe does not rotate at any point. Which designation describes that test position?", "options": {"A": "5G, pipe axis horizontal and fixed while welding around the joint", "B": "1G, pipe axis horizontal and rotated while welding stays near the top", "C": "2G, pipe axis vertical and fixed while welding horizontally around the joint", "D": "6G, pipe axis inclined at 45 degrees and fixed while welding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5G, pipe axis horizontal and fixed while welding around the joint): In 5G the pipe is fixed horizontal, so the welder works flat, vertical and overhead around the joint. In 6G the axis is inclined, and 2G has the axis vertical."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder makes a T joint fillet weld by depositing the bead on top of a horizontal plate where it meets a vertical plate, so the weld axis runs horizontally. An apprentice needs the correct label for the daily weld log. Which AWS designation names this fillet weld position?", "options": {"A": "1F, the flat position for fillet welds with the joint rotated", "B": "3F, the vertical position for fillet welds made in an upward direction", "C": "2F, the horizontal position for fillet welds on a T joint", "D": "4F, the overhead position for fillet welds made under a horizontal plate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (2F, the horizontal position for fillet welds on a T joint): A fillet with a horizontal axis laid against a vertical surface is a 2F weld. Flat has the weld in a flat orientation, vertical runs up or down, and overhead is made from below."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop manager sorts four documents for a structural job: the governing code, an electrode specification, the written welding procedure and a welder test record. A welder asks which one gives the amperage range, travel speed and preheat to use on a particular joint. Which document does the welder need?", "options": {"A": "The governing code, because it lists the exact amperage for every joint type", "B": "The electrode specification, because it dictates preheat for each base metal thickness", "C": "The welder test record, because it states the settings used in the test", "D": "The written welding procedure, because it states the variable ranges for that joint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The written welding procedure, because it states the variable ranges for that joint): Variable ranges for a given joint live in the WPS. The code sets rules, the electrode specification defines the product and the test record shows only what one person was qualified on."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A guided bend specimen from a procedure qualification test shows a single open crack in the middle of the convex surface measuring 3/16 inch in its longest dimension. The lab asks whether the specimen passes under the guided bend acceptance criteria used by both ASME Section IX and AWS D1.1. What is the result?", "options": {"A": "It passes, because open discontinuities up to 1/4 inch are acceptable in any location", "B": "It passes, because only discontinuities that break through the full thickness count", "C": "It fails only if the crack runs to the edge of the specimen", "D": "It fails, because an open discontinuity larger than 1/8 inch is not acceptable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It fails, because an open discontinuity larger than 1/8 inch is not acceptable): Both codes limit open discontinuities on the convex surface to 1/8 inch measured in any direction. A 3/16 inch crack exceeds that limit, so the test fails."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder holds a D1.1 qualification for plate groove welds in the 3G and 4G positions. The foreman wants to assign the welder to a groove weld on small diameter pipe, arguing that plate qualification should carry over to pipe. How should the foreman evaluate this assignment under the code's limits?", "options": {"A": "Plate qualification covers all pipe diameters since the groove and process are identical", "B": "Plate qualification covers small pipe only for the flat and horizontal positions", "C": "Plate tests qualify only large diameter pipe, so small pipe needs its own test", "D": "Plate qualification covers small pipe if the foreman signs a written waiver"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plate tests qualify only large diameter pipe, so small pipe needs its own test): Plate qualification carries over to pipe only above a large diameter limit. Small pipe has tight curvature and access, so a pipe test is required."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabricator qualified a SMAW procedure using an E7018 electrode. A shop engineer proposes switching to an E6010 electrode for the root pass on production joints, reasoning that both are carbon steel SMAW electrodes. Under ASME Section IX, how should the change in filler metal be judged?", "options": {"A": "Both electrodes are carbon steel SMAW, so the same F-number applies and nothing changes", "B": "Filler changes within SMAW are nonessential, so only the WPS wording is updated", "C": "The change needs only a welder retest since procedure variables are unaffected", "D": "Different F-numbers make it an essential variable change requiring requalification"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Different F-numbers make it an essential variable change requiring requalification): E6010 and E7018 fall in different F-number groups because their usability characteristics differ. A change in F-number is an essential variable under Section IX."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A PQR records a test weld on 1 inch thick carbon steel plate. A WPS writer wants to state the base metal thickness range for production, and a colleague suggests the WPS may list any thickness since the process is the same. What is the correct approach to the thickness range?", "options": {"A": "List any thickness desired because the PQR only proves the process works generally", "B": "List only the exact coupon thickness since no range is permitted", "C": "Limit the WPS thickness range to what the code allows for the coupon thickness", "D": "List thicknesses up to twice the coupon thickness under every code"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limit the WPS thickness range to what the code allows for the coupon thickness): The code ties the qualified thickness range to the thickness of the test coupon. A WPS cannot extend beyond that range without further qualification."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop works to both AWS D1.1 for structural frames and ASME Section IX for pressure vessels. An engineer suggests using the D1.1 prequalified WPS approach, with no PQR testing, for the ASME vessel welds too. How should this suggestion be evaluated?", "options": {"A": "Section IX accepts D1.1 prequalification directly, so no PQR testing is needed for vessels", "B": "Section IX accepts prequalification only when the vessel wall is under 1/2 inch", "C": "Section IX accepts prequalification when the owner's inspector verbally approves the joint", "D": "Section IX has no prequalified WPS route like D1.1, so a PQR is normally required"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Section IX has no prequalified WPS route like D1.1, so a PQR is normally required): Prequalification is a D1.1 concept. Section IX expects procedures to be supported by a PQR, with a narrow exception for adopted standard welding procedures."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder is qualified under D1.1 for SMAW on plate. A supervisor wants the same welder on a flux cored arc welding job, reasoning that both are manual processes using a flux and a similar position range. Does the SMAW qualification carry over to FCAW?", "options": {"A": "No, because each welding process is an essential variable needing its own test", "B": "Yes, since flux shielded processes share qualification when the positions match", "C": "Yes, but only for flat and horizontal positions with the same electrode diameter", "D": "No, unless the FCAW wire has the same strength classification as the SMAW electrode"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because each welding process is an essential variable needing its own test): Welder qualification is process specific, and a change of process requires a new test. Similar looking processes do not share qualification."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "During a code audit, an inspector finds that the production welder preheated the joint below the minimum shown on the WPS, although the finished weld passed visual inspection and looks sound. The shop argues that an acceptable looking weld makes the deviation unimportant. Which view matches how WPS compliance works?", "options": {"A": "Welding outside the WPS limits is a noncompliance even if the weld looks acceptable", "B": "The deviation is acceptable because the visual inspection result overrides the WPS limits", "C": "The deviation is acceptable if the welder qualification record remains current", "D": "The deviation is acceptable once the PQR shows the weld metal passed tensile testing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Welding outside the WPS limits is a noncompliance even if the weld looks acceptable): The WPS is the controlling document for production welding. Low preheat can cause cracking that visual inspection cannot detect, so the deviation is noncompliant."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A new foreman asks why codes require both a qualified procedure and qualified welders, noting that one good test seems like enough. The quality manager explains that the two tests answer different questions about a weld. Which explanation matches the purpose of each qualification?", "options": {"A": "Procedure testing proves personal skill, while welder testing proves the weld properties are achievable", "B": "Both tests prove the same thing, and the code requires two only for redundancy", "C": "Procedure testing proves achievable weld properties, while welder testing proves personal skill", "D": "Procedure testing applies to base metal only, while welder testing applies to filler metal only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Procedure testing proves achievable weld properties, while welder testing proves personal skill): A procedure test shows that the method can give the required properties. A welder test shows that a person can follow it and deposit sound metal."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A reduced section tensile specimen from a PQR has a width of 0.75 inch and a thickness of 0.50 inch. It breaks at a load of 33,750 pounds force. The base metal has a specified minimum tensile strength of 70,000 psi. What is the tensile strength result and does it meet that minimum?", "options": {"A": "45,000 psi, which falls below the minimum and fails the requirement", "B": "67,500 psi, which falls below the minimum and fails the requirement", "C": "90,000 psi, which exceeds the minimum and meets the requirement", "D": "135,000 psi, which exceeds the minimum and meets the requirement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (90,000 psi, which exceeds the minimum and meets the requirement): Area is 0.75 × 0.50 = 0.375 in², and 33,750 ÷ 0.375 = 90,000 psi. The wrong values come from dividing by width alone, by thickness alone, or by a halved area."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "An owner's specification calls for ASME Section IX qualification of all welders. The shop's welder was qualified under AWS D1.1 for structural work and holds a current record. The shop wants to use the welder on the ASME pressure job without retesting. Is the D1.1 record acceptable?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because all national welding codes accept one another's performance qualifications", "B": "Yes, since D1.1 tests are stricter than Section IX in every respect", "C": "Yes, as long as the welder's record is less than twelve months old", "D": "Not automatically, because welders must be qualified under the code governing the pressure work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Not automatically, because welders must be qualified under the code governing the pressure work): Each code sets its own qualification rules and ranges, and a record from one code does not transfer on its own. Section IX qualification is needed unless the governing code allows otherwise."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor is bidding a cross country oil transmission pipeline, and the owner asks which governing document typically controls welding of the pipeline girth welds. The estimator compares several documents on the bid list. Which one fits this type of work?", "options": {"A": "AWS D1.5, the code for welding of highway bridges built from steel", "B": "AWS D17.1, the specification covering fusion welding in aerospace applications", "C": "API 1104, the standard for welding of pipelines and related facilities in the field", "D": "ASME B31.3, the code governing welded piping inside process plants"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (API 1104, the standard for welding of pipelines and related facilities in the field): API 1104 is written for cross country pipelines. B31.3 covers piping inside plants, D1.5 covers bridges and D17.1 covers aerospace work."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop is preparing to weld a pressure job under ASME Section IX and has no suitable procedure. A planner lists four possible sequences for the paperwork, and the shop wants the order that matches normal practice before any production weld begins. Which sequence reflects the way a new procedure is normally qualified?", "options": {"A": "Record results on a PQR first, weld the coupon afterward, then write the WPS", "B": "Draft a WPS, weld and test a coupon, then record the results on a PQR", "C": "Qualify the welder first, then write the PQR, then weld a test coupon", "D": "Weld the production joint first, then test a coupon taken from that joint"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Draft a WPS, weld and test a coupon, then record the results on a PQR): The proposed WPS directs the test coupon, and the actual variables and results are then recorded on the PQR. Production welding follows only after the procedure is supported."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A shop wants to use gas tungsten arc welding for a groove weld on a structural steel connection under AWS D1.1. The foreman claims it is prequalified, like the other common arc welding processes. What is the correct position on this process?", "options": {"A": "GTAW is prequalified for root passes when the joint is a prequalified groove detail", "B": "GTAW is not prequalified, so the WPS must be qualified by testing before use", "C": "GTAW is prequalified when the welder holds a current SMAW qualification", "D": "GTAW is prequalified only if the filler wire meets an AWS A5 classification"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (GTAW is not prequalified, so the WPS must be qualified by testing before use): D1.1 prequalifies only specific processes, and GTAW is not among them. A GTAW procedure needs PQR testing like any nonprequalified WPS."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabrication shop is bidding on steel highway bridge girders, and the contract calls out the Bridge Welding Code. The estimator notices the cover carries two organization names and wants to know who publishes it so the shop can order the current edition. Which statement describes this document correctly?", "options": {"A": "It is published by ASME and governs welded boilers and pressure vessels in power plants.", "B": "It is published by API and governs welding on cross-country steel pipelines and related facilities.", "C": "It is published by AWS alone and governs thin sheet steel welded in buildings.", "D": "It is published jointly by AASHTO and the American Welding Society for welded highway bridges."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is published jointly by AASHTO and the American Welding Society for welded highway bridges.): D1.5 is the AASHTO/AWS Bridge Welding Code, written for steel highway bridges where fatigue under traffic loading matters. Boilers belong to ASME, pipelines to API 1104, and sheet steel to D1.3."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A small aerospace supplier welds thin-wall exhaust ducts and engine brackets for aircraft using gas tungsten arc welding. The quality manager needs the AWS specification that covers fusion welding for aerospace applications so the welders can be qualified to the right document. Which specification should the manager name on the procedure and qualification records?", "options": {"A": "AWS D1.1, the Structural Welding Code for steel used in buildings and general structures.", "B": "AWS D1.5, the Bridge Welding Code published jointly with the highway transportation officials.", "C": "API 1104, the standard written for welding of pipelines and related facilities.", "D": "AWS D17.1, Specification for Fusion Welding for Aerospace Applications."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (AWS D17.1, Specification for Fusion Welding for Aerospace Applications.): D17.1 is the AWS document written for fusion welding of aerospace hardware. D1.1 and D1.5 address structural steel and bridges, and API 1104 is a pipeline standard, so none of them fits aircraft ducts."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A pipeline contractor is preparing to weld a new cross-country natural gas transmission line in the field. The project engineer asks the welding supervisor which standard governs both the welding procedures and the welder qualification tests for this kind of work. Which standard does the supervisor identify?", "options": {"A": "API 1104, Welding of Pipelines and Related Facilities, for procedure and welder qualification.", "B": "ASME B31.3, Process Piping, which governs chemical plant and refinery piping inside a fence line.", "C": "AWS D1.1, Structural Welding Code Steel, which governs building frames and general structures.", "D": "AWS D17.1, which governs fusion welding of aircraft and spacecraft hardware."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (API 1104, Welding of Pipelines and Related Facilities, for procedure and welder qualification.): API 1104 is the pipeline welding standard and includes both procedure qualification and welder qualification requirements. B31.3 addresses plant piping, not transmission lines in the field."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "An engineering firm is designing piping for a chemical plant, and the owner's specification references a piping code that addresses design, materials, fabrication, examination and testing for process plants. A new welder asks which code book title that is. Which code does the specification most likely cite?", "options": {"A": "ASME B31.3, Process Piping, which covers chemical, petroleum and related plant piping systems.", "B": "ASME B31.1, Power Piping, which covers steam piping inside electric generating stations.", "C": "API 1104, which covers welding of cross-country pipelines and related facilities.", "D": "AWS D1.5, which covers welding of steel highway bridges and their connections."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (ASME B31.3, Process Piping, which covers chemical, petroleum and related plant piping systems.): B31.3 is the Process Piping code used for chemical and refinery plants. B31.1 covers power plant piping, and the other two documents cover pipelines and bridges."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A student in a school that offers AWS SENSE Level 1 asks the instructor what score is needed to pass a module knowledge exam outside of the safety content. The student wants to plan study time around the minimum requirement. What passing score does the instructor give for these module exams?", "options": {"A": "A score of 60 percent or higher on each module knowledge exam.", "B": "A score of 90 percent or higher on each module knowledge exam.", "C": "A score of 70 percent averaged across all modules taken that term.", "D": "A score of 75 percent or higher on each module knowledge exam."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A score of 75 percent or higher on each module knowledge exam.): SENSE module knowledge exams are passed at 75 percent. The safety exam carries a stricter requirement, which is why it is treated separately from the other modules."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A SENSE Level 1 student passed several module exams with high marks but earned 92 percent on the safety knowledge exam. The student believes the result is good enough because it is far above the 75 percent used elsewhere. What does the SENSE rule actually require for Level 1 safety?", "options": {"A": "A score of 90 percent is required, so the 92 percent result meets the standard.", "B": "A score of 75 percent is required, so the student has passed with room to spare.", "C": "A perfect score of 100 percent is required, so the safety exam must be retaken.", "D": "A score of 80 percent is required, so the student needs only a short review."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A perfect score of 100 percent is required, so the safety exam must be retaken.): Safety knowledge in Level 1 is held to 100 percent because one missed hazard can injure someone. A high score on other modules cannot offset a missed safety answer."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A school administrator is explaining the structure of the AWS SENSE program to parents at an open house. One parent asks which two written standards define the Entry Welder and the Advanced Welder levels of the program. Which pair of standards should the administrator name?", "options": {"A": "QC1 defines the Entry Welder level and QC2 defines the Advanced Welder level.", "B": "QC10 defines the Entry Welder level and QC11 defines the Advanced Welder level.", "C": "QC11 defines the Entry Welder level and QC10 defines the Advanced Welder level.", "D": "D1.1 defines the Entry Welder level and D1.5 defines the Advanced Welder level."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (QC10 defines the Entry Welder level and QC11 defines the Advanced Welder level.): SENSE Level 1 follows QC10 for the Entry Welder and Level 2 follows QC11 for the Advanced Welder. The D1 series are fabrication codes, not program standards."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder qualified in gas tungsten arc welding on a structural job in January. The welder then ran only shielded metal arc welding for the following seven months and is now assigned a GTAW task. According to the standard AWS and ASME qualification continuity rule, what must happen before the welder starts the task?", "options": {"A": "The welder may proceed, because qualification lasts twelve months from the original test date.", "B": "The welder must requalify, because more than six months passed without welding with that process.", "C": "The welder may proceed, because SMAW work keeps every other process qualification active.", "D": "The welder must requalify, because qualification expires after every project."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The welder must requalify, because more than six months passed without welding with that process.): AWS D1.1 and ASME Section IX both treat qualification as continuing only while the welder uses the process within six month intervals. Seven months without GTAW breaks the continuity, so a new test is needed."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder on a structural steel job decides the amperage on the WPS feels too low for a 3G vertical-up pass with E7018 and plans to run 20 amps hotter than the listed range. The inspector is nearby. Who has to approve a change outside the procedure's written parameters, and how?", "options": {"A": "A revised or newly qualified WPS must be approved before welding, not the welder's judgment.", "B": "The inspector may verbally approve the change on the spot because inspectors own all welding procedures.", "C": "The welder may adjust the amperage freely when the weld appears sound after final cleaning.", "D": "The foreman may approve any change that stays within 25 percent of the listed range."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A revised or newly qualified WPS must be approved before welding, not the welder's judgment.): A welder follows the WPS as written, and parameters outside its range require a revised WPS that is supported by qualification. Inspectors verify compliance with the procedure but do not waive it."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "On a warehouse project, the structural drawings show a fillet weld size of 5/16 inch at a beam connection, while the written specification says 1/4 inch for the same connection. The fabricator's detailer asks the welding supervisor how to proceed before cutting any steel. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Use the smaller size from the specification because it saves filler metal and fabrication time.", "B": "Use whichever size the welder finds easier to produce in the shop position available.", "C": "Submit a written request for clarification to the engineer and follow the contract's order of precedence.", "D": "Average the two sizes and weld to the midpoint to split the difference."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submit a written request for clarification to the engineer and follow the contract's order of precedence.): Conflicts between contract documents are resolved by the engineer under the contract's stated precedence, not by the fabricator guessing. Written clarification protects everyone if the decision is later questioned."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A refinery owner is reviewing a new process piping package under ASME B31.3. The contractor proposes to classify a line carrying a highly toxic fluid as normal fluid service to avoid extra examination. Under B31.3, who is responsible for designating the fluid service category for the piping?", "options": {"A": "The welding inspector designates the category based on what the weld looks like at final inspection.", "B": "The welder who fabricates the pipe designates the category when releasing the spool.", "C": "The owner designates the category, so the contractor cannot reclassify the line to cut examination.", "D": "The pipe manufacturer designates the category by stamping the grade on each length."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The owner designates the category, so the contractor cannot reclassify the line to cut examination.): B31.3 places the responsibility for fluid service categorization on the owner, who knows the process hazards. Categories such as Category M carry stricter requirements that a contractor may not relax."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A carbon steel process line in a chemical plant has a design pressure of 200 psig and operates at ambient temperature, so no temperature correction applies to the test. The owner's procedure calls for a hydrostatic leak test per ASME B31.3. What minimum hydrostatic test pressure applies?", "options": {"A": "220 psig, which is 1.1 times the design pressure of 200 psig.", "B": "300 psig, which is 1.5 times the design pressure of 200 psig.", "C": "250 psig, which is 1.25 times the design pressure of 200 psig.", "D": "400 psig, which is 2 times the design pressure of 200 psig."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (300 psig, which is 1.5 times the design pressure of 200 psig.): B31.3 sets the hydrostatic test at 1.5 times design pressure, adjusted for temperature when the test is not run at design temperature. Here 1.5 × 200 = 300 psig, and 1.1 is the factor for pneumatic tests."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A plant piping system with a design pressure of 150 psig cannot be filled with water because the supports were not designed for the weight. The owner approves a pneumatic leak test under ASME B31.3. What is the required minimum pneumatic test pressure?", "options": {"A": "225 psig, which is 1.5 times the design pressure of 150 psig.", "B": "150 psig, which equals the design pressure with no added margin.", "C": "187 psig, which is 1.25 times the design pressure of 150 psig.", "D": "165 psig, which is 1.1 times the design pressure of 150 psig."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (165 psig, which is 1.1 times the design pressure of 150 psig.): B31.3 requires a pneumatic test at 1.1 times design pressure, and 1.1 × 150 = 165 psig. The 1.5 factor belongs to hydrostatic testing, where stored energy is far lower."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A pipeline welder is taking a qualification test under API 1104 on a butt weld in pipe with a 0.375 inch wall. After the coupons are cut from the test joint, the lab will run nick-break tests and bend tests. Which bend tests apply to a wall of this thickness?", "options": {"A": "Side bend specimens only, because pipe welds are tested through the thickness.", "B": "Root bend and face bend specimens, because the wall is not thicker than 0.500 inch.", "C": "Fillet break specimens only, because pipe welds are tested the same way as T-joints.", "D": "Charpy impact specimens only, because API 1104 qualifies welders on toughness."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Root bend and face bend specimens, because the wall is not thicker than 0.500 inch.): API 1104 uses root and face bends for wall thicknesses up to 0.500 inch and side bends for thicker walls. Nick-break specimens are run in addition to the bends."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A steel bridge girder fabricator is welding a tension flange that the contract identifies as a fracture critical member under the bridge code. The welding foreman opens a fresh box of E7018 electrodes and sets it on the bench for the shift. What should the QC inspector do?", "options": {"A": "Stop work and require low hydrogen electrodes to be kept in a heated oven.", "B": "Allow the work because E7018 electrodes need protection only from rain and direct sunlight.", "C": "Allow the work because exposed electrodes can be redried at the end of the shift.", "D": "Allow the work because fracture critical status changes only how often welds are inspected."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop work and require low hydrogen electrodes to be kept in a heated oven.): Bridge welding requires hydrogen controlled electrodes to be kept dry in heated storage, since absorbed moisture adds hydrogen and raises cracking risk in critical tension members. Setting electrodes on a bench breaks that control."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "An employer's records show a welder last welded with the flux cored arc process five months and three weeks ago. The welder was qualified on that process at that time and is now needed on an FCAW task. Under the AWS six month continuity rule, what is the welder's status?", "options": {"A": "The welder must requalify, because any gap longer than three months ends qualification.", "B": "The welder must requalify, because qualification only lasts through the end of that calendar year.", "C": "The welder remains qualified, because the process was used within the six month period.", "D": "The welder remains qualified only if the supervisor signs a waiver for the gap."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The welder remains qualified, because the process was used within the six month period.): Qualification continues as long as the welder has not been away from the process for more than six months. Five months and three weeks is inside the limit, so no retest is needed."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor is starting a new job and needs paperwork to prove a procedure works. The engineer asks for the record that documents the actual test results from welding and testing a qualification coupon, not just the instructions the welders will follow. Which document does the contractor present?", "options": {"A": "The welding procedure specification, which lists only the ranges welders must follow in production.", "B": "The procedure qualification record, which lists the parameters used and the test results obtained.", "C": "The welder performance qualification record, which proves one welder's skill in a given position.", "D": "The material test report, which lists the chemistry of the base metal used."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The procedure qualification record, which lists the parameters used and the test results obtained.): A PQR documents what was actually done and the test results for a coupon, and it supports the WPS. The WPS is the instruction, and a welder record covers the person, not the procedure."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A SENSE Level 1 student scored 72 percent on a welding fundamentals module knowledge exam. The student asks the instructor whether the result counts as a pass because it is close to the mark. Based on the SENSE passing score, what does the instructor tell the student?", "options": {"A": "The exam is passed, because scores within five points of the mark are rounded up.", "B": "The exam is passed, because the module average matters more than a single score.", "C": "Not passed, because the required score is 75 percent, so a retake is needed.", "D": "The exam is not passed, because the required score is 90 percent for all modules."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not passed, because the required score is 75 percent, so a retake is needed.): The SENSE passing score for module knowledge exams is 75 percent, and 72 falls short. Scores are not rounded or averaged across modules to reach a pass."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A SENSE Level 1 student passes every module exam with at least 80 percent, but missed one question on the safety knowledge exam and scored 96 percent. The student asks whether the missed question matters. What is the correct response under SENSE rules?", "options": {"A": "It does not matter, because strong module scores can offset one missed safety question.", "B": "It does not matter, because 96 percent exceeds the 75 percent pass mark for knowledge exams.", "C": "It matters only if the missed question concerned fire prevention.", "D": "It matters, because Level 1 safety needs 100 percent, so the exam is retaken."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It matters, because Level 1 safety needs 100 percent, so the exam is retaken.): Safety content in Level 1 is held to a perfect score because a single misunderstood hazard can cause injury. Other module results cannot make up for a missed safety question."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A visual inspector examining a completed fillet weld on a structural steel connection finds a visible crack extending from the weld toe into the weld metal. The welder says the crack is short and the weld looks sound otherwise. How should the inspector disposition this weld under AWS D1.1 visual acceptance criteria?", "options": {"A": "Accept it, because a crack shorter than the weld size is allowed under static loading.", "B": "Accept it, because cracks near the toe are cosmetic and do not affect strength.", "C": "Hold it for engineering approval, because short cracks are acceptable with permission.", "D": "Reject it, because a crack of any size is unacceptable and must be removed and repaired."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reject it, because a crack of any size is unacceptable and must be removed and repaired.): D1.1 prohibits cracks of any size or location in visual acceptance. The inspector rejects the weld and the repair must remove the entire crack."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "During fabrication, a certified welding inspector sees that the root opening on a groove joint is wider than the WPS permits. The foreman asks the inspector to approve proceeding anyway to keep the schedule. What is the inspector's proper role?", "options": {"A": "Approve the variation, because inspectors hold final authority over every fit up tolerance on the job.", "B": "Report the nonconformance and require correction or engineer approval, because inspectors cannot waive limits.", "C": "Ignore the variation until final inspection, because root openings are the welder's concern.", "D": "Approve the variation if the welder promises to add extra passes to compensate."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the nonconformance and require correction or engineer approval, because inspectors cannot waive limits.): The inspector verifies conformance and reports deviations, but cannot waive requirements that belong to the code, the WPS or the engineer. Extra passes do not fix a variation outside the qualified range."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor is welding refinery process piping under ASME B31.3. The foreman wants to use welders who hold only a structural AWS D1.1 plate qualification from a prior job. Which document does B31.3 rely on for qualifying welders and procedures for this work?", "options": {"A": "AWS D1.1 alone, because plate qualification transfers to any pressure piping job.", "B": "API 1104 alone, because it governs welders on every pipe size and service.", "C": "ASME Section IX, Welding, Brazing, and Fusing Qualifications, with the additions B31.3 states.", "D": "AWS D17.1, because process piping falls under aerospace fusion welding rules."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (ASME Section IX, Welding, Brazing, and Fusing Qualifications, with the additions B31.3 states.): B31.3 directs welding qualifications to ASME Section IX with a few added requirements. A structural plate qualification from D1.1 does not satisfy it automatically."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A general contractor issues an addendum after bids are opened that changes the weld inspection note on several structural drawings. The fabricator's project manager asks whether the revised note is binding even though the original drawings were bid. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because an issued addendum becomes part of the contract documents and governs the revised work.", "B": "No, because only the drawings in hand at bid time can bind the fabricator.", "C": "No, because addenda are informational notes that do not change the contract.", "D": "Yes, but only after the welder signs a separate acknowledgment of the revision."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because an issued addendum becomes part of the contract documents and governs the revised work.): Addenda issued before award revise the bid documents and become part of the contract. The fabricator is responsible for pricing and following them."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A weld shop supervisor wants a record showing the heated oven temperature and the time low hydrogen electrodes were issued to welders on a bridge job, so an auditor can verify moisture control later. Which type of quality record keeping best satisfies this purpose?", "options": {"A": "A weekly summary of weld lengths completed by each crew on the project site.", "B": "A daily electrode control log showing oven temperature, issue times and returned electrodes.", "C": "A purchase order file listing the electrode brand and quantity bought.", "D": "A welder's personal notes about how each electrode felt while running."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A daily electrode control log showing oven temperature, issue times and returned electrodes.): Hydrogen control depends on documented storage and exposure times, so a daily log of oven temperature and issue times supports inspection. Purchase and production records do not show exposure."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A highway bridge contract specifies the Bridge Welding Code, but the fabricator's prequalified procedures were all written under AWS D1.1 for building work. The fabricator argues D1.1 is a recognized AWS code and offers to use those procedures to save time. How should the quality manager respond?", "options": {"A": "Accept, because any AWS structural code may be substituted at the fabricator's choice.", "B": "Accept, because prequalified procedures remain valid under every other AWS code.", "C": "Decline, because the contract names D1.5, so procedures must meet it unless the engineer approves.", "D": "Decline, because D1.1 procedures are banned from every steel job larger than ten tons of material."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline, because the contract names D1.5, so procedures must meet it unless the engineer approves.): The contract documents select the governing code, and substituting another code needs approval from the engineer or owner. Bridge work adds fatigue and fracture requirements that building procedures were not written to meet."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder is repairing a weld on a steel member and finds that the WPS lists E7018 but only E6010 is on the cart. The welder reasons that both are AWS electrodes for the same base metal and that skill matters more than classification. What is the best assessment?", "options": {"A": "Electrode classification is part of the procedure, so substituting E6010 breaks the WPS.", "B": "The substitution is fine, because welder skill outweighs the electrode listed in the procedure.", "C": "The substitution is fine, because both electrodes belong to the same AWS A5.1 family.", "D": "The substitution is fine, because only the amperage is controlled on a WPS."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Electrode classification is part of the procedure, so substituting E6010 breaks the WPS.): Filler metal classification is a controlled item in the WPS, and E6010 is a cellulosic electrode while E7018 is low hydrogen. Mixing them changes weld properties and hydrogen risk, so the repair must follow the written procedure."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A chemical plant owner has labeled a line as Category M fluid service because exposure to even a small amount of the fluid could cause serious, irreversible harm. The contractor wants to apply the minimum examination used on harmless utility lines to cut cost. Which reasoning best settles the dispute?", "options": {"A": "The contractor's bid assumptions control, because contractors know fabrication costs best.", "B": "The inspector's visual impression of the welds controls, since the inspector sees the work.", "C": "The pipe manufacturer's mill certificate controls, since it states the pressure rating.", "D": "The owner's category assignment controls, so Category M requirements apply to that line."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The owner's category assignment controls, so Category M requirements apply to that line.): B31.3 makes the owner responsible for assigning the fluid service category, and Category M triggers added requirements. A contractor cannot downgrade the category to reduce cost."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A plant crew can test a new piping system either hydrostatically with water or pneumatically with compressed air. The supports can carry the weight of water, and the owner has no schedule pressure. A planner proposes air because it avoids draining. Which reasoning supports the better decision?", "options": {"A": "Hydrostatic testing is preferred, because water stores far less energy if a joint fails.", "B": "Pneumatic testing is preferred, because compressed air finds leaks faster and with less risk.", "C": "Pneumatic testing is preferred, because B31.3 requires a higher test pressure for water.", "D": "Hydrostatic testing is preferred, because water raises the weld's strength during the test."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydrostatic testing is preferred, because water stores far less energy if a joint fails.): Compressed gas stores large amounts of energy, so a pneumatic failure can throw fragments, which is why hydrostatic testing is used when practical. Water is nearly incompressible and releases little energy if a joint fails."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder qualified under API 1104 on a field pipeline joint is invited to weld process piping in a refinery. The supervisor says the pipeline qualification should transfer since both jobs involve welding pipe. Which analysis correctly addresses the supervisor's assumption about qualification for this work?", "options": {"A": "The qualification transfers because API 1104 outranks every other piping code used in plants.", "B": "The qualification transfers because API 1104 outranks every piping code in plants.", "C": "The qualification is void because pipeline welders are barred from refinery work.", "D": "Refinery work follows B31.3 and ASME IX, so qualification must meet that code."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refinery work follows B31.3 and ASME IX, so qualification must meet that code.): Each code names its own qualification rules, and B31.3 relies on ASME Section IX. Moving between a pipeline and a plant means checking the governing document, not assuming the test transfers."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A school keeps SENSE exam records and an instructor suggests rounding every student's safety result up to the nearest five percent so more students reach Level 1 completion. A colleague objects. Which argument best explains why the objection is correct?", "options": {"A": "Rounding is fine, because a score near 95 percent already shows safe habits.", "B": "Safety must be a perfect score under SENSE, and altering results breaks program integrity.", "C": "Rounding is fine, because safety scores matter less than welding performance in the shop.", "D": "The objection is wrong, because instructors set their own passing marks for safety."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Safety must be a perfect score under SENSE, and altering results breaks program integrity.): SENSE sets Level 1 safety at 100 percent and the school must report actual results. Changing scores would misrepresent a student's knowledge of hazards that can cause injury."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "An engineer sends a drawing that cites a stricter weld acceptance note than the general code requires. The fabricator's supervisor claims the code always overrides drawing notes since code books outrank project paperwork. Which reasoning is correct for a contract governed by AWS D1.1?", "options": {"A": "The code book wins, so the stricter note can be ignored on this project.", "B": "Whichever is cheaper to meet wins, so the less strict requirement applies.", "C": "The note applies only if the welder agrees in writing to meet the tighter limit.", "D": "The contract documents may modify the code, so the stricter drawing note applies to this project."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The contract documents may modify the code, so the stricter drawing note applies to this project.): D1.1 applies as written unless the contract documents modify it, and an engineer may add requirements. The fabricator must meet the stricter criteria named in the contract."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A welder follows a WPS exactly, but the engineer later discovers the WPS itself contained an error in preheat for the steel grade used on the job. A manager wants to discipline the welder for the failure. Which analysis best assigns responsibility among the parties?", "options": {"A": "The welder is solely responsible, because welders must verify every procedure's accuracy before starting work.", "B": "The inspector is solely responsible, because inspectors write the procedures for the shop.", "C": "The contractor owns the WPS content, and the welder's duty was to follow it.", "D": "No one is responsible, because procedure errors are not covered by the code."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The contractor owns the WPS content, and the welder's duty was to follow it.): Contractors develop and qualify the WPS, and welders are responsible for following the one issued to them. Quality checks should also catch procedure errors, but blame does not fall on the welder for following the document."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A school's SENSE Assessment Coordinator is asked by an instructor to administer a module knowledge exam informally in the shop with notes available, to speed up completion before a holiday break. How should the coordinator respond to protect the program?", "options": {"A": "Decline, since the coordinator administers exams under AWS program rules and must keep testing standard.", "B": "Agree, because the coordinator can set open note rules for any module exam in the shop.", "C": "Agree, because the instructor holds final authority over how each exam is administered.", "D": "Decline, but only for safety, since other exams may be taken with notes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline, since the coordinator administers exams under AWS program rules and must keep testing standard.): The Assessment Coordinator is the school's designated person for running SENSE exams according to AWS rules. Standard conditions keep results meaningful for every school and student."}, {"id": 914, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A bridge fabricator's QC records show welder qualification dates, WPS numbers and PQR references, but the lot traceability of the base steel is missing for a fracture critical girder. An auditor flags the gap. Which reasoning shows why the missing record matters?", "options": {"A": "Traceability ties steel to its mill test report, so lost records leave properties unverified.", "B": "Traceability only matters for paint, so the missing record has no effect on the steel.", "C": "Traceability is optional documentation that only fabricators with time to spare keep.", "D": "Traceability matters only for the welder, who needs to know the steel's color."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Traceability ties steel to its mill test report, so lost records leave properties unverified.): Heat numbers link a piece of steel to its mill test report, which proves chemistry and strength. On a fracture critical girder, losing that link means the material cannot be shown to meet the contract."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A foreman wants to use one welder qualification test record for every project in the shop for years, reasoning that a passed test never expires. An auditor points to the shop's records showing no GMAW work by that welder for eight months. Which conclusion is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Qualification lapses after a gap over six months, so the welder must requalify.", "B": "Qualification does not lapse, so the welder may keep welding GMAW without any retest.", "C": "Qualification lapses only after twelve months of inactivity, so eight months is acceptable here.", "D": "Qualification lapses only when the welder changes employers, not through simple inactivity at work."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Qualification lapses after a gap over six months, so the welder must requalify.): AWS and ASME qualification continues only while the welder uses the process within six month intervals. Eight months without GMAW breaks that continuity and a new test is required."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A contractor is welding steam piping inside an electric generating station, and the foreman asks which ASME piping code governs the design and fabrication of that power plant piping. The previous job was a chemical plant governed by a different B31 section. Which code applies to the steam piping now?", "options": {"A": "ASME B31.3, Process Piping, which covers chemical and refinery plant piping systems.", "B": "ASME B31.1, Power Piping, which covers piping in electric generating stations.", "C": "API 1104, which covers welding of cross-country pipelines and related facilities.", "D": "AWS D1.5, which covers welding of steel highway bridges and their members."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (ASME B31.1, Power Piping, which covers piping in electric generating stations.): B31.1 is the Power Piping code for steam and related systems in generating stations. B31.3 is the process plant code the crew used on the previous job."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A QC inspector on a highway bridge job is asked by a new welder why extra controls apply to the bottom flange of a main girder marked as a fracture critical member. The welder wants a plain explanation of what that label means. What should the inspector say?", "options": {"A": "It is a compression member that carries only dead load, so controls are relaxed in fabrication.", "B": "It is any member painted with a special coating system, so controls cover coating only.", "C": "It is a tension member whose failure would probably collapse the bridge, so controls tighten.", "D": "It is a temporary member removed after erection, so controls apply during shipping."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is a tension member whose failure would probably collapse the bridge, so controls tighten.): Fracture critical members are steel tension members or tension components whose failure would probably cause collapse. That risk is why the bridge code adds material, welding and examination requirements."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "After a visual inspector accepts a pipe weld, a welder relaxes and says the inspector now carries all responsibility for the weld's quality. A coworker disagrees. Which reasoning best describes how responsibility is shared between welder and inspector in a code controlled job?", "options": {"A": "The inspector is responsible for weld quality, because acceptance transfers the responsibility entirely.", "B": "The welder must produce sound welds per the WPS, and inspection only verifies the result.", "C": "Responsibility ends for the welder once the weld is cleaned and handed to inspection staff.", "D": "The inspector shares the welder's work by correcting defects found during each inspection."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The welder must produce sound welds per the WPS, and inspection only verifies the result.): Inspection checks conformance after the fact, while workmanship and following the WPS stay with the welder and contractor. Acceptance by an inspector does not move the duty to build a sound weld."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabricator notices that a drawing and the specification disagree on a weld detail, but the schedule is tight, so the shop builds to the drawing without asking. The engineer later rejects the work as not matching the specification. Which analysis best explains who carries the cost of the rework?", "options": {"A": "The engineer carries it, because the drawings were issued with the conflict in them.", "B": "The welder carries it, because welders must read every specification line by line.", "C": "The fabricator carries it, because proceeding without written clarification put the conflict risk on the shop.", "D": "The owner carries it, because any rejected work is always a change order item."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The fabricator carries it, because proceeding without written clarification put the conflict risk on the shop.): Contract documents place the duty to resolve conflicts on the fabricator through a request for information before building. Choosing silently to follow one document puts the resulting rework risk on the shop."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "governing_documents_and_codes", "question": "A fabrication shop presents a welding procedure for a structural job that is not prequalified, but the file contains no qualification test record for it. The shop says the procedure has worked well on past jobs and should be accepted. Which reasoning best supports the inspector's response?", "options": {"A": "Past success is enough, because experience is accepted as proof of any procedure.", "B": "A verbal statement from the foreman is enough, because supervisors can certify procedures.", "C": "A procedure that is not prequalified needs a qualification record, so past success cannot replace testing.", "D": "The procedure is acceptable, because qualification records apply only to welder tests, not procedures."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A procedure that is not prequalified needs a qualification record, so past success cannot replace testing.): Under D1.1 a WPS that is not prequalified must be qualified by testing, with the results recorded in a PQR. Good production history does not stand in for the test data the code requires."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A shop supervisor hands a trainee the plate setup in the diagram, labeled with its AWS test position, for a SMAW groove test using E7018 electrode on 3/8 inch carbon steel. Before striking an arc, the trainee must pick the travel direction for the groove. Which travel direction is normally used for this test position?", "options": {"A": "Downhill, starting at the top so gravity pulls the molten slag ahead of the advancing puddle.", "B": "Uphill on the root pass, then downhill on the fill and cover passes to balance heat.", "C": "Horizontally from left to right, after tilting the plate flat for each separate pass.", "D": "Uphill, starting at the bottom and building each pass upward as a shelf of weld metal."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uphill, starting at the bottom and building each pass upward as a shelf of weld metal.): Low-hydrogen E7018 is run uphill in the vertical position because each solidified shelf supports the next puddle and gives full penetration. Downhill travel outruns the puddle and traps slag, so it fails the bend test.", "image": "question_images/q_921.png"}, {"id": 922, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A trainee clamps a plate overhead, as the diagram shows, for a Level 2 SMAW groove test and reaches for an electrode one size smaller than the one used on the flat plate. The instructor asks the reason for the change. Which statement best explains why a smaller electrode suits this plate position?", "options": {"A": "A smaller electrode raises the arc voltage enough to push molten metal upward against the plate.", "B": "A smaller electrode carries more current, which dries out the puddle before it can sag.", "C": "A smaller electrode burns its flux faster, leaving a thicker slag layer to hold the puddle.", "D": "A smaller electrode makes a smaller puddle that freezes fast, so surface tension holds it up."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A smaller electrode makes a smaller puddle that freezes fast, so surface tension holds it up.): In the overhead position gravity pulls the puddle away from the joint, so the welder wants the smallest, fastest freezing puddle that still fuses both plates. Current is lowered with the smaller diameter, not raised, and slag thickness does not carry the metal.", "image": "question_images/q_922.png"}, {"id": 923, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder passes a 3/8 inch SMAW groove test made with the plate standing vertical and the joint axis running horizontally, as the diagram shows, and the guided bends are accepted. The shop owner asks which groove welding positions this one test covers under AWS D1.1. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Flat, horizontal and vertical groove welds, because the plate itself stands upright.", "B": "Flat, horizontal and overhead groove welds, since both are reached by rotating the same plate.", "C": "Flat and horizontal groove welds, within the thickness range the test plate qualifies.", "D": "All four groove positions, because the horizontal test is the most demanding of the set."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Flat and horizontal groove welds, within the thickness range the test plate qualifies.): A horizontal groove test qualifies the flat and horizontal positions only. Vertical and overhead work each need a 3G or 4G test, so the upright plate orientation does not extend the range.", "image": "question_images/q_923.png"}, {"id": 924, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A candidate welds a 3/8 inch V-groove test plate in the position the diagram shows, with the plate lying flat and the welder working on top of it, and then passes the guided bend test. The supervisor asks what that single test permits on production work. Which statement is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Groove welds in flat and horizontal positions, since gravity has little effect on a thick plate.", "B": "Groove welds in every position, because the flat test is considered the hardest to pass.", "C": "Groove welds in the flat position only, within the qualified thickness range.", "D": "Groove welds in flat and overhead positions, but not in the horizontal position."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Groove welds in the flat position only, within the qualified thickness range.): The flat test is the easiest position and therefore qualifies only flat groove welds. Each harder position needs its own test, and a flat plate does not grant overhead.", "image": "question_images/q_924.png"}, {"id": 925, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a single-V groove with a labeled root opening and 30 degree bevels on each plate, as prepared for a 3/8 inch SMAW test. A steel backing strip is tack welded behind the joint root before welding begins. What is the main purpose of that backing strip?", "options": {"A": "It supports the root pass so full penetration is reached without burn-through.", "B": "It preheats the root of the joint so the plate does not need preheat before welding.", "C": "It adds alloy to the root bead so the joint matches the plate tensile strength.", "D": "It shields the root from air so shielding gas is not needed on the first pass."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It supports the root pass so full penetration is reached without burn-through.): Backing holds the molten root metal in place across the open gap, so the welder can fuse both bevels and the strip without dropping through. It does not heat the joint, add alloy by design, or act as a gas shield.", "image": "question_images/q_925.png"}, {"id": 926, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a V-groove with a 3 mm root opening on a 3/8 inch test plate that has no backing. The root is welded with GTAW and ER70S-2 filler, then bent in a guided root bend. Which weld root condition would cause this specimen to be rejected?", "options": {"A": "A slightly convex root bead that stays within the allowed reinforcement height on the back side.", "B": "Light heat tint on the root surface left behind by the argon shielding.", "C": "Incomplete fusion on a bevel face, showing as an open line along the bent root.", "D": "Fine, evenly spaced ripples across the root bead from the filler rod dipping rhythm."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incomplete fusion on a bevel face, showing as an open line along the bent root.): A root bend stretches the root surface, so unfused bevel faces open up and exceed the discontinuity limit. Heat tint, ripples and reasonable root reinforcement are cosmetic and do not open under bending.", "image": "question_images/q_926.png"}, {"id": 927, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a butt joint between two 3/8 inch plates fit for a SMAW test, with the labeled hi-lo mismatch at the joint. Under the AWS D1.1 alignment limit for butt joints, which action should the welder take before striking the first arc on this fit-up?", "options": {"A": "Re-fit the plates so the offset falls within the code limit, as this is too big.", "B": "Weld as fitted and grind the high side flush afterward, since bend testing hides misalignment.", "C": "Add an extra cover pass to bridge the offset and skip any further fit-up work.", "D": "Weld the root from the low plate only and accept the step left at the joint."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Re-fit the plates so the offset falls within the code limit, as this is too big.): The code limits offset to a fraction of the thinner plate thickness, and the offset shown is well beyond that. Grinding or burying the step later leaves a stress raiser and a test that can still be rejected.", "image": "question_images/q_927.png"}, {"id": 928, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a T-joint on 3/8 inch plate prepared for a fillet weld test in the 2F position with E7018 electrode. After the fillet is welded, the specimen is broken open in a fracture test. What must the fracture surface show for the test to be accepted?", "options": {"A": "Complete fusion at the root, with no cracks and porosity within the code limit.", "B": "A flat, convex face profile with no visible ripples on the finished cover surface.", "C": "Complete fusion at the toes only, since the root carries less stress in service.", "D": "Slag inclusions limited to the weld center, because the root is compressed in service."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Complete fusion at the root, with no cracks and porosity within the code limit.): A fillet break test exposes the inside of the weld, so the examiner looks for fusion into the root and absence of cracks. The outside profile is judged by visual inspection, not by the fracture.", "image": "question_images/q_928.png"}, {"id": 929, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a double-V groove joint prepared for 3/4 inch plate that will be welded from both sides with GMAW. After finishing the first side, the welder grinds and gouges the back of the joint before welding the second side. What is the purpose of gouging the back?", "options": {"A": "It preheats the back of the joint so the second side needs less interpass heating.", "B": "It widens the root opening so the second side can use a larger electrode diameter.", "C": "It removes the root area down to sound weld metal so the second side fuses fully.", "D": "It reduces the plate thickness so the guided bend jig can bend the specimen more easily."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It removes the root area down to sound weld metal so the second side fuses fully.): The root of the first-side weld can hold slag, lack of fusion and porosity. Cutting back to clean metal lets the second side fuse into it so the joint is truly complete.", "image": "question_images/q_929.png"}, {"id": 930, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The diagram shows a T-joint with the upright plate beveled at its edge, as set up for a groove weld test on 3/8 inch plate. A new student asks why the upright edge is beveled rather than cut square. Which reply is correct?", "options": {"A": "The bevel replaces preheat by reducing the thickness the arc must melt through.", "B": "The bevel is cosmetic and only controls the finished profile of the weld face.", "C": "The bevel opens access so the arc reaches the root and gives complete joint penetration.", "D": "The bevel lets the welder use less filler, which makes the joint stronger than the plate."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The bevel opens access so the arc reaches the root and gives complete joint penetration.): A square upright edge leaves the root out of reach, so a fillet-sized weld cannot fuse through the full thickness. Beveling creates the groove that allows complete penetration from one side.", "image": "question_images/q_930.png"}, {"id": 931, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A candidate passes a 3/8 inch fillet weld test in the overhead position the diagram shows, using E7018 on a T-joint, and the fracture test is accepted. The employer asks which plate fillet positions this single test covers under AWS D1.1. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Flat, horizontal and overhead fillet welds, within the qualified thickness range.", "B": "Overhead fillet welds only, since every position must be tested on its own.", "C": "Flat, horizontal and vertical fillet welds, because overhead is the hardest position.", "D": "All fillet and groove positions, since the overhead test is the most demanding one."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flat, horizontal and overhead fillet welds, within the qualified thickness range.): An overhead fillet test qualifies flat, horizontal and overhead fillets, but vertical work needs a 3F test. A fillet test does not grant any groove positions.", "image": "question_images/q_931.png"}, {"id": 932, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A candidate sets up the plate in the diagram for a vertical groove test and loads E70T-1 gas-shielded flux-cored wire, which is rated for flat and horizontal work. The instructor stops the test before any welding starts. Which statement describes the actual problem?", "options": {"A": "E70T-1 is self-shielded, so the gas supply must be turned off before the vertical test begins.", "B": "E70T-1 is rated for flat and horizontal work, so E71T-1 is the all-position wire.", "C": "E70T-1 has too low a tensile classification, so a 100 ksi wire is required.", "D": "E70T-1 can only run uphill, but the test position requires the downhill progression."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (E70T-1 is rated for flat and horizontal work, so E71T-1 is the all-position wire.): The T-1 wire with the 0 designator is meant for flat and horizontal use, and the 1 designator wire has a faster freezing slag for all positions. The tensile class is the same 70 ksi for both.", "image": "question_images/q_932.png"}, {"id": 933, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A fabrication shop schedules a plate test for a new hire who must weld overhead on a structural frame, and the instructor writes the AWS position code on the test sheet. Which designation identifies a groove weld test made with the plate overhead and the welder working beneath it?", "options": {"A": "4G, where the plate is overhead and the weld is deposited from beneath it.", "B": "3G, where the plate stands vertical and the weld is deposited while traveling uphill.", "C": "2G, where the plate stands vertical and the joint axis runs horizontally across it.", "D": "1G, where the plate lies flat and the weld is deposited from above it."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (4G, where the plate is overhead and the weld is deposited from beneath it.): AWS groove positions run 1G flat, 2G horizontal, 3G vertical and 4G overhead. Only 4G places the joint above the welder so the weld is made from underneath."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A guided bend specimen from a 3/8 inch plate test is examined by the weld shop inspector after bending around the plunger. According to the AWS D1.1 acceptance criteria for bend tests, what is the largest allowed size of any single discontinuity on the convex surface?", "options": {"A": "1/8 inch (3 mm), measured in any direction on the convex surface of the bend.", "B": "1/4 inch (6 mm), measured along the length of the weld on the convex surface.", "C": "1/16 inch (1.5 mm), measured across the width of the weld on the convex surface.", "D": "1/2 inch (13 mm) provided the cracks do not touch the edge of the specimen."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1/8 inch (3 mm), measured in any direction on the convex surface of the bend.): D1.1 allows no single discontinuity over 1/8 inch on the convex surface of a guided bend. The larger values are only close to the separate corner crack allowance, which does not apply to a single open defect."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A shop plans to qualify a welder with a single 3/8 inch groove test plate that is welded and bent successfully. The welder asks what range of base metal thickness that test covers for later production welds under AWS D1.1. Which range is correct?", "options": {"A": "Only 3/8 inch exactly, since thickness is treated as an essential variable.", "B": "From 3/16 inch up to 1 1/2 inches, which is four times the test thickness.", "C": "Unlimited thickness above 1/8 inch, because groove tests exceed the strength of any fillet.", "D": "From 1/8 inch up to 3/4 inch, which is twice the thickness of the test plate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (From 1/8 inch up to 3/4 inch, which is twice the thickness of the test plate.): A groove test in this range qualifies a minimum of 1/8 inch and a maximum of twice the test thickness. Unlimited thickness is only granted by tests of 1 inch plate or greater."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is stacking E7018 stringer beads in a 3/8 inch groove and notices dark slag still sitting in the toes of the previous pass. Production is waiting, but the welder must decide how to clean between passes. Which interpass cleaning practice is correct for this electrode?", "options": {"A": "Brush only the cover pass, because slag from root and fill passes burns off later.", "B": "Chip and wire brush the slag from every pass, especially the toes, before the next pass.", "C": "Wipe each pass with solvent so the following arc dissolves any leftover slag in the toes.", "D": "Grind only the stop and start points, since slag from the pass center lifts out."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chip and wire brush the slag from every pass, especially the toes, before the next pass.): Low-hydrogen slag is hard and glassy, and leftover slag at a toe becomes an inclusion in the next pass. Burning it off or hoping it floats out does not clear it, and solvent adds contamination."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A student welding the fill passes on a 3/8 inch plate with GMAW and ER70S-6 solid wire sees small glassy islands sitting on top of each bead and asks whether they matter. The instructor tells the student what to do before the next pass. What is the correct instruction?", "options": {"A": "Leave them in place, because the next arc remelts silicon islands into the weld.", "B": "Scrape them off only on the root pass, since fill passes run too hot.", "C": "Brush off the glassy silicon islands, since trapped islands can cause inclusions.", "D": "Cover them with a thicker pass, because solid wire produces residue that needs no cleaning."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Brush off the glassy silicon islands, since trapped islands can cause inclusions.): Solid wire leaves thin silicon-manganese islands that can be trapped under the next pass. A quick wire brushing removes them, and they are not harmless just because the wire has no flux core."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is qualifying in the vertical position with GMAW and ER70S-6 on 3/8 inch plate and needs a metal transfer mode that keeps the puddle small and controllable. The shop foreman asks which mode is normally chosen for this out-of-position test. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Spray transfer at high current, whose strong arc force presses the puddle against the plate.", "B": "Globular transfer, which delivers large droplets that gravity carries into the groove.", "C": "Pulsed spray only, since short-circuit transfer is limited to flat position welding.", "D": "Short-circuit transfer, whose lower heat input keeps the puddle small and easy to control."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short-circuit transfer, whose lower heat input keeps the puddle small and easy to control.): Short-circuit transfer runs cool and freezes the puddle quickly, which suits vertical and overhead work. Spray and globular transfer both create fluid puddles that sag when the plate is not flat."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A shop is welding 3/8 inch plate with E71T-1 gas-shielded flux-cored wire, and the welder asks the lead hand which shielding gas the wire is designed to run with. Both the supplier data sheet and AWS A5.20 classification apply. Which gas choice is correct?", "options": {"A": "100 percent argon, which gives the most stable arc on any carbon steel wire.", "B": "98 percent argon and 2 percent oxygen, which is the standard mix for flux-cored wire.", "C": "100 percent CO2, or a 75 percent argon and 25 percent CO2 mix, per the classification.", "D": "100 percent helium, which raises the heat input enough to melt the flux core."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (100 percent CO2, or a 75 percent argon and 25 percent CO2 mix, per the classification.): E71T-1 wire is classified for CO2 or the 75/25 mix, and the core is balanced to those gases. Pure argon gives an erratic arc and poor bead shape with this wire."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A candidate sets up a GTAW root pass on a 3/8 inch carbon steel plate and must choose the polarity at the machine. The welder is using a 3/32 inch ceriated tungsten and argon shielding. Which polarity setting is correct for this job?", "options": {"A": "DCEN, because most of the heat enters the plate while the tungsten stays cool.", "B": "DCEP, because the positive electrode gives a cleaning action that suits carbon steel.", "C": "AC with high frequency, because carbon steel needs the oxide cleaning of both half cycles.", "D": "DCEP, because the extra heat at the tungsten melts the root bead more deeply."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (DCEN, because most of the heat enters the plate while the tungsten stays cool.): On steel, DCEN puts about two thirds of the arc heat into the work and protects the tungsten from overheating. DCEP would overheat and ball the tungsten, which is why it is reserved for very thin special cases."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is running stringer beads on a 3/8 inch groove plate clamped with the joint axis horizontal and sees the lower bead sagging onto the bottom plate. The coach recommends a change in electrode work angle. Which angle adjustment helps hold the puddle in this position?", "options": {"A": "Point the electrode slightly downward, so slag washes ahead of the puddle onto the lower plate.", "B": "Hold the electrode square to the plate, so the bead stays symmetric on both bevel faces.", "C": "Point the electrode slightly upward, so arc force helps support the puddle against gravity.", "D": "Use a wide weave across the groove, so the puddle wets the lower plate fully."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Point the electrode slightly upward, so arc force helps support the puddle against gravity.): Aiming the arc a little upward uses arc force to push the puddle toward the upper plate and keep it from drooping. A downward angle or wide weave adds fluid metal that sags."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder fits a 3/8 inch V-groove test plate with backing, but the root opening was left much too tight, almost closed at the far end. The root pass is run with E7018 at normal settings. Which defect is most likely to appear in this joint?", "options": {"A": "Burn-through at the root, because the narrow gap concentrates too much heat on the backing.", "B": "Excessive root convexity inside the groove, because the narrow gap takes too little filler metal.", "C": "Incomplete root penetration or fusion, because the arc cannot reach the root through the narrow gap.", "D": "Porosity throughout the root bead, because the narrow gap holds moisture from the atmosphere."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Incomplete root penetration or fusion, because the arc cannot reach the root through the narrow gap.): A tight root opening blocks arc access to the bottom of the groove so the bevel faces and backing do not fuse. Burn-through occurs with an excessive opening, not a closed one."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder who has passed both a 3G and a 4G SMAW groove test on 3/8 inch plate asks the weld shop supervisor which plate groove positions the pair of tests covers under AWS D1.1. The supervisor checks the position table. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Flat, horizontal and vertical groove welds only, since the 4G test adds no further range.", "B": "All groove positions on plate, which are flat, horizontal, vertical and overhead.", "C": "Overhead and vertical groove welds only, since flat and horizontal are not covered by hard tests.", "D": "All plate and pipe positions in every diameter, since two positions cover every joint."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (All groove positions on plate, which are flat, horizontal, vertical and overhead.): Passing both 3G and 4G qualifies every plate groove position. The pair does not cover every pipe diameter or the pipe positions, which are tested separately."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is preparing a face bend specimen from a welded 3/8 inch plate and the inspector wants to confirm how the specimen is placed in the jig. In a face bend, where does the weld face sit during bending?", "options": {"A": "On the inside of the bend, compressed against the die in the jig.", "B": "On the neutral axis of the specimen, seeing neither tension nor compression.", "C": "On the side that touches the plunger, where compressive stress is the greatest.", "D": "On the outside of the bend, stretched over the plunger radius in tension."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (On the outside of the bend, stretched over the plunger radius in tension.): In a face bend the weld face is on the convex side, where the metal is stretched and discontinuities open up. A root bend does the opposite and stretches the root surface."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is about to run a 3/8 inch A36 plate test with E7018 in an unheated shop, and the plate thermometer reads 20 degrees F. The inspector asks what should be done before welding. Which action is correct under AWS D1.1 preheat rules?", "options": {"A": "Weld at once, since 3/8 inch plate needs preheat only once it exceeds 3/4 inch thickness.", "B": "Warm the plate to at least 70 degrees F, as the base metal is freezing.", "C": "Warm the plate to 250 degrees F, the usual minimum preheat for A36 in all thicknesses.", "D": "Warm the plate to 400 degrees F, because cold plate behaves like high carbon steel."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Warm the plate to at least 70 degrees F, as the base metal is freezing.): When the base metal is below 32 degrees F it must be warmed to at least 70 degrees F before welding. A high preheat for thin A36 is unnecessary and can damage the plate properties."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder opens a sealed can of E7018 electrodes at the start of the shift and wants to protect the coating from moisture while working on a 3/8 inch plate test. The lead hand says the electrodes must be stored a certain way once the can is open. What is correct?", "options": {"A": "Leave them in an open pouch on the bench, since the coating tolerates shop humidity.", "B": "Keep them in a refrigerator below 40 degrees F, so moisture condenses away from the flux.", "C": "Keep them in a holding oven at 250 degrees F or higher until they are needed.", "D": "Wrap them in a damp rag, so the coating stays flexible and does not crack."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Keep them in a holding oven at 250 degrees F or higher until they are needed.): Low-hydrogen coatings absorb moisture, which introduces hydrogen and risk of cracking, so open electrodes belong in a heated holding oven. Cooling or dampening them makes the problem worse."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder running GMAW on a flat 3/8 inch plate test sees surface porosity on the cover pass, and the instructor notices a shop fan blowing across the weld station. The gas cylinder, regulator and nozzle all look normal. What is the most likely cause of the porosity?", "options": {"A": "The fan is blowing the shielding gas off the puddle, so the airflow should be blocked.", "B": "The amperage is too low to melt the flux, so the fan hides the real cause.", "C": "The electrode stick-out is too short and traps shielding gas inside the finished weld.", "D": "The plate is too cool, so hydrogen escapes too slowly from the solidifying weld."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The fan is blowing the shielding gas off the puddle, so the airflow should be blocked.): Moving air strips the shielding gas away from the molten puddle and lets nitrogen and oxygen in, causing porosity. Solid wire has no flux to melt, and a short stick-out does not trap gas."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder passes a 3G SMAW groove test on 3/8 inch plate and the guided bends are accepted. The employer then assigns an overhead groove weld on a structural frame and says the welder is already qualified. What is the correct response from the welding supervisor?", "options": {"A": "The 3G test covers every position, because vertical is harder than overhead for low-hydrogen electrodes.", "B": "The 3G test covers overhead groove welds only if the welder keeps electrode size the same.", "C": "A vertical test lacks overhead coverage, so a 4G test or 3G plus 4G is needed.", "D": "The 3G test covers overhead after one added visual inspection by a certified inspector."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A vertical test lacks overhead coverage, so a 4G test or 3G plus 4G is needed.): Vertical qualification grants flat, horizontal and vertical groove positions but not overhead. The overhead position needs its own test, and a 3G and 4G pair together grant all positions."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "An inspector examines a bend specimen after bending and finds three open discontinuities on the convex surface that measure 3/32 inch, 1/16 inch and 1/16 inch. None is a corner crack. Under the AWS D1.1 bend test criteria, how should the specimen be ruled?", "options": {"A": "Accept, since each is under 1/8 inch and the 7/32 inch total is under 3/8 inch.", "B": "Reject, since the three discontinuities together exceed the single discontinuity limit of 1/8 inch.", "C": "Reject, because every open discontinuity on a guided bend is disqualifying whatever its size.", "D": "Retest the root bend only, since the face bend criteria allow one open discontinuity."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accept, since each is under 1/8 inch and the 7/32 inch total is under 3/8 inch.): The single limit is 1/8 inch and the sum of discontinuities between 1/32 and 1/8 inch may reach 3/8 inch. Adding 3/32, 2/32 and 2/32 gives 7/32 inch, so the specimen passes."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder passes a 2G groove test with E7018 on 1/4 inch plate, and the shop later asks the same welder to weld 5/8 inch plate in the horizontal position. The supervisor must decide whether the earlier test covers this job. What is the correct decision?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because any groove test over 1/8 inch qualifies every plate thickness in the same position.", "B": "No, a 1/4 inch test covers to 1/2 inch, so 5/8 inch needs a thicker plate.", "C": "Yes, because the test position matters more than thickness when qualifying a welder.", "D": "No, because a 1/4 inch test covers only 1/4 inch plate and nothing thicker or thinner."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, a 1/4 inch test covers to 1/2 inch, so 5/8 inch needs a thicker plate.): A test of this thickness qualifies from 1/8 inch up to twice the tested thickness, which is 1/2 inch here. Plate of 5/8 inch exceeds that maximum and needs a thicker test plate."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder running 3G uphill with E7018 on 3/8 inch plate weaves quickly across the groove without pausing at the edges. The root and bend specimens fail with lack of fusion on one sidewall under a convex bead. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Using too large a root opening, which lets the bead sink away from the sidewall.", "B": "Weaving without a pause at the groove edges, which lets the puddle roll over unmelted sidewall.", "C": "Running the uphill progression, since downhill progression gives better sidewall fusion.", "D": "Travel angle too steep, which forces slag into the toes and prevents fusion on both bevels."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weaving without a pause at the groove edges, which lets the puddle roll over unmelted sidewall.): The convex bead and sidewall lack of fusion show metal piling in the center while the edges do not melt. A short pause at each toe lets the arc wet the sidewall before moving across."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "While welding an overhead groove on 3/8 inch plate, a welder at high amperage and slow travel sees the weld sag in the center and drip along the lower edge. The same electrode gave a clean bead when welded flat. What is the best correction?", "options": {"A": "Raise the amperage further, so the hotter arc burns off the sag as it forms.", "B": "Switch to a larger electrode, so the wider puddle spreads and holds across both plates.", "C": "Hold a longer arc, so the extra voltage pushes the puddle upward against the plate.", "D": "Cut the heat input by raising travel speed and lowering amperage, so the puddle freezes sooner."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cut the heat input by raising travel speed and lowering amperage, so the puddle freezes sooner.): Sagging is a sign that the puddle is too fluid and heavy for gravity. Lowering the heat input and keeping a short arc freezes the metal faster, while a larger electrode or longer arc makes the puddle worse."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder qualified with uphill progression in the vertical position on 3/8 inch plate is assigned to weld the same joint with downhill progression to save time. The supervisor must determine whether the existing qualification covers this change. What is the correct ruling?", "options": {"A": "It does, because both progressions use the same plate position and the same electrode class.", "B": "It does, because downhill travel is an easier technique than uphill travel for the same joint.", "C": "It does, if the welder keeps the same amperage range used in the uphill test.", "D": "It does not, because downhill vertical progression is a separate qualification requiring its own test."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It does not, because downhill vertical progression is a separate qualification requiring its own test.): Vertical progression direction is an essential variable, and a change from uphill to downhill needs a new test. Downhill welding is a different technique that is not covered by the uphill qualification."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A crew must weld 3/8 inch plate outdoors in gusty wind, and the foreman wants a flux-cored wire that does not depend on a gas cylinder. A welder suggests E71T-1 because it was used in the test shop. Which choice best fits the field conditions?", "options": {"A": "The same E71T-1 wire with a higher gas flow, since extra gas beats wind.", "B": "A solid wire with 100 percent argon, since argon is heavier than air and settles.", "C": "A gas-shielded wire inside a tent, since tents cannot affect the quality of the weld.", "D": "A self-shielded flux-cored wire, since its core makes shielding and tolerates moderate wind."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A self-shielded flux-cored wire, since its core makes shielding and tolerates moderate wind.): Self-shielded flux-cored wire makes its own gas and slag from the core, so a breeze does not strip the shielding. Extra gas flow on a gas-shielded wire causes turbulence that pulls air into the puddle."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder passes a 3/8 inch V-groove test made with a steel backing strip, and the employer then assigns an open-root groove weld on a vessel without any backing. The foreman says the earlier bend test already covers it. Which ruling is correct under AWS D1.1?", "options": {"A": "Yes, since backing only adds metal and a welder who can use it can weld without.", "B": "No, a test with backing does not qualify open-root welds, so a new test is needed.", "C": "Yes, since the thickness and position of the test match the vessel joint exactly.", "D": "No, but only the root bend needs to be repeated and the face bend can stand."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, a test with backing does not qualify open-root welds, so a new test is needed.): Backing makes the root pass far easier, so a test using it qualifies only welds that use backing. A test without backing is the harder one and is accepted for both conditions."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder using E7018 on a 3/8 inch plate notices the arc wandering and the bead deflecting toward one end near the work lead clamp, and no electrode change helps. The welder asks how to fix the problem. Which action best addresses the cause?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a smaller electrode, since arc blow disappears at every current below 90 amps.", "B": "Raise the amperage, so the stronger arc overpowers the magnetic field pulling it away.", "C": "Increase the root opening, so the arc has more room to correct itself during welding.", "D": "Move the work lead to the opposite end of the plate to change the magnetic field."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Move the work lead to the opposite end of the plate to change the magnetic field.): Arc blow is caused by magnetic fields from the welding current in DC welding, and changing the ground location changes that field. Current level alone does not remove it, and joint geometry has little effect."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "While running a GTAW root pass on a 3/8 inch plate, a welder accidentally dips the hot tungsten into the puddle and the arc becomes erratic with a ball on the tip. The welder wants to keep welding to finish the pass. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Continue welding, since a ball tip gives a more stable arc than a ground point.", "B": "Raise the amperage, so the contaminated tip burns clean in the arc during the pass.", "C": "Stop, regrind the tungsten to a clean point, and remove contaminated weld metal first.", "D": "Wipe the tip with a rag while hot, so the contamination wipes off without regrinding."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop, regrind the tungsten to a clean point, and remove contaminated weld metal first.): A contaminated tungsten gives an unstable arc and risks tungsten inclusions in the root. The tip must be reground and the affected weld metal removed, since burning it off will not clean it."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder tacks two 3/8 inch plates flat with a parallel joint, and after welding the first side the plates pull up into an angle. The instructor asks how to prevent this on the next test. Which fit-up change best controls angular distortion?", "options": {"A": "Weld both sides of the plate at the same time, since distortion cannot form during welding.", "B": "Preset the plates with a slight angle against the expected pull so they finish flat.", "C": "Clamp the plates only at the ends, so the joint middle is free to shrink.", "D": "Use a larger root opening, since a wider gap removes the shrinkage force on the joint."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Preset the plates with a slight angle against the expected pull so they finish flat.): Weld metal shrinks as it cools and pulls the plates toward the weld side. Presetting the plates away from that direction lets the shrinkage bring them back to flat."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder running GMAW in the flat position on 3/8 inch plate leaves a groove cut into the base metal along both toes of the cover pass. The weld looks narrow and ropey. Which change in technique is most likely to fix this problem?", "options": {"A": "Raise travel speed, so there is less time for the arc to dig into the toes.", "B": "Increase the arc length, so the wider arc covers more of the toe area evenly.", "C": "Hold the gun steady in the center, so the puddle stays away from the toes.", "D": "Slow the travel speed and shorten the arc, so the puddle fills the toes and wets."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Slow the travel speed and shorten the arc, so the puddle fills the toes and wets.): Undercut occurs when the arc melts the edge faster than the puddle can fill it. Slowing down slightly and shortening the arc gives the metal time to flow into the toes."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder fails a 3/8 inch plate bend test under AWS D1.1 and the employer wants the welder retested the same day. The welder asks about the retest provisions for a failed test. Which statement describes the immediate retest option?", "options": {"A": "One retest may be made after a wait of 30 days, and it must pass.", "B": "Three further tests may be made and any one passing result is accepted.", "C": "Two consecutive test welds of the same type may be made, and both must pass.", "D": "No retest is allowed until the welder completes a full course of formal training."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two consecutive test welds of the same type may be made, and both must pass.): The immediate retest rule asks for two consecutive passing test welds of the type that failed. Otherwise the welder may retest after further training or practice as the code describes."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 student prepares a 6 inch schedule 40 carbon steel pipe for a root-and-fill test in a shop with a rotating stand locked in place. The diagram shows the pipe fixed with its welding position labeled. Which statement best describes how the joint is encountered as the welder travels around the pipe?", "options": {"A": "Only the flat position is met because the pipe stays still during welding", "B": "Flat, vertical and overhead segments are all met around the fixed circumference", "C": "The welder stays in the horizontal position the whole way because the pipe axis stays level", "D": "Flat and horizontal segments alternate every quarter turn while the overhead segment is skipped by design"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flat, vertical and overhead segments are all met around the fixed circumference): On a horizontal pipe that cannot be rotated, the joint passes through flat at the top, vertical at the sides and overhead at the bottom. The welder keeps adjusting electrode angle and travel speed as gravity acts on the puddle differently at each point.", "image": "question_images/q_961.png"}, {"id": 962, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A shop wants one coupon that lets a trainee cover every position on pipe groove welds. The instructor draws the pipe fixed at an incline, as the diagram shows, and asks what a successful test would cover. Which conclusion about qualification is correct under typical AWS practice for a pipe test made at this position?", "options": {"A": "A passing test here generally qualifies groove welds in all positions on pipe and plate", "B": "It qualifies only flat and horizontal groove welds because the incline removes any overhead segment", "C": "It qualifies pipe groove welds only and gives no credit toward any plate position", "D": "It qualifies vertical and overhead welds only since no segment is truly flat"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A passing test here generally qualifies groove welds in all positions on pipe and plate): A pipe fixed at about 45 degrees makes the weld pass through every orientation, so passing it covers the full range of positions. Restricting credit to flat and horizontal ignores the overhead portion the welder actually had to weld.", "image": "question_images/q_962.png"}, {"id": 963, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 trainee runs a multi-pass V-groove on 3/8 inch plate. The plate stands upright and the weld axis runs left to right, as the diagram indicates. The first stringer sags toward the lower plate edge. Which adjustment to bead placement and electrode work angle gives the best result?", "options": {"A": "Use one wide weave per layer and tilt the electrode downward so gravity helps", "B": "Stack separate stringer beads from the bottom up and aim the electrode slightly upward", "C": "Raise amperage sharply and lengthen the arc so the puddle stays fluid and levels itself out", "D": "Run beads from the top down and point the electrode straight into the joint"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stack separate stringer beads from the bottom up and aim the electrode slightly upward): In the horizontal groove position, stringer beads placed from the bottom up give each bead a shelf to rest on. A slight upward work angle lets the arc force hold the molten metal against gravity. Wide weaves and hotter puddles make sagging worse.", "image": "question_images/q_963.png"}, {"id": 964, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "During a fixed horizontal pipe test, a welder using a keyhole technique on the root finds the root slightly concave at the bottom of the pipe but flush elsewhere. The diagram shows the pipe orientation. Which explanation and correction best fit what gravity does to the puddle at that location?", "options": {"A": "Gravity pulls the molten root outward at the bottom, so trim heat and hold steady travel", "B": "Gravity lifts the puddle at the bottom, so slow down and raise the current", "C": "The concavity comes from the preheat, so remove the preheat and restart the whole pass", "D": "Gravity acts at the top of the pipe, so tack weld size explains the bottom concavity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gravity pulls the molten root outward at the bottom, so trim heat and hold steady travel): At the bottom of a horizontal fixed pipe the welder works overhead, where gravity pulls a hot, slow puddle downward and outward away from the root, leaving a concave root. Trimming heat and holding steady travel keeps the puddle small enough for surface tension to hold it. More current only deepens the sag.", "image": "question_images/q_964.png"}, {"id": 965, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "Two trainees compare a fixed horizontal pipe test with a fixed pipe test at an inclined axis, like the one in the diagram. One says the inclined test is harder only because the setup is bulkier. Which statement correctly explains why the inclined test is usually considered the tougher of the two for the welder?", "options": {"A": "The incline adds horizontal welding at the sides, so angles keep changing", "B": "The incline removes the vertical portion, leaving only flat and overhead welding", "C": "Pipe weight changes the heat balance, so the incline needs a higher preheat in every case", "D": "The incline slows puddle freezing time, so every pass needs a larger electrode diameter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The incline adds horizontal welding at the sides, so angles keep changing): Weight has nothing to do with the difficulty; the geometry does. A 45 degree axis blends horizontal welding into the vertical and overhead segments, so electrode angle, travel angle and puddle control keep changing. The incline does not remove the vertical portion.", "image": "question_images/q_965.png"}, {"id": 966, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A new student reads a welding procedure that lists the test position as 2G and looks at the diagram of the coupon. The student is asked about the designation itself, since AWS position codes pair a number with a letter. What does the letter G carry as its meaning in that code?", "options": {"A": "G stands for gravity-fed, meaning the electrode feeds downward into a flat joint", "B": "G stands for gas shielded, meaning only gas-shielded processes may be used on the coupon", "C": "G stands for ground clamp, meaning the work lead sits at the lower plate edge", "D": "G stands for groove weld, set apart from a fillet weld designated with an F"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (G stands for groove weld, set apart from a fillet weld designated with an F): AWS position designations pair a number for orientation with a letter for the weld type. G marks a groove weld and F marks a fillet weld. The letter says nothing about the shielding method or work lead placement.", "image": "question_images/q_966.png"}, {"id": 967, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 drawing shows a single V-groove butt joint prepared for an open-root weld, with a gap labeled in millimeters between the plates. The joint will be welded from one side only, with no backing strip. What is the main purpose of that gap at the bottom of the groove?", "options": {"A": "It gives the filler metal a place to collect so fewer passes are required overall", "B": "It reduces shrinkage stress by allowing the plates to move freely during every pass", "C": "It guarantees the shielding gas can leave the joint before the cap pass is started", "D": "It lets the arc reach the bottom of the joint so both root faces fuse fully"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It lets the arc reach the bottom of the joint so both root faces fuse fully): With a one-sided open-root joint, the gap lets the arc and puddle penetrate through the thickness and fuse both root faces. A gap does not reduce the number of passes and does not by itself relieve shrinkage stress.", "image": "question_images/q_967.png"}, {"id": 968, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The drawing shows a V-groove butt joint with the root opening and bevel angle labeled on each plate. A fitter proposes doubling the root opening to make the first pass easier, with the plate thickness unchanged. What is the most likely consequence of that change for the finished weld?", "options": {"A": "More filler metal, more heat input and more distortion are needed to fill the wider joint", "B": "Less filler metal is needed because the larger gap lets each pass sink deeper", "C": "Shrinkage stops because the plates no longer touch each other along the joint", "D": "The root pass no longer needs a keyhole since the gap is wider than a keyhole"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (More filler metal, more heat input and more distortion are needed to fill the wider joint): A bigger gap adds volume to fill, so the welder uses more filler and more passes, which puts more heat into the plate and raises distortion. It does not stop shrinkage, and the welder still has to control penetration at the root.", "image": "question_images/q_968.png"}, {"id": 969, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A drawing calls for the root opening shown, but the fitter's gauge reads only about half of that value along the length of the joint. The welder runs the root pass at the normal travel speed and amperage for the shown opening. Which defect is most likely to appear at the root?", "options": {"A": "Excessive root reinforcement inside the bore, because the gap is too wide for the amperage", "B": "Incomplete penetration, because the narrow gap keeps the arc from reaching the root", "C": "Crater cracking along the root, because tight joints raise the carbon content of the weld", "D": "Porosity throughout the root, because the gap traps shielding gas against the plates"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Incomplete penetration, because the narrow gap keeps the arc from reaching the root): A root opening tighter than the drawing calls for leaves less room for the arc to melt through, so the root faces may not fuse fully. Excess reinforcement is a wide-gap symptom, so it points the wrong way here.", "image": "question_images/q_969.png"}, {"id": 970, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A pipe shop changes a joint preparation from a 30 degree bevel per plate to the 37.5 degree bevel shown on the drawing, keeping the same wall thickness and root opening. The welding supervisor is estimating filler and time for each joint. Which effect should the supervisor expect when filling the groove?", "options": {"A": "A larger groove volume that calls for more filler metal and usually extra fill passes", "B": "A smaller groove volume that cuts the total filler use and weld time noticeably", "C": "No change in filler use because only root opening and thickness control the groove volume", "D": "A lower chance of sidewall fusion problems since the groove walls are steeper"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A larger groove volume that calls for more filler metal and usually extra fill passes): A bigger bevel angle widens the groove, so more metal is needed to fill it. The walls are less steep, not steeper, so the wider groove does not by itself prevent lack of sidewall fusion.", "image": "question_images/q_970.png"}, {"id": 971, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "On the open-root V-groove in the drawing, a welder sees a small round hole form at the leading edge of the puddle and holds it steady while advancing. Another trainee asks what would happen if that hole were allowed to close up completely partway along the joint. What is the most likely result?", "options": {"A": "The root becomes stronger because extra metal fills the gap and fuses both faces fully", "B": "The bore turns oxidized because the shielding gas can no longer escape through the gap", "C": "Penetration is lost and the root may be only partly fused, leaving a defect", "D": "Heat input drops so the heat-affected zone disappears along the entire root length"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Penetration is lost and the root may be only partly fused, leaving a defect): The keyhole is evidence that the arc is melting through both root faces. When it closes, the puddle bridges the gap without full fusion underneath. Closing it does not strengthen the weld or change shielding conditions.", "image": "question_images/q_971.png"}, {"id": 972, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "After the root pass on the V-groove in the drawing, a welder cleans the bead and runs a second pass at somewhat higher current on the same joint, a 3/8 inch wall carbon steel pipe. Which purpose does this pass serve before the remaining fill layers are added?", "options": {"A": "It replaces the cap pass by providing the final crown width and surface appearance", "B": "It cools the joint quickly so the root bead can shrink back to the root opening", "C": "It fuses into the root bead, burns out slag pockets and fills edge undercut", "D": "It enlarges the keyhole so the later passes reach deeper into the plate thickness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It fuses into the root bead, burns out slag pockets and fills edge undercut): The hot pass follows the root while it is still warm and uses more heat to wash out slag in the corners and bridge undercut on the groove edges. It is not the cap and does not widen the keyhole.", "image": "question_images/q_972.png"}, {"id": 973, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A trainee in a Level 2 pipe class hears the instructor say the root pass of a groove weld made from one side is the most critical bead in the joint. In pipe welding terminology, which description best defines that root pass?", "options": {"A": "The final layer laid over the groove to give the weld its crown and finished appearance", "B": "A second pass that reheats the previous bead to relieve residual stress in the pipe", "C": "A light seal bead run across the outside of the joint before fit-up begins", "D": "The first bead deposited in the bottom of the joint, fusing both root faces together"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The first bead deposited in the bottom of the joint, fusing both root faces together): The root pass is the first layer placed at the base of the groove, where it joins the two root faces. The final layer is the cap, and a pass that adds heat after the root is the hot pass."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A pipe fitter tells a trainee to leave a short flat surface at the bottom of each bevel before tacking, instead of cutting the bevel all the way to a sharp edge. What name does AWS practice give this unbeveled surface at the root of the pipe end?", "options": {"A": "The crown, which controls the final height of the finished cap pass over the groove", "B": "The toe, which marks the line where the weld metal meets the pipe wall surface", "C": "The throat, which sets the effective size of the finished weld from root to face", "D": "The root face, often called the land, which keeps the thin edge from burning away"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The root face, often called the land, which keeps the thin edge from burning away): The land or root face is the unbeveled part left at the bottom of the prep. It gives the root pass something to melt into and resists burn-through. The crown, toe and throat all describe features of the finished weld."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A fitter prepares two 4 inch pipe ends with a 37.5 degree bevel and a small land, then sets a gap before tacking. During the test the welder burns through at the root in several spots along the joint. Which preparation change is most likely to help?", "options": {"A": "Remove the land entirely so the arc reaches the root edges with less heat", "B": "Increase the bevel angle again so the groove walls take more heat from the arc", "C": "Increase the land slightly or reduce the gap so the root edges hold under the arc", "D": "Open the gap much wider so the molten metal has more space to fall through"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increase the land slightly or reduce the gap so the root edges hold under the arc): Burn-through means the root edges are too thin or too far apart to support the puddle. A slightly larger land or tighter gap gives the arc more metal to fuse. Removing the land or widening the gap would worsen the problem."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 instructor demonstrates tacking a pipe joint before the root pass, using four small tacks around the circumference of a 6 inch pipe. Which feature should a good tack weld have so the root pass can incorporate it easily?", "options": {"A": "A tall rounded crown that stands proud so the root pass can build on it", "B": "Feathered ends that taper smoothly so the root bead can fuse across each tack", "C": "A deep notch at each end so the root pass reaches the full wall thickness", "D": "A coating of anti-spatter gel so the tack does not bond to the root bead later"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Feathered ends that taper smoothly so the root bead can fuse across each tack): Tacks that are ground or built with tapered ends let the welder run the root straight through without a lump that causes lack of fusion. A tall crown or a notch creates the very discontinuity the welder is trying to avoid."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder tacks a 6 inch pipe joint with four tacks of very different sizes, one placed exactly where the root pass will start. The start of the root shows poor fusion at that point. What is the best corrective practice for the next joint in the same assignment?", "options": {"A": "Make every tack as large as possible and begin each pass directly on top of one", "B": "Place a single tack at the start point so the arc strikes on solid metal", "C": "Skip tack welds entirely and hold the pipe by hand while the root is deposited", "D": "Keep tacks small and evenly spaced, and start the root on a ground, feathered area"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep tacks small and evenly spaced, and start the root on a ground, feathered area): Small, feathered, evenly spaced tacks hold alignment without leaving bulky spots that cause cold starts. Starting the pass on a lump of cooled metal invites poor fusion. Hand holding gives no consistent root opening."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A trainee tacks a 4 inch pipe joint with four short tacks, one at each quarter, and finds that the gap closes up at the last tack while the opposite side opens. The gap was even before the first tack. Which cause explains this movement best?", "options": {"A": "Weld metal contracts as it cools, pulling the joint toward each new tack", "B": "The pipe expands permanently when tacked, so the gap opens near the last tack made", "C": "Tack welds contain more hydrogen, so they pull gaps open in the direction of travel", "D": "The pipe stops moving after the first tack, so any later change is a measurement error"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weld metal contracts as it cools, pulling the joint toward each new tack): Each tack contracts on cooling and pulls adjacent metal with it, so sequencing and spacing matter. Alternating tack locations keeps the gap even. Hydrogen content does not drive this distortion, and the pipe certainly keeps moving."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A stainless steel pipe is being welded with GTAW, and the inside of the joint will be exposed to hot air during the root pass unless something is done. What is the purpose of purging the pipe interior with argon before and during the root pass?", "options": {"A": "It displaces air from the bore so the root backside does not oxidize or sugar", "B": "It cools the bore so the root pass can be welded without preheat", "C": "It pushes the molten root outward to form a larger crown on the outside surface", "D": "It removes mill scale from the outer surface before the cap pass begins on the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It displaces air from the bore so the root backside does not oxidize or sugar): Without a purge gas, hot stainless steel on the back of the root combines with oxygen and forms a rough, heavily oxidized surface called sugaring. Purging does not replace preheat or clean the outside surface."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder prepares a GTAW root on a stainless pipe, installs dams at each end, and plans to start welding as soon as argon begins flowing into the bore. What should the welder do first to prevent oxidation on the root backside?", "options": {"A": "Purge long enough to replace the air, then verify low oxygen before striking an arc", "B": "Weld immediately because argon is heavier than air and sweeps itself clear in seconds", "C": "Close the vent fully so pressure builds high enough to hold the puddle up", "D": "Switch to a higher purge flow only after the first inch of root has been deposited"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Purge long enough to replace the air, then verify low oxygen before striking an arc): Purging needs enough time to exchange the volume of the pipe, and the oxygen level should be checked, often with an analyzer. Welding right away traps air in the bore. Sealing the vent can build pressure that blows out the molten root."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A GTAW root pass on stainless pipe shows a concave, pushed-in root, and the welder suspects the purge setup. The purge was started with the vent nearly closed and the flow set high. Which observation would most strongly support too much purge gas pressure as the cause?", "options": {"A": "The root shows heavy buildup hanging in the bore with a bright metallic surface throughout", "B": "The backside is dark gray throughout with a uniform grain structure and no depression", "C": "The tack welds look discolored in the same shade on both the outside and the inside", "D": "The root surface is pushed inward toward the arc side, leaving a visible depression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The root surface is pushed inward toward the arc side, leaving a visible depression): Excess internal pressure can push the puddle toward the arc side, leaving a concave root that looks like suck-back. Dark gray color points to poor purge quality or air in the bore, not to too much pressure."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A hot pass is run at a higher current than the root pass on a carbon steel pipe weld made with SMAW. The class discusses why the hot pass exists in the sequence of passes. What is one common benefit of the hot pass after an SMAW root?", "options": {"A": "It replaces the grinding step by removing the root bead entirely from the bore", "B": "It melts away trapped slag and smooths root edges so later passes fuse cleanly", "C": "It protects the cap pass from cracking by cooling the groove below room temperature", "D": "It adds the final crown height so that no cap pass is needed after it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It melts away trapped slag and smooths root edges so later passes fuse cleanly): Because the hot pass uses more heat while the root is still warm, it floats out slag and washes in the sharp edges of the root. It does not remove the root, cool the groove, or take the place of a cap."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder finishes the root pass on a 6 inch carbon steel pipe, then lets it cool and skips cleaning before the hot pass. Slag lines appear in the hot pass bead. Which practice would have prevented these inclusions on the next joint?", "options": {"A": "Increase the travel speed so the slag has no time to form behind the arc itself", "B": "Use a longer arc length so the slag floats off before it can solidify", "C": "Chip and wire brush the root bead, especially the toes, before the hot pass", "D": "Apply a thick flux paste to the root so it neutralizes the slag layer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chip and wire brush the root bead, especially the toes, before the hot pass): Slag left in the toes of the root bead can be trapped by the next pass, so cleaning between passes is required with SMAW. Speed and arc length do not remove slag that has already solidified."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A student finishing the last layer of a groove weld on a 1/2 inch plate asks the instructor what the final layer is meant to do for the whole joint. What is the primary job of the cap pass, also called the cover pass?", "options": {"A": "It fuses the two root faces together at the very bottom of the groove", "B": "It burns out slag pockets left in the root corners", "C": "It holds the pipe ends in alignment before any other weld is made", "D": "It fills the groove to the surface with a uniform face that blends in"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It fills the groove to the surface with a uniform face that blends in): The cap completes the weld by filling the groove and giving a uniform crown with smooth toes. Fusing the root faces is the root pass and aligning the pipe ends is the job of the tacks."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder runs the cap pass on a V-groove pipe joint and finds the finished cap is only as wide as the groove, with the toes sitting right at the bevel edges and slight undercut visible. Which cap profile should a typical code-based visual inspection expect instead?", "options": {"A": "A cap that sits below the pipe surface so it stays under the base metal thickness", "B": "A very tall, narrow cap that adds as much strength to the joint as possible", "C": "A cap that leaves the bevel edges exposed so the inspector sees the fusion lines", "D": "A cap that extends slightly past the groove edges with a modest, even reinforcement height"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A cap that extends slightly past the groove edges with a modest, even reinforcement height): Codes want the cap to cover the groove edges with limited reinforcement, since too little causes underfill and too much creates stress risers. A recessed cap is underfilled, and a tall narrow one fails visual limits."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A pipe cap pass has a high crown, sharp toes and rolled-over edges, and the welder argues that the extra height makes the weld stronger. A senior welder disagrees on a fatigue-loaded pipe. Which reason best supports the senior welder's view?", "options": {"A": "Extra reinforcement lowers the preheat temperature that the next joint will need for the same grade", "B": "A high crown makes the pipe stiffer, which allows a thinner root pass elsewhere", "C": "Excess reinforcement with sharp toes concentrates stress, so it is held to code limits", "D": "Tall caps cool faster and therefore remove all residual stress from the whole joint afterward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Excess reinforcement with sharp toes concentrates stress, so it is held to code limits): A tall crown with abrupt toes creates stress risers where cracks start, so codes limit reinforcement and require smooth blending. Added height does not remove residual stress or reduce preheat needs."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder doing an open-root pipe weld uses a keyhole technique and watches the hole at the leading edge of the puddle. The hole starts to grow larger than the target size as the pipe heats up. What adjustment should the welder make first?", "options": {"A": "Slow down and add current so the hole grows large enough to see the bore", "B": "Stop and weld over the hole with a heavy weave to close it quickly", "C": "Reduce heat or speed up travel slightly so the hole returns to its normal size", "D": "Change to a longer arc length so the puddle spreads over a wider area"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce heat or speed up travel slightly so the hole returns to its normal size): An oversized keyhole means too much heat is going into the root, so the welder trims heat input or moves faster. Slowing down or adding current makes the hole grow and risks burn-through."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 trainee practicing an open-root pass on pipe is told to watch the leading edge of the puddle closely. The instructor asks why the keyhole matters, since it appears only when the root is welded from one side with an open gap. Which statement best describes why a keyhole is useful during that pass?", "options": {"A": "It shows that the pipe has been purged of all air before welding", "B": "It proves that the cap pass will have the correct crown height", "C": "It gives a visible sign that the arc is melting through both root faces", "D": "It confirms that the tack welds were made with the correct filler wire or rod"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It gives a visible sign that the arc is melting through both root faces): A keyhole is a small opening at the leading edge of the puddle that shows full melt-through of the root faces. It says nothing about purge quality, tack filler, or the cap crown."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "One welder sets a 30 degree bevel on each pipe end with a 1/16 inch land. A second welder uses the same bevel but removes the land entirely, hoping to make the root pass easier on a 3/8 inch wall pipe. Which concern is most likely with the second preparation?", "options": {"A": "The root will have excess reinforcement since the groove is wider at the bottom", "B": "The cap pass cannot be completed because the bevel is too shallow overall for filler", "C": "The tack welds will crack because a land is needed to hold the filler metal", "D": "Knife-edge roots melt away quickly, so burn-through and uneven root width become likely"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Knife-edge roots melt away quickly, so burn-through and uneven root width become likely): A land supports the root pass by giving the arc some thickness to melt. Without it, the thin edges burn off and the root width varies. The land has no effect on whether the cap can be completed."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A fabrication shop bevels and fits pipe in a jig, then tacks at four points for a spool with tight length tolerances. A welder complains the joint distorts when welded in one continuous direction around the circumference. Which sequencing change best controls the distortion?", "options": {"A": "Weld all the way around in one direction so the shrinkage is concentrated at the end", "B": "Add heat to the opposite side after welding to stretch the pipe back to straight", "C": "Remove the tacks first so the pipe can shift freely and relieve all stress", "D": "Use a balanced sequence, such as alternating sides of the pipe, so shrinkage forces offset"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use a balanced sequence, such as alternating sides of the pipe, so shrinkage forces offset): Alternating the weld sequence balances the shrinkage forces and keeps the pipe aligned. Welding one direction piles distortion up at the end, and removing tacks gives up the alignment the jig provided."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "The SENSE Level 2 pipe knowledge exam is written without naming a specific welding process, so candidates trained on different processes sit the same questions. Which kind of content stays the same no matter which process the candidate is trained on?", "options": {"A": "Exact amperage settings for each electrode brand and diameter used in a given shop or school", "B": "Wire feed speed charts for a single machine model made by one maker", "C": "Shielding gas flow readings that only apply to one specific nozzle size", "D": "Joint preparation, positions and pass sequence terms that apply to every process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Joint preparation, positions and pass sequence terms that apply to every process): The pipe module tests concepts like bevels, root openings, positions and pass names, which carry across processes. Machine settings and brand-specific charts change with the equipment, so they are not what the exam covers."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A candidate trained on SMAW and another trained on GTAW both sit the same Level 2 pipe knowledge exam. The test asks about root opening, bevel angle and weld position. Why can both candidates answer these questions from their own training without any process-specific study?", "options": {"A": "Both processes use identical electrodes, so the same filler knowledge covers every question", "B": "The exam only asks about shielding gas, which both processes use in the same way", "C": "These topics describe the joint and the position, not the process that deposits the metal", "D": "The exam is written for GTAW only and the SMAW candidate is expected to guess"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (These topics describe the joint and the position, not the process that deposits the metal): Joint geometry and weld position are independent of how the metal is deposited, so any trained welder can answer. SMAW does not use shielding gas or the same filler as GTAW, so those statements are wrong."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A teacher builds a pipe unit for students who will be tested on one process in the shop but on a process-neutral knowledge exam on paper. The unit must fit a limited number of lab hours, so planning matters. Which teaching plan best prepares students for both assessments?", "options": {"A": "Teach only machine settings so students can score well on any process test they meet", "B": "Teach prep, fit-up, positions and pass names with every process, then add process settings separately", "C": "Skip joint preparation because the knowledge exam does not cover it", "D": "Teach the exam in the shop only and avoid any written practice questions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Teach prep, fit-up, positions and pass names with every process, then add process settings separately): A plan that builds the process-neutral foundations once and then layers process details covers both the exam and the shop test. Settings alone miss what the exam asks, and joint prep is central to pipe work."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "In a Level 2 fabrication module, students build a simple structural frame from a drawing supplied by the instructor, and the class lists the steps in order. The instructor wants the correct order before anyone strikes an arc or cuts any steel. Which task normally comes first in the build sequence?", "options": {"A": "Reading the drawing, then laying out and cutting the members to size", "B": "Welding the cap passes on every joint before checking the layout against the drawing", "C": "Painting the finished frame so the weld areas are protected", "D": "Grinding the weld toes smooth before the parts are cut"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reading the drawing, then laying out and cutting the members to size): Fabrication starts with understanding the drawing, then layout and cutting, then fit-up and welding. Painting and finish grinding belong at the end of the sequence."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "Students assemble a small pipe rack in a fabrication project. The drawing shows four welded joints and an overall length tolerance, and the first attempt came out long on one end. Which approach to weld order best helps hold the overall length within tolerance?", "options": {"A": "Fully weld the first joint before any tacks are made on the other joints", "B": "Weld all four joints from one end toward the other without any tacks", "C": "Tack the full assembly first, then weld in a balanced order so shrinkage is spread evenly", "D": "Skip tacks and measure the length only after the whole project is complete and cooled down"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tack the full assembly first, then weld in a balanced order so shrinkage is spread evenly): Tacking the full assembly locks the geometry, and a balanced welding order stops shrinkage from piling up at one end. Fully welding one joint first lets the rest of the frame drift."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A student fabrication project has pipe supports that must line up with holes in a base plate. The student cuts and welds the pipe stands first and drills the plate later, and several stands do not match the holes. Which change in planning would have prevented this problem?", "options": {"A": "Check the drawing dimensions and dry fit the parts together before final welding", "B": "Use a larger electrode so the stands can be pulled into the holes by weld shrinkage", "C": "Weld all stands at once and remove the plate if the holes do not match", "D": "Skip measuring because modern fabrication stays within tolerance on its own"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check the drawing dimensions and dry fit the parts together before final welding): A dry fit against the drawing catches layout and hole-position errors before they are locked in with welds. Shrinkage from a bigger electrode is not a reliable way to adjust alignment."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A Level 2 student welds a 2G test plate in the morning and a 5G pipe coupon in the afternoon, and the instructor says the same open-root V-groove skills are needed for both coupons. Which skill carries over most directly from the plate test to the pipe test?", "options": {"A": "Controlling the root puddle so both faces fuse without sagging or burn-through", "B": "Rotating the work to keep the weld in the flat position the whole time", "C": "Using a longer arc on the pipe because the groove is wider than on plate", "D": "Skipping interpass cleaning because pipe has smaller groove surfaces than plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Controlling the root puddle so both faces fuse without sagging or burn-through): Both tests demand controlling the root puddle against gravity, which is the common skill. Neither lets the welder rotate the work, and interpass cleaning is needed for both."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder on a 5G pipe stops partway through the root to check a tack, then restarts the root. The restart shows a pinhole and a slight cold lap. Which restart practice would best reduce the chance of this defect on the next pass?", "options": {"A": "Restart on a cold surface with a long arc so the puddle fills the gap", "B": "Skip grinding and strike the arc directly on the crater without any overlap", "C": "Grind a feathered taper at the stop, restart on it and overlap the previous bead", "D": "Increase the amperage a great deal so the old crater melts through to the bore"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grind a feathered taper at the stop, restart on it and overlap the previous bead): A feathered stop gives the new bead something to fuse into, and overlapping the previous bead completes the tie-in. Striking on an untouched crater or a cold start leaves lack of fusion."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "A welder is told that a fixed pipe test starts near the bottom and is welded upward on both sides to the top using SMAW with a vertical-up root. Which travel direction best matches this practice on each half of the pipe?", "options": {"A": "Downhill from the twelve o'clock position toward six o'clock on each half", "B": "Straight around the full circumference without stopping at either half of the pipe", "C": "Across the pipe from the three o'clock position to the nine o'clock position", "D": "Uphill from the six o'clock position toward twelve o'clock on each half"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uphill from the six o'clock position toward twelve o'clock on each half): The standard approach is to start near the bottom and travel uphill on each half, which gives better penetration control than downhill travel. Welding straight around without a stop is not the pattern described."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "level_2_plate_and_pipe_welding", "question": "When an instructor describes the passes on a multi-pass pipe groove in a Level 2 class, students hear the term hot pass used for the layer right after the root, and they must write a definition. What does the term hot pass refer to?", "options": {"A": "The pass made last, usually at lower current, to give the weld its final shape", "B": "The pass made before tacking, usually to preheat the pipe ends evenly", "C": "The pass made right after the root, usually at higher current, to clean up the root", "D": "The pass made on the outside only, usually to cool the weld and root together"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The pass made right after the root, usually at higher current, to clean up the root): A hot pass follows the root while it is still warm and uses more heat to clean up the root and add strength. The final shaped layer is the cap, and tacking comes before any pass is made."}];
